Abagtha,m,אֲבַגְתָא(Ancient Hebrew),Biblical,,,,,,,,"Etymology unknown, possibly derived from a prefixed form of the Old Persian name Bagadata. Other sources claim it may mean ""fortunate"", from the Avestan word gabata ""fortunate one"" or Sanskrit baga ""fortune"", or (less likely) ""father of the wine press"" from Hebrew אָב (ʾav) meaning ""father"" and גת (gat) meaning ""winepress"" or ""wine vat"". In the Book of Esther, this is the name of one of the seven eunuchs of the court of King Ahasuerus of Persia."
Abahai,,ᠠᠪᡴᠠᡳ(Manchu)|阿巴亥(Chinese),Manchu,,,,,,,,"Possibly from the Manchu abdaha (""leaf""). This was the personal name of Nurhaci's fourth primary consort, who was given the posthumous name Empress Xiaoliewu. The usage of this name to refer to Hong Taiji is apparently now considered to have been mistaken on the part of Western scholars, he is never recorded in contemporary sources as bearing this name."
