Abderos,m,Ἄβδηρος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Uncertain etymology, probably a hellenised form of a Phoenician name. Alternatively, could mean ""the man with the whip"", from Hesychian Greek ἄβδης (abdes) meaning ""whip, scourge"". In Greek mythology, Abderos was considered a divine hero as a son of the god Hermes, or in some accounts of Poseidon or Thromius. He is most notably known for his tragic role in Heracles' eighth labour, where he was eaten by the Mares of Diomedes."
