Abenámar,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Abenámar is the main character of El romance de Abenámar anonimously written and set in 1431. Abenámar is either King Yusuf IV (Abenalmao) himself or one of his courtiers. The poem is a dialogue between Abenámar and King John II of Castille, who wishes for complete possession of Granada (that occurs only in 1492)."
Abenanka,f,アベナンカ(Ainu Katakana),Ainu,/a.be.nan.ka/,,,,,,,"Probably from アベ (abe) meaning ""fire"" and ナンカ (nanka), combined from ナン (nan) meaning ""face"" and カ (ka) meaning ""top."""
Abenchara,f,,"Spanish (Canarian, Rare)",/a.βen.ˈt͡ʃa.ɾa/(Spanish),,,,,,,"Of Guanche origin, possibly from *abănăšar(a) meaning ""great tear"" or ""great separation"". This was the name of the wife of Tenesor, the last guanarteme or king of Gáldar on the island of Gran Canaria, during the European conquest of the Canary Islands in the late 15th century. She was also the mother of Guayarmina."
Abentín,m,,"Aragonese (Rare, Archaic)",a-ben-TIN,,,,,,,Aragonese form of Aventinus.
Abeona,f,,Roman Mythology,,,,,,,,"Abeona is the ancient Roman goddess of outward journeys, departures, and safe travels. She is best known for protecting children taking their first steps, as well as watching over individuals as they leave their homes or comfort zones for the first time."
Abequa,f,,Ojibwe,,,,,,,,Variant of Abeque.
Abeque,f,,Ojibwe,,,,,,,,"Possibly means ""she stays at home"" in Ojibwe, from Ojibwe abi ""s/he is at home, sits in a certain place"" and ishkwii ""s/he stays behind"" or nazhikewabi/anzhikewabi ""s/he lives alone, is home alone, sits alone""."
