Aberria,f,,Basque (Archaic),[a.β̞e̞.ri.a],,,,,,,"Coined in the 19th century by Sabino Arana Goiri who based it on Basque aberri ""fatherland; homeland"" (ultimately derived from Basque aba ""father"" and herri ""country; village; people, nation""). The name was in use until 1939. After the Spanish Civil War, however, women answering to Aberria were forced to change their name due to its ""conflict-laden meaning""."
Abertha,f,,Welsh,Aberrtha,,,,,,,"Means ""sacrifice"" in ancient Welsh."
Aberthol,m,,Arthurian Cycle (Modern)|Popular Culture,,,,,,,,"Means ""sacrificial"" in Welsh, from aberth ""sacrifice"". This was the name of an evil druid in the movie 'Arthur and Merlin' (2015)."
Aberu,,愛鈴(Japanese Kanji)|あべる(Japanese Hiragana)|アベル(Japanese Katakana),"Japanese (Modern, Rare)",/ä.be̞.ɾɯ̟ᵝ/,,,,,,,"Transliterated form of Abel (for boys), in use among the Japanese in recent years.For girls, it can also be written into kanji as 愛鈴 with 愛 (ai, ito.shii, kana.shii, me.deru, o.shimu, mana, a) meaning ""love, affection"" and 鈴 (rei, rin, suzu, beru), read like the English word bell, which is what that kanji means.It is very rarely used."
Aberuagba,,,Yoruba,,,,,,,,"Means ""the one who respects elders"" in Yoruba, derived from a meaning ""one person"", bẹrù meaning ""fear, be afraid"" and àgbà meaning ""elder""."
Aberycusgentylis,m,,Obscure,,,,,,,,"Aberycusgentylis Balthropp, baptized 25 January 1648 in Iver, Buckinghamshire, England, was named in honour of the Italian-born Oxford professor and jurist Alberico Gentili (1552-1608) via the Latinized form of his name: Albericus Gentilis."
Abesa,m,აბესა(Georgian),Georgian (Archaic),,,,,,,,Short form of Abesalom.
Abesalom,m,აბესალომ(Georgian),Georgian|Literature|Theatre,,,,,,,,"Georgian form of Abessalom, which is the hellenized form of the Hebrew name 'Avshalom (see Absalom).In Georgian literature, Abesalom is the name of a central character from the medieval Georgian folk epic Eteriani. He also features in the opera that was based on the tale, namely Abesalom da Eteri (1919) by Zacharia Paliashvili (1871-1933)."
