Abida,m,אֲבִידָע(Ancient Hebrew),Biblical,,,,,,,,"Interpreted as meaning ""my father took knowledge"", ""the father knows"" or ""father of knowledge"", derived from Hebrew אָב (ʾav) meaning ""father"" combined with י (i), the letter yod (which probably turns אָב (ʾav) into a possessive form, meaning either ""my father"" or ""father of"", depending on the context) and the verb ידע (yada') meaning ""to know"" (as well as ""to have intimate relations""). This name is mentioned in the Old Testament as belonging to a grandson of Abraham and Keturah through their son Midian. He had four brothers: Ephah, Epher, Hanoch and Eldaah."
