Abijam,m,אֲבִיָּם(Ancient Hebrew),Biblical,,,,,,,,"From Hebrew אֲבִיָּם (ʼĂḇīyyām) meaning ""my father is Yam"" or ""my father is the sea"" (or ""father of the sea""), derived from אָב (ʾav) ""father"" and ים (yam) meaning ""sea"" (which was also used to mean ""west"" (i.e. seaward) as well as ""the future"", as 'east' corresponds to 'past'); alternatively the last element could be the name of Yam, a Semitic god of the sea. This name was borne by the second king of Judah (who was also known as Abijah)."
