Abundance,f,,"English (Puritan, Rare)",,,,,,,,"From the English word, ultimately from Latin abundantia ""fullness, plenty"". This name was used in the 17th century by Puritans, referring to the abundance of God's blessings."
Abundância,f,,Portuguese (Archaic),,,,,,,,Portuguese form of Abundantia.
Abundancia,f,,Roman Mythology (Hispanicized),,,,,,,,"Spanish form of Abundantia. This is also the Spanish word for ""abundance""."
Abundâncio,m,,Portuguese (Archaic),,,,,,,,Portuguese form of Abundantius.
Abundancio,m,,Spanish,,,,,,,,Spanish form of Abundantius.
Abundancja,f,,"History (Ecclesiastical, Polonized)",a-boon-DAN-tsya(Polish),,,,,,,Polish form of Abundantia.
Abundancjusz,m,,"History (Ecclesiastical, Polonized)",,,,,,,,Polish form of Abundantius.
Abundantia,f,,Roman Mythology|Late Roman|Italian,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Abundantius. She was the Roman personification of abundance, prosperity and good fortune, portrayed as distributing grain and money from a cornucopia. (The mythological character has survived in French folklore as Lady Hobunde.) The name was also borne by an Italian saint martyred during the persecutions of emperor Diocletian."
Abundantius,m,,Late Roman,,,,,,,,"Derived from Latin abundans ""overflowing, abundant"". This was the name of several Christian saints."
Abundanzio,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Abundantius.
Abundi,m,აბუნდი(Georgian),Basque (Archaic)|Catalan (Archaic)|Georgian (Archaic),,,,,,,,"Basque, Catalan and Georgian form of Abundius."
Abundia,f,,Spanish (Rare),/a.ˈbun.dja/,,,,,,,Feminine form of Abundio.
Abúndio,m,,Portuguese,,,,,,,,Portuguese form of Abundius.
Abungu,f,,Luo,,,,,,,,"Means ""of the forest"" in Luo."
Abu Quhafa,m,أبو قحافة(Arabic),Arabic,[ʔa.bu‿.qu.ˈħaː.fa],,,,,,,"Means ""father of Quhafa"" in Arabic. The name Quhafa is of unknown meaning. Abu Quhafa was the father of the first Rashidun caliph, Abu Bakr (Abd Allah). His name was in fact a kunya (a nickname) using Abu, his real name is Uthman ibn Amir 1 ibn Amr."
Aburu,m,,Dagbani,,,,,,,,"One of the royal gates of Dagbong Kingdom denoting ""Corrupted name of Abdulai"""
Abusaid,m,Абусаид(Chechen),Chechen,,,,,,,,Combination of Abu and Said.
Abuse-not,f,,English (Puritan),/ə.ˈbjuːs.nɒt/,,,,,,,"In reference to 1 Corinthians 9:18, ""What is my reward then? Verily that, when I preach the gospel, I may make the gospel of Christ without charge, that I abuse not my right in the gospel."""
Abush,m,,Oromo,,,,,,,,"Means, 'little boy' often used to denote the youngest child of a family."
Abusolt,m,Абусолт(Chechen),Chechen,,,,,,,,"Derived from Arabic أَبُو (ʾabū) meaning ""father of"" combined with the title سُلْطَان (sulṭān) meaning ""Sultan, king, ruler""."
Abusupyan,m,Абусупьян(Chechen),Chechen|Dagestani,,,,,,,,"Derived from Arabic أَبُو (ʾabū) meaning ""father of"" combined with the name Supyan."
Abuto,f,,Luo,,,,,,,,"Means ""I have hidden"" in Luo."
Abu'u,f,,Tsou,/a.ɓu.ʔu/,,,,,,,Not available.
Abuy,m,,Romani (Archaic),,,,,,,,Diminutive of Abraham.
Abuya,m,,Ancient Hebrew,,,,,,,,Variant of Abijah.
Abuyazid,m,Абуязид(Chechen),Chechen,,,,,,,,"From Arabic أبو (ʾabū) meaning ""father"" and زَادَ (zāda) ""to add, to increase""."
Abuyazit,m,Абуязит(Chechen),Chechen,,,,,,,,Variant of Abuyazid.
Abuzər,m,,Azerbaijani,,,,,,,,Azerbaijani form of Abuzar.
Abuzar,m,أبو ذر(Arabic),Arabic,/ʔa.buː.ˈðarr/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Arabic أبو ذر (see Abu Dharr).
Abuzer,m,,Turkish,,,,,,,,Turkish form of Abu Dharr.
Åbwin,m,,Walloon,/ɔ.ˈbwɛ̃/,,,,,,,Walloon form of Aubin.
Aby,m,,Malayalam,EBI,,,,,,,Diminutive of Abraham.
Abyad,m,,Jewish|Judeo-Arabic,,,,,,,,Refers to a person with fair skin from the Arabic word abyad.
Abyalat,f,,Amharic,,,,,,,,Not available.
Abyan,m,,Somali,ab-yan,,,,,,,"From the Somali word Abyee which translates to ""to complete or to perfect"""
Abyasa,m,,Indonesian Mythology|Javanese,/a.ˈbjɔ.sɔ/(Javanese),,,,,,,Javanese form of Vyasa.
Abye,m,አቢይ(Ge'ez),Ge'ez,,,,,,,,"Means ""big, large"" in Ge'ez."
Abygael,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",/ˈæb.ɪ.ɡeɪl/,,,,,,,Variant of Abigail.
Abygail,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)|Filipino (Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Abigail.
Abygale,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",/ˈæb.ɪ.ɡeɪl/,,,,,,,Variant of Abigail.
Abygayl,f,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",/ˈæb.ɪ.ɡeɪl/(American English),,,,,,,Variant of Abigail.
Abyl,m,Абыл(Kazakh),Kazakh,,,,,,,,Kazakh form of Abul. This name is also frequently used as a short form of Abylay.
Abylai,m,Абылай(Kazakh)|ابىلاي(Kazakh Arabic),Kazakh,,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Abylay.
Abylay,m,Абылай(Kazakh),Kazakh,,,,,,,,"Combination of Abyl with the Kazakh noun ай (ay) meaning ""moon"". As such, the meaning of this name is basically ""father of the moon"".This name is usually given in honour of the Kazakh ruler Abylay Han (1711-1781), who is known in English as Ablai Khan."
Abylaykhan,m,Абылайхан(Kazakh)|ابلايخان(Kazakh Arabic),Kazakh,[ɑ.bɯ.ˈlɑɪ.xɑn],,,,,,,"From the Kazakh personal name Abylay meaning ""father of the moon"" combined with the Turkic title khan (Mongolian: kaan) meaning ""ruler, leader""."
Abyssinia,f,,"English (American, Rare)",/ˌæ.bɪ.ˈsɪ.nɪ.ə/(American English),,,,,,,"From the name of the former name of the Ethiopian Empire or the ancient African region Abyssinia, which is derived from a Latinized form of Arabic الْحَبَشَة (al-ḥabaša) ""Abyssinia"", from حَبَش (ḥabaš) ""Abyssinian"", possibly derived from Egyptian ḫbzwt ""beard""."
Abyzbikä,f,Абызбикә(Bashkir),Bashkir,,,,,,,,"From the Bashkir абыз (abyz) meaning ""educated"" and feminine name element бикә (bikä)."
Abyzou,f,,Near Eastern Mythology,,,,,,,,"Appears to be a corrupted form of the Greek ἄβυσσος ábyssos ""abyss"", the Greek itself was borrowed from Akkadian Apsu or Sumerian Abzu."
Abzal,m,Абзал(Kazakh)|ابزال(Kazakh Arabic),Kazakh,,,,,,,,Kazakh form of Afzal.
Abzari,m,,Arabic|Urdu,,,,,,,,"Means ""seeds, spice, seedsman, one who sows"" in Arabic."
Abzyan,f,Абзян(Udmurt),Udmurt,,,,,,,,"Derived from Arabic أَب‎ (ab) meaning ""father"" and Persian جان‎ (jan) meaning ""soul""."
Əcəbnaz,f,,Azerbaijani (Rare),a-jab-NAHS,,,,,,,"Means ""wonderful coquetry"", ultimately from Arabic عجب ('ajab) meaning ""wonder, amazement"" and Persian ناز (nāz) meaning ""delight, comfort, coquetry, affectation""."
Acacallide,f,,Greek Mythology (Italianized),,,,,,,,Italian form of Acacallis.
Acacallis,f,Ἀκακαλλίς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"This was a word for the narcissus flower used in Crete. It is said to mean ""unwalled"" in Greek, possibly from the Greek negative prefix α (a) with an element related to ἀκή (ake) ""point, edge"". In Greek mythology this name was borne by a Cretan princess, the daughter of Minos, who was loved by the god Apollo and bore him sons: Miletus, Amphithemis and Garamas. This character was called Ἀκάλλη (Akalle) by Apollodorus, a name likely derived from Greek ἀκαλλής (akalles) meaning ""without charms""."
Acace,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,French form of Akakios via Acacius.
Açach,m,,Medieval Jewish|Medieval Spanish,,,,,,,,Medieval Judeo-Spanish form of Isaac attested in Navarre.
Acachimal,m,,Nahuatl,,,,,,,,"Means ""reed shield"" in Nahuatl, from acatl ""reed, cane"" and chimalli ""shield""."
Acácia,f,,Portuguese (Brazilian),/a.ˈka.si.ɐ/(Brazilian Portuguese),,,,,,,Portuguese form of Acacia.
Acácio,m,,Portuguese,,,,,,,,Portuguese form of Akakios.
Acacio,m,,History (Ecclesiastical)|Spanish (Rare),,,,,,,,Spanish and Italian form of Akakios.
Acacus,m,Ἄκακος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"From Greek ἄκακος (akakos) meaning ""harmless, guileless, innocent"", ultimately derived from ἀ (a), a negative prefix, combined with κάκη (kake) meaning ""evil"" (see Akakios). In Greek mythology, Acacus, a son of Lycaon, founded the city of Acacesium and raised the infant Hermes there."
Academus,m,"Ἀκάδημος, Ἑκάδημος(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek,/ˌæ.kə.ˈdiː.məs/(English),,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Ἀκάδημος (Akademos), of uncertain etymology. The first element is probably the same as in ἑκάεργος (hekaergos), which means ""far-working, striking from afar"" or ""free-working"". This was the name of a legendary Athenian hero, and a site sacred to Athena in Athens was supposedly named after him. This site was later used by Plato as the grounds for a gymnasium and school, and the English word academy ultimately derives from it. This gave rise to the folk etymology of ""silent district"", drawing from ἀκή (ake) ""silence"" and δῆμος (demos) ""district, country; the people""."
Acadia,f,,"American (Rare)|Spanish (Mexican, Rare)",ə-KAY-dee-ə(American),,,,,,,"From the name of a colony in New France in North America, derived from Arcadia and coinciding with Mi'kmaq suffix -akadie, meaning ""place of abundance"". This is also the name of a National Park in Maine."
Acadie,f,,French (Rare),/a.ka.di/,,,,,,,French form of Acadia.
Acai,,,"English (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"From the name of the Açaí palm; derived from Old Tupi asa'y or ybasa'y, meaning ""fruit that expels water""."
Acaic,m,,Catalan,,,,,,,,Catalan form of Achaikos (via Achaicus).
Acaico,m,,Italian,ah-KAH-ee-koh,,,,,,,Italian form of Achaikos.
Acaime,m,,Guanche,a-KIE-me,,,,,,,"From Guanche *akăyəm, meaning ""small""."
Acaimo,m,,"Guanche|Spanish (Canarian, Rare)","a-KIE-mo(Guanche, Canarian Spanish)",,,,,,,Variant of Acaime.
Acaire,m,,Provençal|Niçard,,,,,,,,Niçard form of Acarius.
Acala,f,"अचल(Hindi, Sanskrit)","Indian (Modern, Rare)|Sanskrit (Modern, Rare)",AH cah lah,,,,,,,"Means ""immovable, steady; constant; unceasing"", from Sanskrit a ""without"" and cala ""moving""."
Acallaris,f,Ἀκαλλαρίς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"This was the name of the wife of King Tros in some accounts of Greek mythology, making her the queen of Troy."
Acalle,f,Ἀκάλλη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Form of Acacallis found in the Bibliotheca, a compendium of Greek myths."
Acamapichtli,m,,Nahuatl|Aztec,/aː.ka.maː.ˈpit͡ʃ.t͡ɬi/(Classical Nahuatl),,,,,,,"Means ""a handful of reed arrows"". Name borne by several rulers of Tenochtitlan."
Acamar,,,Astronomy,/ˈæ.kə.mɑːr/,,,,,,,"Derived from Arabic Ākhir an-nahr, meaning ""end of the river"". This is the traditional name of the star Theta Eridani in the constellation Eridanus."
Acamas,m,Ἀκάμας(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Akamas. This was the name of several characters in Greek mythology, one of whom was the son of Theseus and Phaedra. According to Virgil, he took part in the Trojan War and was one of the men who hid inside the Trojan horse and was thus smuggled into the city."
Acamitl,m,,Nahuatl,,,,,,,,"Means ""reed arrow"" in Nahuatl, from acatl ""reed"" and mitl ""arrow, dart""."
Acan,m,,Mayan Mythology|Alur,ah-CON|ə-SHAHN,,,,,,,"Means ""groan"". This is the name of the Mayan God of wine and celebration."
Acanorex,m,,Taíno,,,,,,,,"Acanorex was a Taíno cacique (chief) on Hispaniola, a Caribbean island."
Acanthe,,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of both Acantha and Acanthus.
Acanthis,f,Ἀκανθίς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Literature,ə-KAN-this,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Ἀκανθίς (Akanthis), which was the name of a type of finch, known as thistle finch in English, literally meaning ""prickly"" from Greek ἄκανθα (akantha) ""thorn, prickle, thistle"" (compare Acantha, Acanthus). In Greek mythology this was the name of a sister of Anthus; after her brother was eaten by a horse, she and her family members were all transformed by Zeus into birds, with Acanthis becoming the thistle finch. This was also used by the 1st-century BC Roman poet Propertius for an old witch in one his poems."
Acanthus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),AH-kan-thus(Greek Mythology)|/ə.ˈkæn.θəs/(English),,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Άκανθος (Akanthos), which is derived from Greek ακανθος (akanthos), the name of a plant with large spiny leaves. The plant ultimately derives its name from Greek ακη (ake) ""thorn, point"" and Greek ἄνθος (anthos) ""flower, blossom"". Also see Acantha. This was the name of several characters in Greek mythology, including a son of Autonous and Hippodamia who was named after the acanthus bush, a plant common in his infertile homeland. Acanthus was also the name of multiple ancient Greek cities. Finally, in real life, this name was borne by Acanthus of Sparta, a sportsman who participated in the Olympic Games of 720 BC."
Acar,,עָכָר(Ancient Hebrew),Turkish|Ancient Aramaic,ah-JAHR|A-kar(Aramaic),,,,,,,"Means ""fearless"" or ""clever"" in Turkish."
Acard,m,,Anglo-Norman,,,,,,,,Variant of Achard.
Acardio,m,,Spanish (Rare),Ar-ca-dio,,,,,,,Spanish form of Archibald.
Acario,m,,"Italian (Archaic)|History (Ecclesiastical, Italianized)|Theatre|Spanish (Rare, Archaic)",a-KA-ryo(Italian),,,,,,,"Italian and Spanish form of Acharius. The 7th-century Frankish saint Acharius, bishop of Noyon-Tournai, is known as Acario in Italian and Spanish. This was used by Gigio Artemio Giancarli for a character in his play La Zingana (1545). It was also used by Lope de Rueda for one of the main characters in his play Medora (1567)."
Acarius,m,,Frankish (Latinized)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Variant of Acharius. Saint Acarius (died 14 March 642) was bishop of Doornik and Noyon, which today are located on either side of the Franco-Belgian border. He was especially attentive to the poor and afflicted, whose needs he enjoyed relieving and calming their suffering."
Acarnan,m,Ἀκαρνάν(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Ἀκαρνάν (Akarnan), which is derived from Greek ἀκαρνάν (akarnan), which is said to mean ""laurel"", but can also refer to a kind of thistle. In Greek mythology, Acarnan was the son of Alcmaeon. After his father's death he fled to South-Epirus, which was also known as Acarnia, derived from his name."
Acarsu,f,,Turkish,,,,,,,,"Means ""stream, river, running water"" in Turkish."
Acasia,f,,"American (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Acacia.
Acaste,f,Ἀκάστη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),/ə.ˈkæs.tiː/(English),,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Ἀκάστη (Akaste), probably a feminine form of Akastos (see Acastus). This name is borne by two characters in Greek mythology. One was an Oceanid nymph and companion of Persephone, the other was the nurse of the daughters of king Adrastus of Argos."
Acastus,m,Ἄκαστος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),/ə.ˈkæs.təs/(English)|/ˈa.kas.tos/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Ἄκαστος (Akastos), which is possibly derived from Ancient Greek ἄκαστος (akastos) meaning ""maple tree (Acer monspessulanum)"". Alternatively, it could derive from κέκασμαι (kekasmai) meaning ""to excel, to shine"" combined with the intensifier prefix ἀ- (a-) (compare Castor). In Greek mythology, Acastus was the king of Iolcus, the oldest son of Pelias. He was one of the Argonauts."
Acat,,,Mayan|Classic Mayan|Mayan Mythology,,,,,,,,Acat was a deity in Maya mythology associated with the process of tattooing.
Acatl,,,Nahuatl|Mexican,ah-katl(Nahuatl),,,,,,,"Means ""reed, cane"" in Nahuatl. This is the thirteenth day-sign of the tonalpohualli."
Acatlo,m,,Nahuatl (Archaic),,,,,,,,Variant of Acatl.
Acaunissa,f,,Gaulish,,,,,,,,"Derived from Gaulish acaunon or acauno- ""stone""."
Acaxoch,f,,Nahuatl,,,,,,,,"From acaxochitl ""reed flower"", the name of an aquatic plant with red or white flowers."
Acaxochitl,f,,Nahuatl|Mexican (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Acaxoch.
Acaymo,m,,Spanish (Canarian)|Guanche,"a-KIE-mo(Canarian Spanish, Guanche)",,,,,,,Variant of Acaime or else of Akaymo.
Acazio,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Akakios and variant of Acacio.
Acbor,m,,Biblical,,,,,,,,Variant of Achbor.
Acca,f,"അക്കാ, അക്ക(Malayalam)",Roman Mythology|Indian (Christian)|Malayalam|Anglo-Saxon,/ˈɑk.kɑ/(Old English),,,,,,,"In Roman legend this was another name of Larentia, the foster mother of the twins Romulus and Remus, and wife of the shepherd Faustulus. In her honour the Romans celebrated in December a feast called Larentalia or Accalia. This name also occurs in Virgil's Aeneid, belonging to a companion of Camilla."
Accalia,f,,Roman Mythology (?)|English (Rare),ə-KAY-lee-ə(English),,,,,,,"According to questionable sources, such as baby name books and websites, this was another name for Acca, the human foster-mother of Romulus and Remus in Roman legend, also known as Acca Larentia (see Acca). It coincides with the Latin word Accalia referring to a Roman festival celebrating Acca Larentia, meaning literally ""things pertaining to Acca"".A known bearer of this name is Accalia Hipwood, an English radio personality in Dubai."
Accalon,m,,Arthurian Cycle,AK-ə-lahn,,,,,,,Variant of Accolon.
Accamma,f,അക്കാമ്മ(Malayalam),Indian (Christian)|Malayalam,,,,,,,,Form of Rebecca used by Malayalam-speaking Syriac Christians in the Indian state of Kerala.
Accepted,m,,English (Puritan),/æk.ˈsɛp.tɪd/,,,,,,,Referring to being accepted into the Kingdom of God.
Accia,f,,Ancient Roman|English (Rare),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Accius.
Accius,m,,Ancient Roman,AKEEUS(Latin),,,,,,,"Roman nomen perhaps meaning ""a call, summons"" or ""of Acca"". A notable bearer was the tragic poet Lucius Accius."
Acco,m,,Gaulish|History|Medieval German,AH-ko,,,,,,,"Derived from Gaulish *acu- ""swift, quick, fast"". Acco was a chief of the Senones in Gaul, who induced his countrymen to revolt against Julius Caesar in 53 BC."
Accolon,m,,Arthurian Cycle,AK-ə-lahn,,,,,,,"Of uncertain origin, perhaps a derivative of the Gaulish name Acco (itself from Gaulish *acu- meaning ""fast, swift, quick""). Sir Accolon, also spelled Accalon, is a character in Arthurian legends, possibly first appearing in the Post-Vulgate Suite du Merlin (c.1230-40). He is a knight from Gaul and the paramour of Morgan le Fay, who uses him as an unwitting participant in a plot against her half-brother Arthur; sensing the deception, Arthur defeats Accolon in battle. Thomas Malory included the tale in his 15th-century compilation of Arthurian legends Le Morte d'Arthur. Sir Accolon also appears in subsequent modern Arthurian works."
Accorsa,f,,Medieval Italian,,,,,,,,"Italian accorsa from Latin accursia ""aided, helped"".The hypocoristic Corsa was more common than the full form."
Accorso,m,,Medieval Italian,,,,,,,,Masculine form of Accorsa.
Accursa,f,,Sicilian,,,,,,,,Variant of Accursia.
Accursia,f,,Sicilian (Rare),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Accursio.
Accursio,m,,Sicilian (Rare),ak-KOOR-syo,,,,,,,Italian name derived from Accorso and short form of Bonaccurso.
Accursiu,m,,Sicilian,,,,,,,,Variant of Accursio.
Ace,m,,Sundanese,,,,,,,,Variant of Asep.
Acefie,f,,Haitian Creole,/a.se.fi/,,,,,,,Variant of Acephie.
Aceituna,f,,Obscure,/a.θei̯.ˈt̪u.na/(European Spanish)|[a.sei̯.ˈt̪u.na](Latin American Spanish),,,,,,,"Means ""olive"" in Spanish."
Acejandro,m,,"American (Hispanic, Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Likely a combination of Ace 1 and Alejandro.
Acel,f,,Filipino,,,,,,,,The name of the Philippine singer Acel Bisa-Van Ommen. It was probably created from her full given name Maria Cecilia.
Acela,f,,Spanish (Mexican),,,,,,,,Not available.
Aceledor,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"A king in Arthur's service in Floriant et Florete. He joined Arthur's campaign against Maragoz, an enemy"
Aceley,f,,"English (Archaic)|English (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Not available.
Acelia,f,,Old Swedish|Swedish (Rare),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Assela.
Acelin,m,,Medieval French,,,,,,,,Double diminutive of Asce.
Acelina,f,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Variant of Aceline.
Aceline,f,,Medieval French|Medieval English,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Acelin.
Açelya,f,,Turkish,[ɑ.ˈt͡ʃɛl.jɑ],,,,,,,"Derived from Turkish açelya ""azalea""."
Acelyn,f,,"English (American, Modern)",AYS-lin(American English),,,,,,,Variant of Acelynn.
Acelynn,f,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",AYS-lin(English),,,,,,,"Feminine elaboration of Ace 1 using the popular name suffix lyn, possibly inspired by Aislynn or the medieval name Aceline."
Acen,,,English,,,,,,,,Acen:The meaning of the name
Acención,m,,Spanish (Mexican),/a.sen.ˈsjon/(Mexican Spanish),,,,,,,Variant of Ascensión.
Aceng,m,,Sundanese,,,,,,,,Variant of Ujang or Asep.
Acenith,f,,"English (American, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Variant of Asenith.
Acennan,m,,"English (American, Modern)",a-ken-nan(American English),,,,,,,"From the word acennan meaning ""to beget, bring forth, produce"" in Old English. The word acennan is of Old English origin but the name Acennan is modern. Acennan is a character in Warrior of Woden by Matthew Harffy."
Aceolus,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Saint Aceolus of Amiens worked as a sub-deacon who was studying for the priesthood when he was arrested and murdered as part of the persecutions of Emperor Diocletian in 303 near Amiens, France."
Acep,m,,Sundanese,/ˈa.t͡ʃɛp/,,,,,,,Variant of Asep.
Acephie,f,,Haitian Creole,/a.se.fi/,,,,,,,Variant of Asefi.
Acepsima,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Acepsimas (see Akepsimas).
Acépsimas,m,,History (Hispanicized),,,,,,,,Spanish form of Acepsimas.
Acepsimas,m,,"History (Ecclesiastical, Latinized)",,,,,,,,Latinized form of Akepsimas. Saint Acepsimas was a 4th-century bishop of Hnaita in western Persia who was martyred in 376.
Acèpsimes,m,,Catalan,,,,,,,,Catalan form of Acepsimas.
Acer,m,,Jewish,,,,,,,,Medieval variant of Asher.
Acerbus,m,,"English (American, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Means ""Sarcastic, sardonic"" in Latin."
Acerina,f,,Guanche|Spanish (Canarian),,,,,,,,"Derived from Aceró, which is the name of a territorial demarcation in the island of La Palma, meaning ""strong place"". This name was borne by the wife of the Guanche mencey (leader) Tanausú."
Acesander,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Akesandros.
Acesandro,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Acesander.
Acesias,m,Ἀκεσίας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"From Ancient Greek ἄκεσις (akesis) ""healing, cure"". This was apparently the name of a famous Ancient Greek physician, known exclusively through the proverb Ἀκεσίας ἰάσατο (Akesias iasato) meaning ""Acesias cured him"", referring to an ailment that has been made worse through treatment."
Acesius,m,Ἀκέσιος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/ə.ˈsiː.ʒəs/(English),,,,,,,"From Ancient Greek ἄκεσις (akesis) ""healing, cure"". This was an epithet of Apollo in Ellis, as well as the name of a bishop who live during the reign of Constantine."
Aceso,f,Ἀκεσώ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),ə-KAY-so,,,,,,,"From the Greek Ἀκεσώ (Akeso), derived from ἄκεσις (akesis) meaning ""healing, curing"". She was the Greek goddess of healing (wounds) and curing (illness); unlike her sister Panakeia (Panacea), Aceso personified the process of a curing rather than the cure itself."
Acessamenus,m,Ἀκεσσάμενος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Akessamenos.
Acessima,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Acepsima.
Acestes,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Akestes. In Greek mythology, this was the name of a hero of Trojan origin, who founded Segesta on Sicily. In a trial of skill, Acestes shot his arrow with such force that it caught fire. He helped Aeneas when the latter arrived on Sicily after his wanderings."
Acestor,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Akestor. Bearers of this name include two sculptors and a tragic poet.In Greek mythology, this was an epithet of the god Apollo. It was also the name of a character, who was slain by Achilles during the Trojan War."
Acestorides,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Akestorides. A notable bearer of this name was Acestorides of Corinth, who became tyrant of Syracuse in the 4th century BC."
Aceton,m,,"English (Modern, Rare)",/ˈeis.tən/,,,,,,,Combination of Ace 1 and the popular name suffix ton.
Acetyn,m,,"English (Modern, Rare)",/ˈeis.tən/,,,,,,,Variant of Aceton.
