Aekkawit,m,เอกวิทย์(Thai),Thai,/ʔeːk̚˨˩.ka˨˩.ˈwit̚˦˥/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai เอกวิทย์ (see Ekkawit).
Aekkawut,m,เอกวุฒิ(Thai),Thai,/ʔeːk̚˨˩.ka˨˩.ˈwut̚˦˥/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai เอกวุฒิ (see Ekkawut).
Aeko,f,"亜依子, 亜衣子, 安衣子(Japanese Kanji)|アエ子, アヱ子(Kanji/Katakana)|あえこ(Japanese Hiragana)|アエコ(Japanese Katakana)",Japanese (Rare),AH-E-KO,,,,,,,"This name can be used as 亜依子, 亜衣子 or 安衣子 with 亜 (a, tsu.gu) meaning ""Asia, come after, rank next"", 安 (an, yasu, yasu.i, yasu.maru, yasu.raka, a) meaning ""cheap, contented, low, peaceful, quiet, relax, rested"", 依 (i, e, yo.ru) meaning ""consequently, depend on, due to, reliant, therefore"" and 衣 (i, e, kinu, -gi, koromo) meaning ""clothes, dressing, garment.""This name can also be used as アエ子 with アエ (the 2 katakana characters that combine 'Ae') with 子. Alternatively, the 'エ' can be replace by the outdated katakana character 'we'/'ヱ' (pronounced the same as 'エ').This name is very rarely given to girls, if given at all. Also, girls whose name is written as one of the kanji combinations seen above may most likely be called Aiko."
Aël,,,"Breton (Gallicized)|French (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Gallicized form of Ael.
Ael,,,Breton|Ingush,A el,,,,,,,"Derived from Breton ael ""angel"", this is considered the Breton form of Angelus."
Aela,f,,Breton (Modern),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Ael and cognate of Angela.
Aelaina,f,,"American (Modern, Rare)",a-lay-nuh,,,,,,,Variant of Alaina.
Aelan,m,عِلان(Arabic),Arabic|Muslim,,,,,,,,"Possibly a transcription of عِلان meaning ""announcement, proclamation"" in Arabic."
Aelani,f,,Hawaiian,a-E-lani,,,,,,,"Means ""fine sky, delicate"" in Hawaiian from 'ae meaning ""fine, smooth, soft"" and lani meaning ""sky, heaven""."
Aelbert,m,,Medieval English (Anglicized)|Dutch,,,,,,,,Variant of Æthelbert.
Aelbrecht,m,,Medieval Dutch,,,,,,,,Variant of Albrecht.
Ældiet,f,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Variant of Ealdgyð found in Domesday Book.
Aeldrick,m,,Medieval English|Anglo-Saxon|Germanic,AYL-drik(Middle English)|EHL-drik(Middle English),,,,,,,"Variant of either Aldric or Eldric/Eldrick. See also Elric, Æðelric/Æthelric."
Aele,,,East Frisian (Archaic),,,,,,,,Variant of Ale 2 recorded in the 17th century for men and in the 15th and 17th centuries for women in East Frisia.
Aelens,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"Father of Escol, a follower of king Arthur."
Aelesia,f,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Medieval English variant of Alicia.
Aeleva,f,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,"Younger form of Old English Ælfgifu created with the Germanic elements ael meaning ""hall, temple"" and ewa meaning ""ever."" Compare Aelfeva."
Aelez,f,,Breton (Rare),/ˈɛː.les/|/ˈɛː.le/,,,,,,,"Variant of Aela. The name coincides with Breton aelez ""angels""."
Ælfbeorht,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements ælf meaning ""elf"" and beorht meaning ""bright""."
Aelfeva,f,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Younger form of Ælfgifu.
Ælfġeard,m,,Anglo-Saxon,"/ælf,jæ͜ɑrd/(Old English)",,,,,,,"Derived from Old English ælf ""elf"" and ġeard ""yard, enclosure"" (from gardaz). Cognate to Old Norse Álfgerðr, a feminine name in contrast."
Ælfgeat,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Old English cognate of Algautr, derived from the elements ælf meaning ""elf"" and Geat referring to a member of a North Germanic tribe from present-day Götaland in southern Sweden (note, the legendary hero Beowulf was a Geat)."
Ælfhelm,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Composed of the Old English elements ælf ""elf"" and helm ""helmet, protection""."
Ælfhere,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements ælf ""elf"" and here ""army"", making it a cognate of Alfher."
Ælfhun,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from Old English ælf ""elf"" and perhaps hun ""bear cub""."
Ælfmær,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈælf.ˌmæːr/(Old English)|[ˈæɫv.ˌmæːr](Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements ælf meaning ""elf"" and mære ""famous""."
Ælfrun,f,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements ælf ""elf"" and run ""secret, mystery"". It is a cognate of the Old Norse name Alfrún."
Ælfwald,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,Variant of Ælfweald.
Ælfwaru,f,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements ælf ""elf"" and waru ""guard, protection; care, watch"" (compare weard and wær)."
Ælfweald,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements ælf ""elf"" and weald ""leader, ruler""."
Ælfwold,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,Variant of Ælfweald.
Aelgeat,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,Variant of Æthelgeat.
Aelgyth,f,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Variant of Ælfgyð.
Aelhaearn,m,,Welsh,,,,,,,,"Derived from Welsh ael meaning ""(eye)brow"" and haearn ""iron"". This was the name of a 7th-century saint."
Aelhard,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Variant of Adelhard.
Aelhearn,m,,Welsh,,,,,,,,Variant of Aelhaearn.
Aelia,f,,Breton (Modern),E-lya,,,,,,,Variant of Aela.
Aelian,m,,English|History,,,,,,,,English form of Aelianus. A bearer of this name was Claudius Aelianus - often called Aelian in English - a Roman author and philosopher from the 3rd century AD.
Aelid,f,,Manx (Archaic),,,,,,,,Not available.
Aelin,f,"애린, 에린(Korean Hangul)",Literature|Korean,/e.rin/,,,,,,,Possibly inspired by Aylin. It is the name of a character in the 'Throne of Glass' series by Sarah J. Maas.
Aelinde,f,,Medieval French,,,,,,,,Not available.
Aelinor,f,,English (Rare),EL-ə-nawr,,,,,,,Variant of Eleanor.
Aélis,f,,Medieval Occitan (Gallicized)|Occitan (Gallicized),,,,,,,,Occitan form of Aalis.
Aelís,f,,Gascon,,,,,,,,Original Gascon form of Aélis.
Aëlla,f,,Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,Variant of Aella.
Ælle,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈæl.le/(Old English),,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Old English æl- ""all, complete"". It was borne by several Anglo-Saxon kings, including the legendary first king of the South Saxons."
Ælli,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,Sparse records tell of a King Ida of Bernicia and a King Ælli or Ælle of Deira in the middle of the 6th century.
Aellic,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,"Combination of Anglo-Saxon elements ael meaning ""hall, temple"" and lic with the contested meaning of ""like"" or ""body."""
Aellin,f,,Medieval German,,,,,,,,Not available.
Ælling,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the name Ælli, Ælla or Alla combined with the suffix ing."
Aelliott,f,,English (American),,,,,,,,Not available.
Aello,f,Ἀελλώ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/eɪ.ˈɛl.oʊ/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἄελλα (aella) meaning ""whirlwind, tempest, gust of wind"". This was the name of a ""storm-swift"" harpy in Greek mythology. It was also the name of one of Actaeon’s dogs."
Aellopos,f,Ἀελλόπος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Variant of Aellopous. This was the form used by Homer (in his Iliad, as an epithet of the goddess Iris)."
Aellopous,f,Ἀελλόπους(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""swift as a whirlwind"", literally ""whirlwood-footed"", from Greek ἄελλα (aella) meaning ""a stormy wind, whirlwind"" and πούς (pous) ""foot"". This was an epithet of the Greek goddess Iris, the swift messenger of the gods. According to later sources this name belonged to one of the Harpies. (Compare also Aello, Aella.)"
Aellopus,f,Ἀελλόπους(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),ay-EHL-ə-pəs,,,,,,,Latinized form of Aellopous.
Aelm,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Not available.
Aelmer,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Variant of Ethelmaer.
Aelod,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,"Variant of Adelold, possibly a form of Adalwald or Æthelwold."
Aelphaba,f,,Literature,EL-fə-bə,,,,,,,"Variant of Elphaba. In the novel 'Wicked: The Life and Times of the Wicked Witch of the West' (1995), this was the name of a legendary saint, Elphaba's namesake, who disappeared behind a waterfall for hundreds of years to read a book."
Ælric,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Variant of Æthelric or Ælfric; see also Elric.
Aelrun,f,,Medieval English|German (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Alruna.
Ælsi,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Variant of Alsige.
Aelson,m,,Portuguese (Brazilian),,,,,,,,Variant of Élson.
Aeltgen,f,,Dutch (Archaic)|Medieval Dutch|Frisian,AHLT-gen,,,,,,,"Archaic Dutch form of Aaltje, a diminutive of Adelheid."
Aeltje,f,,"Dutch (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Obsolescent variant of Aaltje primarily used in the 1600s and briefly between the 1930s and the 1960s.
Aelwen,f,,Welsh,,,,,,,,"Derived from Welsh ael ""brow"" and gwen ""white; fair; blessed"". This name has been used from the early 20th century onwards."
Aelwenn,f,,"Breton (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Breton form of Aelwen.
Aelwiin,m,,Medieval Dutch,,,,,,,,Variant of Alewijn.
Aëlys,f,,French (Modern)|Folklore,/a.e.lis/,,,,,,,Likely an elaboration of Aël modelled after Maëlys.
Aemelia,f,,English,ə-MEE-lee-ə|ə-MEEL-yə,,,,,,,Alternate spelling of Aemilia. Some versions of Shakespeare's Comedy of Errors spell Aemelia this way.
Ae-mi,f,"애미(Korean Hangul)|愛美, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/ɛ.mi/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 愛 (ae) meaning ""love"" combined with 美 (mi) meaning ""beautiful"". Other hanja combinations can form this name as well."
Aemilian,m,,English|History,,,,,,,,English form of Aemilianus. This name was borne by a Roman Emperor from the 3rd century AD.
Aemily,f,,Obscure,,,,,,,,Likely a variant of Emily.
Aemin,f,에민(Korean Hangul),Korean,Ae-Min,,,,,,,Not available.
Aemma,f,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Created by author George R. R. Martin for a character in his series ""A Song of Ice and Fire"". It is possibly derived from Emma. In the series, Aemma Arryn is the mother of Rhaenyra Targaryen, a claimant to the throne of Westeros."
Aemmi,f,,American (Rare),AY-mee,,,,,,,Variant of Amy.
Aemon,m,,Literature|Popular Culture,,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Amon. This is the name of a character in George R. R. Martin's 'A Song of Ice and Fire' series, as well as the TV show based on the books 'Game of Thrones'."
Aemond,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Created by author George R. R. Martin for a character in his series ""A Song of Ice and Fire"" and the television series ""House of the Dragon"". Prince Aemond is a member of House Targaryen. He is the second son of King Viserys I Targaryen by his second wife, Queen Alicent Hightower, and the younger brother of King Aegon II Targaryen."
Aemylia,f,,Literature|Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"Variant of Aemilia used by Edmund Spenser for a character in his epic poem The Faerie Queene (1590). Aemylia is one of the maidens featured in Book IV, which deals with friendship. She falls in love with Amyas, a squire 'of low degree', and they attempt to elope but are captured."
Aen,,恩(Sawndip),Zhuang,,,,,,,,"Means ""grace"" in Zhuang."
Aena,f,애나(Korean Hangul),Korean,/e.na/,,,,,,,"From 愛(ae) meaning ""love"" combined with 娜(na) meaning ""beautiful, elegant"" or 奈(na) meaning ""apple tree"". Other hanja combinations can also form this name."
Aenar,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Created by author George R. R. Martin for a character in his series ""A Song of Ice and Fire"". In the series, Aenar Targaryen is an ancestor of the Targaryen monarchs in Westeros."
Aendir,m,,Old Norse (Rare),EN-DIR,,,,,,,"From Old Norse endir. Meaning “end”, “ending”, “conclusion”."
Ændrider,m,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,Variant of Eindriði.
Aene,,,Arabic,,,,,,,,Variant transcription of Ain.
Aenea,f,,Literature,i-NEE-ə|ə-NEE-ə,,,,,,,"Possibly intended to be a feminine form of Aeneas, or possibly taken from the Latin word aēneus meaning ""made of copper, made of bronze; brazen"" (feminine aēnea), a derivative of aes ""copper, bronze"". This was used by the author Dan Simmons in his 'Hyperion' series of science fiction novels, where it belongs to a character who first appears in 1996."
Aeneasina,f,,"Obscure (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Perhaps a feminine form of Aeneas
Aenesidemus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Ainesidemos. Known bearers of this name include the ancient Greek philosopher Aenesidemus (1st century BC) and a tyrant of Leontini (5th century BC).
Aenete,f,Αἰνήτη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latin form of the Greek Αἰνήτη (Ainete), derived from Greek αἰνητός (ainetos) meaning ""praiseworthy, deserving praise"", from αἰνέω (aineo) ""to praise"". In Greek mythology Aenete was the wife of Aeneas, the legendary founder of the ancient Thracian city of Aenus."
Aeng,f,앵(Korean Hangul)|櫻(Korean Hanja),Korean,AYNG,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 櫻 (aeng) meaning ""cherry, cherry blossom""."
Aeng-ha,f,"앵하(Korean Hangul)|櫻霞, 櫻夏(Korean Hanja)",Korean,AYNG-HAH,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 櫻 (aeng) meaning ""cherry, cherry blossom"" combined with 霞 (ha) meaning ""mist, haze, rosy clouds"" or 夏 (ha) meaning ""summer"". This name can be formed using other hanja combinations as well."
Aeng-hwa,f,앵화(Korean Hangul)|櫻花(Korean Hanja),Korean,AYNG-HWA,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 櫻 (aeng) meaning ""cherry, cherry blossom"" combined with 花 (hwa) meaning ""flower, bloom, anger""."
Ænglandsfari,m,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,Old Norse variant of Englandsfari.
Ae-ni,f,애니(Korean Hangul),Korean (Rare),/ɛ.ni/,,,,,,,Korean borrowing of Annie.
Aenne,f,,German,,,,,,,,Variant of Anna.
Ænnibrantr,m,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,"Derived from Old Norse enni ""forehead"" and brattr ""steep""."
Aénohe,m,,Cheyenne,,,,,,,,"Means ""hawk, winter hawk"" in Cheyenne."
Aénȯhea'eotse,m,,Cheyenne,ahn-HAY-ə-ay-OOZH,,,,,,,"Means ""attacking/charging hawk"", from the Cheyenne aénohe 'hawk' and -a'eotse 'attack/charge'."
Aénȯhéévé'hȧhtse,m,,Cheyenne,,,,,,,,"Means ""flying hawk"" in Cheyenne."
Aénȯheméóná'e,f,,Cheyenne,,,,,,,,"Means ""hawk trail woman"" in Cheyenne."
Aénȯhenéhovóhe,m,,Cheyenne,,,,,,,,"Means ""chasing hawk"" in Cheyenne."
Aenon,m,,Biblical,,,,,,,,"From a place name mentioned briefly in the New Testament, which may be the Greek form of Hebrew ay-yin ""spring, natural fountain"". The Gospel of John (3:23) identifies it as a place near Salem where John the Baptist performed baptisms.It is occasionally, if rarely so, used as a given name."
Aénor,f,,Breton,,,,,,,,Breton form of the french name Éléonore.
Aenys,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Created by author George R. R. Martin for a character in his series ""A Song of Ice and Fire"". In the series, Aenys Targaryen is the second Targaryen monarch in Westeros."
Æodbaldus,m,,Anglo-Saxon (Latinized),,,,,,,,"A person mentioned in Bede. The form there is Æodbaldum, which is the accusative.Possibly a form of Eadbald."
Aeolia,f,Αἰολία(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),/ai̯.o.lí.aː/(Attic Greek)|/ɛ.o.ˈli.a/(Koine Greek)|/e.o.ˈli.a/(Byzantine Greek)|/iː.ˈəʊl.ɪ.ə/(English),,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Αἰολία (Aiolia), itself derived from αἰόλος (aiolos) meaning ""moving, swift"" (see Aiolos)."
Æon,f,,Popular Culture,EE-ahn,,,,,,,"Used by animator Peter Chung for his character Æon Flux, from the name of a type of spirit being in Gnosticism, an emanation from the Godhead, ultimately from a Proto-Indo-European base meaning ""vital force, life, long life, eternity"".In Greek mythology this was an alternative name of the god Chronos."
Aeon,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Roman Mythology,/ˈae̯.oːn/(Classical Latin),,,,,,,Latinized form of Aion. Aion became a symbol during the Roman Empire and as such coins with his imagery were issued.
Aeone,f,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,"Possibly a variant of Ione, borne by British singer-songwriter Aeone Victoria Watson (1959-)."
Ae-oyna-kamuy,m,オイナカムイ(Ainu Katakana),Far Eastern Mythology,,,,,,,,"A Kamuy (god or spirit) in Ainu mythology. Described as a man encircled by smoke and flame, under which he wears a coat of bark, he is said to have taught humans domestic skills, earning him the moniker Aynurakkur (""father of humanity"")."
Aep,m,,Sundanese,/ˈa.ɛp/,,,,,,,"Variant of Asep. It can also be used as a diminutive of masculine names containing the sound aep (or other similar sounds), such as Saepuloh."
Aequa,f,,Ancient Roman,,,,,,,,"The name ""aequa"" originates from Latin, where it is a feminine form of the adjective meaning ""equal"" or ""even."" It is derived from the Latin word ""aequus,"" which conveys a sense of balance or sameness in quantitative or qualitative terms."
Aequitas,,,Roman Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""equity, equality, fairness"" in Latin. During the Roman Empire, Aequitas as a divine personification was part of the religious propaganda of the emperor, under the name Aequitas Augusti, which also appeared on coins. She is depicted on coins holding a cornucopiae and a balance scale (libra), which was more often a symbol of ""honest measure"" to the Romans than of justice."
Aer,m,,Medieval Latin,,,,,,,,"This name means ""air"" in Latin."
Ae-ra,f,애라(Korean Hangul),Korean,AY-RAH,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 愛 ""love"" and 羅 ""net for catching birds"". A famous bearer is South Korean actress Shin Ae-ra (1969-)."
Aerabella,f,,English (Rare),ayr-ə-BEL-lə,,,,,,,Variant of Arabella.
Aeracura,f,,Celtic Mythology,,,,,,,,"Of unclear origin. It has been connected with Latin aes, aeris ""copper, bronze, money, wealth"" as well as era ""mistress"" and the name of the Greek goddess Hera.This was the name of a goddess of Celtic origin, associated with Proserpina and the Roman underworld god Dis Pater. She may originally have been an earth goddess, associated with such attributes of fertility as the cornucopia and apple baskets; she may have also been associated with Silvanus and the Rhine Valley."
Aeralyn,f,,Obscure (Modern),,,,,,,,Variant of Aaralyn.
Aerandir,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Aerandir is a Sindarin word for 'Sea Wanderer'.Aerandir's kindred and birthdate are unspecified, but it is assumed that he lived in Arvernien, in the Havens of Sirion.When Eärendil was resolved to sail to Aman to plead for Valar's help, Aerandir went with him, as did Falathar and Erellont. When they reached the shores of the Blessed Realm, he and the others remained aboard while Eärendil sought the Valar. After Eärendil's embassy was complete, Eönwë, herald of Manwë, came to Aerandir and the others and set them upon another ship. Manwë then sent a great wind to blow them east back to Middle-earth.His fate afterwards is unknown."
Aere,,,Marshallese,ayhr-AY,,,,,,,"Aere means ""storm"" in Marshallese and is also used for naming typhoons in the West Pacific basin."
Aerebella,f,,Obscure,ayr-ə-BEL-ə(North American English),,,,,,,Variant of Arabella.
Aerecura,f,,Roman Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,Form of Aericura occurring in Roman mythology.
Aerenia,f,,Malaysian (Rare),,,,,,,,Not available.
Aeres,f,,"Welsh (Modern, Rare)",Similar to English Iris,,,,,,,"Allegedly directly taken from Welsh aeres ""heiress"". Seems restricted to the Carmarthen district, in South Wales."
Aerfen,f,,Welsh Mythology,/ˈai̯r.vɛn/,,,,,,,"Welsh form of Aerten, the name of a Brythonic goddess of fate. Aerten is derived from Proto-Celtic *agro- ""carnage, slaughter"" (cf. Agrona) and *tan-nu ""to broaden, to spread"" or *ten-n-d-o- ""to break, to cut"". She had a shrine at Glyndyfrdwy on the River Dee in North Wales, and according to legend presided over the outcome of wars. Cymric legend tells of her being invoked during the struggles between the native Cymry (Welsh) and the Anglo-Saxon invaders and, according to local legend, three human sacrifices had to be drowned in the Dee near her shrine to ensure success in any battles to come.In former times Aerfen has been equated with the classical Fates (as all Celtic deities had to have a Roman counterpart). In truth, however, it's probably more accurate to equate her with the Irish Morrigan, herself a deity of battle fates."
Aergia,f,Ἀεργία(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/eɪ.ˈɜr.dʒə/,,,,,,,"Means ""idleness"" in Ancient Greek, derived from the negative prefix α (a) and ἔργον (ergon) ""work"". This was the name of the Greek personification of sloth and idleness."
Aergol,m,,Old Welsh,,,,,,,,"Old Welsh form of Agricola, meaning ""farmer"""
Ae-ri,f,"애리(Korean Hangul)|愛利, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 愛 (ae) meaning ""love"" and 利 (ri) meaning ""gains, advantage, profit, merit"". Other hanja combinations are possible. A famous bearer is South Korean actress Jung Ae-ri (1960-)."
Aeri,f,"あえり(Japanese Hiragana)|亜永利, 亜絵里, 亜江里, 亜枝利, 阿永里, 阿英里, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese (Rare),AH-EH-REE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 亜 (a) meaning ""second, Asia"", 永 (e) meaning ""eternity, lengthy, long"" combined with 利 (ri) meaning ""profit, benefit"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Aerial,f,,"English (American, Modern)",EHR-ee-əl(American English),,,,,,,"Variant of Ariel. The spelling is apparently influenced by the English adjective aerial meaning ""pertaining to the air; ethereal"" (ultimately from Greek ἀήρ (aer) ""air"")."
Aeriana,f,,English (Modern),er-ee-AHN-ə,,,,,,,"Variant of Ariana, influenced by Greek aer ""air""."
Aerianna,f,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Variant of Arianna influenced by the Greek aer meaning ""air"""
Aerias,m,,"African American (Modern, Rare)",air-ee-US,,,,,,,Variant of Arias.
Aeric,m,,"English (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Eric.
Aerica,f,,"Portuguese (Brazilian, Rare)|English (American, Rare)|Greek Mythology",,,,,,,,"Variant of Erica. Alternatively, one of the seven Hesperides (nymphs of the evening) from Greek mythology according to Hyginus' Fabulae."
Aericura,f,,Celtic Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"The origins of this name are uncertain; probably a Latinized form of a Celtic name, although some Latin roots have been suggested (including aes, genitive aeris, ""brass, copper, bronze, money, wealth""; and era, genitive erae, ""mistress of a house""). This was the name of a Romano-Celtic chthonic underworld goddess, described by archaeologist Miranda Aldhouse-Green as a Gaulish Hecuba."
Aerie,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",ER-ee|EE-ree,,,,,,,"Diminutive of names beginning with Aer, coinciding with the English word aerie, ""a bird of prey's nest""."
Aeriel,,,English (Modern),ER-ee-əl,,,,,,,Variant of Aerial.
Aeriella,f,,Obscure (Modern),,,,,,,,Variant of Aeriel.
Aerielle,f,,English (Modern),,,,,,,,Variant of Aerial.
Aerielyn,f,,Obscure,,,,,,,,"Variant of Arielyn, a combination of the name Ariel with the popular name suffix lyn."
Aerienne,f,,American (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Arianne.
Aerilyn,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Aaralyn.
Aerilynn,f,,American,,,,,,,,Combination of Aerie and Lynn.
Aerin,,"애린, 에린(Korean Hangul)",English|Korean,/e.rin/,,,,,,,Variant of Erin or Aaron.
Æringærðr,f,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,Old Norse variant of Arngerðr.
Æringunnr,f,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,"Combination of Ancient Scandinavian ǫrn ""eagle"" and gunnr ""battle, fight""."
Ærinolf,m,,Old Swedish,,,,,,,,Old Swedish form of Arnulfr.
Ærinví,f,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,"Old Norse name with several possible meanings. The first element can be derived from Old Norse ǫrn or ari, both meaning ""eagle"". It's also identical to Old Swedish ærin (Old Norse arinn) ""hotbed, hearth"".The second name element could be derived from Old Norse -ví, from Ancient Germanic *-wīhō, referring to female fighters or priests. Another possible meaning is that it's derived from Old Norse vé (Old Swedish vī) ""sanctuary""."
Aerion,m,,African American,EHR-ee-ahn,,,,,,,Possibly an elaboration of Aaron.
Aerious,m,,Obscure (Modern),AYR-ee-əs,,,,,,,Possibly a variant of Areios.
Aeris,f,,Popular Culture|English,ER-is,,,,,,,"Variant of Aerith, due to translation confusion. It may also be considered a variant of Eris."
Aerith,f,,"Popular Culture|English (American, Modern, Rare)",ER-ith(English),,,,,,,From a near-anagram of the English word earth. This is the name of a character in the 1997 videogame 'Final Fantasy VII'.
Aeriwentha,f,,Obscure,,,,,,,,This was the real name of American sprinter Mae Faggs (1932-2000).
Aerloff,m,,East Frisian (Archaic),,,,,,,,Variant of Arnulf recorded in the 15th century in East Frisia.
Aernd,m,,Medieval Dutch,,,,,,,,Variant of Arend.
Aernde,m,,Medieval Dutch,,,,,,,,Variant of Arend.
Aerne,m,,Medieval Dutch,,,,,,,,Variant of Arend.
Ærnfastr,m,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,Old Norse variant of Arnfastr.
Ærngautr,m,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,"Old Norse combination of ǫrn ""eagle"" and gautr ""goth""."
Ærngun,f,,Old Swedish,,,,,,,,Old Swedish form of Æringunnr.
Aernken,m,,Medieval Dutch,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Arend.
Aernkiin,m,,Medieval Dutch,,,,,,,,Variant of Aernken.
Aernkijn,m,,Medieval Dutch,,,,,,,,Variant of Aernken.
Ærnolf,m,,Old Swedish,,,,,,,,Old Swedish form of Arnulfr.
Aernou,m,,Walloon,/aʀ.ˈnuː/,,,,,,,Walloon form of Arnould.
Aernt,m,,Medieval Dutch,,,,,,,,Variant of Arend.
Ærnulfr,m,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,Variant of Arnulfr.
Aero,f,,"Greek Mythology|Greenlandic|English (American, Modern, Rare)|Popular Culture",/ˈɛə.ɹəʊ/(American English),,,,,,,"Perhaps derived from the Greek verb ἀείρω (aeiro), αἴρω (airo) ""to lift up, to raise"". In Greek mythology, Aero (also called Haero, Aerope and Maerope) was a princess of the island of Chios, a daughter of Oenopion and Helice. She was beloved by Orion, who was, in consequence, blinded by her father."
Aerolynn,f,,Obscure (Modern),,,,,,,,Not available.
Aeropa,f,,Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,Elaboration of Aerope.
Aerope,f,Ἀερόπη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Greek Ἀέροπος (Aeropos) (see Aeropus). In Greek myth Aerope was a daughter of King Catreus of Crete, granddaughter of Minos and wife of Atreus (to whom she was married after the death of his son and her first husband Pleisthenes). Her sons, Agamemnon and Menelaus, were generally believed to be Atreus' sons. However, Aerope was faithless to Atreus, being seduced by his brother Thyestes. She gave Thyestes the golden lamb (or the fleece) which made him king of Mycenae."
Aeropus,m,Ἀέροπος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀὴρ (aer) meaning ""air, wind"" and ὄψ (ops) meaning ""eye"". This was the name of two kings of Macedon."
Aert,m,,Medieval Dutch,AH:RHT,,,,,,,Medieval Dutch form of Aart.
Aerten,f,,Celtic,,,,,,,,"Derived from agro-, ""carnage"", and tan-nu, ""to broaden"" or ""to spread"", or ten-n-do-, ""to break"" or ""to cut""."
Aeru,f,,Greenlandic,,,,,,,,Greenlandic younger form of Aero.
Aeruna,f,,Greenlandic,,,,,,,,"Combination of Aeru and the suffix -na, indicating a personal name."
Aery,m,,English,,,,,,,,Not available.
Aeryal,f,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",/ˈɛɹ.i.əl/(English),,,,,,,Variant of Aerial.
Aeryk,m,,English (Modern),ER-ik,,,,,,,Variant of Eric.
Aeryn,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",/ˈɛɹ.ɪn/|/ˈɛɹ.ən/,,,,,,,Variant of Aaryn or Eryn. Aeryn is one of the female aliens on the show Farscape.
Aerynn,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",/ˈɛɹ.ɪn/,,,,,,,Variant of Aaron or Erin.
Aerys,f,,Filipino,Air-is,,,,,,,Not available.
Æsa,f,,Old Norse|Icelandic|Faroese,/ˈɛ.aː.sa/(Faroese),,,,,,,"Old Norse, Icelandic and Faroese variant of Ása."
Aesacus,m,Αἴσακος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Αιςακος (Aisakos), probably meaning ""branch of myrtle"". In Greek mythology he was a Trojan prince (son of Priam by Alexirhoe) who loved the naiad Asterope, but, fleeing his advances, she stepped on a venomous adder and died. In grief, Aesacus hurled himself into the sea, but was changed into a bird by Tethys (the Titan who personified the sea)."
Aesah,f,,Indonesian,,,,,,,,Indonesian form of Aisha.
Aesara,f,Αἰσάρα(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of the Greek Aisara. Aesara of Lucania was a Pythagorean philosopher who wrote a work called On Human Nature, of which scholars today only have a one-page fragment."
Æsbiorn,m,,Old Swedish,,,,,,,,Old Swedish form of Ǣsbiǫrn.
Ǣsbiǫrn,m,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,Old Norse variant of Ásbiǫrn.
Æscferð,m,,Anglo-Saxon Mythology,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is Old English æsc ""ash tree"". The second element may be Old English ferhð ""soul, spirit, mind, life"" (compare Unferð) or a variant form of Old English friþ ""peace"" (in which case this is a variant of the attested Old English name Æscfrith). This name occurs in the Old English poem 'The Battle of Maldon'."
Æscfrith,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Probably derived from Old English æsc ""ash tree"" and friþ ""peace""."
Æsche,m,,"Danish (Rare, Archaic)",EHSK,,,,,,,Variant of Eske.
Æschere,m,,Anglo-Saxon|Anglo-Saxon Mythology,/ˈæʃ.xe.re/(Old English)|[ˈæʃ.he.re](Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements æsc ""ash tree"" and here ""army"". This name occurs in the 8th-century epic poem 'Beowulf' belonging to King Hroðgar's most trusted adviser; Æschere is killed by Grendel's mother in her attack on Heorot after Grendel's death."
Aeschillus,m,,"Swedish (Latinized, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Latinized form of Eskil.
Aeschines,m,,Ancient Greek,/ˈae̯s.kʰi.neːs/(Latin)|/ˈɪs.kɪ.niːz/(English),,,,,,,Latinized form of Aischines.
Aeschrion,m,Αἰσχρίων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek αἰσχρός (aischros) meaning ""shameful, dishonouring, ugly"" combined with the patronymic suffix ίων (ion)."
Æscmann,m,,Anglo-Saxon,[ˈæʃ.ˌmɑn](Old English),,,,,,,"Probably originally a byname from Old English æscmann ""sailor, pirate"", i.e. one who sailed in an ash-wood boat, from the elements æsc ""ash tree"", sometimes referring to a kind of light ship, and mann ""person, man""."
Æscmund,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈæʃ.ˌmund/(Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements æsc ""ash tree, spear"" and mund ""protection""."
Æscwig,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements æsc ""ash tree; spear (made of ash wood)"" and wig ""war, battle"". This name was borne by a 10th-century bishop of Dorchester."
Æscwine,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈæʃ.ˌwi.ne/(Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements æsc ""ash tree"" and wine ""friend""."
Æscwulf,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements æsc ""ash tree"" (poetically ""spear"" or ""boat"") and wulf ""wolf""."
Aesepus,m,Αἴσηπος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"From the Aesepus river, of uncertain etymology. This was the name of a god personifying the river in Greek mythology, as well as of one of his grandsons, who died in the Trojan War alongside his twin brother Pedasus."
Ǣsgærðr,f,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,Variant of Ásgærðr.
Æsgerður,f,,Icelandic (Rare),,,,,,,,Icelandic younger form of Ǣsgærðr.
Aesha,f,عائشة(Arabic),Arabic,/ˈʕaː.ʔi.ʃah/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Aisha.
Aeshan,m,જેરેમી લાંબુ જીવો(Gujarati),Gujarati,JEH-rə-mee,,,,,,,"Gujarati for something or other. I don't know, I didn't do any research"
Ae-shim,f,"애심(Korean Hangul)|愛沈, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 愛 (ae) meaning ""love, affection"" and 沈 (sim) meaning ""sink, submerge, addicted to"". Other hanja combinations are possible."
Æsir,m,,Icelandic|Norse Mythology,,,,,,,,Icelandic masculine form of Æsa. This is the name of a character in Norse mythology.
Ae-sook,f,"애숙(Korean Hangul)|愛淑, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/ɛ.sʰuk̚/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Korean Hangul 애숙 (see Ae-suk).
A'essu,m,,African Mythology,AYE~SUE,,,,,,,"One who provides direct connections to others to benefit everyone and consistently seeks new information to provide to those connected. Relative to the English word ""Learned"" and African name ""Sekou"" (SAY~KOO)"
Aesta,f,,Obscure,/ˈā.stə/,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from the Latin word aestas, meaning ""summer""."
Ǣstríðr,f,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,East Nordic variant of Ástríðr.
Ae-suk,f,애숙(Korean Hangul)|愛淑(Korean Hanja),Korean,,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 愛 (ae) meaning ""love, affection"", and 淑 (suk) meaning ""good, pure, virtuous, charming"". Other Hanja combinations are possible."
Aet,f,,Estonian,,,,,,,,Estonian cognate of Agatha.
Aeta,f,,English (British),,,,,,,,"This name derives from the a palm tree called the Aeta Palma (Mauritia Flexuosa), discovered in British Guiana and named by a the botanist William Davis Lamb who then used the name for his daughter."
Aeternitas,f,,Roman Mythology,,,,,,,,Aeternitas was a Roman goddess and the personification of eternity.
Aethalides,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Aithalides. In Greek mythology, Aethalides was the herald of the Argonauts."
Aethalion,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Aithalion. In Greek mythology, Aethalion was a sailor who was turned into a fish by Dionysus."
Aethalus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Aithalos. In Greek mythology, Aethalus is a man whose son was killed by Aeneas during the Trojan War."
Aethan,m,,Biblical Latin,,,,,,,,Form of Ethan used in the Latin Old Testament.
Aethel,f,,"English (British, Archaic)",,,,,,,,"Variant of Ethel. This name was borne by Aethel Tollemache (c. 1875-1955), a British suffragette and pacifist activist."
Æðelbald,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from Old English æðele ""noble"" and beald ""bold, brave"". Æthelbald was King of Wessex from 855 or 858 until his death in 860."
Æthelbert,m,,Medieval English|Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,Variant of Æðelberht.
Æðelburh,f,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈæ.ðel.burx/(Old English)|/ˈæ.ðel.burˠx/(Old English),,,,,,,"Means ""noble fortress"", derived from the Old English elements æðele ""noble"" and burg ""fortress."""
Æthelfled,f,,Anglo-Saxon|Medieval English|History,,,,,,,,Variant of Æthelflæd.
Æðelgar,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈæ.θel.ˌɡɑːr/(Old English)|[ˈæ.ðel.ˌɡɑːr](Old English),,,,,,,"Means ""noble spear"", from Old English æðele ""noble"" and gar ""spear"". It is a cognate of Adalgar."
Æthelgeat,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements æðele meaning ""noble"" and Geat referring to a member of the North Germanic tribe from present-day Götaland in southern Sweden (note, the legendary hero Beowulf was a Geat)."
Æðelgifu,f,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈæ.θel.ˌji.fu/(Old English)|[ˈæ.ðel.ˌji.vu](Old English),,,,,,,"Means ""noble gift"", from the Old English elements æðele ""noble"" and giefu ""gift""."
Æðelgyð,f,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements æðele ""noble"" and guð ""war"". It is a cognate of Germanic Adalgund. This was the name of an Anglo-Saxon saint (Æthelgyth of Coldingham)."
Æðelhad,f,,Anglo-Saxon,"/ˈæː.θel,xɑːd/(Old English)",,,,,,,"Old English form of Alice and Adelaide, derived from the Old English elements æðele ""noble"" and hād ""nature, character"" (from haiduz). Cognate to German Adelheid."
Æðelheard,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,Old English cognate of Adalhard.
Æthelhelm,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements æðele ""noble"" and helm ""helmet, protection"" (making it a cognate of Adalhelm). By the mid-10th century, this name had become reduced to Æthelm (see Athelm)."
Æðelhere,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈæ.θel.ˌxe.re/(Old English)|[ˈæ.ðel.ˌhe.re](Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements æðele ""noble"" and here ""army""."
Æðelhild,f,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈæ.θel.ˌxild/(Old English)|[ˈæ.ðel.ˌhiɫd](Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements æðele ""noble"" and hild ""battle"". It is a cognate of Adalhild."
Aethelmer,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Variant of Æðelmær.
Æðelmod,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈæ.ðel.moːd/(Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements æðele ""noble"" and mōd ""mind, spirit"" (from mōdaz). This was the name of a King of Hwicce."
Æðelmund,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈæ.θel.ˌmund/(Old English)|[ˈæ.ðel.ˌmund](Old English),,,,,,,Old English cognate of Adalmund.
Aethelnoth,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Variant of Æthelnoð.
Aethelsi,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Variant of Æthelsige recorded in Domesday Book.
Æthelsige,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Variant of Æðelsige, an Old English name derived from the elements æðele ""noble"" and sige ""victory""."
Æthelswith,f,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements æðele ""noble"" and swiþ ""strong"". Æthelswith was the daughter of King Æthelwulf of Wessex, and wife of King Burgred of Mercia. This name is cognate to the Germanic Adalsind."
Æthelswiþ,f,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,Alternate spelling of Æthelswith.
Æthelwald,m,,Anglo-Saxon|History,,,,,,,,"Variant of Æthelweald. Also compare Æthelwold. A known bearer of this name was Æthelwald Moll, an 8th-century king of Northumbria."
Æthelward,m,,Anglo-Saxon|Medieval English,,,,,,,,Variant of Æthelweard.
Æthelwealh,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈæ.θel.ˌwæ͜.ɑlx/(Old English)|[ˈæ.ðel.ˌwæ͜.ɑɫx](Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the elements æðele ""noble"" and wealh ""foreigner, Celt"" meaning ""noble foreigner, noble Celt"". This was the name of the first Christian king of Sussex Æthelwealh of Sussex."
Æðelwig,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈæ.θel.ˌwiːj/(Old English)|[ˈæ.ðel.ˌwiːj](Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements æðele ""noble"" and wig ""war, battle""."
Æthelwin,m,,Anglo-Saxon|Medieval English,,,,,,,,"Variant of Æþelwine (see Æthelwine), which itself is a variant of Æðelwine."
Æthelwold,m,,Anglo-Saxon|Medieval English,,,,,,,,"Variant of Æthelweald. Also compare Æthelwald. A known bearer of this name was Æthelwold of East Anglia, a 7th-century king of East Anglia."
Æthelwulf,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,Variant of Æðelwulf.
Æthelwynn,f,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements æðele ""noble, famous"" and wynn ""joy, rapture, pleasure""."
Aethenoth,m,,Folklore|Pet,,,,,,,,"Likely a variant or corruption of Æthelnoð. This was the name of the horse of Lady Godiva who rode down the streets of Coventry, England in the nude."
Aetherius,m,,Late Greek (Latinized)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Aitherios.This name was borne by multiple saints, the earliest of which were the 4th-century saints Aetherius of Chersonesus (in Crimea) and Aetherius of Nicomedia (now İzmit in Turkey)."
Aethion,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Aithion. In Greek mythology, Aethion was an Ethiopian chief who was killed by Perseus."
Aethlem,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"A hound needed by Culhwch, as one of his tasks, to hunt the boar Twrch Trwyth. Other tasks required that Culhwch find certain people to manage Aethlem. Arthur obtained the dog for his kinsman. After the hunt, Aethlem could not be found and was never seen again."
Aethlios,m,Ἀέθλιος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,"/a.é.tʰli.os/(Attic Greek)|/a.ˈe.θli.os/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)",,,,,,,"Means ""winning the prize, running for the prize"" in Greek."
Aethlius,m,Ἀέθλιος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Aethlios.
Aethon,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Aithon. This is the name of several characters in Greek mythology, one of them being the father of Hypermestra."
Aethra,f,"Αἴθρα, Αἴθρη(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),/ˈiː.θrə/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek αἴθρα (aithra) meaning ""clear sky, fair weather"" or ""air"", cognate to Aether. This was the name of several characters in Greek mythology, including the mother of Theseus, who was a handmaiden of Helen in Troy, and an Oceanid who is sometimes called the wife of Atlas."
Aethusa,f,Αἵθουσα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,Variant of Aithusa.
Aetia,f,,Ancient Roman,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Aetius.
Ætill,m,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,Variant of Eitill.
Aetion,m,Ἀετίων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἀετός (aetos) meaning ""eagle"". In Greek mythology, Aetion was one of the defenders of Thebes against the Seven."
Aetje,,,East Frisian,,,,,,,,Variant of Ate 3 with the diminutive suffix -je recorded in the 17th century for men and in the 16th and 18th centuries for women in East Frisia.
Aetke,,,East Frisian,,,,,,,,Variant of Ate 3 with the diminutive suffix -ke.
Aetko,m,,East Frisian (Archaic),,,,,,,,Variant of Ate 3 with the diminutive suffix -ko.
Aetna,f,Αἴτνη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Αἴτνη (Aitne), possibly derived from Proto-Indo-European *h₂eydʰ- meaning ""fire"" or ""to ignite, burn"" via archaic Sicilian *aið-na ""the fiery one"", making it a cognate of Ancient Greek αἴθω (aitho) ""to burn, blaze, kindle"". Alternatively, it could derive from Phoenician attuna meaning ""furnace, oven, chimney"". In Greek mythology, this was the name of the nymph of Mount Etna, a volcano in Sicily under which the god Hephaestus was said to have his forge."
Aetnaeus,m,Αἰτναῖος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Αἰτναῖος (Aitnaios) meaning ""of Mount Etna"", a volcano in Sicily (see also Aetna). This was an epithet of several Greek gods associated with Mount Etna, including Zeus."
Aetolus,m,Αἰτωλός(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),/iː.ˈtoʊ.ləs/(English)|/ai̯.tɔː.lós/(Attic Greek)|/ɛ.to.ˈlos/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Uncertain etymology. This was the name of several characters in Greek mythology, including a king of Elis and eponymous founder of Aetolia in Greek mythology, a son of Endymion."
