Agesilaus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|History,,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Agesilaos. This was an epithet of the Greek god Hades, as well as the name of two kings of Sparta."
Agesilay,m,"Агесилай(Bulgarian, Russian)",Bulgarian|Russian,,,,,,,,Bulgarian and Russian form of Agesilaos (see Agesilaus).
Agesipolis,m,Ἀγησίπολις(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|History,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is either derived from Greek ἆγειν (agein) meaning ""to carry, to fetch"" or from Greek ἄγω (ago) ""to guide, to lead"" (also see Agis). The second element is derived from Greek πόλις (pólis) meaning ""city, city state"". This name was borne by three Agiad kings of Sparta."
Agesistratos,m,"Ἁγησίστρατος, Ἀγεσίστρατος(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἄγω (ago) meaning ""to lead"" and stray (stratos) meaning ""army"". Alternatively, could be a Doric Greek form of Hegesistratos."
Agestratos,m,Ἀγέστρατος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""host-leading, one who leads the army"" in Ancient Greek, derived from ἄγω (ago) meaning ""to lead, guide"" and στρατός (stratos) meaning ""army"". This was the name of a Trojan warrior killed by Ajax the Great in Greek mythology. It was also an epithet of the goddess Athena."
