Aglanor,m,Ἀγλάνωρ(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"" (see Aglaos) combined with the Greek noun ἀνήρ (aner) meaning ""man""."
Aglaodoros,m,Ἀγλαόδωρος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀγλαόδωρος (aglaodoros) meaning ""bestowing splendid gifts"", which consists of the Greek adjective ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"" (see Aglaos) combined with the Greek noun δῶρον (doron) meaning ""gift"".This was also an epithet of Demeter, the Greek goddess of the harvest, agriculture, sacred law and the cycle of life and death."
Aglaokarpos,m,"Ἀγλαόκαρπος, Ἀγλαοκάρπος(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀγλαόκαρπος (aglaokarpos) meaning ""bearing beautiful fruit"", which consists of the Greek adjective ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"" (see Aglaos) combined with the Greek noun καρπός (karpos) meaning ""fruit"" (see Carpus).This was an epithet of Demeter, the Greek goddess of the harvest, agriculture, sacred law and the cycle of life and death."
Aglaomachos,m,Ἀγλαόμαχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"" (see Aglaos) combined with the Greek noun μάχη (mache) meaning ""battle""."
Aglaonice,f,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Aglaonike. This name was borne by a Greek astronomer from the 1st or 2nd century BC.
Aglaonike,f,Ἀγλαονίκη(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"" (see Aglaos) combined with the Greek noun νίκη (nike) meaning ""victory""."
Aglaonoe,f,Ἀγλαονόη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"" (see Aglaos) combined with the Greek noun νόος (noos) meaning ""mind"".In Greek mythology, Aglaonoe is the name of one of the Sirens."
Aglaope,f,Ἀγλαόπη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"" (see Aglaos) combined with either the Greek noun ὄψ (ops) meaning ""voice"" or the Greek noun ὤψ (ops) meaning ""eye, face, countenance"".In Greek mythology, Aglaope is the name of one of the Sirens."
Aglaophanes,m,Ἀγλαοφάνης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"" (see Aglaos) combined with Greek φανής (phanes) meaning ""appearing"" (see Theophanes)."
Aglaophantos,m,"Ἀγλαοφαντος, Ἀγλαόφαντος(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"" (see Aglaos) combined with the Greek adjective φαντός (phantos) meaning ""visible"", which is ultimately derived from the Greek verb φαντάζω (phantazo) meaning ""to make visible""."
Aglaopheme,f,"Ἀγλαόφημη, Ἀγλαοφήμη(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀγλαόφημος (aglaophemos) meaning ""of splendid fame"", which consists of the Greek adjective ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"" (see Aglaos) combined with the Greek noun φήμη (pheme) meaning ""prophetic saying"" as well as ""rumour"" and ""(good) report, repute, fame"".In Greek mythology, Aglaopheme is the name of one of the Sirens."
Aglaophon,m,Ἀγλαοφῶν(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/aː.ɡla.o.pʰɔ̂ːn/(Attic Greek)|/a.ɣla.o.ˈɸon/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)",,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"" (see Aglaos) and φωνή (phone) meaning ""voice"" as well as ""sound, tone"".This was the name of an ancient Greek painter, who is thought to have lived around the late 6th or early 5th century BC."
Aglaophonos,,"Ἀγλαόφωνος, Ἀγλαοφώνος(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἀγλαόφωνος (aglaophonos) meaning ""with a splendid voice"", which consists of ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"" (see Aglaos) combined with φωνή (phone) meaning ""voice"" as well as ""sound, tone"".In Greek mythology, Aglaophonos is the name of one of the Sirens."
Aglaophonus,,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Aglaophonos. In Greek mythology, Aglaophonus is the name of one of the Sirens."
Aglaos,m,"Ἄγλαος, Ἀγλαός(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"". Also compare the related name Aglaia."
Aglaosthenes,m,Ἀγλαοσθένης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"" (see Aglaos) combined with the Greek noun σθένος (sthenos) meaning ""vigour, strength""."
Aglaostratos,m,Ἀγλαόστρατος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"" (see Aglaos) combined with the Greek noun στρατός (stratos) meaning ""army""."
Aglaotime,f,Ἀγλαοτίμη(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Aglaotimos.
Aglaotimos,m,Ἀγλαότιμος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀγλαότιμος (aglaotimos) meaning ""splendidly honoured"", which consists of the Greek adjective ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""splendid, shining, bright"" (see Aglaos) combined with the Greek verb τιμάω (timao) meaning ""to honour, to esteem, to revere"".This was also an epithet of Demeter, the Greek goddess of the harvest, agriculture, sacred law and the cycle of life and death."
Aglaura,f,,Theatre,,,,,,,,"Variant of Aglaurus used by Sir John Suckling for the title character in his 1637 play Aglaura, set in Persia."
Aglauro,f,,Portuguese (Rare),,,,,,,,Portuguese form of Aglaurus.
Aglauros,f,Ἄγλαυρος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/ə.ˈɡlɔːr.əs/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἄγλαυρος (aglauros), a variant of ἀγλαός (aglaos) meaning ""bright, shining, splendid"". The name is also written as Ἄγραυλος (Agraulos), in which case it could be from ἄγραυλος (agraulos) meaning ""rural, rustic, of the fields"". In Greek mythology, Aglauros was the eldest daughter of the Athenian king Cecrops, and appears in various legends."
Aglaurus,f,Ἄγλαυρος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),/ə.ˈɡlɔː.rəs/(English),,,,,,,Latinized form of Aglauros.
Aglaus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Aglaos. This name was borne by a Roman governor of Crete from the 3rd century AD.In Greek mythology, Aglaus is one of the sons of king Thyestes of Olympia, whom he had with a Naiad."
Aglavra,f,Аглавра(Bulgarian),Bulgarian (Rare),,,,,,,,Bulgarian form of Aglaurus.
Agleja,f,,Croatian,ah-GLAY-ah,,,,,,,Croatian form of Aglaea (see Aglaia).
Aglent,f,,Medieval Hungarian,,,,,,,,Old Hungarian form of Ágnes.
Aglika,f,Аглика(Bulgarian),Bulgarian,,,,,,,,Variant of Iglika.
Aglinda,f,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"The maiden daughter of King Nascor.The devil tricked her brother, Nabor, into raping her by a spring, but Aglinda prayed, and Nabor fell dead.In memory of Aglinda's plight, the spring was called the Spring of the Virgin, and it was enchanted to paralyze visiting non-virgin knights. Erec became one of its victims."
Aglinde,f,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"The maiden daughter of King Nascor. The devil tricked her brother, Nabor, into raping her by a spring, but she prayed, and Nabor fell dead. In memory of her plight, the spring was called the Spring of the Virgin, and it was enchanted to paralyze visiting non- virgin knights. Erec became one of its victims"
Agliss,f,,English (Rare),AH-GLISS,,,,,,,"Derived from the English-speaking words ""a glistening,"" meaning shining or glittering."
Agloolik,,,Inuit Mythology,,,,,,,,"In Inuit mythology, Agloolik is a spirit that lives underneath the ice and gives aid to fishermen and hunters."
Aglore,f,,French (Archaic),,,,,,,,Not available.
Aglovale,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"Aglovale de Galis is the eldest legitimate son of King Pellinore of Galis (Wales), introduced in the Vulgate Lancelot."
Agmánd,m,,Medieval Hungarian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Agmund,m,,Germanic|Medieval Scandinavian|Norwegian (Rare),,,,,,,,"Form of Agmundr. The first element of this name is derived from ag, an uncertain element for which a few possible origins exist. The accepted explanation is that it comes from Proto-Germanic *agjo, which means ""sharp, pointed."" Because of that, it also means ""edge"", as in the sharp cutting side of a sword - which is why the meaning of the element has ultimately come to be ""sword"". See also Anglo-Saxon ecg (in e.g. Egbert) or Old High German ekka (New High German ecke, which means ""corner""). Other possibilities for the meaning and origin of ag include: Gothic agan ""to fear, to be afraid"", Old Norse agi ""unrest, agitation"", Gothic ahjan ""to believe"" and Old Norse aka ""to do, to act."" The second element of this Germanic name is derived from Old High German mund ""protection."""
