Agnodike,f,Ἁγνοδίκη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|History,[haŋ.no.dí.kɛː](Greek Mythology),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἁγνός (hagnos) meaning ""pure, chaste, holy"" and δίκη (dike) meaning ""justice"". According to legend, this was the name of the first woman to legally practice medicine in Ancient Greece (4th century BC), having disguised herself as a man to study in Egypt, then to work in Athens. Agnodike was so successful that rival doctors accused her of seducing her female patients; when she revealed herself to be a woman during trial, they attempted to charge her for illegally practicing medicine. Her patients, many of them wives of important Athenians, defended her, and so she was allowed to continue working. She was most likely not an actual historical figure, as the earliest account of her existence is found in Fabulae, a Latin handbook of mythology authored in approximately the 2nd century AD."
Agnokrite,f,Ἁγνοκρίτη(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἁγνός (hagnos) meaning ""pure, chaste, holy"" and κριτής (krites) meaning ""judge, critic""."
