Agravain,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,The name of a nephew of King Arthur in Arthurian tales.
Agravaine,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"a Variant of Agravain The second and probably the most unpleasant son of King Lot and Queen Margawse of Orkney (sometimes Anna), nicknamed aux dures mains (‘of the hard hands’). Brother of Gawain, Gaheris and Gareth, and Mordred. He married Laurel, the niece of Lionors and Lynette. He is a nephew of King Arthur."
Agreo,m,,Italian|Spanish,,,,,,,,Italian and Spanish form of Agreus.
Agrëpina,f,,Kashubian,,,,,,,,Kashubian form of Agrippina.
Agrestes,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"Derived from Latin agrestis meaning ""rural, rustic, wild, brutish"", from ager ""field, farm"". This is the name of an ancient pagan king of Camelot in the Old French Arthurian romance 'Estoire del Saint Graal' (""History of the Holy Grail"", 1220-35). In a time long before Arthur, God punishes Agrestes for massacring the Christians in Camelot (converted by Josephus, son of Joseph of Arimathea and guardian of the Holy Grail) with madness and death; the king commits suicide by diving into a fire after eating his hands and slaughtering his family."
Agreus,m,Ἀγρεύς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek αγρεύς (agreus) meaning ""hunter"". In Greek mythology, Agreus was the name of one of the Panes, three spirits or aspects of the god Pan. It was also an epithet of the rustic deity Aristaeus, as well as of Apollo, Poseidon, and Bacchus."
Agrevain,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"A variant of Agravain. The second and probably the most unpleasant son of King Lot and Queen Margawse of Orkney (sometimes Anna), nicknamed aux dures mains (‘of the hard hands’). Brother of Gawain, Gaheris and Gareth, and Mordred. He married Laurel, the niece of Lionors and Lynette. He is a nephew of King Arthur."
Agricola,,,Ancient Roman|Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,"Means ""farmer; grower"" in Latin from ager; agri meaning ""field, land"" combined with the verb colere meaning ""to cultivate; to grow"".Currently it is an Italian feminine adjective meaning ""agricultural; farming; rural""."
