Aithon,m,Αἴθων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ǎi̯.tʰɔːn/(Attic Greek)|/ˈɛ.θon/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek αἴθων (aithon), which can mean ""fiery, burning, fierce; tawny, red-brown"" as well as ""fire, burning heat"", ultimately derived from αἴθω (aitho) meaning ""to ignite, to light (up), to kindle, to burn""."
Aithra,f,"Αἴθρα, Αἴθρη(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ǎi̯.tʰraː/(Attic Greek)|/ˈɛ.θra/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,Original Greek form of Aethra.
Aithusa,f,Αἴθουσα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Popular Culture,,,,,,,,"Feminine participle of Ancient Greek αἴθων (aithon) meaning ""fiery, burning, blazing, gleaming"" as well as ""red-brown, tawny"", derived from αἴθω (aitho) meaning ""to light up, kindle, burn, blaze"". It coincides with the unrelated word αἴθουσα (aithousa) meaning either ""portico, veranda"" or ""poison hemlock"". In Greek mythology, Aithousa was the daughter of Poseidon and Alcyone, and a lover of Apollo with whom she had Eleuther. In the BBC television series Merlin (2008-2012), Aithusa is the name of a white dragon summoned by Merlin. On the show the name is said to mean ""light of the sun"" in Ancient Greek."
