Akbugha,m,,Near Eastern Mythology|Turkic Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""white serpent"" or ""white dragon"", derived from Turkic ak meaning ""white"" combined with Turkic büge or büke meaning ""serpent, snake"" as well as ""dragon"" (ultimately from Proto-Turkic böke). Over time, the latter word eventually took on the secondary and figurative meaning of ""wise person"", due to the fact that the dragon was regarded as a representation of wisdom.The second element of this name is not to be confused with the derivatives of Proto-Turkic *buka meaning ""bull"" (see Bugha).In Turkic mythology, Akbugha is the god of health and healing/medicine."
Akbul,m,,New World Mythology,,,,,,,,"Is a Mayan mythology name, which means, 'of the night.'"
Akça,,,Turkish,,,,,,,,"Means ""pale"" in Turkish."
