Alcántara,f,,"Spanish (Mexican, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Given in honour of the 16th-century Spanish saint and mystic Peter of Alcántara (1499-1562). The place name Alcántara is itself from Arabic القنطرة (al-Qanṭarah) meaning ""the bridge""."
Alcathoe,f,Ἀλκαθόη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Ἀλκαθόη (Alkathoê), which is derived from Greek αλκη (alke) ""strength"" and θοός (thoos) ""swift, nimble"". In Greek myth Alcathoe (or Alcithoe) was one of the Minyades, the three daughters of King Minyas of Orchomenus in Boeotia, Greece."
Alcathous,m,Ἀλκάθοος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,"/al.ká.tʰo.os/(Attic Greek)|/al.ˈka.θo.os/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)|/æl.ˈkæθ.oʊ.əs/(English)",,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀλκή (alke) meaning ""strength, prowess, force"" and θοός (thoos) meaning ""quick, swift"". This was the name of several characters in Greek mythology."
