Alozie,f,,Nigerian,alozee,,,,,,,"From the Nigerian, meaning ""branch""."
Alpa,f,,Indian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Alpagut,m,,Medieval Turkic,[al-pa-gut],,,,,,,"The name Alpagut is a venerable title and proper name of Old Turkic origin, a compound formed from Alp (meaning ""heroic, brave, noble, strong, champion, victor, powerful, legendary"") and the archaic suffix +ağut. According to the Dīwān Lughāt al-Turk by Mahmud al-Kashgari, an Alpagut is depicted as a ""solitary, invincible warrior who fights alone without seeking aid, a paragon of individual martial prowess and sovereign spirit."" It carries a profound weight of historical autonomy and battlefield excellence, symbolizing one who stands as an unbreakable sentinel of the people."
Alpaida,f,,Germanic (Anglicized),,,,,,,,English variant of Alpaïde.
Alpaïde,f,,History (Gallicized)|Frankish (Gallicized),,,,,,,,"French form of Alpaidis. This name belonged to the mother of Charles Martel, the 8th-century Frankish military and political leader."
Alpamysh,m,Алпамыс(Kazakh),Kazakh,[ɑɫ.pɑ.ˈmɯs],,,,,,,"Derived from Alp (""Brave,"" ""Hero,"" ""Warrior"") and the ancient Turkic intensifier suffix -amış (signifying ""supreme likeness,"" ""magnitude,"" or ""becoming the ultimate archetype""). Rather than a mere title, this linguistic fusion elevates the root to mean ""The Ultimate Warrior,"" ""The Giant Hero,"" or ""The Hyper-Alp."" Alpamysh is the legendary protagonist of the Epic of Alpamysh, representing the archetype of the nomadic savior. The name conveys immense physical strength, profound chivalry, and an unyielding commitment to justice. It is the definitive name for an individual destined to lead and protect."
Alpan,f,,Etruscan Mythology,,,,,,,,The Etruscan goddess of love and the underworld. She belongs to the Lasas and is usually portrayed naked.
Alpana,f,আলপনা(Bengali),Bengali,/ˈal.po.na/,,,,,,,"From the name of a Bengali folk art form consisting of coloured motifs painted on floors and walls using rice flour paint. The word is ultimately derived from Sanskrit आलिम्पन (alimpana) meaning ""whitening, painting""."
Alpanu,f,,Etruscan Mythology,,,,,,,,"The Etruscan goddess of the underworld, associated with rebirth, revenge, and triumph out of suffering."
Alpár,m,,Hungarian|Hungarian,,,,,,,,Hungarian borrowing of Alper.
Alpay,m,,Turkish,,,,,,,,"Means ""Brave moon"" in Turkish. Alp meaning ""Brave"" and Ay meaning ""Moon""."
Alpe,m,,Finnish (Rare),AHL-pe,,,,,,,Variant of Alpo.
Alpert,m,,Germanic|Medieval German,,,,,,,,Variant of Albert.
Alpertunga,m,,Turkish,/ɑl.peɾ.tun.gɑ/,,,,,,,"It is composed of the three words alp ""brave"" or ""hero"", er ""soldier"" or ""male"" and tunga ""tiger"". A mythical hero who was mentioned in Turkic mythology and the Epic of Alp Er Tunga. The name is sometimes mentioned as the khan of Scythia. See Alper."
Alpertus,m,,Germanic (Latinized)|Medieval German (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Alpert.
Alpez,m,ალფეზ(Georgian),Georgian (Rare),,,,,,,,Georgian form of Alphaeus.
Alph,m,,"English (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Alternate form of Alf 2.
Alphabeta,f,,Obscure,ahl-fah-BEH-tah,,,,,,,"Alphabeta Swithinbank (-1849) died in Hunslet, Yorkshire.Her name was a combination of the two first Greek letters, alpha (Α, α) and beta (Β, β)."
Alphaea,f,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Alphaeus.
Alphais,f,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Name of a 12th century French woman, venerated by the Catholic Church as a Blessed."
Alphandery,m,,Judeo-Spanish,,,,,,,,Not available.
Alphard,m,,Astronomy|English|German,,,,,,,,"Derived from Arabic الفرد (al-fard) ""the solitary one"". Alphard is the brightest star in the constellation Hydra, with no other bright stars near it."
Alphardus,m,,Dutch (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Alphard.
Alpharetta,f,,"English (American, Archaic)",,,,,,,,"Derived from the name of a suburb in the American city of Atlanta, which itself is derived from Alfarata, the name of a fictional Native American girl in the popular 19th-century parlor song ""The Blue Juniata"". Also compare Alpharita."
Alpharita,f,,Caribbean,,,,,,,,"This name is best known for being the name of the Cuban-Jamaican singer Rita Marley (b. 1946), who was born as Alpharita Constantia Anderson. She is the widow of the Jamaican reggae singer Bob Marley (1945-1981). Given that she was born in the Spanish-speaking country of Cuba, her name is most likely a variant spelling or form of Alvarita.However, since both of her parents clearly originated from an English-speaking country (it is unclear which one - it could be Jamaica, but it could just as easily be the United States) and were thus native English speakers, it is possible that they had actually somehow been inspired by the American song ""The Blue Juniata"" when they named their daughter. The song features a fictional Native American girl named Alfarata, you see. Alternatively, they could have been inspired by Alpharetta, which is the name of a suburb in the American city of Atlanta. The suburb derives its name from the Native American girl in the aforementioned song ""The Blue Juniata""."
Alphart,m,,German,,,,,,,,German variant of Alphard.
Alphé,m,,Louisiana Creole (Rare),al-FEH,,,,,,,Variant of Alphée.
Alphecca,f,,Astronomy,/æl.ˈfɛk.ə/,,,,,,,"Means ""the broken"", taken from Arabic نير الفكّة (nayyir al-fakka) meaning ""the bright (star) of the broken (ring of stars)"". This is the traditional name of the star Alpha Coronae Borealis."
Alphée,m,,Biblical French,,,,,,,,French form of Alphaeus.
Alphège,m,,"Norman|French (Quebec, Archaic)|History (Ecclesiastical)",,,,,,,,Norman form of Alphege and French and Norman variant of Elphège.
Alpheiaia,f,Ἀλφειαία(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Epithet of the Greek goddess Artemis which was derived from the name of the river god Alpheios, who loved her. Artemis Alpheiaia was worshipped at Letrini in Elis and on the island of Ortygia near Syracuse; she also shared an altar with Alpheios at Olympia. Under this name she was associated with the wearing of masks, largely due to the legend that Artemis and her nymphs escaped the pursuit of Alpheios by covering their faces."
Alpheios,m,Ἀλφειός(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Late Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived either from Greek ἀλφή (alphê) meaning ""produce, gain, profit"" or from Greek ἀλφός (alphos) which meant ""whiteness"" as well as ""white leprosy"", a cognate of Albus. In Greek mythology he was a river deity, the personification of the Alpheios, the longest river in the Peloponnese. The son of Oceanus and Tethys, he fell madly in love with the Nereid Arethusa and pursued her under the sea to Sicily. There she pleaded to Artemis who changed her into a fountain. The river Alpheios then worked its way underground to mingle with the waters of Arethusa."
Alphekka,f,,Astronomy,/æl.ˈfɛ.kə/,,,,,,,Variant of Alphecca.
Alphenor,m,Ἀλφήνωρ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is not entirely certain, as there are two possible etymologies for it. The first is that it is derived from the Greek noun ἀλφή (alphe) meaning ""produce, gain, profit"", which is etymologically related to the Greek verb ἀλφάνω (alphano) meaning ""to bring in, to yield, to fetch (a price)"".The other possibility is that it is derived from the Greek noun ἀλφός (alphos), which was a rare word that originally meant ""white, whiteness"" (cognate to Latin Albus) but later came to refer to leprosy or a similar type of skin disease that is characterized by the formation of dull white lesions on the skin. There were other words more commonly used for the colour of white in ancient Greece, namely the noun λευκόν (leukon) and the adjective λευκός (leukos). The same goes for leprosy: this was usually known as λεύκη (leuke). Each of these three words is cognate to Latin Lux.The second element of this name is derived from the Greek noun ἀνήρ (aner) meaning ""man"". As such, the meaning of the name as a whole is either ""a man's gain"" or ""white man"" (as in, a white-haired man, i.e. an old and wise man). The meaning of ""leprous man, leper"" is highly improbable (at least for a given name), due to the heavy stigma that surrounded leprosy at the time.In Greek mythology, Alphenor is the name of one of the Niobids."
Alpheratz,f,,Astronomy|Filipino (Rare),,,,,,,,"Derived from Arabic surrat al-faras (""the navel of the mare""). This is a traditional name of the star Alpha Andromedae in the constellation Andromeda."
Alphesiboea,f,Ἀλφεσίβοια(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Ἀλφεσίβοια (Alphesiboia) meaning ""much-courted"" (literally ""earning cattle, bringing in oxen"", from ἀλφάνω (alphano) meaning ""to bring in, yield, earn"" and βοῦς (bous) meaning ""bullock, bull, ox""). This was an epithet of maidens whose parents received many oxen or cattle as gifts from their suitors. In Greek mythology this was the name of several characters, including a daughter of Phegeus who married Alcmaeon."
Alpheus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Biblical (Latinized)|English,al-FEE-əs(English),,,,,,,Variant of Alphaeus and Latinized form of Alpheios.
Alphia,f,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,"Contracted form of Adelphia, which itself is a short form of Philadelphia. However, in some cases, this name is a feminine form of the English masculine name Alfie. A recent example of this is the 18th child born to the Radford family, who were featured in a Channel 4 documentary. Her full name is Hallie Alphia Beau: she was named after her deceased brother Alfie."
Alphie,m,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,"Diminutive of Alphard and Alphonso. In some cases, it is also a variant of Alfie."
Alphild,f,,Scandinavian,AL-fild,,,,,,,Variant of Alfhild.
Alphine,f,,"English (American, Rare)",,,,,,,,Perhaps a feminine form of Alphaeus or Alphonse.
Alphious,m,,English,,,,,,,,Possible variant of Alpheus.
Alphons,m,,Dutch|Dutch (Surinamese)|Flemish|Afrikaans|German|German (Austrian)|German (Swiss),,,,,,,,Variant of Alfons.
Alphonsa,f,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Alphonsus (see Alfonso). Saint Alphonsa (1910-1946) adopted this as a monastic name in honour of Saint Alphonsus Liguori (1696-1787).
Alphonsia,f,,"Italian (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Adalfuns, and Alphonsus."
Alphonsina,f,,Dutch (Rare),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Alphonsus.
Alphonsos,m,"Αλφόνσος, Αλφώνσος(Greek)",Greek|Germanic (Hellenized),,,,,,,,Original Greek form of Alphonsus (see Alfonso).
Alphred,m,,English,,,,,,,,Variant of Alfred.
Alphy,,ആൽഫി(Malayalam),Indian (Christian),,,,,,,,Possibly a variant of Alfie.
Alphys,f,,Popular Culture,,,,,,,,Meaning unknown. This is the name of a character in the popular RPG game 'Undertale'.
Alpi,m,,Finnish,AHL-pi,,,,,,,Finnish variant of Albin.
Alpia,f,,Pictish,,,,,,,,Possible 7th century Pictish princess. Etymology unknown.
Alpiinus,m,,Finnish,,,,,,,,Finnish form of Albinus.
Alpina,f,,Indonesian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Alpine,m,,English,,,,,,,,"Late Middle English from Latin Alpinus, from Alpes ‘Alps.’"
Alpkan,m,آلپ‌کان(Ottoman Turkish),Turkish|Ottoman Turkish,AHLPKAHN(Turkish),,,,,,,"The name Alpkan originates from Old Turkic roots. It is a compound of Alp and Kan. Alp means ""hero"" or ""brave warrior"" and was commonly used in names of ancient Turkic warriors and rulers, such as Alp Er Tunga. Kan means ""ruler"" or ""sovereign,"" related to the historical title ""Khan."" The combination reflects the ideal of a heroic and noble leader in Turkic culture. The name itself is rarely attested in medieval sources and is more commonly used in modern Turkey as a revival of traditional Turkic naming elements."
Alpona,f,আলপনা(Bengali),Bengali,/ˈal.po.na/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Bengali আলপনা (see Alpana).
Alprecht,m,,Medieval German,,,,,,,,Variant of Albrecht.
Alprentice,m,,African American,,,,,,,,Not available.
Alpysbai,m,Алпысбай(Kazakh),Kazakh,,,,,,,,Not available.
Alqama,m,علقمة(Arabic),Arabic,,,,,,,,"Alqama is an Arabic name for boys that refers to a fruit of a plant known al-ḥanẓal (known as bitter apple and desert gourd in English, scientific name Citrullus colocynthis). It is also used to mean “bitterness”."
Al-qaqa,m,القعقاع(Arabic),Arabic,,,,,,,,Not available.
