Alruna,f,,German|Medieval German,,,,,,,,"Germanic name, in which the second element was derived from Old High German runa or Old Norse rún meaning ""secret lore, rune"" (Proto-Germanic *rūnō). Modern-day scholars consider it a younger form of the Old High German name Ailrun, in which the first element is agi ""terror"" (which quite often became ail- in names). At the same time, it is also a contracted form of Adelruna (or Adelrun, Adelrune), in which the first element is adal ""noble"". Lastly, those same academics also consider it a younger form of the Old Norse name Alfrún, in which the first element is alfr ""elf"".The Old Norse cognate Ǫlrún occurs in the Eddic poem the Völundarkviða, belonging to a Valkyrie who marries the archer Egil. This form of the name was borne by an 11th-century Bavarian recluse, Alruna of Cham, a patron saint of pregnancy. Also see Aurinia. It coincides with the name of the mandrake plant, being alruna in Swedish, alrune in Danish and Norwegian and Alraune in German."
