Amanita,f,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,"From the name of a genus of mushrooms (mostly comprised of poisonous species). The genus' name derives the Latin amanita, itself from the Ancient Greek amanítis (αμανίτης) 'fungus'"
Amanitaraqide,f,,Meroitic,,,,,,,,"Meaning uncertain. Possibly means ""begotten of Amun"", deriving from the Meroitic element terike (""to beget"") and Amun, the Egyptian god of the sun and air. Name borne by a Kandake of Kush, who held the title from 21 CE to 41 CE."
Amanitore,f,,Meroitic,,,,,,,,"Meaning uncertain. Name borne by a Kandake of Kush. Amanitore is often mentioned in contemporary texts as being a co-regent with Natakamani, who was the son of Amanishakheto."
