Amoroldo,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"The Italian version of Morholt, the Irish giant slain by Tristan. In La Tavola Ritonda, the name is also given to Morholt’s son, Golistant, when Tristan knighted him and bestowed the kingdom of Ireland upon him.The younger Amoroldo took a queen named Vermiglia, and developed a vast kingdom that included parts of England, Logres, Aquitaine (Aquitain), and Gaul. Arthur eventually made him a Knight of the Round Table.He went to war with King Alois of North Wales over a castle called Lerlinte, enlisting Tristan as his champion, while Alois retained the services of Lancelot. Arthur brokered a peace, but Alois renewed the war after the Grail Quest. In this second campaign, Amoroldo was slain by Lancelot.His name Amoroldino is Italian for Marhaus, and the name is used for both father and son in the Italian romance."
Amoron,m,,Mormon,,,,,,,,a Nephite officer and spy (c. AD 380-400)
Amoros,m,,Medieval Occitan,,,,,,,,"Directly taken from the Old Occitan masculine adjective amoros ""characterized by love; loving, amorous"", ultimately from Medieval Latin amorōsus."
Amorosa,f,,Spanish (Rare)|Medieval Basque,,,,,,,,"As a Spanish name, Amorosa is derived from Spanish amoroso, amorosa ""loving; caring; affectionate"".As a Basque name, its origin and meaning are uncertain, although there certainly is a possibility that it is simply an adoption of the Spanish name. In any case, the name was first recorded in Pamplona in 1285."
Amoroso,m,,Spanish (Rare),Ah-mor-o-so,,,,,,,"Means ""loving (of god)"" in Spanish."
Amoru,m,"あもる(Japanese Hiragana)|亜望留, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,AH-MO-ṘUU,,,,,,,"From Japanese 亜 (a) meaning ""second, Asia"", 望 (mo) meaning ""hope, wish"" combined with 留 (ru) meaning ""to stay"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Amory,,,Literature|English (Rare),/ˈæm.ə.ɹi/,,,,,,,Transferred use of the surname Amory.
Ámós,m,,Biblical Hungarian,,,,,,,,Hungarian form of Amos.
Amós,m,,Portuguese|Spanish|Catalan,/ɐ.ˈmɔʃ/(European Portuguese)|/a.ˈmɔɪ̯s/(Brazilian Portuguese)|/a.ˈmos/(Spanish),,,,,,,"Portuguese, Spanish, and Catalan form of Amos."
Amôs,m,,Franco-Provençal,,,,,,,,Franco-Provençal form of Amos.
'Amosa,m,,Hawaiian|Biblical Hawaiian,ah-MOS-ah,,,,,,,"Older Hawaiian form of Amos, as well it appears in the Bible in Hawaiian."
Amoso,m,,Esperanto,,,,,,,,Esperanto form of Amos.
Amossu,m,,Corsican,/a.ˈmɔ.su/,,,,,,,Corsican form of Amos.
A-mốt,m,,Biblical Vietnamese,/ʔaː˧˧.mot̚˧˦/(Vietnamese)|/ʔaː˧˧.mok̚˦˧˥/(Vietnamese)|/ʔaː˧˧.mok̚˦˥/(Vietnamese),,,,,,,Vietnamese form of Amos.
Amotken,m,,New World Mythology,,,,,,,,"The creator deity of the Salish, North American Indians, he dwells in heaven, solitary and alone."
ʾAmot-milqart,f,𐤀𐤌𐤕𐤌𐤋𐤒𐤓𐤕(Phoenician),Phoenician,,,,,,,,"Means ""servant of Melqart""."
Amotocaye,f,,Nahuatl,,,,,,,,"Derived from Nahuatl tocaye ""person with a name, illustrious person"" combined with either amo ""not, un-"" or the possessive prefix amo- ""your""."
Amouel,m,"Ἀμουήλ, Ἀμουὴλ(Ancient Greek)",Biblical Greek,,,,,,,,"Greek form of Hammuel, as it first appeared in the Septuagint."
Amoun,m,"Ⲁⲙⲟⲩⲛ, Ⲁⲙⲩⲛ(Coptic)",Coptic,,,,,,,,Coptic form of Amon (also compare Amun).
Amous,m,عاموس(Arabic),Arabic,/ʕa.mūs/,,,,,,,"Arabic version of “Amos”, a minor prophet in the bible.Meaning:”to carry;borne by God”"
Amouskositte,m,,Cherokee,,,,,,,,"Meaning, ""dreadful water."""
Amow,,,Cree,,,,,,,,Means 'Bee' in Cree.
Amoz,m,אָמוֹץ(Ancient Hebrew),Biblical,,,,,,,,"Meaning strong, Amoz was the father of the prophet Isaiah."
Ampa,f,อำภา(Thai),Thai,/ʔam˧.ˈpʰaː˧/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Ampha.
Ampai,,อำไพ(Thai),Thai,/ʔam˧.ˈpʰaj˧/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai อำไพ (see Amphai).
Ampaiwan,f,อำไพวรรณ(Thai),Thai,/ʔam˧.pʰaj˧.ˈwan˧/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai อำไพวรรณ (see Amphaiwan).
Ampám,m,,Aguaruna,,,,,,,,"Etymology unknown, possibly from the Awajún ampámpag, a kind of wild plant traditionally used for medicinal reasons."
Ampara,f,,Spanish (Mexican),,,,,,,,Variant form of Amparo.
Amparing,f,,Filipino,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Amparo.
Amparito,f,,Spanish|Asturian,am-pa-REE-to,,,,,,,Diminutive of Amparo.
Ampawn,,อัมพร(Thai),Thai (Rare),/ʔam˧.ˈpʰɔːn˧/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Amphorn.
Ampel,m,Ампел(Russian),Russian,,,,,,,,Russian form of Ampelus via Ampelos.
Ampelia,f,,Spanish (Latin American),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Ampelius.
Ampélio,m,,"Portuguese (Brazilian, Rare)",,,,,,,,Portuguese form of Ampelios (see Ampelio).
Ampeliu,m,,Sicilian,,,,,,,,Sicilian form of Ampelius.
Ampeliusz,m,,"History (Ecclesiastical, Polonized)",ahm-PE-lyoosh(Polish),,,,,,,Polish form of Ampelius.
