Ámpelo,m,,Spanish,,,,,,,,Spanish form of Ampelus via Ampelos.
Ampelo,m,,Italian (Archaic),,,,,,,,Italian form of Ampelos.
Ampelos,,Ἄμπελος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἄμπελος (ampelos) meaning ""vine, grapevine"". In Greek mythology it belonged to a satyr who was transformed into the first grapevine, loved by the god Dionysos, as well as to one of the eight hamadryad daughters of Oxylos, associated with vines such as wild grapes and bryony."
Ampelosi,m,ამპელოსი(Georgian),Georgian (Archaic),,,,,,,,Georgian form of Ampelos.
Ampelus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Ampelos. Used by a saint martyred by Diocletian in 302.
Amper,m,Ампер(Russian),Soviet,,,,,,,,"Means ""ampere (electricity measurement unit)"" in Russian."
Ampflise,f,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"Form of Amphelise used by the 13th-century poet Wolfram von Eschenbach for two characters in his Middle High German romance Parzival, one of whom was the queen of France during the time of Uther."
Ampha,f,อำภา(Thai)|อัมพา(Thai),Thai|Thai,/ʔam˧.ˈpʰaː˧/|/ʔam˧.ˈpʰa˦˥/,,,,,,,"From Thai อำ (am) meaning ""hidden, concealed"" and ภา (pha) meaning ""light, ray""."
Amphai,,อำไพ(Thai)|ອຳໄພ(Lao),Thai|Lao,/ʔam˧.ˈpʰaj˧/(Thai)|/ʔàm.ˈpʰái/(Lao),,,,,,,"Means ""beautiful, pretty, fine, handsome"" in Thai and Lao."
Amphaiwan,f,อำไพวรรณ(Thai),Thai,/ʔam˧.pʰaj˧.ˈwan˧/,,,,,,,"From Thai อำไพ (amphai) meaning ""bright, brilliant, beautiful, pretty"" and วรรณ (wan) meaning ""colour, tint""."
Amphan,f,อำพัน(Thai)|ອຳພັນ(Lao),Thai|Lao,/ʔam˧.ˈpʰan˧/(Thai),,,,,,,"Means ""amber"" in Thai and Lao."
Amphandros,m,Ἀμφανδρος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἀμφί (amphi) meaning ""on both sides, in all directions, surrounding"" as well as ""around, about, near"" and ἀνδρός (andros) meaning ""of a man""."
Amphawan,,อัมพวัน(Thai),Thai,/ʔam˧.pʰa˦˥.ˈwan˧/,,,,,,,"Means ""mango orchard, mango grove"" in Thai."
Amphay,,ອຳໄພ(Lao),Lao,/ʔàm.ˈpʰái/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Lao ອຳໄພ (see Amphai).
Amphelisa,f,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Variant of Amphelise.
Amphelisia,f,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Variant of Amphelise.
Amphiale,f,Ἀμφιάλη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἀμφίαλος (amphialos) meaning ""between two seas, living amid seas"", derived from ἀμφίς (amphis) ""surrounding, around, between"" and ἅλς (hals) ""sea, brine, salt"" (genitive ἁλός). In Greek mythology this name belonged to a Rhodian woman, the mother by Lernus of Cleodorus."
Amphialos,m,Ἀμφίαλος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἀμφίαλος (amphialos) meaning ""between two seas, sea-girt, living amid seas"", derived from ἀμφίς (amphis) ""surrounding, around, between"" and ἅλς (hals) ""sea, brine, salt"" (genitive ἁλός)."
Amphianassa,f,Αμφιάνασσα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Amphianax, derived from ἀμφί (amphi) meaning ""around, on both sides"" and ἄνασσα (anassa) meaning ""queen, lady"" (used as a term of address for goddesses). According to some Greek legends, Amphianassa was the mother of Anthea by Proetus, a king of Argos; in others, she was one of his daughters, and she and her sister Amphinoe both had an affair with the soothsayer Melapodas (perhaps a variant of Melampus), taking refuge in the caves of Mount Lapithas when they became pregnant."
Amphianax,m,Ἀμφιάναξ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀμφί (amphi) meaning ""on both sides, in all directions, surrounding"" as well as ""around, about, near"" and ἄναξ (anax) meaning ""master, lord, king"".In Greek mythology, this is the name of a king of Lycia."
Amphiaraos,m,Ἀμφιάραος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ˌæm.fi.ə.ˈreɪ.əs/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀμφί (amphi) meaning ""surrounding, around, near, on both sides"" and ἀράομαι (araomai) meaning ""to pray, vow, curse"", which is related to the Greek noun ἀρά (ara) meaning ""prayer, invocation, curse"". This noun is sometimes used in the context of evil, which then essentially gives it the meaning of ""prayer for evil, curse"". As a result, some people associate this noun with a Greek word that is similar in outward appearance and meaning, namely ἀρή (are) ""bane, ruin"". This word is said to be etymologically related to the name of the Greek god Ares. Because of all of this, some people believe that the second element of the name Amphiaraos refers to either a curse or the god Ares, causing them to interpret the name's meaning as either ""doubly cursed"" or ""twice Ares-like""."
Amphiaraus,m,Ἀμφιάραος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Amphiaraos. This was the name of the legendary king of Argos, the seer and healer, who was honoured as a god."
Amphibalus,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"From Late Latin amphibalus meaning ""cloak"". Saint Amphibalus was originally an anonymous character in the legend of Saint Alban. Alban had noticed the piety and devotion of this Christian priest, and gradually been converted by it into becoming a Christian himself. Alban concealed the priest in his home when a local prince was seeking him out during a persecution of Christians. Learning that the priest was in Alban's home, the prince sent solders to search him out. Alban took the cloak (amphibalus) of the priest, robed himself in it, and presented himself to the soldiers as the priest, allowing the true priest to escape. Later accounts tell how the priest, now named Amphibalus from his cloak, himself converted others, including Saints Stephanus and Socrates, was captured in Wales, and martyred in Verulam (St Albans). Remains identified as belonging to Amphibalus were discovered at Redbourn in Hertfordshire, England, near the town of St Albans, in 1178, and placed in the Abbey Church."
Amphicles,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Amphikles.
Amphicrates,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Amphikrates. This name was borne by a king of the Greek island Samos, who lived in the 7th century BC."
Amphictyone,f,Ἀμφικτυόνη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀμφικτίονες (amphiktiones) meaning ""neighbours, they that live nearby"", itself from ἀμφίς (amphis) meaning ""surrounding, around, between"" and κτίζω (ktizo) meaning ""to found, build, people""."
Amphidaitas,m,Ἀμφιδαίτας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Mycenaean Greek name probably derived from ἀμφίς (amphis) meaning ""surrounding, around, between"" and either δαίς (dais) meaning ""meal, banquet"" or δαίω (daio) meaning ""to divide, share; to host (a feast)""."
Amphidamas,m,Ἀμφιδάμας(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/æm.ˈfɪ.də.məs/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἀμφί (amphi) meaning ""around, on both sides"" and δαμάζω (damazo) meaning ""to tame, subdue""."
Amphika,f,อัมพิกา(Thai),Thai,/ʔam˧.pʰi˦˥.ˈkaː˧/,,,,,,,Thai form of Ambika.
Amphikles,m,Ἀμφικλῆς(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἀμφί (amphi) meaning ""around, on both sides, surrounding"" as well as ""about, near; regarding"" and κλέος (kleos) meaning ""glory""."
Amphikrateia,f,Αμφικρατεια(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Amphikrates.
Amphikrates,m,Ἀμφικράτης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/am.pʰi.krá.tɛːs/(Attic Greek)|/am.ɸi.ˈkra.tis/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)",,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀμφί (amphi) meaning ""on both sides, in all directions, surrounding"" as well as ""around, about, near"" and κρατος (kratos) meaning ""power, strength""."
Amphillis,f,,"English (British, Archaic)|Medieval English",,,,,,,,"A form of Amphelise. On this name Richard Stephen Charnock wrote in 1882: 'Found as a Christian name in the parish register of Northrepps Hall, Norwich, and also in those of North Benfleet, Essex. From Greek ἀμφίλαλος (amphilalos), loquacious.'"
Amphilochios,m,Ἀμφιλόχιος(Ancient Greek),Late Greek|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Variant of Amphilochos. This was the name of an Illyrian martyr and saint from the 2nd century AD.
Amphilochius,m,,Late Greek (Latinized)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Amphilochios. This name was borne by several saints and bishops.
Amphilochos,m,Ἀμφίλοχος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,"/am.pʰí.lo.kʰos/(Attic Greek)|/am.ˈɸi.lo.xos/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)",,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀμφί (amphi) meaning ""on both sides, in all directions, surrounding"" as well as ""around, about, near"" and λόχος (lochos) meaning ""ambush"", a word that later came to signify a tactical sub unit of the ancient Greek army. The word is ultimately derived from the Greek verb λοχάω (lochao) meaning ""to lie in wait for, to waylay"" as well as ""to snare""."
Amphilochus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Amphilochos. In Greek mythology, Amphilochus was the son of Amphiaraus and Eriphyle, and brother of Alcmaeon. He was one of the Epigonoi."
Amphiloque,m,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Amphilochus.
Amphimachos,m,Ἀμφίμαχος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἀμφί (amphi) meaning ""around, on both sides, surrounding; about, near"" or ""regarding, concerning"" and μάχη (mache) meaning ""battle"". Compare the word ἀμφιμάχομαι (amphimachai) meaning ""to fight around, to fight for; to besiege"". This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including a son of King Priam, two suitors of Penelope, and a Greek warrior hidden inside of the Trojan Horse."
Amphimachus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Amphimachos. This name is borne by several characters in Greek mythology, one of them being a king of Elis."
Amphimaque,m,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Amphimachus.
Amphimedon,m,Ἀμφιμέδων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/æm.ˈfɪ.mɪ.ˌdɒn/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἀμφί (amphi) meaning ""around, on both sides"" and μέδων (medon) meaning ""ruler""."
Amphinome,f,Ἀμφινόμη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Ἀμφίνομος (Amphinomos) (see Amphinomus). This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including one of the fifty Nereids."
Amphinomus,m,Ἀμφίνομος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Meaning ""grazing all about."" He was the son of King Nisos and one of the suitors of Penelope that was killed by Telemachus."
Amphion,m,Ἀμφίων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀμφί (amphi) meaning ""on both sides, in all directions, surrounding"" as well as ""around, about, near"". This name is borne by several characters in Greek mythology, one of them being a son of Zeus by the nymph Antiope."
Amphiro,f,Ἀμφιρώ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""surrounding flow"", from Ancient Greek ἀμφί (amphi) meaning ""around, near, on both sides"" and ῥόος (rhoos) meaning ""stream, flow, current"" (see rhoe ""stream, river, flow""). This was the name of an Oceanid in Greek mythology."
Amphithea,f,Ἀμφιθέα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Amphitheos, derived from ἀμφίς (amphis) meaning ""surrounding, around, on both sides"" and (thea) meaning ""goddess"". Alternatively, the second element could instead derive from θέα (thea) meaning ""view, sight"", giving this name the meaning ""view from all sides"". This was the name of several women in Greek mythology, as well as being an alternative name for Hemithea."
Amphithemis,m,Ἀμφίθεμις(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἀμφί (amphi) meaning ""on both sides, in all directions, surrounding"" as well as ""around, about, near"" and θέμις (themis) meaning ""law of nature, divinely ordained justice, that which is laid down"".This is the name of two characters from Greek mythology: one is a horned centaur and the other a son of Apollo."
Amphitheos,m,Ἀμφίθεος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Literature,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἀμφίς (amphis) meaning ""surrounding, around, on both sides"" and θεός (theos) meaning ""divine; deity, god"". Amphitheos is the name of a character in Aristophanes' play The Acharnians, who claims to be immortal due to his being descended from the goddess Demeter."
Amphitryon,m,Ἀμφιτρύων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""harassing either side"", derived from Ancient Greek (amphi) ""on both sides, surrounding"" and τρύω (tryo) meaning ""to wear out, distress, rub down"". This was the name of the husband of Alcmene and the stepfather of Herakles."
Amphol,m,อำพล(Thai),Thai,/ʔam˧.ˈpʰon˧/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai อำพล (see Amphon).
Amphorn,,อัมพร(Thai),Thai,/ʔam˧.ˈpʰɔːn˧/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai อัมพร (see Amphon).
Amphoterus,m,"Ἀμφότερος, Ἀμφοτερός(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἀμφότερος (amphoteros) meaning ""either, both (of two)""."
Amphyllis,f,,English (Archaic),,,,,,,,"Variant of Amphelise, perhaps influenced by Phyllis. This name was borne by Amphyllis Twigden Washington (1601-1655), an ancestress of George Washington."
Ampie,m,,Afrikaans,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Abraham.
Ampika,f,อัมพิกา(Thai),Thai,/ʔam˧.pʰi˦˥.ˈkaː˧/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai อัมพิกา (see Amphika).
Ampil,,,Khmer,ahm-PEEL,,,,,,,"Ampil means ""tamarind"" in Khmer-Ampil has been used to name 2 tropical cyclones after Bopha was replaced with Ampil."
Ampiloke,m,ამფილოქე(Georgian),Georgian (Archaic),,,,,,,,Georgian form of Amphilochios.
Ampinga,m,,Malagasy,,,,,,,,"Means ""shield"" in Malagasy."
Ampke,f,,"Afrikaans (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Not available.
Ample,f,,"English (American, Rare)",,,,,,,,From the English word ample.
Ampleas,,,Obscure,,,,,,,,Not available.
Ampleen,f,,Obscure,,,,,,,,May derive from the English word ample.
Amples,,,"English (American, Rare)",,,,,,,,Not available.
Ampless,,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,Not available.
Amplias,f,Ἀμπλίας(Ancient Greek),English (Archaic)|Biblical Greek|Biblical,,,,,,,,"English vernacular form of Amphelisia (see also Ampflise), which as an English name survived until the 19th century mainly as Amplias, also as Amphillis, Amphlis. All forms possibly derive from Greek ampelos ""vine"" (compare Ampelio), though Charnock suggested ""speaking two languages"". No examples of the name noted since the 1880s."
Ampliatos,m,Ἀμπλιᾶτος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Roman (Hellenized)|Biblical Greek,,,,,,,,"Hellenized form of Ampliatus. This name only occurs in some versions of the Greek New Testament, such as the Nestle-Aland (NA) Greek New Testament and the United Bible Societies' (UBS) Greek New Testament. In other versions of the Greek New Testament, the man in question is called Amplias instead."
Ampliatus,m,,Ancient Roman|Biblical|Biblical Latin,,,,,,,,"Roman cognomen which was derived from Latin ampliatus meaning ""widened, extended, enlarged, amplified"". The word ultimately comes from the Latin verb amplio meaning ""to widen, to extend, to enlarge"".This is the name of a Roman Christian mentioned in Paul's letter to the Romans."
Amplie,m,,Biblical Romanian,,,,,,,,Romanian form of Amplias.
Amplius,m,,Obscure,,,,,,,,See Ampleas.
Amplonia,f,,Medieval Dutch|Dutch (Rare),ahm-PLOH-nee-ah,,,,,,,"Medieval Dutch variant form of Apollonia. This name has survived to modern times, but barely so, as it is quite rare in the Netherlands these days: in 2014, there were less than 10 living bearers (of all ages) in the entire country. Most living bearers are elderly as well, so the name is set to become even rarer in the future."
Ampol,m,อำพล(Thai),Thai,/ʔam˧.ˈpʰon˧/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai อำพล (see Amphon).
Ampòlet,m,,Lombard,,,,,,,,Cremish Lombard variant of Impòlet.
Ampon,f,อำพร(Thai)|อำพล(Thai),Thai|Thai,/ʔam˧.ˈpʰɔːn˧/|/ʔam˧.ˈpʰon˧/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai อำพร (see Amporn).
Ampsicora,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Hampsicora.
Ampúsh,m,,Aguaruna,,,,,,,,"Means ""owl"" or ""cricket"" in Awajún."
Ampyx,m,Ἄμπυξ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""woman's diadem"" or ""headband"" in Greek. It is the name of several characters in Greek mythology."
Amqor,m,,Guanche,,,,,,,,"Means ""big"" in Guanche."
Ámr,m,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,"Means ""black, loathsome, reddish brown, dark"" in Old Norse."
Amr,m,,Arthurian Cycle|Welsh Mythology,,,,,,,,"According to the 9th-century Historia Brittonum, Amr is the son of Arthur."
Amra,f,Амра(Abkhaz),"Bosnian|Abkhaz|English (Rare, ?)",/ˈam.rə/,,,,,,,"Derived from Arabic عَمَرَ (ʿamara) meaning ""to live long, to thrive""."
Amraagni,m,,Hinduism,amraagni,,,,,,,"The protector,one who serves for his motherland"
Amraan,m,,Indian,AM - RAN,,,,,,,Not available.
Əmrah,m,,Azerbaijani,,,,,,,,"Possibly the Azerbaijani form of Emrah. Alternatively, it might come from an Arabic word meaning ""happy""."
Amrah,f,,Pakistani,,,,,,,,Pakistani form of Amra.
Amr Allah,m,أمر الله(Arabic),Arabic,/ʔam.ruɫ.ˈɫaːh/,,,,,,,"Means ""command of Allah"" from Arabic أمر (ʾamr) meaning ""order, command, decree"" combined with الله (Allāh)."
Amran,m,عمران(Arabic)|আমরান(Bengali),Arabic|Malay|Indonesian|Bengali,/ʕam.ˈraːn/(Arabic)|/ˈam.ran/(Indonesian),,,,,,,Variant of Imran.
