Anbesa,m,,Amharic (Modern),,,,,,,,"Means ""Lion"" in Amharic."
Anbessa,m,,Tigrinya,,,,,,,,"Means ""lion"" in Tigrinya."
Anbeta,f,,Albanian (Rare)|Albanian,An-bè-ta,,,,,,,Not available.
Anbian,m,岸边(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 岸 (àn) meaning ""beach, shore"" and 边 (biān) meaning ""edge, border, side, margin""."
Anbiao,m,"安标, etc.(Chinese)|安標, etc.(Traditional Chinese)",Chinese,/an.pjɑʊ̯/,,,,,,,"From Chinese 安 (ān) meaning ""quiet, peace"" combined with 标 (biǎo) meaning ""tip of a tree; mark, sign"". Other hanzi combinations are possible."
Anbin,m,岸滨(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 岸 (àn) meaning ""beach, shore"" and 滨 (bīn) meaning ""beach, coast, river bank""."
Anbjørn,m,,Norwegian (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Arnbjørn.
Anbold,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Not available.
Anbortse,f,,Basque (Rare),,,,,,,,Coined by Sabino Arana Goiri and Koldo Elizalde as a Basque equivalent of Ambrosia.
Anbros,m,,Basque,,,,,,,,Short form of Anbrosio.
Anbrosio,m,,Basque,,,,,,,,Basque form of Ambrosius.
Anbrozo,m,,Venetian,,,,,,,,Venetian form of Ambrose.
Ancaeus,m,Ἀγκαῖος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),/æn.ˈsiː.əs/(English)|/aŋ.kâi̯.os/(Attic Greek)|/aɲ.ˈɟɛ.os/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from a descendent of the Proto-Indo-European root *h₂enk- meaning ""curve, bent"", possibly ἀγκάς (angkas) meaning ""in the arms"". This was the name of two members of the Argonauts in Greek mythology, one a son of Poseidon, the other of king Lycurgus of Arcadia."
Ancaius,m,Ἀγκαῖος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Variant latinization of Ancaeus.
Ancalagon,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"A character in JRR Tolkien's works, the greatest of the fire dragons. The name is derived from the fictional Sindarin language and is possibly a compound of the word anc meaning ""jaws"" and the root word alak meaning ""rushing""."
Ancalimë,f,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Means ""most bright"" in Quenya. This was the name of the first Ruling Queen of Númenor in Tolkien's works. She was the daughter of Erendis and Tar-Aldarion."
Ancamna,f,,Celtic Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"A water goddess from Continental Celtic mythology known from inscriptions in the area of modern-day France and Germany.Her name has only been left in its Latinized form Ancamna; there have, however, been attempts to reconstruct a Gaulish form: Ankambona, which has then been interpreted to mean ""very crooked river"" (from Proto-Celtic *an-kambo-abon-ā)."
Ancasta,f,,Celtic Mythology,,,,,,,,Ancasta is the name of a little known goddess of Roman Britian.
Anče,f,Анче(Macedonian),Macedonian,,,,,,,,"Diminutive of Ana, Ane and Aneta."
Ancée,m,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Ancaeus.
Ancel,m,,English (Archaic)|Anglo-Norman (?)|Medieval French (?),AN-sel,,,,,,,Variant of Ansel.Famous bearers include Methodist minister Ancel Henry Bassett (1809–1886) and physiologist Ancel Keys (1904–2004).
Ancèlica,f,,Sicilian,,,,,,,,Sicilian form of Angelica.
Ancèlicu,m,,Sicilian,,,,,,,,Sicilian form of Angelico.
Ancelin,m,,"Medieval French|French (Rare)|French (Quebec, Rare)","AWN-SU-LEN(French, Quebec French)",,,,,,,"Medieval French diminutive of Anselme. There are also instances where this name is the masculine form of Anceline, which is a French diminutive of Ancelle."
Anceline,f,,Medieval French|French (Rare)|English (Rare)|Dutch (Rare),AWN-SU-LEEN(French)|an-sə-LEEN(English)|ahn-sə-LEE-nə(Dutch)|ahn-sə-LEEN(Dutch),,,,,,,"French diminutive of Ancelle. There are also instances where this name is the feminine form of Ancelin, which is a French diminutive of Anselme.It is worth noting that in the English-speaking world, there are very likely cases where this name is a feminization of Ansel. Also, in (at least) the Netherlands, there have been cases where this name is a variant spelling of Angeline."
Ancelle,f,,"French (Archaic)|French (African, Rare)|French (Quebec, Archaic)|English (Rare)|Dutch (Rare)","AN-SEL(French, Quebec French)|an-SEL(African French)|ahn-SEL-lə(Dutch)|ahn-SEL(Dutch)",,,,,,,"French variant of Ancille, which has also been used in non-francophone countries over time. Note that in the English-speaking world, there are very likely cases where this name is a feminization of Ansel.The name has always been extremely rare in the French-speaking world, which is probably because the association with the French word ancelle ""female servant"" is too close. It appears that (where the francophone world is concerned) the name is no longer in use in France itself these days, but predominantly in African countries where French is one of the official national languages, such as Cameroon and the Republic of the Congo."
Ancellina,f,,Romani (Archaic),,,,,,,,Not available.
Ancelmo,m,,Spanish (Latin American)|Portuguese (Brazilian)|Filipino (Rare),/an.ˈsel.mo/(Latin American Spanish)|/ɐ̃.ˈsɛw.mu/(Brazilian Portuguese),,,,,,,Variant of Anselmo.
Ancera,f,,Judeo-Anglo-Norman,,,,,,,,Variant of Anchera.
Ancerika,m,,New World Mythology,,,,,,,,"The sun god of the Tapirape, Brazil."
Ancha,f,Анча(Russian),Russian (Rare),ahn-chah,,,,,,,Diminutive of Anna or Anastasiya.
Anchal,f,आंचल(Hindi),Hindi,,,,,,,,"From Sanskrit अञ्चल (añcala), referring to the end of sari (a traditional dress worn by women in the Indian subcontinent)."
Anchalee,f,อัญชลี(Thai),Thai,/ʔan˧.t͡ɕʰa˦˥.ˈliː˧/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai อัญชลี (see Anchali).
Anchali,f,อัญชลี(Thai),Thai,/ʔan˧.t͡ɕʰa˦˥.ˈliː˧/,,,,,,,"Means ""salutation, expression of reverence"" in Thai, ultimately from Sanskrit अञ्जलि (añjali)."
Anchan,f,安婵(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 安 (ān) meaning ""quiet, calm, peaceful"" and 婵 (chán) meaning ""beautiful, lovely""."
Anchana,f,อัญชนา(Thai),Thai,/ʔan˧.t͡ɕʰa˦˥.ˈnaː˥˩/,,,,,,,Thai form of Anjana.
Anchao,m,岸潮(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 岸 (àn) meaning ""beach, shore"" and 潮 (cháo) meaning ""tide""."
Anchasa,f,อัญชสา(Thai),Thai,/ʔan˧.t͡ɕʰa˦˥.ˈsaː˩˩˦/,,,,,,,"Derived from Sanskrit अञ्जसा (anjasa) meaning ""true, straight, right""."
Ánchel,m,,Aragonese,,,,,,,,Aragonese form of Angelus.
Anchela,f,,Aragonese,,,,,,,,Aragonese form of Angela.
Anchelica,f,,Aragonese,an-CHEH-li-ka,,,,,,,Aragonese form of Angelica.
Ánchels,f,,Aragonese,AHN-chels,,,,,,,Aragonese form of Ángeles.
Ancheng,m,岸成(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 岸 (àn) meaning ""beach, shore"" and 成 (chéng) meaning ""become, succeed"" or ""completed, finished""."
Ancher,m,,"Judeo-Anglo-Norman|Danish (Americanized, Modern, Rare)",AN-ker,,,,,,,Masculine form of Anchera.
Anchera,f,,Judeo-Anglo-Norman,,,,,,,,"Judeo-Anglo-Norman name derived from Old High German ans ""god""."
Anchiale,f,Αγχιαλη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"The name of the Titan goddess of the warming heat of fire, the sister of Prometheus and mother of the metal-working Daktyloi. Her name may be derived from ank-, a prefix meaning ""to reveal or uncover"" and further elements meaning ""heat"". Further it might refer to ανκαλε (ankale), a bundle of firewood carried under the arm."
Anchik,f,Анчик(Russian),Russian (Rare),,,,,,,,Diminutive of Anastasiya or Anna.
Anchin,m,Анчин(Mongolian Cyrillic)|ᠠᠩᠴᠢᠨ(Traditional Mongolian),Mongolian,/ˈan.t͡ɕʰiŋ/,,,,,,,"Means ""hunter"" in Mongolian."
Anchinoë,f,Ἀγχινόη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,Variant of Achiroë.
Anchiroe,f,Ἀγχιρόη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""flowing near"" in Ancient Greek, derived from ἄγχι (anchi) meaning ""near, nigh"" or ""like, resembling"" and ῥοή (rhoe) meaning ""flow, stream, river"". This was the name of several naiads and nymphs in Greek mythology, as well as of a consort of Sithon who was also called Anchinoe or Archinoe."
Anchirrhoë,f,,Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Variant of Achiroë.
Anchita,f,,Indian (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Ankita.
Ancho,f,ანჩო(Georgian),Georgian,,,,,,,,"Diminutive of Ana, Anana and Anano."
Anchor,m,,English,,,,,,,,"The name is either a masculine form of Anchoretta (finally going back to the Welsh name Angharad) or used with the literal meaning ""anchor""."
Anchoret,f,,Medieval English,/ˈaŋ.kə.rɛt/(Middle English),,,,,,,Early Anglicization of Welsh Angharad.
Anchoretta,f,,Medieval English (Latinized),/aŋ.kə.ˈrɛ.tə/(Middle English),,,,,,,Latinization of Anchoret.
Anchulee,f,อัญชุลี(Thai),Thai,/ʔan˧.t͡ɕʰu˦˥.ˈliː˧/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai อัญชุลี (see Anchuli).
Anchuli,f,อัญชุลี(Thai),Thai,/ʔan˧.t͡ɕʰu˦˥.ˈliː˧/,,,,,,,Variant of Anchali.
Anči,f,,Croatian,AHN-chee,,,,,,,Diminutive of Ana.
Ańcia,f,,Silesian,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Anna and Ana.
