Andraemon,m,Ἁνδραίμων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek,"/an.drǎi̯.mɔːn/(Attic Greek)|/an.ˈdrɛ.mon/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)|/æn.ˈdriː.mən/(English)",,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Ἁνδραίμων (Andraimon), which is probably derived from Greek ἀνήρ (aner) meaning ""man"" (genetive ἀνδρός (andros)) and αἷμα (aima) meaning ""blood"". This name was borne by roughly five characters in Greek mythology, one of them being one of the suitors of Odysseus' wife Penelope."
