Androgeus,m,Ἀνδρόγεως(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Ἀνδρόγεως (Androgeos), derived from ανδρος (andros) meaning ""of a man"" and the genitive of γῆ (ge) meaning ""earth, land"". In Greek mythology, Androgeus was the son of the Cretan king Minos and his wife Pasiphaë. This was also the name of a Greek soldier in the Aeneid."
Androgey,m,"Андрогей(Bulgarian, Russian)",Bulgarian|Russian,,,,,,,,Bulgarian and Russian form of Androgeus.
Androhey,m,Андрогей(Ukrainian),Ukrainian,,,,,,,,Ukrainian form of Androgeus.
Androkleides,m,Ἀνδροκλείδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""son of Androkles"" in Greek, derived from the name Androkles combined with the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides)."
Androklis,m,Ανδροκλής(Greek),Greek (Rare),an-dro-KLEES,,,,,,,Modern Greek form of Androkles.
Androklos,m,,Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Variant of Androkles. This was the name of the third recorded Ancient Greek Olympic victor during the 8th-century BCE.
Androkydes,m,Ἀνδροκύδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀνδρός (andros) meaning ""of a man"" combined with κῦδος (kydos) meaning ""glory, renown""."
Androlochos,m,Ἀνδρόλοχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἀνδρός (andros) meaning ""of a man"" combined with the Greek noun λόχος (lochos) meaning ""childbirth"" as well as ""ambush"". Through the latter meaning, the word later came to signify a tactical sub unit of the ancient Greek army, which further strengthened the word's connection to combat and the military. As such, this name most likely means something along the lines of ""to ambush a man"" or ""military man"". However, it is also quite possible that its meaning is ""birth of a man"" or ""born a man"" (probably in the figurative sense), so one should not be too quick to disregard either possibility and instead keep both in mind. Also compare Aristolochos."
Androlochus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Androlochos. This name was borne by a prominent citizen of the city of Elis, who was killed by the Achaeans during the Battle of Leontion in 217 BC."
