Antandros,m,Ἄντανδρος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/án.tan.dros/(Attic Greek)|/ˈan.tan.dros/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)",,,,,,,"Means ""opposing men"" or ""equal to men"", derived from Ancient Greek ἀντί (anti) meaning ""against, opposed, compared to, like"" and ἀνδρός (andros) meaning ""of a man"". Also compare the adjective ἄντανδρος (antandros) meaning ""instead of a man, as a substitute"".Also compare the Greek name Antenor, which is closely related and essentially means the same."
