Anthe,f,Ἄνθη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ˈæn.θiː/(English),,,,,,,"Means ""full bloom; blossom, bloom"" in Ancient Greek, from ἄνθος (anthos) ""flower"". In Greek mythology, Anthe was one of the Alcyonides, nymphs of fair weather. After the death of their father Alcyoneus at the hands of Heracles, they threw themselves into the sea in their grief and were transformed into kingfishers. A moon of Saturn was named after her."
