Antheus,m,Ἀνθεύς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἀνθέω (antheo) meaning ""to blossom, to bloom"", ultimately from ἄνθος (anthos) meaning ""flower, blossom"" (compare Anthea). This was the name of several characters in Greek mythology, including a soldier of Dionysus, a son of Antenor accidentally killed by Paris during a game, and a youth murdered by Queen Cleoboea for rejecting her advances."
Anthia,f,,History (Ecclesiastical)|Literature,,,,,,,,Possibly a variant of Anthea. This name was borne by a 2nd-century Illyrian saint who was martyred with her son Eleutherius during the persecutions of the Roman emperor Hadrian. It was also used by Xenophon of Ephesus for the lover of Habrocomes in his 2nd-century novel An Ephesian Tale (one of the sources for Shakespeare's äRomeo and Juliet).
