Apellicon,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Apellikon. A known bearer of this name was Apellicon of Teos, a famous book collector from the 1st century BC."
Apellikon,m,"Ἀπελλικῶν, Ἀπελλίκων(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived the name of the god Apollo combined with Ancient Greek ἱκέτης (iketes) meaning ""suppliant, one who comes looking for aid or protection"", ultimately from ἵκω (iko) ""to come, to reach out""."
Apellis,m,Απελλής(Greek),Greek (Rare),a-pe-LEES,,,,,,,Modern Greek form of Apelles.
Apeloko,f,,Yoruba,,,,,,,,Means “apocalypse” in Yoruba.
Apelone,f,,Old Danish|Danish,/a.b̥ə.ˈloː.nə/(Old Danish)|/a.blɔ.ˈ.nə/(Danish),,,,,,,Danish form of Apollonia.
Apelonia,f,,Medieval Portuguese,,,,,,,,Medieval variant of Apolónia.
Apemantos,m,Ἀπήμαντος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἀπήμαντος (apemantos) meaning ""unharmed, unhurt""."
Apemantus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Theatre,,,,,,,,Latinized form of Apemantos. This is the name of a cynical and misanthropic philosopher in the play Timon of Athens by William Shakespeare.
Apemerukoyan-mat-unamerukoyan-mat,f,,Far Eastern Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""rising fire sparks woman"" or ""rising cinder sparks woman"" in Ainu. It is the full name of the goddess Kamuy-huci."
Apemosyne,f,Ἀπημοσύνη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""freedom from harm, safety"" or ""harmlessness, innocence"" in Ancient Greek. This was the name of a daughter of King Catreus in Greek mythology. She was able to outrun the god Hermes when he pursued her, though he eventually caught her by laying a trap in her path."
Apendi,m,,Indonesian,,,,,,,,Indonesian variant of Effendi.
Apenisa,m,,Fijian,U-pin-ee-zah,,,,,,,Fijian form of Ebenezer.
Apep,m,,Egyptian Mythology,/ˈæ.pɛp/|/ˈɑː.pɛp/,,,,,,,"Apep is the Egyptian personification of darkness and disorder, often depicted as a giant serpent or snake. He was the main enemy of the sun god Ra."
Aper,m,,"Ancient Roman (Rare)|History (Ecclesiastical, Rare)",,,,,,,,"A Roman cognomen of uncertain meaning. It is possibly derived from the Latin word aper meaning ""boar"" or apro meaning ""wild boar"". This was the cognomen of 4 people in the Severan family tree including a Roman aristocrat, Gaius Septimius Severus Aper."
Āperahama,m,,Māori,AH-per-rah-ha-ma,,,,,,,Māori form of Abraham.
Âperât,m,,Greenlandic,A:-pe-ra:t,,,,,,,Greenlandic form of Abraham.
Apesanahkwat,m,,Menominee,,,,,,,,Not available.
Apex,m,,"English (Modern, Rare)",AY-peks,,,,,,,"From the English word ""apex""."
Apfia,f,,Indonesian (Rare)|Italian (Archaic)|Moldovan (Archaic)|Biblical Romanian,,,,,,,,Cognate of Apphia.
Aphai,,อภัย(Thai),Thai,/ʔa˨˩.ˈpʰaj˧/,,,,,,,"Means ""fearlessness, absence of danger"" or ""forgiveness, pardon"" in Thai."
Aphaia,f,Ἀφαία(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Ancient Greek ἀφανής (aphanes) meaning ""unseen, invisible, disappearing, secret"" (see a and phanes). Aphaia was a Greek goddess who was worshipped almost exclusively at a single sanctuary on the island of Aegina in the Saronic Gulf. She originated as early as the 14th century BCE as a local deity associated with fertility and the agricultural cycle, but later came to be identified with the goddesses Athena and Artemis and with the nymph Britomartis."
Aphaphon,f,อาภาพร(Thai),Thai,/ʔaː˧.pʰaː˧.ˈpʰɔːn˧/,,,,,,,"From Thai อาภา (apha) meaning ""light, brightness"" and พร (phon) meaning ""blessing""."
Aphaporn,f,อาภาพร(Thai),Thai,/ʔaː˧.pʰaː˧.ˈpʰɔːn˧/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai อาภาพร (see Aphaphon).
Apharée,m,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Aphareus.
Aphareus,m,Ἀφαρεύς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Uncertain etymology, possibly derived from Ancient Greek ἄφαρ (aphar) meaning ""quickly, suddenly"", or perhaps the toponym Φαραί (Pharai). Other possibilities are ἀφαρής (aphares) meaning ""without"" and ἄφαρος (apharos), which can mean ""unploughed, untilled"" as well as ""unclad, naked"". The latter possibility is ultimately derived from the Greek negative prefix ἄ- (a) combined with Greek φάρος (pharos), which can mean ""plough"" as well as ""a large pierce of cloth, web"". This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including a king of Messenia and a centaur. Aphareus was also the name of an ancient Greek tragedian and orator from the 4th century BC."
Aphatsara,f,อาภัสรา(Thai),Thai,/ʔaː˨˩.pʰat̚˦˥.sa˨˩.ˈraː˧/,,,,,,,"From Thai อาภัสระ (aphatsara) meaning ""bright, clear, shining"", ultimately from Sanskrit आभास्वर (abhasvara). This is also the name of a heavenly realm in Buddhist tradiiton."
Apheidas,m,Ἀφείδας(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""unsparing, not miserly"" in Greek, derived from the Greek negative prefix ἀ (a) combined with the Greek adjective φειδός (pheidos) meaning ""sparing, thrifty"".This is the name of several characters from Greek mythology."
Apheleia,f,,Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"From the Greek goddess “Apheleia”, the goddess of ease, simplicity and good primitivity."
Aphelele,,,Xhosa|Zulu,AP-u-LAY-lay(Xhosa),,,,,,,"Means ""complete, whole, all present"" in Xhosa and Zulu. Often given to the last child to be born in a family."
Aphelia,f,,English (British),,,,,,,,Variant of Ophelia.
Aphiah,m,אֲפִיחַ(Ancient Hebrew),Biblical Hebrew,,,,,,,,"Means ""I will make to breathe"", from Hebrew פּוּחַ (púakh) ""to breathe, to blow"". Aphiah, of the tribe of Benjamin, was an ancestor of King Saul."
Aphiban,,อภิบาล(Thai),Thai,/ʔa˨˩.pʰi˦˥.ˈbaːn˧/,,,,,,,"Means ""protect, guard, look after"" in Thai, ultimately from Sanskrit अभिपाल (abhipāla)."
