Aphthonios,m,Ἀφθόνιος(Ancient Greek),Late Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from either the Greek noun ἀφθονία (aphthonia) meaning ""freedom from envy"" as well as ""abundance"" or the Greek adjective ἄφθονος (aphthonos) meaning ""without envy"" as well as ""abundant, bounteous, plentiful"".Both words are ultimately derived from the Greek negative prefix ἀ (a) and the Greek noun φθόνος (phthonos) meaning ""envy, ill will""."
Aphthonius,m,,Late Greek (Latinized)|Late Roman|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Aphthonios. Notable bearers of this name include the Greek sophist and rhetorician Aphthonius of Antioch (4th century AD) and the Roman writer Aelius Festus Aphthonius (4th century AD). Saint Aphthonius was a Persian Christian and ordained priest. He was arrested and slain for the faith during the reign of King Shapur II.
