Ares,f,,Catalan,/ˈa.ɾəs/,,,,,,,"Derived from Catalan Mare de Déu de les Ares (""Mother of God of the Ares""), which is the name of a sanctuary located on Coll d'Ares, a Catalan mountain pass in the Pyrenees. It is the site of a Marian devotion, which is why Catalan parents bestow this name upon their daughters."
Aresander,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Aresandros.
Aresandros,m,Ἀρέσανδρος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἀρέσκω (aresko) meaning ""to satisfy, please"" as well as ""to atone, make amends"", combined with ἀνδρός (andros) meaning ""of a man""."
Aresas,m,Ἀρέσας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from ἀρέσκω (aresko) meaning ""to please, satisfy; to make amends"". Aresas of Lucania was the sixth head of the Pythagorean school in succession from Pythagoras himself."
