Arete,f,Ἀρήτη(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,/aː.rɛ̌ː.tɛː/(Classical Greek),,,,,,,"Appears to mean ""she who is prayed for"" from the Ancient Greek verb ἀράομαι (araomai) ""to pray to"", related to the noun ἀρά (ara) ""prayer, vow, curse""; also compare the Ionic Greek noun ἀρητή (arete) which meant ""sacred"", ""cursed"" or ""prayed"". Alternatively it could be related to Ares, the name of the Greek god of war. In Greek mythology Queen Arete was the wife of Alcinous and mother of Nausicaa. A famous bearer was the 4th-century BC Greek philosopher Arete of Cyrene, daughter of Aristippus who was himself a pupil of Socrates and the founder of the Cyrenaic school of philosophy.This name should not be confused with Ancient Greek Ἀρετή (see Arete)."
Arétée,m,,History (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Aretaeus.
Areteo,m,,Italian|Spanish,,,,,,,,Italian and Spanish form of Aretaeus.
