Aruvkhan,f,Арувхан(Karakalpak Cyrillic),Karakalpak,[ɑ.ruv.ˈxɑn],,,,,,,"A traditional feminine name of Turkic origin, predominantly used within the Karakalpak and Kazakh nominal systems. The name is an authentic Kipchak-Nogay compound formed by two distinct elements:Aruv (арув): The modern Karakalpak and Nogay phonetic realization of the Common Turkic root $arïg$ (or $*arïq$), meaning ""pure,"" ""clean,"" ""virtuous,"" or ""physically beautiful."" This specific sound shift ($-ıg/-ıq \rightarrow -uv$) distinguishes it from the Southeastern/Karluk branches (which favor ariq) and Southwestern/Oghuz branches (which favor arı).Khan (хан / қан): The historical Central Asian sovereign title khan or qan (""ruler,"" ""monarch""). While traditionally masculine in political administration, it has a deep, documented history across Eurasian nomadic dynasties as a prestigious feminine suffix used to denote noble or high-born lineage.Modern comparative historical linguistics and Hunnolo-Turkological studies identify Aruvkhan as the direct, living linguistic continuation of the Proto-Turkic compound $*arïyqan$ (""the pure princess/ruler"").This ancient compound is famously recorded in 5th-century Byzantine diplomatic manuscripts by the historian Priscus of Panium, who visited the court of Attila the Hun in 448 AD and recorded the name of Attila's chief Empress as Erekan (also transliterated as Herekan). In Western traditions and secondary Germanic heroic legends, this original name was later contracted and popularized as Kreka (or Herka/Helche).The phonetic evolution from the 5th-century Hunnic primary recording to the modern Karakalpak name follows established morphophonemic laws of the Steppe languages, proving that the modern name directly preserves the Empress's true title: Hunnic (Priscus's Greek Transcription): Erekan : Proto-Turkic *Arï(y)-qan > Middle Turkic: Arïg-khan > Modern Karakalpak: Aruvkhan"
