Ashford,m,,English (Rare),ASH-fərd,,,,,,,"Transferred use of the surname Ashford, which itself is derived from the name of one of several places called Ashford in England. All but one of these derive the second element of their name from Old English ford meaning ""ford"" - for the one in North Devon, it is derived from Old English worō or worth meaning ""enclosure"".As for the first element of their name: for most places, it is derived from Old English æsc(e) meaning ""ash tree(s)"". But for Ashford in the county of Kent, it is derived from Old English æscet or æsceat meaning ""clump of ash trees"". And for the one in the county of Surrey (formerly Middlesex), it is derived from the medieval personal name Eccel, which itself is ultimately derived from Old English ecg or ecca meaning ""sword"".As such, depending on the place that the ancestor of a bearer of the surname originally came from, the meaning of the surname can be: ""ford by the (clump of) ash trees"", ""enclosure full of ash trees"" and ""Eccel's ford""."
