Asius,m,Ἄσιος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Uncertain etymology. Possibly derived from Ancient Greek ἆσαι (asai), an inflected form of ἄω (ao) ""to satiate"" ultimately related to ἥδομαι (hedomai) ""to be pleased, enjoy oneself"". Alternatively, it could derive from ἄσιος (asios), an epithet of uncertain meaning applied to a meadow, often interpreted as ""Asian, of Asia"". In the Iliad, Asius is a Trojan hero from Arisbe, the son of Hyrtacus. He was killed in battle after foolishly not heeding the advice of Polydamas."
