Aufroy,m,,Medieval French,,,,,,,,"Medieval French form of Alfried, which is a short form of Adalfried and thus not to be confused with Alfred. Also compare Godefroy and Geoffroy.This name is sometimes listed as a medieval French form of the Germanic name Ansfried, but that should technically be incorrect, as names starting with Ans- typically do not become Au- in medieval French. Only names that start with Al- do, such as Albert (which becomes Aubert) and Albin (which becomes Aubin). Also compare Avery."
Auge,f,"Αὔγη, Αὐγή(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ˈɔː.dʒiː/(English)|/ǎu̯.ɡɛː/(Attic Greek)|/ˈaβ.ʝi/(Koine Greek)|/ˈav.ʝi/(Byzantine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Greek αὐγή (auge) meaning ""light of the sun, sunbeam"", ""bright light"", or ""dawn"". In Greek mythology Auge was the daughter of Aleus, king of Tegea, and mother of the hero Telephus by Heracles. According to Hyginus this name also belonged to one of the Horae, namely the goddess who personified the first light of day. It was also borne by a martyr venerated in Orthodox Christianity, who was one of seven female followers of Saint Blaise of Sebaste who were decapitated after him in 316 under the governor Agricola."
