Aycen,m,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Ayson.
Ayçiçek,f,,Medieval Turkic,[ai-chee-CHEHK]|[ay-chi-chek],,,,,,,"Ayçiçek is an authentic, highly esteemed medieval Turkic name derived from two pure Old Turkic roots: Ay (moon, symbol of beauty, light, and spiritual elevation) and Çiçek / Çeçek (flower, blossom). The name carries a dual semantic framework:1. The Literal Botanical Meaning: It refers directly to the Sunflower (Helianthus annuus), a plant that instinctively turns its face to track the light, symbolizing loyalty, warmth, radiance, and natural abundance.2. The Poetic/Cosmic Meaning: It translates to ""The Moon-Flower"" or ""The Flower of the Moon,"" representing a celestial, pristine blossom that blooms in darkness, radiating a soft, mesmerizing light.Historical Figures & Regal LineageUnlike many modern botanical names, Ayçiçek carries a deeply rooted medieval aristocratic lineage across Central Asia:The 8th-Century Uyghur Empire: In historical administrative fragments and Old Uyghur civil documents dating back to the 8th century, a high-born royal princess is explicitly recorded under the archaic spelling Ay-çeçek, highlighting the name’s early elite status within the steppes.The 12th-Century Khwarazmian Empire: The name achieved immense historical gravity as the name of Ayçiçek Hatun, the mother of the legendary, unyielding ruler Jaloliddin Manguberdi (Celaleddin Harzemşah) and wife of Alaeddin Tekiş. Celebrated in Central Asian chronicles for her fierce dignity, political influence, and resilience during the catastrophic Mongol invasions, her figure transformed the name into a symbol of matriarchal fortitude.For parents, Ayçiçek is a luminous and historically grounded choice. It symbolizes a child who possesses both the delicate grace of a flower and the radiant, guiding presence of the moon. It implies a personality that naturally seeks light, exhibits deep ancestral resilience, and brings natural elegance and warmth to everyone around her."
