Bartleby,m,,Literature,BAHR-təl-bee,,,,,,,"This name was apparently invented by the 19th-century writer Herman Melville, who perhaps intended it to mean ""Bartholomew's town"" from the medieval English name Bartle, a diminutive of Bartholomew, combined with the English place name suffix -by meaning ""farm, settlement"", from Old Norse býr. Additionally, Melville may have been inspired by the similar-sounding name Barnaby."
