Begoyim,f,Бегойым(Uzbek),Uzbek,[bɛ.ɡɔ.ˈjɪm],,,,,,,"A regal name derived from the Old Turkic title Beg (meaning ""ruler,"" ""lord,"" ""noble,"" or ""leader"") and the honorific suffix oyim (denoting a lady of high status, mistress, or royalty). It translates to ""The Noble Lady"" or ""The Lady Leader."" This name is an exquisite tribute to inherent dignity, leadership potential, and aristocratic poise. It paints a picture of a person who carries herself with natural grace, commanding respect not through force, but through a calm, unshakable nobility of character that reflects her noble lineage and dignified soul."
Begtabeg,m,ბეგთაბეგ(Georgian),Georgian (Archaic),,,,,,,,"Derived from the Ottoman Turkish gubernatorial title بكلربكی‎ (beglerbegi) meaning ""bey of the beys"" or ""head of the beys"". In turn, it is derived from the Turkish military title beg meaning ""chieftain, master"".The given name Begtabeg is best known for being the name of the eponymous ancestor of the Georgian noble family of Begtabegishvili, who lived in the 17th century. He was a capable scribe and calligrapher, whose best known work is his manuscript of the medieval Georgian epic The Knight in the Panther's Skin."
Begtse,m,,Mongolian Mythology,,,,,,,,"Begtse ""Begtse the Great Coat of Mail"" is the lord of war in Tibetan Buddhism, originally a pre-Buddhist war god of the Mongols, the name Begtse (Wylie: Beg tse) is a loanword from Mongolian begder, meaning ""coat of mail"". He is also given the name and epithet Jamsaran (Wylie: lCam sring), meaning ""Great Coat of Mail"", which is a translation of the Mongolian."
Begtütmish,f,,History,,,,,,,,The name of a sister of Ibaqa Beki and Sorghaghtani Beki.
