Belgrano,m,,Soviet,,,,,,,,"Name given in honor of the Argentine General Manuel Belgrano, one of the founding fathers of that country."
Belhaj,,بالحاج(Arabic),Arabic (Maghrebi),,,,,,,,Not available.
Belhassen,m,بلحسن(Maghrebi Arabic),Arabic (Maghrebi),,,,,,,,Transferred use of the surname Belhassen.
Belhonor,f,,Medieval Italian,,,,,,,,"Derived from Latin bella ""beautiful, charming, pleasant"" and honor ""honor""."
Beli,m,,Norse Mythology|Old Norse|Spanish,,,,,,,,"Derived from Old Norse belja meaning ""to roar"". This is the name of a jotunn in Norse mythology."
Belia,f,,Medieval Jewish|Medieval English|Judeo-Anglo-Norman|Dutch|Various,,,,,,,,"Variant of Bela, Bella and Beila."
Béliam,m,,"French (Quebec, Rare, Archaic, ?)",,,,,,,,Not available.
Belián,m,,Hungarian,,,,,,,,"Hungarian name derived from Slavic бѣлъ (belu) ""white""."
Beliana,f,,Obscure,,,,,,,,Could be a combination of Bella and Ana.
Beliande,f,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"Daughter of the King of Norgales (North Wales), the mother of Galihodin.Galihodin was the nephew (or natural son) of Galeholt."
Belianís,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Belianís of Greece is the hero of a Spanish chivalric romance novel, ""The honour of chivalry"", following in the footsteps of the influential Amadis de Gaula. An English abridgement of this novel was published in 1673. It is best known today because it was one of the books spared during the expurgation of Don Quixote's library in Chapter 6 of Part I of Don Quixote."
Belianz,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"One of four miscreant brother knights killed by Gawaine in ""Diu Crône"" by Heinrich von dem Türlin, c. 1230"
Beliar,m,,Judeo-Christian-Islamic Legend,,,,,,,,Variant of Belial.
Belias,m,,Gnosticism,,,,,,,,Variant of Belial.
Belica,f,,Spanish,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Isabel.
Belicar,m,,Guanche,,,,,,,,The name of a Guanche mencey (leader) from Tenerife.
Belicia,f,,"Spanish (Rare)|American (Hispanic, Rare)|American (Rare)",bay-LEE-see-ah(Spanish),,,,,,,Not available.
Belida,f,,Judeo-Spanish,,,,,,,,Variant of Bellida.
Belide,f,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"A character in Arthurian legend who fell in love with Tristan while he was living and serving in Faramon’s court. When Tristan did not reciprocate, she became enraged and staged a rape scene for which Tristan was convicted and sentenced to execution."
Belief,m,,English (Puritan),,,,,,,,"From the Old English word geleafa, meaning ""faith or trust."""
Believe,,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Late Old English belȳfan, belēfan, alteration of gelēfan, of Germanic origin; related to Dutch geloven and German glauben, also to lief."
