Benzaiten,f,"弁才天, 弁財天(Japanese Kanji)|べんざいてん(Japanese Hiragana)",Japanese Mythology,/be̞n.d͡zäi.te̞ɴ/(Japanese),,,,,,,"Means ""eloquent goddess"", from Japanese 弁才 (benzai) meaning ""eloquence"" and 天 (ten) meaning ""sky, heavens, deva, god"". The name was historically written as 弁財天, with the second kanji 財 (zai) meaning ""money, wealth, riches"".This is the name of a Japanese goddess revered as both a Buddhist deity and a Shinto kami. She is associated with speech, wealth and music and is considered the Japanese form of the Hindu goddess Saraswati. She is often depicted with a biwa, a kind of pear-shaped lute."
