Beray,f,,Turkish (Modern),beh-RIE,,,,,,,"Beray is a modern Turkish female name. It is highly probable that it is a compound name formed by the combination of the Turkish verb root ber- (an archaic variant of ver-, meaning ""to give"") and the common noun and suffix ay meaning ""moon"". Under this etymology, the name conveys the meaning of ""giving the moon"" or ""bringing light like the moon"". Alternatively, if interpreted as an Old Turkic volitional/wish phrase, it can mean ""may it give light like the moon"".Another strong Turkic possibility is that it derived from the word beri (meaning ""this side"" or ""towards here"") combined with ay, which through phonetic dropping yields the meaning ""the moon that comes closer"" or ""the moon that is near us"".While the prefix Ber- superficially resembles the Persian preposition bar (بر) or the Arabic roots bara'a (برآء) or barat (براءة), a Semitic or Iranian origin is highly implausible. The phonological evolution of the name strictly follows Turkish vowel harmony and morphotactics. Borrowings from Arabic Bera'at or Bera traditionally preserve their distinct phonetic markers (like the glottal stop or long vowels) in Turkish orthography (e.g., Berat or Bera), whereas Beray functions purely as a native Turkic compound both in rhythm and semantic structure."
