Bertilak,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"Bertilak de Hautdesert is revealed to be the true name of the Green Knight, a heroic character of the Matter of Britain, originating in the 14th-century poem Sir Gawain and the Green Knight and the related medieval work The Greene Knight.The name ""Bertilak"" may derive from bachlach, a Celtic word meaning ""churl"" (i.e. rogueish, unmannerly), or from ""bresalak"", meaning ""contentious"". The Old French word bertolais is a form of ""Bertilak"" in the Arthurian tale Merlin from the Lancelot-Grail cycle. Notably, the 'Bert-' prefix means 'bright', and the '-lak' can mean either 'lake' or ""play, sport, fun, etc""."
