Beyla,f,,Norse Mythology|Yiddish,BAY-lah,,,,,,,"From the reconstructed Proto-Norse name *Baunila, possibly meaning ""little bean"" or ""little swelling"". In Norse mythology, Beyla is a servant of the god Freyr with her husband Bryggvir."
Beylan,f,,Medieval Turkic,[BIE-lahn]|[bay-LAHN],,,,,,,"Beylan is a variant of the name Baylan. In many regions of Anatolia (including Sivas, Ankara, and Kayseri), the name carries the meanings of ""graceful,"" ""coquettish,"" ""delicate,"" ""well-mannered,"" ""polite,"" or ""mature.""It is derived from the Old Turkic root bay, meaning ""rich,"" ""great,"" ""noble,"" or ""lord."" This root is also the basis for the masculine title Bay and the feminine title Bayan. While Baylan is the more traditional spelling, Beylan reflects a common phonetic shift in various Turkish dialects where the a vowel softens toward an e."
Beyle,f,,Judeo-French,,,,,,,,Variant of Bele.
Beylem,f,,Turkish,[bɛj.lɛm],,,,,,,"A uniquely poetic Turkic name derived from the root Beyle- (an archaic variant of Bağla-, meaning ""to tie,"" ""to bind,"" or ""to connect""). Etymologically, it is the direct ancestor to the modern word Bağlam (""context"" or ""connection""). In historical usage, a Beylem is a ""corsage"" or a ""gathered bouquet,"" a collection of beautiful elements bound together into one harmonious whole. Symbolically, the name represents an individual who brings people together, a ""binder"" of hearts, or one who holds the threads of a narrative or community together in a state of grace. It is a name for a person who creates order out of beauty."
Beyllida,f,,Judeo-Spanish,,,,,,,,Variant of Bellida.
Beyllita,f,,Judeo-Spanish,,,,,,,,Variant of Bellida.
