Bleibefromm,m,,German (Rare),,,,,,,,"Meaning ""stay pious"". The name is literally made up of the two German words bleibe, meaning ""stay"", and fromm, meaning ""pious"" or ""devout""."
Bleiddud,m,,Medieval Welsh,,,,,,,,Variant of Bleiddudd. Bleiddud was Bishop of St David's (then known as Menevia) in Wales from 1061 to 1071.
Bleiddudd,m,,Medieval Welsh,[ˈbləɪ.ðɪð],,,,,,,"Welsh form of the Brythonic name *Bledjojüd- meaning ""wolf lord"", from Proto-Brythonic *blėð meaning ""wolf"" (Welsh blaidd) and *jʉð meaning ""lord"" (Welsh udd)."
Bleiddwn,m,,Welsh Mythology,[blɛi̯ð.wn],,,,,,,"Thought to mean ""Wolf-Man"", from Welsh blaidd meaning ""wolf"".Bleiddwn is the offspring of brothers Gwydion and Gilfaethwy when they were transformed into wolves. Math took him and transformed him into a human being, baptising him Bleiddwn (""wolf-man"")."
Bleiden,f,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"In Henrich’s Diu Crône, a lady at Arthur’s court who failed in a chastity test."
Bleio,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Not available.
Bleiz,m,,Breton,/ˈblɛjs/|/ˈblɛj/,,,,,,,"Derived from Breton bleiz ""wolf; gray"" and thus nowadays commonly considered the Breton equivalent of French Loup, this name was in former times also used as a phonetic approximation to Blois and given in honor of the Blessed Charles of Blois, Duke of Brittany.From the early 1600s onwards, when every given name had to be linked with a Catholic saint, Bleiz was also used as a Breton bona fide equivalent of Saint Blaise due to its phonetic similarities."
