Brímir,m,,Icelandic,,,,,,,,Variant of either Brimir or Brími.
Brimir,m,,Norse Mythology|Icelandic|Faroese,,,,,,,,"Probably related to Old Norse brim meaning ""surf, surface of the sea"". Alternatively, it could mean ""flickerer"", from brími ""fire"". This is possibly another name for Ymir in Norse mythology. It is also the name of Odin’s sword, and eventually became a poetic word meaning ""sword""."
Brimo,f,Βριμώ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""the terrible one, the grim one, the snorting one, the angry one"", derived from Ancient Greek βρίμη (brime) meaning ""strength, power, threat, menace"" or ""bellowing, roaring"". This was the name of a minor goddess worshipped in Thessaly, as well as an epithet of several Ancient Greek goddesses associated with the underworld, most notably Hecate, Demeter, and Persephone."
Brimrún,f,,Icelandic,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old Norse elements brim ""surf, surge; surface of the sea"" and rún ""secret lore, rune""."
