Budo,m,武道(Japanese Kanji),Japanese,,,,,,,,"From Japanese 武 (bu) meaning ""warrior"" or ""martial"" and 道 (do) meaning ""way"" or ""path"". Together, 武道 (Budo) refers to ""martial arts"" or ""the way of martial arts,"" emphasizing both physical technique and spiritual development."
Budoc,m,,History (Ecclesiastical)|Breton Legend,"BU-dok(History, Breton Legend)",,,,,,,"Derived from Old Celtic boudi ""victory"". However, folk etymology likes to associate this name with beuziñ meaning ""drown"", with the intended meaning of ""saved from the waters"". In Breton legend this is the name of a 6th century saint, son of Azenor."
Budog,m,,Breton,,,,,,,,Variant of Budoc.
Budogan,m,,Breton (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Budog.
Budok,m,,"History (Ecclesiastical, Polonized)",,,,,,,,Polonized version of Budoc.
Budolf,m,,Danish,,,,,,,,Danish form of Botulf.
Budou,,"ぶどう(Japanese Hiragana)|武道, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,BUU-DO:,,,,,,,"Means ""grape"" in Japanese or from Japanese 武道 (budou) meaning ""Japanese martial arts"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Budsaba,f,บุษบา(Thai),Thai,/but̚˨˩.sa˨˩.ˈbaː˧/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai บุษบา (see Butsaba).
Budsarakam,f,บุษราคัม(Thai),Thai,/but̚˨˩.sa˨˩.raː˧.ˈkʰam˧/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai บุษราคัม (see Butsarakham).
Budsarakham,f,บุษราคัม(Thai),Thai,/but̚˨˩.sa˨˩.raː˧.ˈkʰam˧/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Thai บุษราคัม (see Butsarakham).
Budu,m,ბუდუ(Georgian),Georgian (Rare),,,,,,,,"Meaning uncertain. It is speculated to be of Hurrian origin, with the meaning of ""servant"".The name has also been associated with Russian буду (budu) meaning ""I will (be)"" and even been interpreted as such, but that is nothing more than a folk etymology that arose when Georgia was still under Russian rule.A notable bearer of this name is the Georgian soccer player Budu Zivzivadze (b. 1994)."
Budymyr,m,Будимир(Ukrainian),Ukrainian (Archaic),/bu.ˈdɪ.mer/,,,,,,,"From Ukrainian будує мир (buduye myr), which means ""builds peace"". The name means ""one who spreads peace""."
Budyona,m,Будёна(Russian),Soviet,,,,,,,,Taken from surname of Semyon Budyonyy.
Budzimir,m,,"Polish (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from Polish budzić ""to (a)wake, to (a)rouse"". Also compare Croatian buditi ""to awaken, to (a)rouse"". The second element is derived from Slavic mir ""peace"". As such, the meaning of this name is roughly ""awakening of peace"" or ""peaceful awakening""."
Budzislaŭ,m,Будзіслаў(Belarusian),Belarusian (Rare),,,,,,,,Belarusian form of Budislav.
Budzisław,m,,Polish (Archaic),,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from Polish budzić ""to (a)wake, to (a)rouse"". Also compare Croatian buditi ""to awaken, to (a)rouse"". The second element is derived from Slavic slav ""glory"". As such, the meaning of this name is roughly ""awakening of glory"" or ""glorious awakening""."
Budzisława,f,,"Polish (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Feminine form of Budzisław.
Bue,m,,Danish|Norwegian (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Búi.
Buel,m,,English,,,,,,,,Variant of Buell.
Buelah,f,,English,byoo-lah,,,,,,,Variant of Beulah.
Buell,m,,English,,,,,,,,Transferred use of the surname Buell.
Buena,f,,Spanish|Judeo-Spanish,bwena,,,,,,,"Means ""good"" in Judeo-Spanish."
Buenaestruga,f,,Judeo-Spanish,,,,,,,,Variant of Bonastruga.
Buenastruga,f,,Jewish|Judeo-Spanish,bweh-nah-stroo-gah,,,,,,,Variant of Bonastruga.
Buenaventurada,f,,"Spanish (Philippines, Rare)",/bwɛ.na.bɛn.tu.ˈɾa.da/(Filipino Spanish),,,,,,,"Derived from Spanish bienaventurada meaning ""blessed"", with the spelling influenced by Buenaventura."
Buenaventuranza,f,,"Spanish (Philippines, Rare, Archaic)|Pampangan (Rare, Archaic)",/bwe.nɐ.ben.tʊ.ˈɾan.sɐ/(Filipino Spanish)|/bwɛ.nə.bɛn.tʊ.ˈɾan.sə/(Pampangan),,,,,,,"Derived from Spanish bienaventuranza meaning ""beatitude"", with the spelling influenced by Buenaventura. This name was most common (though was still extremely rare) in the provinces of Batangas and Pampanga."
Buenavista,f,,"Spanish (Philippines, Rare)|English (American, Archaic)",,,,,,,,"Derived from Buena meaning ""good"", combined with Vista meaning ""View"", which was derived from an identical-sounding place name."
Buendía,m,,Judeo-Spanish,/bwẽn̪.ˈd̪i.a/,,,,,,,"Derived from Spanish buen día, meaning ""good day"" and making it a cognate of Bonjour."
Bueno,m,,Judeo-Spanish,bwe-no,,,,,,,Masculine form of Buena.
Buensuceso,,,Spanish (Rare)|Filipino (Rare),/bwen.su.ˈθe.so/(European Spanish)|/bwen.su.ˈse.so/(Latin American Spanish),,,,,,,"From a Spanish title of the Virgin Mary, Nuestra Señora del Buen Suceso, meaning ""Our Lady of the Good Event,"" referring to the Purification of Mary and the Presentation of Jesus.In the Philippines, where a statue of Mary is enshrined under the title of Our Lady of the Good Event of Parañaque in St. Andrew's Cathedral of Parañaque, it is mostly masculine in usage."
Buer,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Meaning unknown. This is the name of a demon who appears in several works on demonology, including the 17th-century grimoire The Lesser Key of Solomon (author unknown), the Pseudomonarchia Daemonum (1563) by Dutch physician Johann Weyer and the Dictionnaire Infernal (1818) by French occultist Jacques Collin de Plancy."
Buetur,m,Буэтур(Yakut),Yakut,,,,,,,,Yakut form of Peter.
Bueyo,f,,Spanish (Rare),/ˈbwe.ʝo/,,,,,,,"From the Marian title Virgen de Bueyo, patron saint of Albelda de Iregua (La Rioja). The name Bueyo comes from a small settlement in the vicinity where oxherding was common (cf. Spanish buey ""ox"")."
Bufang,,"步芳, etc.(Chinese)",Chinese|Hui,,,,,,,,Not available.
Bufawn,f,,African,,,,,,,,Not available.
Buffi,f,,"English (American, Rare)",BUF-ee(American English),,,,,,,Variant of Buffy.
Buffie,f,,English (Rare),/ˈbʌf.i/,,,,,,,Variant of Buffy.
Buford,m,,English (American),,,,,,,,Transferred use of the surname Buford.
