Cahus,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"Son of Yvain the Bastard and one of Arthur’s squires in Perlesvaus.Arthur announced that he was going to take Cahus on a pilgrimage to the Chapel of St. Augustine in the White Forest. On the night before the scheduled journey, Cahus had a dream that he visited a shrine near the chapel, took a golden candle, and was stabbed in the side by a Black Knight.He awoke in Arthur’s castle and found to his astonishment that he was holding a golden candle, and had a dagger stuck in his side. He perished of the wound after presenting the candle to his king.A similar story is related about an unnamed attendant of Arthur’s in John of Glastonbury’s chronicle."
Cahyadi,m,,Indonesian,/t͡ʃah.ˈja.di/,,,,,,,"From Indonesian cahaya meaning ""light"" combined with adi meaning ""first"" in Indonesian or ""beautiful, good, valuable"" in Javanese."
Cahyani,f,,Indonesian,/t͡ʃah.ˈja.ni/,,,,,,,"From Indonesian cahaya meaning ""light"" (of Sanskrit origin) combined with the feminine suffix -ni."
