Calliteles,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Kalliteles. A known bearer of this name was the Olympic victor Calliteles of Laconia (also known as Sparta). He had won the wrestling contest during the 68th Olympiad in 508 BC.
Callithea,f,Καλλιθέα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),/kə.ˈlɪ.θi.ə/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek κάλλος (kallos) meaning ""beautiful"" combined with either θέα (thea) meaning ""sight, view"" or θεά (thea) meaning ""goddess"". In Greek mythology, this was a variant name for Callithyia."
Callithoe,f,Καλλιθόη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek κάλλος (kallos) meaning ""beauty"" and θοός (thoos) meaning ""quick, swift"". Callithoe was a daughter of Celeus in Greek mythology."
Callithyia,f,Καλλίθυια(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),/ˌkæ.lɪ.ˈθaɪ.ə/(English),,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Ancient Greek κάλλος (kallos) meaning ""beauty"" and θύω (thyo) meaning ""to offer in sacrifice, kill, burn"" as well as ""to celebrate"". Alternatively, the second element could derive from either θυία (thyia), referring to the Juniperus foetidissima plant, or θύον (thyon) meaning ""cypress-pine"". Callithyia, also called Callithoe and Callithea, was an Argive princess and the first priestess of Argive Hera in Greek mythology. Callithyia was also the name of a daughter of Aeolus, keeper of the winds."
Calliupa,f,,Sicilian,,,,,,,,Sicilian form of Calliope.
