Campanula,f,,English (Rare),kam-pə-NYOO-lə,,,,,,,"From the name of the flower, which means ""little bell"" in Latin, diminutive of Late Latin campana ""bell"" (originally ""metal vessel made in Campania"", region around Naples). The flower is widespread across the whole temperate regions of Europe, but has the most species diversity in the Mediterranean region. This name has been used rarely since the late 19th century when flower names became popular."
Campaspe,f,Καμπάσπη(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (?),/kæm.ˈpæs.piː/(English),,,,,,,"Unknown etymology. Campaspe, also called Pancaste and Pacate, was the purported mistress or concubine of Alexander the Great, and a prominent citizen of Larissa. There are no contemporary sources recording her existence, which may be apocryphal. Her name came to be used as a poetic term for a man’s mistress in English."
