Canace,f,Κανάκη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),/ˈkæ.nə.ˌsiː/(English),,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Greek κανάκι (kanaki) meaning ""caresses, endearments, praise"", or perhaps from the word’s older root καναχή (kanakhe) meaning ""sharp sound; ring or clang of metal, clattering"". In Greek mythology, this was the name of a daughter of Aeolus and Enarete, and lover of Poseidon. In a variant tale, Canace was instead in a relationship with her own brother Macareus. This tradition made them children of a different Aeolus, the lord of the winds (or the Tyrrhenian king), and his wife Amphithea."
