Canethus,m,Κάνηθος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),/kə.ˈniː.θəs/(English),,,,,,,"Etymology uncertain. Possibly related to Ancient Greek κάνη (kane) meaning ""reed"" and the Mycenaean suffix -thos, or perhaps to κανθός (kanthos) meaning ""eyelid, corner of the eye"". This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including one of the fifty sons of King Lycaon. It was also the name of a mountain near Chalcis."
