Carmanor,m,Καρμάνωρ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),kahr-man-or(Greek Mythology),,,,,,,"Uncertain etymology, possibly of pre-Greek origin; however, some theories draw a relation to the Ancient Greek word κάρμα (karma) meaning ""shorn, skimmed, cropped"" (from κείρω (keiro) ""to shear""). Another connects it to West Semitic krm meaning ""vineyard"". In Greek mythology, this was the name of a Cretan priest said to have purified the god Apollo after he killed Python. By some accounts he was the grandfather of the Cretan harvest goddess Carme, and may have been a demi-god of the harvest himself. This was also the name of a son of Dionysos."
