Cathay,f,,American (Rare),,,,,,,,"Derived from the Mongolian word khitan via its variant form cat(h)ai - the meaning is uncertain, but it refers to the Khitan people. Cathay is the anglicized form of cat(h)ai, and was the archaic name for China. There is also an other possible etymology for this name, which is completely different than the aforementioned and only applies to female bearers of this name living in an English-speaking country: in their case, Cathay may be a variant of Cathy."
Cathaysa,f,,Guanche|Spanish (Canarian),,,,,,,,"Allegedly means ""beautiful (big) daisy"" in the Guanche language. Recorded as the name of a 7-year-old Guanche girl who was sold as a slave in Valencia in 1494. Cathaysa is also the title of a song recorded by singer Pedro Guerra in the 1980s."
