Catigern,m,,Old Welsh (?),[ca.ˈtɪ.ɡern],,,,,,,"Meaning ""battle-prince"", stemming from a Common Brittonic form reconstructed as *katu-tigernos, formed with the root *katu- (""combat""; cf. Gaul. catu- ""combat, battle"", Old Irish. cath ""battle, troop"") attached to tigernos (""lord, master""; c. Gaul. tigerno-, Old Irish. tigern, Old Welsh. tegyrned, Old Breton. Tigern).The name Catiherno, borne by a Breton priest c. 509—521, may also be related.Catigern (Welsh: Cadeyrn Fendigaid) is a figure of Welsh tradition, said to be a son of Vortigern, the tyrannical King of the Britons, and the brother of Vortimer.He appears briefly in Geoffrey of Monmouth's pseudohistorical chronicle Historia Regum Britanniae, in a section adapted from the Historia Brittonum account. His battle with Horsa is the subject of John Lesslie Hall's poem ""The Death of Horsa"", and he appears as a minor character in William Henry Ireland's play ""Vortigern and Rowena"", which was initially touted as a lost work by William Shakespeare."
