Chun-ja,f,춘자(Korean Hangul),Korean,CHOON-JAH,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 春 ""spring"" and 子 ""child"". Chun-ja was the tenth-most popular name for Korean girls born in 1940. The same characters can be read Haruko in Japanese. It is one of a number of Japanese-style names ending in ja that were popular when Korea was under Japanese rule, but declined in popularity afterwards. By 1950 there were no names ending in ja in the top ten."
Chunjian,,"春键, 春健, 春剑, 纯键, 纯建, etc.(Chinese)|春键, 春健, 春劍, 纯键, 純建, etc.(Traditional Chinese)",Chinese,/ʈ͡ʂʰwən.t͡ɕjɛn/,,,,,,,"From Chinese 春 (chūn) meaning ""spring (season)"" or 纯 (chún) meaning ""pure, clean, simple"" combined with 键 (jiàn) meaning ""door bolt, lock, key"", 健 (jiàn) meaning ""strong, healthy"", 剑 (jiàn) meaning ""sword, sabre"" or 建 (jiàn) meaning ""build, establish"". Other character combinations can form this name as well."
