Cincinnato,m,,Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,Italian form of Cincinnatus. A known bearer of the name was Italian sculptor Cincinnato Baruzzi (1796-1878).
Cincinnatus,m,,Ancient Roman,[kɪŋ.kɪn.ˈnaː.tʊs](Classical Latin)|/ˌsɪn.sɪ.ˈneɪ.təs/(English),,,,,,,"From a Roman cognomen meaning ""having curly hair"". Lucius Quinctius Cincinnatus (519-430 BC) was a statesman of the Roman Republic who became a famous model of Roman virtue by the time of the late Republic. A known bearer of the name was American poet Cincinnatus Heine Miller, known by his pen name Joaquin Miller (1837-1913)."
Cindazunda,f,,"Germanic (Portuguese-style, ?)",,,,,,,,"Possibly a Portuguese form of Chindaswinth.Cindazunda was the daughter of Hermerico, king of the Suebi, in the territory that would become Spain's Galicia and the north of Portugal. She married Attaces, king of the Alans, in the early 5th century. This Suebi princess is immortalized in history as a symbol of the city of Coimbra, in Portugal, and her image appears in the official coat of arms of the city."
