Copreus,m,Κοπρεύς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Κοπρεύς (Kopreus), which is derived from Greek κόπρειος (kopreios) meaning ""full of dung, filthy"", from κόπρος (kopros) meaning ""excrement, dung"". Needless to say, this is quite an unflattering name to have, though it originally may have had a more positive connotation and been associated with the ownership of cattle, rather than just the cattle's dung. It may have also been given as an apotropaic name in families with high infant mortality, or to children left abandoned on a κοπρών (dung heap), who were often taken in and raised as slaves. In Greek mythology, Copreus was the herald of King Eurystheus."
