Corneel,,,Dutch (Rare)|Flemish,kawr-NAY:L(Dutch),,,,,,,"Short form of both Cornelis and Cornelius (for men) and Cornelia (for women), but the name is most often encountered on men. It is unisex in the Netherlands, but strictly masculine in Flanders, the Dutch-speaking part of Belgium.Notable bearers of this name include the Flemish soccer player Corneel De Ceulaer (b. 1947), the Flemish author Corneel van Kuyck (1919-1991) and the Flemish scientist and Nobel Prize winner Corneille ""Corneel"" Heymans (1892-1968)."
Corneelke,,,Dutch (Rare)|Flemish (Rare),kawr-NAY:L-kə(Dutch),,,,,,,"Diminutive of Corneel as well as of the related names Cornelis and Cornelius (for men) and Cornelia (for women).This name is only used informally, meaning: so far it has not been used as an official name on birth certificates. It is also rather old-fashioned and one is more likely to encounter it in Flanders (the Dutch-speaking part of Belgium) than in the Netherlands, because the diminutive suffix -ke is used more often there than in the Netherlands, where the diminutive suffix -je is generally preferred (as such, see Corneeltje)."
Corneeltje,,,Dutch (Rare),kawr-NAY:L-tyə,,,,,,,"Diminutive of Corneel as well as of the related names Cornelis and Cornelius (for men) and Cornelia (for women).This name is predominantly feminine in the Netherlands, especially as an official name on birth certificates. For men, the name is nowadays exclusively an informal name, but this was not always the case in the past."
