Coro,f,,Spanish (European),/ˈko.ɾo/(European Spanish),,,,,,,"Means ""choir"" in Spanish, taken from the title of the Virgin Mary Nuestra Señora del Coro (meaning ""Our Lady of the Choir""). She is the patroness of the city of Donostia/San Sebastián in the Basque province of Gipuzkoa."
Coroebus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Koroibos. A known bearer of this name was the Olympic victor Coroebus of Elis, who won the stadion race at the 1st Olympiad in 776 BC."
Corolenia,f,,Romani (Archaic),,,,,,,,Corruption of Caroline.
Corom,m,,Mormon,,,,,,,,"A middle Jaredite king, son of Levi."
Coromoto,f,,Spanish,/ko.ɾo.ˈmo.to/,,,,,,,"Taken from the Venezuelan Spanish title of the Virgin Mary Nuestra Señora de Coromoto, meaning ""Our Lady of Coromoto,"" the name taken from the cacique (chief) of a local Indian tribe, known as the Cosmes, who, legend says, twice witnessed the Virgin Mary.Our Lady of Coromoto is regarded as the patron saint of Venezuela, similar to Our Lady of Guadalupe in Mexico and Our Lady of Luján in Argentina. For this reason the name is most common in Venezuela but it also has some use in other Spanish-speaking countries."
Coronación,f,,Spanish (Rare),ko-ro-na-THYOHN(European Spanish)|ko-ro-na-SYOHN(Latin American Spanish),,,,,,,Spanish form of Coronacion.
Coronacion,f,,"Spanish (Philippines, Rare)",/ko.ɾo.na.ˈʃɔn/(Filipino Spanish),,,,,,,"Derived from Spanish coronación, meaning ""coronation"", referring to the idea that the Virgin Mother of God was physically crowned as Queen of Heaven after her Assumption."
Coronada,f,,Spanish,/ko.ɾo.ˈna.ða/,,,,,,,"Means ""crowned"" in Spanish, taken from the titles of the Virgin Mary Nuestra Señora de la Coronada and Virgen de la Coronada, meaning ""Our Lady of the Crowned"" and ""The Virgin of the Crowned"", respectively. Also compare Corona, Incoronata. She is the patron saint of Villafranca de los Barros in the western province of Badajoz (also declared the patroness of the vineyards in 2012 by the then archbishop of Mérida-Badajoz, Santiago García Aracil) and is also venerated at the hermitage in Calañas in the Andalusian province of Huelva. This name is also used in other Spanish-speaking countries, mostly in Central America."
Coronation,f,,Romani,,,,,,,,Not available.
Coronatus,m,,Late Roman|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Derived from Latin coronatus meaning ""crowned"".This name was borne by at least two saints."
Coronda,f,,African American (Rare),,,,,,,,"Likely an invented name, possibly a combination of Corinna and Rhonda. Also compare Sharonda."
Corone,f,Κορώνη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Means ""crow"". Corone was a young princess who was saved from Poseidon by Athena in Ovid's Metamorphoses."
