Cushla,f,,"English (Australian, Rare)|English (New Zealand, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Derived form Irish Gaelic cuisle ""pulse"". This name was created in the early 1800s from the Irish term of endearment cuisle mo cridhe (usually anglicized as Cushla Macree, in former times also Cushlamachree) which translates to ""pulse of my heart""; it is popularly interpreted to mean ""beat of my heart"".This name has hardly ever been used in Ireland itself."
Cushman,m,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,From a Ashkenazi Jewish or German surname that probably derived from a Germanized form of the Ancient Greek given name Kosmas (see Cosmo). A famous bearer of the name is American Republican politician Cushman Kellogg Davis (1838–1900).
Cusi,f,,Quechua (Hispanicized)|Inca (Hispanicized),,,,,,,,"From Quechua kusi meaning ""joy, happiness"". A known bearer was Cusi Huarcay, a 16th-century princess and queen consort of the Inca Empire. In modern times, it is borne by American playwright Cusi Cram (1967-; born to a Bolivian father and a Scottish mother)."
