Cutheard,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"Means ""famously brave"", derived from Old English cuþ ""known, familiar"" and heard ""brave, hardy"".A known bearer of this name was the 10th-century bishop Cutheard of Lindisfarne."
Cuthflæd,f,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈkuːθ.ˌflæd/(Old English),,,,,,,"Means ""famous beauty"", derived from Old English cuþ ""known, familiar"" and flæd, possibly meaning ""beauty""."
