Cymir,m,,"African American (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Variant of Symir, possibly influenced by Cy."
Cymme,f,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Cyneburga.
Cymo,f,Κυμώ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""wavy"", from Ancient Greek κῦμα (kyma) meaning ""swell, wave, flood; sprout, fetus"". This was the name of one of the Nereids in Greek mythology."
Cymochles,m,,Literature|Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"From the Greek elements κῦμα (kyma) meaning ""swell of the sea, wave, billow"" and κλέος (kleos) meaning ""glory"". In Edmund Spenser's poem The Faerie Queene (1590) this is the name of a knight characterized by indecision and fluctuations of the will. He and his fiery brother Pyrochles symbolize emotional maladies that threaten temperance. The two brothers are both slain by Prince Arthur in Canto VIII."
Cymodoce,f,Κυμοδόκη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek κῦμα (kyma) meaning ""wave"" and δοχή (dokhe) meaning ""receptacle, container"", ultimately from δέχομαι (dekhomai) meaning ""to accept, receive"".In Greek mythology, Cymodoce was a sea nymph and one of the fifty Nereids."
Cymopolea,f,Κυμοπόλεια(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Κυμοπόλεια (Kymopoleia) meaning ""wave walker"", derived from κῦμα (kyma) ""wave, billow"" and the verb πολέω (poleô) ""to go about, range over"". According to Hesiod she was a daughter of Poseidon and wife of Briareus. She may have also been known as Cymatolege."
Cymothoe,f,Κυμοθόη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek κῦμα (kyma) meaning ""wave, billow"" and θοός (thoos) meaning ""swift, quick"". Cymothoe was a sea nymph and one of the fifty Nereids in Greek mythology."
Cymphonique,f,,"African American (Modern, Rare)",Sym-fone-eek,,,,,,,"Variant of Symphony using the suffix -ique (e.g., from Monique). Cymphonique Miller is a famous bearer."
Cymphony,f,,"African American (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Symphony.
Cymry,f,,English,ˈkɪmrɪ,,,,,,,"Means ''Welsh'', plural of Cymro ''Welshman''."
Cyn,f,,English,SIN,,,,,,,Short form of Cynthia.
Cynan,m,,Welsh,[ˈkəˑ.nan],,,,,,,Welsh cognate of Conan.
Cynane,f,"Κυνάνη, Κυννάνη(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Ancient Greek κύων (kyon) meaning ""dog"" (genitive κυνός). A famous bearer was Cynane (4th century BC), half-sister to Alexander the Great. She was the daughter of Philip by Audata, an Illyrian woman, and the mother of Adea Eurydice."
Çynar,f,,Turkmen,,,,,,,,Turkmen form of Çınar.
Cynara,f,,Literature,,,,,,,,"A Greek ""plant"" name, from a genus of thistles, of which a leading member is the purple flowered artichoke.Probably originated from Zinara, in the Aegean, hence it is also considered a ""place"" name. The poet Horace sang of Cynara. Ernest Dowson revived the ancient Greek favourite with the poem, ""I have been faithful to thee, Cynara..."""
Cynda,f,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Cindy.
Cynddelw,m,,Welsh,,,,,,,,"Welsh name of uncertain origin, perhaps from an Old Celtic element meaning ""high, exalted"" combined with Welsh or Old Celtic delw ""image, effigy""."
Cynde,f,,"American (Modern, Rare)",SIN-dee,,,,,,,Variant of Cindy.
