Daiphron,m,"Δαίφρων, Δαΐφρων(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Can mean ""warlike, fiery"" or ""wise prudent"", derived from Ancient Greek φρήν (phren) meaning ""mind, heart, emotions"" combined with either δάϊς (dais) meaning ""war, battle"" (perhaps related to δαίω (daio) ""to burn"") or a conjugated form of ἐδάην (edaen) meaning ""to learn, to teach, to know"". In Greek mythology, this was the name of two sons of King Aegyptus, both of whom married a Danaid (Adiante and Scaea respectively) and were killed by their new wife on their wedding night."
