Datrice,f,,African American,,,,,,,,Not available.
Datsko,m,Дацько(Ukrainian),Medieval Ukrainian|Ukrainian (Rare),dutsy-KO(Ukrainian),,,,,,,"Diminutive or vernacular form of an unknown name. There were 147 cossacks with this name in 1649 Zaporozhian Army register.Datsko is still used in Transcarpathia as a diminutive of Andriy, so it may have been derived from this name in the 17th century as well. Other theories of origin include: Dasiy, Dariy, Danylo, Damian, other names containing d, such as Bohdan, Yordan. Alternatively it could have been derived from Dazhboh (see Dazhdbog) via its Polish form, Dadzbog."
Datua,m,დათუა(Georgian),Georgian (Rare),,,,,,,,"Often listed as a diminutive of Davit (compare names like Datiko and Dato), this name might actually be of pagan origin, in which case it is derived from the Old Georgian noun დათჳ (datwi) meaning ""bear"".A known bearer of this name was the Georgian feudal lord Datua Biskaia (17th century)."
Datuali,m,,Filipino|Maguindanao,,,,,,,,"From the Philippine title datu meaning ""chief, leader"" and the given name Ali 1."
Datuna,m,დათუნა(Georgian),Georgian,,,,,,,,"Diminutive of Davit and of names that are possibly of pagan origin and derived from Old Georgian დათჳ (datwi) meaning ""bear"", such as Datua."
Dã Tượng,m,,"Vietnamese (Rare, Archaic)",/zaː˦ˀ˥ tɨəŋ˧˨ʔ/|/jaː˧˨ tɨəŋ˨˩ʔ/|/jaː˨˩˦ tɨəŋ˨˩˨/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Vietnamese 野象 (dã tượng) meaning ""wild elephant"". This was the name of a general of the Trần dynasty."
Datura,f,,Obscure,/dæ.ˈtʊɹ.ə/,,,,,,,"From the name of the poisonous flower, derived from Hindi धतूरा (dhatūrā)."
Datus,m,,"Dutch (Rare, Archaic)",DAH:-tus|DAH:-təs,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Latin datus, meaning ""given, offered"" or ""gift"". In the Netherlands, it was occasionally given as a middle name in the nineteenth century, but it is not used at all these days."
Datuu,,,Aleut,/da.tuː/,,,,,,,"Ancient Aleut personal name meaning ""Having a big eye"" or ""Having big eyes"""
Datya,f,דַּתְיָה(Hebrew),Hebrew (Rare),,,,,,,,"Means ""God's religion"" in Hebrew, from דָּת (dat) meaning ""religion"" and יָה (yah) referring to the Hebrew God."
