Drvaspa,f,,Persian Mythology,,,,,,,,"The name of a Zoroastrian goddess whose name means ""with solid horses"". Her role in ancient religion is unknown."
Dryana,f,,Obscure,,,,,,,,Not available.
Dryantilla,f,,Ancient Roman,DRIE-an-ti-lah(Latin),,,,,,,"Sulpicia Dryantilla was a Roman Empress, the wife of Regalianus, the Roman usurper against Gallienus. Very little is known about her except that she was the daughter of Claudia Ammiana Dryantilla and Sulpicius Pollio, an accomplished senator and officer under Caracalla. She most likely died in 260/261 along with her husband, when he was killed by a coalition of his own people and the Rhoxolani."
Dryas,m,Δρύας(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek|Late Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek δρῦς (drys) meaning ""tree, timber; oak"". This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including one of the fifty sons of King Aegyptus, killed by his wife, either Hecabe or Eurydice, on their wedding night."
Dryden,m,,English (Rare),DRIE-dən,,,,,,,"Transferred use of the surname Dryden.A famous namesake is English poet, playwright and critic John Dryden (1631-1700)."
Dryhthelm,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/dryxt.heɫm/(Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements dryht ""troop, army"" and helm ""helmet, protection"". This was the name of a monk associated with the monastery of Melrose known from the Historia Ecclesiastica gentis Anglorum of Bede."
Dryhtnoð,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈdryxt.noːθ/(Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements dryht ""troop, army"" and noð ""boldness, daring""."
Dryhtweald,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈdryxt.wæ͜ɑld/(Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements dryht ""troop, army"" and weald ""powerful, mighty"" or ""authority, leader""."
Dryllina,f,,Old Swedish (Rare),,,,,,,,Obscure Old Swedish name of unknown origin.
Dryope,f,Δρυόπη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/ˈdraɪ.əp.iː/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from either δρύοψ (dryops) meaning ""woodpecker"", or from δρῦς (drys) meaning ""tree, oak"" combined with ὄψ (ops) meaning ""face, eye"". This name was borne by at several characters in Greek mythology, including the mother of Amphissus who was turned into a black poplar tree, a maenad woman who gave birth while dragging a sacred bull by the horns, and a nymph who kidnapped Hylas."
Dryops,m,Δρύοψ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"This name is either derived from Greek δρύοψ (dryops) meaning ""woodpecker"", or it is a masculine form of Dryope. This name was borne by two characters in Greek mythology: one was king Dryops of Oeta, the other was a son of king Priam of Troy."
Drypetis,f,Δρυπετις(Ancient Greek),Old Persian (Hellenized),/dry.pɛ̂ː.tis/|/ðry.ˈpi.tis/,,,,,,,Of uncertain etymology. Drypetis was the daughter of Stateira I and Darius III of Persia. She was married to Hephaestion and was rumored to have been killed by Roxana to remove potential rivals.
Drysen,m,,English (Modern),,,,,,,,Variant of Dryson.
Držimir,m,,Croatian,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from Serbo-Croatian držati ""to hold"", which is ultimately derived from Proto-Slavic dьržati ""to hold"". The second element is derived from Slavic mir ""peace""."
Držimira,f,,Croatian (Rare),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Držimir.
