Dýri,m,,Icelandic (Rare)|Old Norse,,,,,,,,"Derived from Old Norse dýr ""animal"", but also associated with the Icelandic adjective dýr meaning ""valuable, expensive, precious""."
Dyrim,f,,Literature,DIE-rim,,,,,,,"Dyrim is the fourth of the seven bells used by necromancers and the Abhorsen in Garth Nix's Old Kingdom trilogy. Dyrim is the Speaker, the bell that grants or removes the power of speech to the listener."
Dyrk,m,,English (Rare)|German (Rare),,,,,,,,"English and German variant spelling of Dirk. Also compare Dyrck.This name was borne by Dyrk Hesshaimer (1958-2015), one of the illegitimate sons of the American aviator Charles Lindbergh (1902-1974)."
Dýrleif,f,,Icelandic,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old Norse elements dýrr ""dear, valuable"" and leif ""inheritance, legacy, remnant"". Alternatively, the first element could instead be dýr ""wild animal, beast"" (see djúr)."
Dyrmit,m,Дырмит(Abkhaz),Abkhaz (Rare),,,,,,,,Abkhaz form of Demetrius.
Dýrmundur,m,,Icelandic (Rare),/ˈtir.mʏn.tʏr/,,,,,,,"Derived from Old Norse dýrr ""dear, precious"" combined with mundr ""protection"". Alternatively, the first element could be interpreted as dýr ""animal, beast"" (see djúr). The name was first used in the 1860s, after a woman had a dream in which she was told to name her son Dýrmundur instead of Guðmundur."
Dyrrhachius,m,Δυρράχιος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Ancient Greek δυσ- (dys-) ""bad, hard, unfortunate"" and ῥαχία (rhakhia) ""rocky shore, flood tide, roaring waves"". This was the name of a son of Poseidon and Melissa in Greek mythology, the ancient town Dyrrhachion (also called Dyrrachium) being named after him."
