Dzuku,m,ძუკუ(Mingrelian),Mingrelian (Rare),,,,,,,,"Meaning as of yet unknown. This name was once extremely common amongst Mingrelian men, to the point that Georgians (and possibly other Kartvelian peoples as well) used the name as a slang term to denote a Mingrelian man, especially in jokes.The phenomenon described above is not unique; it is observed across many cultures around the world. In the Anglosphere, the best known example are probably Irishmen, who would informally be referred to as a Paddy (plural: Paddies) because Patrick was such a common name amongst Irishmen (in honour of saint Patrick, the patron saint of Ireland).Last but not least, a notable bearer of this name was Porpile ""Dzuku"" Lolua (1877-1924), a Mingrelian singer who collected and preserved more than a hundred Mingrelian folk songs by recording them on a phonograph, which earned him the moniker ""Patriarch of Mingrelian folk songs""."
