Echephron,m,Ἐχέφρων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ɪ.ˈkɛf.rən/(English)|/ɪ.ˈkɛf.ˌrɒn/(English),,,,,,,"Means ""sensible, thoughtful, prudent"" in Ancient Greek, derived from ἔχω (echo) meaning ""to have, to hold, to possess"" combined with φρήν (phren) meaning ""mind, heart, emotions"". This is the name of several characters in Greek mythology, one of them being a son of King Priam of Troy."
Echeyde,m,,Guanche Mythology|Spanish (Canarian),"eh-CHAY-de(Guanche Mythology, Canarian Spanish)",,,,,,,"Echeide or Echeyde is the name that the Aboriginal Guanches gave to Teide, a volcano in Tenerife (Canary Islands, Spain). As most of the chroniclers transmitted, the Guanches (aboriginal people from Tenerife) conceived of the mountain as the place that housed the forces of evil, mainly the evil figure of Guayota. Even other more recent authors affirm that Teide would be for the ancient canaries a kind of Axis Mundi (a connection between Heaven and Earth or the higher and lower realms).Also, this is a variant of Echedey."
Echichinwo,,,African,,,,,,,,"Ikwerre name. Used in Rebisi Kingdom,Port Harcourt,(Nigeria). Meaning 'a baby delivered at the appropriate time'"
Echiko,f,"えちこ(Japanese Hiragana)|エチコ(Japanese Katakana)|愛千子, 愛知子, 悦子, 越子, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese (Rare),E-CHEE-KO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 愛 (e) meaning ""love, affection"", 千 (chi) meaning ""thousand"", 知 (chi) meaning ""wisdom"" or 智 (chi) meaning ""knowledge, wisdom"" combined with 子 (ko) meaning ""child"". Other kanji combinations are possible.Famous bearer of this name is Japanese former volleyball player and Olympic champion Echiko Maeda (1952-)."
Echiney,m,Эчинэй(Yakut),Yakut,,,,,,,,Not available.
Echion,m,Ἐχίων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ɛ.ˈkaɪ.ɒn/(English)|/e.kʰǐː.ɔːn/(Attic Greek)|/e.ˈçi.on/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἔχις (echis) meaning ""viper, adder"". Echion is the name of several characters in Greek mythology, including a son of Hermes, one of the surviving Spartoi (men grown from dragon teeth sown by Cadmus), and a suitor of Penelope."
Echione,f,,Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Possibly a feminine form of Echion. In the Fabulae attributed to Hyginus, this name is listed as belonging to a female hound of Actaeon (who was killed by his own hounds after the goddess Diana changed him into a stag)."
