Egeria,f,,Roman Mythology,i-JER-ee-ə|i-JEER-ee-ə,,,,,,,"Possibly connected to Greek αἴγειρος (aigeiros) meaning ""black poplar"", a type of tree (species Populus nigra). In Roman mythology this was the name of a nymph best known for her liaisons with Numa Pompilius, the legendary second king of Rome (after Romulus). According to the 2nd-century writer Sextus Pompeius Festus, pregnant women offered sacrifices to Egeria in order to bring out the baby (note, Latin egerere means ""to bring out"")."
