Electryone,f,Ἠλεκτρυώνη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,ee-lek-tree-O-nee,,,,,,,"Derived from either Ancient Greek ἠλέκτωρ (elektor) meaning ""shining, brilliant; the beaming sun, fire"" or from the related ἤλεκτρον (elektron) meaning ""amber"". This was the name of a daughter of the sun god Helios and island goddess Rhodos in Greek mythology, also called Alectrona. She may have been worshipped as a goddess of the morning or of waking from sleep on the island of Rhodes.This was also a patronymic byname of Alcmene, daughter of Electryon."
Eleder,m,,Basque,[e.ˈle.deɾ],,,,,,,"From Basque ele (""word"") and eder (""beautiful""), thus meaning ""eloquent"". This name was coined by Nikolas Ormatxea (1888-1961) in his poem 'Euskaldunak'."
