Elem,m,Элем(Russian),Soviet|Russian|Portuguese (Brazilian),/ˈɛ.lʲɪm/(Russian),,,,,,,"This name was created by Communist parents who were eager to reject traditional names. It is usually a combination of the surnames Энгельс (Engels), Ленин (Lenin) and Маркс (Marks), which refer to Friedrich Engels (1820-1895), Vladimir Lenin (1870-1924) and Karl Marx (1818-1883). As such, one could say that the name is intended to commemorate the creation of the former Soviet state, since the aforementioned men had a profound influence on its establishment, despite the fact that two of them were long dead by the time the state came to be.However, there have been cases where there is a different etymology behind the name. In these cases, the name was either derived from French elle aime meaning ""she loves"" or inspired by Elam Harnish (see Elam), the protagonist of the novel Burning Daylight (1910) written by the American author Jack London (1876-1916). The late Russian film director Elem Klimov (1933-2003) is said to be among the people whose name came from the latter."
Elema,m,,Hawaiian|Judeo-Anglo-Norman,e-LE-ma,,,,,,,Hawaiian form of Elmer.
Elemar,f,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Medieval English form of Eleanor.
Elemiah,m,עַלֵּמִיָּה(Ancient Hebrew),Judeo-Christian-Islamic Legend,,,,,,,,"Possibly means ""Yahweh is hidden"" or ""youth of Yahweh"", derived from Hebrew עֶלֶם (elem) meaning ""something kept out of sight, youth, young man"" combined with יָהּ (yah) referring to the Hebrew God. This is the name of an angel belonging to the order of the Seraphim. He is one of the 72 angels bearing the explicit name of God (the Shem HaMephorash), as interpreted by some Christian Kabbalists."
Elemír,m,,Slovak,,,,,,,,Possibly related to Elemér.
Elemmírë,,,Literature,,,,,,,,"The name Elemmírë was a Quenya word that meant ""star jewel"", which comes from the words elen, meaning ""star"" and mírë, meaning ""jewel"". The form of the word does not specify gender.Elemmírë was best known for composing the Aldudénië, which lamented the poisoning of the Two Trees and the Darkening of Valinor, all of which Elemmírë was likely to have witnessed."
