Enenra,m,"煙々羅, 煙羅煙羅(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese Mythology,EN-EN-RA,,,,,,,"From Japanese 煙 (en) meaning ""smokey"", 々, an iteration mark, indicating that the previous kanji should be repeated combined with 羅 (ra) meaning ""lightweight fabric"" or sometimes spelt as ""enraenra"" which is built from Japanese 煙 (en) meaning ""smokey"", 羅 (ra) meaning ""lightweight fabric"", 煙 (en) meaning ""smokey"" combined with 羅 (ra) meaning ""lightweight fabric"".In Japanese mythology, this is the name of a yōkai that is composed of smoke. It was first featured in the Konjaku Hyakki Shūi, ca. 1781. It resides in bonfires and when it emerges, it takes the form of a human. It is said that an enenra can only be seen by the pure of heart."
