Epenetus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Biblical|English (Puritan),,,,,,,,"Variant of Epaenetus, which is the latinized form of the Greek name Epainetos."
Eper,f,,Hungarian,,,,,,,,"Means ""strawberry"" or ""mulberry"" in Hungarian."
Eperjes,f,,Medieval Hungarian,/ˈɛ.pɛr.jɛʃ/(Hungarian),,,,,,,"Older form of Eperke. The name coincides with (current) dialectal Hungarian eperjes ""strawberry patch""."
Eperke,f,,Hungarian,/ˈɛ.pɛr.kɛ/,,,,,,,"Derived from Hungarian eper ""strawberry (on the ground); mulberry (on a tree)"" (ultimately from Proto-Ugric *äppärĕ-kə “strawberry”)."
Eperulo,,,Bandial,,,,,,,,"Means ""to scream at the top of one's voice"" in Bandial."
Epestemia,f,,"Moldovan (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Variant of Epistimia.
Epha,f,,English (Rare),EE-fuh,,,,,,,Variant of Ephah.
Ephah,,,Biblical Hebrew,,,,,,,,"In the Hebrew Bible, Ephah was the name of three people: one of Midian's five sons (a descendant of Abraham via Keturah), a son of Jahdai (a descendant of Judah), and a concubine of Caleb."
Ephai,m,,Biblical Hebrew,EE-fye|o-FAH-ee,,,,,,,"Means ""gloomy"" or ""bird-like"" in Hebrew. This is a character in the Old Testament."
Epham,f,,Scottish,,,,,,,,Form of Euphame
Ephantus,m,,Kikuyu,,,,,,,,Not available.
Ê-pháp-ra,m,,Biblical Vietnamese,,,,,,,,Vietnamese form of Epaphras.
Ephelia,f,,"American (South, Rare)|Louisiana Creole (Rare)",,,,,,,,Possibly a variant of Ophelia.
Ephemera,f,,English,,,,,,,,"Any transitory written or printed matter not meant to be retained or preserved. The word derives from the Greek, meaning things lasting no more than a day."
Epher,m,,Biblical Hebrew,,,,,,,,"Epher was a grandson of Abraham, according to Gen. 25:4, whose descendants, Jewish historian Flavius Josephus claimed, had invaded Libya. Josephus also claimed that Epher's name was the etymological root of the continent Africa. According to the Bible, he was a son of Midian."
Ephesia,f,Ἐφεσία(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Late Roman|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Ephesius. Also see the Italian form Efisia. In Greek mythology this was an epithet of the goddess Artemis meaning ""of Ephesus"", Ephesus being a city in Asia Minor famous for a temple dedicated to her, which was one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World."
Ephesians,,,English (Rare),/ə.ˈfi.ʒənz/,,,,,,,"From the book of the Bible Ephesians, named after the ancient Greek city of Ephesus."
Ephialtes,m,Ἐφιάλτης(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/e.phi.ál.tɛːs/(Attic Greek)|/e.ɸi.ˈal.tis/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"From ἐφιάλτης (ephialtes) meaning ""nightmare"" in Ancient Greek, possibly of pre-Greek origin. Folk etymology often derives it from ἐφάλλομαι (ephallomai) ""to jump at, to throw oneself onto somebody"" (itself from ἐπι- (epi-) ""on, upon"" and ἅλλομαι (hallomai) ""to leap, to jump""), but possibly related to ἠπίαλος (epialos) meaning ""ague, fever"". In Greek mythology this is the name of a giant who is one of the Aloidae, a son of Aloeus and brother of Otus. Ephialtes also appears in Dante's Inferno (14th century), where he is chained in a great pit that separates the eighth and ninth circles of Hell."
Ephigenia,f,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Variant of Iphigenia. Saint Ephigenia of Ethiopia (also known as Iphigenia of Ethiopia; 1st century), is a folk saint whose life is told in the Golden Legend as a virgin converted to Christianity and then consecrated to God by St. Matthew the Apostle, who was spreading the Gospel to the region of ""Ethiopia,"" which in this case is understood to be located in the regions south of the Caspian Sea."
Ephippos,m,Ἔφιππος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἔφιππος (ephippos) meaning ""on horseback, riding, mounted"", which consists of Greek ἐπί (epi) meaning ""upon, on"" and the Greek noun ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse""."
Ephippus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Ephippos. Known bearers of this name include the Greek comic poet Ephippus of Athens (4th century BC) and the Greek historian Ephippus of Olynthus (4th century BC).
Éphise,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,French form of Ephysius.
Ephlal,m,אֶפְלָל(Ancient Hebrew),Biblical,EF-lahl(Biblical English),,,,,,,"From the Hebrew word פְלָל(palal) meaning ""pray"". In the Bible, Ephlal was the son of Zabad and the father of Obed."
Ephod,m,,Biblical,,,,,,,,"Ephod was the father of Hanniel, a prince of the Tribe of Manasseh. (Num. 34:23)."
Ephorus,m,Ἔφορος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Latinized),/ˈɛf.ər.əs/(English),,,,,,,"Means ""overseer, guardian, ruler"" in Ancient Greek, ultimately derived from ἐπί (epi) meaning ""over, on, upon"" and οὖρος (ouros) meaning ""guardian, watcher"". This was the name of two ancient Greek philosophers from Cyme."
Ephraem,m,,Biblical (Latinized)|Jewish (Latinized)|History,,,,,,,,Latinized form of the Hebrew name 'Efrayim via its hellenized form Ephraim.
Ephrahim,m,,English,,,,,,,,Variant of Ephraim.
Éphraïm,m,,French,,,,,,,,French form of Ephraim.
Ephraïm,m,,Dutch|Flemish|French (Belgian)|Judeo-French,,,,,,,,Dutch form of Ephraim and French variant of Éphraïm.
Ephraimia,f,,Hebrew,Ef-ruh-MEE-uh,,,,,,,Feminine form of Ephraim.
Ephräm,m,ܐܦܪܝܡ(Syriac),German (Rare),ef-rem,,,,,,,Ephräm der Syrer is the German form of the name of St. Ephrem the Syrian.
Ephram,m,,English,EE-frəm|E-frəm,,,,,,,Variant of Ephraim.
Ephramina,f,,"English (American, Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Feminine form of Ephram.
Ephratah,f,,Biblical (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Ephrath.
Ephrayim,m,,Hebrew (?),,,,,,,,Transliterated form of Ephraim.
Éphrem,m,,French,,,,,,,,French form of Ephrem.
Ephriam,m,,"English (American, Rare)",EF-rye-UM(American English)|EEF-ree-um(American English),,,,,,,Varaint of Ephraim.
Ephron,m,,Ancient Hebrew,,,,,,,,"Means ""small deer"" in Hebrew."
Ephy,f,,English,/ˈɛf.i/,,,,,,,Variant of Effie.
Ephyra,f,Ἐφύρα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Probably from the former name of the ancient city Corinth, which may have derived from Ancient Greek ἐπωπή (epope) meaning ""lookout place, observation post"" (from epi ""on, upon"" and ops ""eye""). It has also been connected to πῦρ (pyr) ""fire"", though this is likely folk etymology. This was the name of both an Oceanid and a Nereid in Greek mythology, possibly being the same individual."
Epianassa,f,Ἐπιάνασσα(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἐπί (epi) meaning ""on, upon"" and ᾰ̓́νασσᾰ (anassa) meaning ""queen"" (feminine form of ἄναξ (anax) ""lord, master"")."
Epic,,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",EHP-ik(American English),,,,,,,"From epic (adj.) 1580s, ""pertaining to or constituting a lengthy heroic poem,"" via French épique or directly from Latin epicus, from Greek epikos, from epos ""a word; a tale, story; promise, prophecy, proverb; poetry in heroic verse"" (from PIE root *wekw- ""to speak""). Extended sense of ""grand, heroic"" first recorded in English 1731."
Epicaste,f,Ἐπικάστη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Greek ἐπί (epi) meaning ""on, upon, over"" and κέκασμαι (kekasmai) meaning ""to surpass, to excel, to shine"" (pluperfect κέκαστο (kekasto)). This was the name of multiple women in Greek mythology, including a daughter of Nestor who was supposedly the mother of Homer."
Epicelena,f,,Medieval English (Rare)|Obscure,,,,,,,,"Possibly related to the Greek verb ἐπικελεύω (epikeleuo) meaning ""to exhort, to encourage, to cheer on"" and the noun ἐπικέλευσις (epikeleusis) ""a cheering on, exhortation""."
Epichares,m,Ἐπιχάρης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἐπιχαρής (epichares) meaning ""gratifying, agreeable"". It consists of Greek ἐπί (epi) meaning ""upon, on"" combined with either the Greek noun χαρά (chara) meaning ""joy, delight, happiness"" or the Greek noun χάρις (charis) meaning ""grace, kindness"" (see Chares).Known bearers of this name include a Greek tyrant of Sicyon (4th century BC) and an Olympic victor (4th century BC)."
Epicharis,f,Ἐπίχαρις(Ancient Greek),"Ancient Greek|French (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἐπίχαρις (epicharis) meaning ""pleasing, charming"". This was the name of a 1st-century Roman freedwoman who was a member of the Pisonian conspiracy against the emperor Nero. It was also borne by two early Christian martyrs.After the Revolution, it was occasionally used as a feminine given name in France."
Epicharmos,m,Ἐπίχαρμος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/e.pí.kʰar.mos/(Attic Greek)|/e.ˈpi.xar.mos/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)",,,,,,,"From Greek ἐπιχαίρω (epichairo) meaning ""to rejoice over, exult, gloat"", ultimately derived from epi (epi) meaning ""on, upon"" and χαίρω (chairo) meaning ""to be full of cheer, to rejoice""."
Epicharmus,m,Ἐπίχαρμος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Epicharmos. Name of Greek dramatist and philosopher, Epicharmus of Kos."
Epicles,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Epikles. This name was borne by an eponymous archon of Athens from the 2nd century BC.
Epictet,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Romanian form of Epiktetos via its latinized form Epictetus. This is the name of one of the earliest saints of Romania.
Épictète,m,,History (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Epiktetos via its latinized form Epictetus.
Epicteto,m,,Spanish (Rare),,,,,,,,Spanish form of Epiktetos via its latinized form Epictetus.
Epictetus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),/e.pik.ˈteː.tus/(Latin)|/ˌɛ.pɪk.ˈtiː.təs/(English)|/ˌɛ.pɪk.ˈtaɪ.təs/(English),,,,,,,Latinized form of Epiktetos.
Epicure,m,,Theatre,,,,,,,,"Form of Epicurus used by Ben Jonson for a character in his play 'The Alchemist' (first performed 1610), perhaps taken directly from the English word epicure meaning ""one who gives himself up to sensual pleasure"" (literally ""follower of Epicurus"")."
Epicydes,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Epikydes. This was the name of a Carthaginian general from the 3rd century BC, who was of Greek descent through his father."
Epie,m,,African,A pier,,,,,,,Bakossi Cameroonian Coastal Bantu
Epifan,m,,Polish (Rare)|Bulgarian (Rare)|Russian (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Epiphanios.
Epifani,m,,Catalan|Polish,/ə.pi.ˈfa.ni/(Catalan)|/ɛ.pʲi.ˈfa.ɲi/(Polish),,,,,,,Catalan and Polish form of Epiphanius.
Epifánia,f,,Hungarian,eh-pee-FAA-nee-ah,,,,,,,"Cognate of Epiphany, meaning ""manifestation""."
Epifânio,m,,Portuguese (Rare),,,,,,,,Portuguese form of Epiphanius.
Epifaniusz,m,,Polish,,,,,,,,Polish form of Epiphanios.
Epifaniy,m,"Епифаний(Bulgarian, Russian)|Епіфаній(Ukrainian)",Bulgarian (Archaic)|Russian (Archaic)|Ukrainian (Archaic),,,,,,,,Bulgarian and Ukrainian form of Epiphanios (see Epifanio) as well as an alternate transcription of Russian Епифаний (see Yepifaniy).
Épigmène,m,,French (Archaic),,,,,,,,French form of Epigmenius.
Epigmenia,f,,Spanish (Mexican),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Epigmenius.
Epigmenio,m,,Italian (Archaic)|Spanish (Mexican),,,,,,,,"Italian and Spanish form of Epigmenius. Most known bearers of this name are Mexican, and they include the insurgent Epigmenio González Flores (1781-1858), the priest and politician Epigmenio de la Piedra (1792-1873), the judoka Epigmenio Exiga (b. 1950) and the film producer Epigmenio Ibarra."
Epigmenius,m,,"Late Greek (Latinized, Hypothetical)|History (Ecclesiastical)",,,,,,,,"Latinized form of the unattested Greek name Epeigmenios, derived from the Ancient Greek word ἠπειγμένως (epeigmenos) meaning ""hurriedly, with urgency"" or επειγόμενος (epeigomenos) meaning ""hurried, rushed"", participles of ἐπείγω (epeigo) meaning ""to press hard, impel, urge on, hasten"".This name is best known for being the name of the 4th-century saint Epigmenius of Rome, who was martyred during the reign of the Roman emperor Diocletian (died in 311 AD). Some sources suggest that he may possibly be the same person as saint Pigmenius of Rome (second half of the 4th century AD)."
Epik,m,,African American,,,,,,,,Variant of Epic.
Epikasta,f,,Serbian,,,,,,,,"Serbian version of Epicaste (Ἐπικάστη), a name attributed to five women in Greek mythology, for example Epicaste, mother of Homer by Telemachus (son of Odysseus)."
Epikles,m,Ἐπικλῆς(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/e.pi.klɛ̂ːs/(Attic Greek)|/e.pi.ˈklis/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἐπί (epi) meaning ""upon, on"" combined with κλέος (kleos) meaning ""glory""."
Epiktet,m,Эпиктет(Russian),Russian (Archaic),,,,,,,,Russian form of Epiktetos.
Epiktitos,m,Επίκτητος(Greek),"Greek (Cypriot, Rare)",,,,,,,,Modern Greek form of Epiktetos.
Epikydes,m,Ἐπικύδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἐπικυδής (epikydes) meaning ""glorious, distinguished"", which consists of Greek ἐπί (epi) meaning ""upon, on"" and κῦδος (kydos) meaning ""glory, renown"" (see Thucydides)."
Epilais,f,Ἐπιλάις(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Uncertain etymology. Possibly derived from Ancient Greek ἐπιλαΐς (epilais), referring to a kind of small bird, or from ἐπιληΐς (epileis) meaning ""booty, plunder; gained in war"", or perhaps a feminine form of Epilaus. In Greek mythology, this was the name of one of the fifty daughters of King Thespius and Megamede."
Epilico,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Epilycus.
Epilycus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Epilykos. This name was borne by an Athenian comic poet.
Epilykos,m,Ἐπίλυκος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἐπί (epi) meaning ""upon, on"" combined with either λύκος (lykos) meaning ""wolf"" or λύκη (lyke) meaning ""light, morning sunshine""."
Epim,m,Епим(Mari),Mari,,,,,,,,Mari form of Yefim.
Epimach,m,,Polish (Rare),eh-PEE-makh,,,,,,,Polish form of Epimachus.
Epimachos,m,Ἐπίμαχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Late Greek,/e.ˈpi.ma.xos/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἐπί (epi) meaning ""upon, on"" combined with μάχη (mache) meaning ""battle"". Compare the Ancient Greek word ἐπίμαχος (epimachos) meaning ""equipped for battle"" as well as ""ally, helper"" and ""contended for, contested; easily attacked""."
Epimachus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Epimachos. A known bearer of this name was Epimachus of Athens, a renowned Athenian engineer and architect from the 3rd century BC."
Epímaco,m,,Spanish,,,,,,,,Spanish form of Epimachus.
Epimaco,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Epimachus.
Épimaque,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,French form of Epimachus.
Epimeleia,f,Ἐπιμέλεια(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"From Ancient Greek ἐπιμέλεια (epimeleia) meaning ""care, attention""."
Epimeles,m,Ἐπιμέλης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Probably derived from Ancient Greek ἐπιμελής (epimeles) meaning ""careful, anxious, attentive"" or ""cared for, cared about""."
Epimenes,m,Ἐπιμένης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἐπιμένω (epimeno) meaning ""to stay on, tarry, persist, continue"", composed of ἐπί (epi) ""upon, on"" and μένω (meno) ""to stay, remain; to last, withstand""."
Epimenides,m,Ἐπιμενίδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""son of Epimenes"" in Greek, derived from the name Epimenes combined with the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides).A notable bearer of this name was Epimenides of Cnossos, a semi-mythical Greek seer and philosopher-poet from the 6th or 7th century BC."
Epimetej,m,Епиметеј(Serbian),Bosnian|Croatian|Serbian,,,,,,,,"Bosnian, Croatian and Serbian form of Epimetheus."
Epimeteo,m,,Italian|Spanish,,,,,,,,Italian and Spanish form of Epimetheus.
Epimeteu,m,,Catalan|Portuguese|Romanian,,,,,,,,"Catalan, Portuguese and Romanian form of Epimetheus."
Epimeteusz,m,,Polish,,,,,,,,Polish form of Epimetheus.
Épiméthée,m,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Epimetheus.
Épiméthée,m,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Epimetheus.
Epinike,f,Ἐπινίκη(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Epinikos.
Epinikos,m,Ἐπίνικος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἐπίνικος (epinikos) meaning ""of victory"", which consists of Greek ἐπί (epi) meaning ""upon, on"" and the Greek noun νίκη (nike) meaning ""victory""."
Epione,f,Ἠπιόνη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἤπιος (epios) meaning ""soothing"". In Greek mythology this was the name of a goddess of healing. She was the wife of the medicine god Asklepios."
Epipane,m,ეპიფანე(Georgian),Georgian (Archaic),,,,,,,,Georgian form of Epiphanes and/or Epiphanius.
Epiphaine,f,,French,,,,,,,,Rare old feminine French variant of Epiphania.
Épiphane,m,,French (Rare),,,,,,,,French form of Epiphanes and Epiphanius.
Epiphane,,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Variant of Epiphany.
Epiphaneios,m,Ἐπιφάνειος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek επιφανεια (epiphaneia) meaning ""appearance, manifestation"", which itself is ultimately derived from Greek ἐπί (epi) ""upon, on"" combined with Greek φαίνω (phaino) ""to reveal, to shine""."
Epiphania,f,Ἐπιφάνεια(Ancient Greek),History (Ecclesiastical)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of the Greek name Ἐπιφάνεια (Epiphaneia), a feminine form of Epiphanes. This name was borne by an 8th-century Italian saint from Pavia. Also compare Epifania."
Épiphanie,f,,French (Rare)|French (African),,,,,,,,"French form of Epiphania. It coincides with French épiphanie meaning ""realisation, epiphany"" and the French form of the Western Christian holiday known as Epiphany in Anglophone countries."
Epiphanny,f,,"African American (Rare)|English (American, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant misspelling of Epiphany.
Epiphron,m,Ἐπίφρων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἐπίφρων (epiphron) meaning ""thoughtful"". It consists of Greek ἐπί (epi) meaning ""upon, on"" combined with either the Greek noun φρόνις (phronis) meaning ""prudence, wisdom"" or the Greek verb φρονέω (phroneo) meaning ""to think"" as well as ""to be minded"". Both words are ultimately derived from the Greek noun φρήν (phren) meaning ""midriff"" as well as ""mind, intellect, wits"".In Greek mythology, this is the name of a daemon (spirit) of prudence, shrewdness, thoughtfulness, carefulness and sagacity."
Epipole,f,Ἐπιπολή(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""surface, on top, above"" in Ancient Greek. In Greek mythology, Epipole was a Euboean woman who disguised herself as a man in order to join the Greek army against Troy. When her ruse was discovered by Palamedes, she was stoned to death by her fellow soldiers."
Epistemia,f,"Епистемия(Moldovan Cyrillic, Russian)","Moldovan (Rare, Archaic)|Russian (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Variant of Epistimia.
Epistemon,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Ancient Greek word ἐπιστήμη (episteme) meaning ""knowledge"".This was the name of one of the central characters in Daemonologie, a medieval compendium on witchcraft written by King James VI of Scotland and later England."
Epistheneia,f,Ἐπισθένεια(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Probably derived from Ancient Greek ἐπισθένω (epistheno) meaning ""to have strength enough"", composed of ἐπί (epi) ""on, upon, after"" and σθένος (sthenos) ""vigour, strength""."
Episthenes,m,Ἐπισθένης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Probably derived from Ancient Greek ἐπισθένω (epistheno) meaning ""to have strength enough"", composed of ἐπί (epi) ""on, upon, after"" and σθένος (sthenos) ""vigour, strength""."
Epistima,f,Епистима(Russian),Medieval Russian|Russian (Archaic)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Russian form of Episteme.
Epistimia,f,"Епистимия(Moldovan Cyrillic, Russian)",Moldovan (Rare)|Russian (Archaic)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Romanian and Russian form of Episteme.
Epistina,f,Епистина(Russian),Russian,,,,,,,,Variant of Epistinia.
Epistinia,f,Епистини́я(Russian),Russian (Rare),,,,,,,,Russian form of Epistheneia.Epistinia Stepanova (1882–1969) was the mother of eight sons who died in the war and a ninth son who died from his wounds received in the war.
Epístrofo,m,,Spanish,,,,,,,,Spanish form of Epistrophus.
Epistrofo,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Epistrophus.
Epistrophos,m,Ἐπίστροφος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ɪ.ˈpɪs.trə.fəs/(English),,,,,,,"Means ""ranging about, twisting, winding"" or ""concerned with, maintaining relations with"", derived from Ancient Greek ἐπί (epi) meaning ""upon, on"" combined with στρέφω (strepho) meaning ""to twist, turn (around), direct""."
Epistrophus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Epistrophos. This is the name of several characters in Greek mythology.
Epitácio,m,,"Portuguese (Brazilian, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Probably comes from Greek επιτακτες (epitaktes), which means ""leader, commander""."
Epitacio,m,,Spanish,[e.pi.ˈta.sjo](Latin American Spanish)|[ɛ̝.pɪ.ˈta.ʃo](Filipino Spanish),,,,,,,Not available.
Epiteto,m,,Portuguese,,,,,,,,Portuguese form of Epiktetos via its latinized form Epictetus.
Epitteto,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Epiktetos via its latinized form Epictetus.
Epityches,m,Ἐπιτύχης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""successful, hitting the mark"" in Ancient Greek."
Epitychia,f,Ἐπιτυχία(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Epityches.
Epixena,f,Ἐπιξένα(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Epixenos.
Epixenos,m,Ἐπίξενος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἐπί (epi) meaning ""upon, on"" combined with Greek ξένος (xenos) meaning ""foreign, strange"" as well as ""foreigner, guest""."
Epkuv,m,Епкув(Khanty),Khanty|Mansi,,,,,,,,Khanty and Mansi form of Efim.
Epluribus,m,,"English (American, Rare)",/i.ˈplʊ.rɪ.bəs/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Latin phrase e pluribus unum ""out of many, one"", used as the de facto motto of the United States until 1956. This name is typically paired with Unum."
Eponin,f,,Hungarian (Rare),,,,,,,,Hungarian form of Eponine.
Eponina,f,,Portuguese (Brazilian),,,,,,,,Not available.
Eponu,m,,Chuukese,,,,,,,,"Means ""navigator"" in Chuukese."
Eponychos,m,"Ἐπώνυχος, Ἀπύγχις(Ancient Greek)|ⲉⲡⲟⲛⲩⲭⲟⲥ(Coptic)",Coptic (Hellenized),,,,,,,,"Hellenized form of Ebonh, ultimately from Egyptian Iufankh."
Eporedorix,m,,Old Celtic,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Celtic element epo(s) ""horse"", combined with redo ""to travel, to run"" and rix ""king."" The name would thus mean ""king of the running horses"" - perhaps ""king of the cavalry"" is a little bit more appropriate. That is the usual explanation for the name. However, the name might also have been derived from Celtic eporeto ""chariot"" combined with rix ""king"", which would give the meaning ""king of the chariot(s)."" Either way, it is clear that the name is connected with horses and their speed."
Epp,f,,Estonian,,,,,,,,"Of uncertain origin and meaning. Theories include a derivation from Estonian hepp ""lively"" and a variant of Ebe."
Eppa,m,,English,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Epaphroditus.
Eppelina,f,,English (Archaic),,,,,,,,Not available.
Eppie,f,,Scots,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Elspeth.
Eppo,m,,"Dutch|German (Rare, Archaic)",EP-o,,,,,,,"Simplified short form of names containing the German name element ebur ""boar""."
Eppow,f,,Cornish,,,,,,,,Cornish form of Elizabeth
Eppu,,,Finnish,EHP-puw,,,,,,,"A Finnish diminutive of such names as Emil and Erik. Famous bearers are the Finnish band Eppu Normaali, author and actress Eppu Nuotio (born Eija-Riitta Nuotio) and actor Eppu Salminen (born Jarmo Juhani Salminen). Eppu is also the Finnish name of the Peanuts character Linus van Pelt."
Eppy,f,,"English (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Alternate form of Eppie.
Ép-ra-im,m,,Biblical Vietnamese,/ʔɛp̚˧˦.zaː˧˧.ʔim˧˧/(Vietnamese)|/ʔɛp̚˦˧˥.ʐaː˧˧.ʔim˧˧/(Vietnamese)|/ʔɛp̚˦˥.ɹaː˧˧.ʔim˧˧/(Vietnamese),,,,,,,Vietnamese form of Ephraim.
Epraksia,f,ეპრაქსია(Georgian),Armenian|Georgian (Rare),,,,,,,,Armenian and Georgian form of Eupraxia (see Yevpraksiya).
Eprem,m,ეფრემ(Georgian),Georgian (Rare),,,,,,,,"Georgian form of Ephrem. This name was borne by two Catholicos-Patriarchs of Georgia: the first lived in the late 15th century, the other in the 20th century."
Epro,f,,Finnish,,,,,,,,Finnish short form of Efrosiina.
Eprosine,f,ეფროსინე(Georgian),Georgian,,,,,,,,Georgian form of Euphrosyne.
