Erichthonius,m,Ἐριχθόνιος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),/ɛ.rɪk.ˈθoʊ.ni.əs/(English)|/e.rikʰ.tʰó.ni.os/(Attic Greek)|/e.rix.ˈθo.ni.os/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Latinized form of Ἐριχθόνιος (Erichthonios), which is of uncertain etymology, possibly pre-Greek. Folk etymology connects it to various Ancient Greek words, including ἐρέχθω (erechtho) ""to rend, break"" and χθών (kthon) ""earth"" combined with either ἔριον (erion) ""wool"" or ἔρις (eris) ""strife, discord"". This was the name of two mythical kings in Greek mythology, one the ruler of ancient Athens, an autochthonous son of Hephaestus and Gaia raised by the goddess Athena. Early Greek texts do not distinguish between him and Erechtheus, his grandson, but by the 4th century BC they were distinct figures. The other was a king of Troy and son of Dardanus."
