Erka,f,,"Germanic Mythology|German (Modern, Rare)",ER-ka,,,,,,,"A short form derived from Old High German erkan ""pure, genuine"".Erka is a wife of King Etzel (Attila) in the Niebelungenlied and the legendary founder of the German city Erkelenz."
Erkaitz,m,,Basque (Rare),/eɾ.ˈkajt͡s̻/,,,,,,,"Possibly from the name of a mountain in Gipuzkoa (thus a variant of Arkaitz) or from Basque irkaitz meaning ""smile""."
Erkal,m,,Turkish,/er-kal/,,,,,,,"Erkal combines Er (""man,"" ""early,"" ""valiant"") and Kal (""to stay,"" ""to remain""). It signifies ""the one who remains brave"" or ""the one who stands firm through time."" It symbolizes ""the anchor in the storm and the enduring legacy of the masculine spirit."" The name denotes longevity of character and constancy of virtue."
Erkam,m,,Turkish,/er-kam/,,,,,,,"Erkam is a synthesis of Er (""man,"" ""valiant"") and Kam (""shaman,"" ""healer,"" ""seer""). It represents ""the valiant seer"" or ""the one who bridges the human and the cosmic."" It symbolizes ""the union of grounded physical reality and spiritual vision.""While some attempt to map this to Arabic origins, the indigenous Turkic etymology remains paramount in pre-Islamic traditions where the Kam was the central figure of social, medicinal, and spiritual life. The Arabic loan-word ""Erqam"" theory fails to explain the pervasive use of the Er-Kam construction within Central Asian linguistic structures that predate the adaptation of Arabic lexicography."
Erkanberht,m,,Germanic,,,,,,,,"Derived from Old High German erkan ""pure, holy, genuine"" and beraht ""bright."""
