Erlik,m,𐰼𐰠𐰃𐰝(Orkhon Inscriptions),Turkic Mythology,/ɛɾ.lik/,,,,,,,"""Erlik"" is derived from er (""man/male"") and the suffix -lik (""power/rank""), translating as ""the one who possesses power"" or ""authority."" He represents the necessary darkness and the harsh reality of cosmic order. According to the Radloff-recorded versions, he is often cast as the ""elder brother"" or the ""first creation"" who challenged the supreme order, leading to his role as the master of the underworld. Ögel emphasizes that in many variations, he is not a ""Satanic"" figure, but the dark regulator of the universe, representing the inevitable end and the test of the human spirit. The confusion regarding his relationship to Kayra Han or Ülgen is clarified by seeing him as the shadow-side of creation; the byproduct of existence, not an external evil, but a foundational, though fallen, principle of authority."
