Esrigün,f,,Turkish,ehs-ree-GEWN,,,,,,,"An archaic and highly literary name found in Chagatai and Old Anatolian Turkish literature. Derived from the verb esrimek, which means ""to enter a state of trance, to become ecstatic, inspired, or spiritually intoxicated'"" a term deeply tied to the mystical practices of kam/kaman (shamans) achieving a trance-like state (esrimek/ecstasy) to bridge the physical and spirit worlds.Combined with the suffix -gün, it describes a ""wildly blowing wind, an exciting storm, or a dizzying, enthusiastic day,"" beautifully symbolizing divine passion, overwhelming natural energy, and artistic inspiration."
