Eudaimon,m,Εὐδαίμων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Late Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""fortunate, happy, prosperous, wealthy"" in Ancient Greek, literally ""possessed by/blessed with good spirit"", derived from εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" and δαίμων (daimon) meaning ""god, goddess, spirit; divine power, destiny, fate"". This was also the name of a type of beneficial spirit in Greek mythology."
Eudaimonia,f,Εὐδαιμονία(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/juː.dɪ.ˈmoʊn.i.ə/(English)|/eu̯.dai̯.mo.ní.aː/(Attic Greek)|/eβ.ðɛ.mo.ˈni.a/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Means ""happiness, prosperity, good fortune, well-being"" in Ancient Greek, a feminine form of Eudaimon. In Greek mythology this was the name of one of the younger Graces (Charites), the personification of happiness, prosperity and opulence, depicted only on a vase."
Eudaitidas,m,Ευδαιτίδας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Probably derived from Ancient Greek (eu) meaning ""good"" and either δαίτη (daite) meaning ""feast, banquet"" or δαίω (daio) meaning ""to divide, share; to host (a feast)"", combined with the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides)."
