Euippos,m,Εὔιππος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek εὔιππος (euippos) meaning ""delighting in horses"" and ""well-horsed"", derived from the Greek elements εὖ (eu) ""good"" and ἵππος (hippos) ""horse"". This was the name of multiple characters in Greek mythology, including a son of King Thestius who, together with his brothers, was killed by Meleager; it also occurs in Homer's Iliad belonging to a Lycian who was slain by Patroclus in the Trojan War."
Euis,f,,Sundanese,/ˈɤ.is/,,,,,,,"From Sundanese geulis meaning ""beautiful, pretty""."
Euiseongbuon,f,,Korean,,,,,,,,Not available.
Euiseongbuwon,m,,Korean,,,,,,,,Not available.
Euitet,m,,Old Dutch,,,,,,,,Not available.
Eujean,m,,English (Rare),/ˈju.d͡ʒin/|/ju.ˈd͡ʒin/,,,,,,,Variant of Eugene.
Eujena,f,,Popular Culture,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Eugene.
Eûjène,m,,Walloon,,,,,,,,Walloon form of Eugène.
Eûjènîye,f,,Walloon,,,,,,,,Walloon form of Eugénie.
Eüjènn,m,,Gallo,,,,,,,,Gallo form of Eugène.
Eukarpia,f,Εὐκαρπία(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun εὐκαρπία (eukarpia) meaning ""fruitfulness"", which consists of Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" and the Greek noun καρπός (karpos) meaning ""fruit"".This was the name of an ancient Greek divine personification of fertility. Eukarpia was also used as an epithet for several ancient Greek gods."
Eukarpides,m,Εὐκαρπίδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""son of Eukarpos"" in Greek, derived from the name Eukarpos combined with the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides)."
Eukarpos,m,Εὔκαρπος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὔκαρπος (eukarpos) meaning ""fruitful"", which consists of Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with the Greek noun καρπός (karpos) meaning ""fruit""."
Euke,m,,Finnish (Rare),,,,,,,,Finnish short form of Eugenius.
Eukelade,f,Ευκελάδη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Astronomy,/juː.ˈkɛl.ə.diː/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὐκέλαδος (eukelados) meaning ""well-sounding, melodious"", composed of εὖ (eu) ""good, well"" and κέλαδος (kelados) meaning ""loud noise, shout, clamour"" as well as ""musical sound, clear voice"". John Tzetzes, a 12th century Byzantine poet and grammarian, listed Eukelade as one of nine original Muses, though she is not mentioned in any other historical source. A moon of Jupiter is named after her."
Euken,m,,Basque,,,,,,,,Basque form of Eugene.
Eukleia,f,Εὔκλεια(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὔκλεια (eukleia) meaning ""good repute, glory"", which consists of εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" combined with κλέος (kleos) meaning ""glory"". Also compare the masculine name Eukles, which is closely related.In Greek mythology, Eukleia was the personification of good repute and glory, frequently depicted as an attendant of Aphrodite, in whose entourage she represented the good repute of a chaste bride. Eukleia was also an epithet of the goddess Artemis, under which she was worshipped in Boeotia."
Eukles,m,"Εὐκλῆς, Εὐκλής(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,/eu̯.klɛ̂ːs/(Attic Greek)|/eɸ.ˈklis/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Means ""of good report, famous"", derived from Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" and κλέος (kleos) meaning ""glory"". Also compare Eukleides (see Euclid)."
Euklid,m,,Croatian|German,E-oo-klid(Croatian)|oy-KLEET(German),,,,,,,German and Croatian form of Euclid.
Eukrates,m,Εὐκράτης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὖ (eu) ""good, well"" combined with κράτος (kratos) ""power""."
Eukratides,m,Εὐκρατίδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""son of Eukrates"" in Greek, derived from the name Eukrates combined with the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides)."
Eulaalia,f,,Finnish,,,,,,,,Variant of Eulalia.
Eulabee,f,,Obscure,,,,,,,,"Possibly a combination of Eula and Bee, or perhaps a derivative of the Greek noun εὐλάβεια (eulabeia) meaning ""discretion, caution"" (see Eulabeia). A notable bearer of this name was Eulabee Dix Becker (1878-1961), an American portrait painter."
Eulabeia,f,Εὐλάβεια(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun εὐλάβεια (eulabeia) meaning ""discretion, caution"". In Greek mythology, Eulabeia was the spirit and personification of discretion, caution and circumspection."
Eulabios,m,"Εὐλάβιος, Ευλάβιος(Ancient Greek)",Late Greek|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun εὐλάβεια (eulabeia) meaning ""discretion, caution"" (see Eulabeia). Also compare the Greek adjective εὐλαβής (eulabes) meaning ""taking hold well, holding fast, clinging"" as well as ""discreet, cautious, undertaking prudently""."
Eulade,m,,Medieval French|French (African)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,French form of Euladius. Eulade of Nevers was a Pre-congregational saint and first bishop of the Diocese of Nevers in France.
Euladie,f,,"French (Huguenot)|French (Quebec)|French (Acadian, Archaic)|Louisiana Creole (Archaic)",,,,,,,,Feminine form of Eulade.
Euladius,m,,History (Ecclesiastical)|French (Archaic),,,,,,,,"Probably an incorrect form of Elladius, an archaic French form of Helladius. The first element could be influenced by Ancient Greek εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well""."
Eulah,f,,English,,,,,,,,Variant of Eula.
Eulair,m,,French (Rare),,,,,,,,Masculine form of Eulaire.
Eulaire,f,,History (Ecclesiastical)|French (Archaic),,,,,,,,French form of Eularia and variant of Aulaire.
Eulala,f,,English,yoo-LAY-lə,,,,,,,Contracted form of Eulalia.
Eulale,f,,Basque (Rare),,,,,,,,Coined by Sabino Arana Goiri and Koldo Elizalde as a Basque equivalent of Eulalia and Eulalie.
