Eulimene,f,Εὐλιμένη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὐλίμενος (eulimenos) ""with good harbours"", composed of εὖ (eu) meaning ""good, well"" and λιμήν (limen) meaning ""harbour, port; haven, refuge, shelter"". This was the name of a nereid in Greek mythology, as well as of a daughter of Cydon who was offered as a virgin sacrifice but found to be pregnant after her death."
Eulina,f,,Portuguese (Brazilian),,,,,,,,Possibly derived from Aeolia or else a diminutive of Eulalia.
Eulis,f,,Sundanese,/ˈɤ.lis/,,,,,,,Variant of Euis.
