Eutaxia,f,Εὐταξία(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""good arrangement, good order, discipline"" in Ancient Greek, derived from εὔτακτος (eutaktos) ""orderly, well-behaved"", composed of εὖ (eu) ""good"" and τάσσω (tasso) ""to arrange, put in order, appoint, command""."
Eutelo,m,,Galician (Rare),,,,,,,,Not available.
Eutene,f,,English,,,,,,,,Not available.
Eutharic,m,,Germanic|History,,,,,,,,"The meaning and origin of the first element is rather uncertain: we know that it comes from eutha, but we don't exactly know where eutha itself comes from. But there are a few possibilities. It might come from Old Norse jôdh ""child"" or from ehva ""horse."" The element might also be related to the first element of Jorunn. The second element in the name comes from rîcja ""powerful, strong, mighty."" The second element is also closely related to Celtic rîg or rix and Gothic reiks, which all mean ""king, ruler."" Eutharic was the name of the husband of Amalaswintha, daughter of Theodoric the Great (king of the Ostrogoths)."
Eutharich,m,,German,,,,,,,,German form of Eutharic.
Eutharicus,m,,Germanic (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Eutharic.
Eutheda,f,,"English (American, Rare, Archaic)",U-thee-duh(American English),,,,,,,Not available.
Euthemios,m,,English,yoo-THEE-mee-uhs,,,,,,,Alternate spelling of Euthymios
Euthenia,f,Εὐθηνία(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek εὐθηνία (euthenia) meaning ""prosperity, plenty, abundance"". In Greek mythology Euthenia was the personification of abundance and plenty."
Euthum,m,,Ancient Oscan,,,,,,,,Perhaps derived from a Greek name.
Euthycles,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Euthykles. This name was borne by an eponymous archon of Athens, who lived in the 4th century BC."
Euthycritus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Euthykritos. This name was borne by an eponymous archon of Athens, who lived in the 2nd century BC."
Euthydème,m,,History (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Euthydemus.
Euthydemos,m,Εὐθύδημος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/eu̯.tʰý.dɛː.mos/(Attic Greek)|/eɸ.ˈθy.ði.mos/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὐθύς (euthys) meaning ""straight, direct"" and δῆμος (demos) meaning ""the people""."
Euthydemus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Euthydemos. This name was borne by two Greco-Bactrian kings: one lived in the 3rd century BC and the other lived in the 2nd century BC.
Euthykles,m,Εὐθυκλῆς(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/eu̯.tʰy.klɛ̂ːs/(Attic Greek)|/eɸ.θy.ˈklis/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὐθύς (euthys) meaning ""straight, direct"" and κλέος (kleos) meaning ""glory""."
Euthykritos,m,Εὐθύκριτος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὐθύς (euthys) meaning ""straight, direct"" and κριτός (kritos) meaning ""chosen, picked out, separated"" or ""choice, excellent"" (related to (krites) ""judge, critic"")."
Euthyme,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,French form of Euthymios via Euthymius.
Euthymenes,m,Εὐθυμένης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὐθύς (euthys) meaning ""straight, direct"" and μένος (menos) meaning ""power, strength, spirit"". A known bearer of this name was Euthymenes of Massalia, an ancient Greek explorer from the 6th century BC."
Euthymides,m,Εὐθυμίδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""son of Euthym(i)os"" in Greek, derived from the name Euthymos or Euthymios combined with the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides).This was the name of an Athenian potter and vase painter from the 6th century BC."
Euthymos,m,Εὔθυμος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective εὔθυμος (euthymos) meaning ""kind, generous"" as well as ""cheerful"" (see Euthymius).This name was borne by an Olympic victor from the Greek region of Locris (484 BC)."
Euthymus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Euthymos.
Euthynikos,m,Εὐθύνικος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek εὐθύς (euthys) meaning ""straight, direct"" and νίκη (nike) meaning ""victory""."
Euthyphron,m,Εὐθύφρων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/eu̯.tʰý.pʰrɔːn/(Attic Greek)|/eɸ.ˈθy.ɸron/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)|/ef.ˈθi.fron/(Constantinopolitan Greek)",,,,,,,"Means ""right-minded, whole-hearted, sincere"" in Greek, derived from εὐθύς (euthys) meaning ""straight, direct"" and φρήν (phren) meaning ""mind, heart, emotions""."
