Florens,m,,Late Roman|Dutch (Rare)|German (Rare)|Ancient Roman|English,FLO-rens(Dutch),,,,,,,"Roman cognomen which was derived from Latin florens meaning ""prosperous, flourishing"" (see Florence).In the Dutch-speaking world, this name is the Dutch form of Florentius, whereas in the German-speaking world, it is a variant spelling of Florenz.Notable bearers of this name include the 3rd-century Christian author Tertullian (whose full name was Quintus Septimius Florens Tertullianus), the German politician Florens von Bockum-Dolffs (1802-1899) and the 14th-century Dutchman Florens Radewyns, who co-founded the religious community of the Brethren of the Common Life."
Florența,f,,Romanian,flo-REN-tsa,,,,,,,Romanian form of Florentia (see Florence). Note that Florența is also the Romanian name for the Italian city of Florence.
Florenta,f,,Spanish (Rare),,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Florente as well as a variant form of Florencia, which is the main Spanish form of Florentia.There might possibly also be some cases where this name is a contracted form of Florentina."
Florente,m,,Spanish (Rare),,,,,,,,"Spanish form of Florens as well as a variant form of Florencio, which is the main Spanish form of Florentius."
Florentí,m,,Catalan,,,,,,,,Catalan form of Florentinus.
Florentiaan,m,,Dutch (Archaic),,,,,,,,Dutch form of Florentianus.
Florentian,m,,English (Archaic)|German (Archaic),,,,,,,,"English and German form of Florentianus. This name was borne by saint Florentian, a 5th-century bishop from North Africa who was forced into exile by the Vandal king Gaiseric (also known as Geiseric and Genseric) for continuing to adhere to Orthodox Christianity."
Florentiana,f,,Late Roman|Dutch (Rare),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Florentianus. This name is not to be confused with the similar-looking name Florentina.
Florentianus,m,,Late Roman,,,,,,,,"Extended form of Florentius (see Florence). This name is not to be confused with the similar-looking name Florentinus.Florentianus was the name of a 6th-century maior domus (""Mayor of the Palace""), who served the Frankish king Childebert II (570–595)."
Florentien,,,French (Archaic)|Dutch (Rare),FLAW-RAHN-TYEN(French)|flo-rən-TEEN(Dutch),,,,,,,French form of Florentianus (strictly masculine) and Dutch variant of Florentine (predominantly feminine).
Florentienne,f,,"French (Archaic)|French (Belgian, Archaic)|French (Quebec, Archaic)",FLAW-RAHN-TYEN(French),,,,,,,French form of Florentiana.
Florentijn,,,Dutch (Rare),flo-rən-TAYN,,,,,,,"Dutch form of Florentinus (for men) and Florentina (for women), but the name is most often encountered on men. It is unisex in the Netherlands, but strictly masculine in Flanders, the Dutch-speaking part of Belgium.This name is not to be confused with the Dutch word Florentijn meaning ""Florentine"" (as in, an inhabitant of the Italian city of Florence), even though it is etymologically related.A notable bearer of this name is the Dutch artist Florentijn Hofman (b. 1977)."
Florentín,m,,Spanish (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Florentino.Known bearers of this name include the Spanish martyr Florentín Felipe Naya (1856-1936) and the Paraguayan pianist and composer Florentín Giménez (b. 1925).
Florentinas,m,,Lithuanian (Rare),,,,,,,,Lithuanian form of Florentinus.
Florentīns,m,,Latvian (Archaic),,,,,,,,Latvian form of Florentinus.
Florentios,m,Φλωρέντιος(Greek),Greek,,,,,,,,"Modern Greek spelling of Phlorentios, which is the late Greek form of Florentius."
Florențiu,m,,Romanian (Rare),,,,,,,,Romanian form of Florentius (see Florence).
Florentiy,m,Флорентий(Russian),Russian,,,,,,,,Russian form of Florentius (see Florence).
Florenty,m,Флорентий(Russian),Polish (Archaic)|Russian (Rare),,,,,,,,Polish form of Florentius (see Florence) and Russian variant transcription of Florentiy.
Florentyn,m,,Polish (Rare),,,,,,,,Polish form of Florentinus.
Florentzia,f,,Basque (Rare),,,,,,,,Basque form of Spanish Florencia (compare Florence).
Florenz,m,,German (Rare)|Dutch (Rare),/ˈflo.ʁɛnts/(German),,,,,,,"German form of Florentius and Dutch variant spelling of Florens. Note that Florenz is also the German name for the Italian city of Florence.A famous bearer of this name was Florenz ""Flo"" Ziegfeld Jr. (1867-1932), an American Broadway impresario who was the creator of the Ziegfeld Follies as well as the husband of the American actress Billie Burke (1884-1970)."
Florenza,f,,Italian|Spanish (Archaic),,,,,,,,Italian variant of Fiorenza and Spanish variant of Florencia and/or Florenta.
Florenzia,f,,Italian,,,,,,,,Variant of Fiorenza.
Florenziana,f,,Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,Italian form of Florentiana.
Florenziano,m,,Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,Italian form of Florentianus.
Florenzo,m,,Italian (Rare)|Spanish (Archaic),,,,,,,,Italian variant of Fiorenzo and Spanish variant of Florencio and/or Florente.
Flores,f,,English,FLOORZ,,,,,,,"Diminutive of Flora, Florence and other names beginning in Flo."
Florese,f,,Medieval Italian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Floresha,f,,Obscure (Modern),,,,,,,,Possibly an elaboration of Flor.
Floresita,f,,Spanish (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Florecita.
Floressa,f,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,Elaborated form of Flora.
Floresta,f,,"Portuguese (Brazilian, Rare)|Spanish (Philippines, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Transferred use of the surname Floresta. It may also occasionally be given in reference to Dionísia Gonçalves Pinto (1810-1885), better known as Nísia Floresta Brasileira Augusta or simply Nísia Floresta, a Brazilian educator, translator, writer, poet, philosopher, and feminist."
Florestan,m,,French (Rare)|German (Rare)|Theatre|Literature|Polish (Archaic),/flɔ.ʁɛ.stɑ̃/(French),,,,,,,"Obscure southern French name probably derived from Latin florescere ""to flower; to put forth blooms"".A known bearer is Florestan I, Prince of Monaco (1785 - 1856, born Tancrède Florestan Roger Louis Grimaldi).Florestan is also a character in Ludwig van Beethoven's opera ""Fidelio"" (1814)."
Florestano,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Florestan.
Florestine,f,,French (Rare)|Louisiana Creole|French (Quebec),,,,,,,,"French feminine form of Florestan. This was borne by Princess Florestine of Monaco (1833-1897), daughter of the Monegasque prince Florestan I."
Florëszk,m,,Kashubian,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Florión.
Florëszka,f,,Kashubian,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Flóra and Floriana.
Floreta,f,,Albanian|Gascon|Romanian,,,,,,,,Cognate of Floretta.
Floretia,f,,Medieval French,,,,,,,,"Derived from Latin floretia ""floweriness""."
Floréye,f,,Walloon,,,,,,,,Walloon form of Florie.
Florfina,f,,Filipino,,,,,,,,Not available.
Flori,m,,Romansh (Archaic)|Judeo-Spanish|Albanian,FLO-ree,,,,,,,Variant of Fluri.
