Fryni,f,Φρύνη(Greek),Greek,FREE-nee,,,,,,,Modern Greek form of Phryne.
Frynne,f,,Literature,,,,,,,,Possibly a variant of Phryne. The name appears in George R. R. Martin's A Song of Ice and Fire series of fantasy novels.
Frytha,f,,Literature,Free-tha,,,,,,,Possibly an Anglicized form of Fríða. It was used by Rosemary Sutcliff for a character in her children's historical novel The Shield Ring (1956).
Ftatateeta,f,,Theatre,FTAH-TAH-TEE-TAH|fə-tah-tə-TEE-tə,,,,,,,"This name was invented by George Bernard Shaw for a character in his play Caesar and Cleopatra. She is Cleopatra's nurse. The name was used by ventriloquist Jimmy Nelson for a puppet of his, a cat. Though she was female, her voice was based on Ed Wynn's. The scientific name of Kleopatrina ftatateeta was given to a fossil coral of the Permian period."
Ftima,f,فاطمة(Maghrebi Arabic),Arabic (Maghrebi),,,,,,,,"Alternate transcription of Arabic فاطمة (see Fatima), chiefly used in Morocco."
Fûad,m,فووادئە(Kurdish Sorani),Kurdish,,,,,,,,Kurdish form of Fuad.
Fuád,m,,Arabic (Magyarized),,,,,,,,Hungarian variant of Fuad
Fuadi,m,,Indonesian,,,,,,,,Indonesian variant of Fuad.
Fuafiva,m,,Polynesian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Fuai,f,芙皑(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 芙 (fú) meaning ""hibiscus"" and 皑 (ái) meaning ""brilliant white""."
Fuaito,m,"ふぁいと(Japanese Hiragana)|渉愛人, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FUU-AH-EE-TO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 渉 (fu) meaning ""ford, go cross, transit, ferry, import, involve"", 愛 (ai) meaning ""love, affection"" combined with 人 (to) meaning ""person"". Other kanji combinations are possible.Usage of this name is, most likely, influenced by the word fight."
Fualupe,,,Samoan|Tongan,foo-a-lu-pe,,,,,,,"Means ""fruit dove"" in Samoan and Tongan."
Fuan,f,芙安(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 芙 (fú) meaning ""hibiscus"" and 安 (ān) meaning ""quiet, calm, tranquil, peaceful""."
Fuani,f,,Kongo,,,,,,,,Kongo form of Fanny.
Fubao,f,馥宝(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 馥 (fù) meaning ""fragrance"" and 宝 (bǎo) meaning ""treasure, jewel; precious, rare""."
Fubuki,f,"ふぶき(Japanese Hiragana)|フブキ(Japanese Katakana)|輔雪, 芙吹, 風蓬姫, 甫歩綺, 夫武季, 富部杞, 楓蒲騎, 婦海妃, 不夫利, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FOO-BOO-KEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 吹雪 (fubuki) meaning ""snowstorm, blizzard"". Other kanji combinations are usable."
Fucan,f,,Bontoc (Hispanicized),/fu.ka̝n/,,,,,,,Hispanicized form of Fukan.
Fuchan,f,"馥禅, 馥婵(Chinese)",Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 馥 (fù) meaning ""fragrance, aroma"" and 禅 (chán) meaning ""meditation, contemplation"" or 婵 (chán) meaning ""beautiful, lovely""."
Fuchang,f,芙嫦(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 芙 (fú) meaning ""hibiscus"" and 嫦 (cháng), the name of a moon goddess."
Fucheng,,"富城, etc.(Chinese)",Chinese|Chinese (Cantonese),/fu.tʂʰəŋ/(Chinese)|/fʷuː.ʈʂʰəŋ/(Chinese)|/fu.sɪŋ/(Cantonese),,,,,,,"From Chinese 富 (fù) meaning ""abundant, rich, wealthy"" combined with 城 (chéng) meaning ""castle, city"". Other hanzi combinations are possible."
Fuchsia,f,,"English (British, Rare)|Literature",/ˈfjuː.ʃə/(English),,,,,,,"From Fuchsia, a genus of flowering plants, itself named after the German botanist Leonhart Fuchs (1501-1566), whose surname means ""fox"" in German.It was most famously used by British author Mervyn Peake for the character Fuchsia Groan in his Gormenghast books (1946-1959)."
Fuci,f,芙慈(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 芙 (fú) meaning ""hibiscus"" and 慈 (cí) meaning ""kind, loving, charitable, benevolent""."
Fuco,m,,Galician,,,,,,,,Hypocoristic of Francisco.
Fud,m,,Obscure,,,,,,,,"The nickname of two Jazz musicians in the 20th century, Fud Candrix from Belgium and Fud Livingston from the USA."
Fude,f,筆(Japanese Kanji)|ふで(Japanese Hiragana)|フデ(Japanese Katakana),Japanese,/ɸɯ̟ᵝ.de̞/,,,,,,,"This name is used as 筆 (hitsu, fude) meaning ""handwriting, (painting/writing) brush,"" originally made up of 文 (fumi) meaning ""writing(s)"" and 手 (te) meaning ""hand."" The phrase had undergone sound shifts, first from 'fumite' to 'funde' and then from that to what is now 'fude.'Fude was uncommon in the Edo period (1603-1868) and in the Meiji period (1868-1912) before becoming very uncommon in the Taishō period (1912-1926)."
Fudhail,m,,Malay|Indonesian,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Fadil.
Fudji,f,ふぢ(Japanese Hiragana),Japanese (Archaic),,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Japanese Kanji 藤 or Japanese Hiragana ふぢ (see Fuji). It was used in the Edo Period.
Fudo,m,,Japanese Mythology,FOO-DO,,,,,,,"Fudo-Myo-o is the god of fire and wisdom in Japanese Buddhist mythology, and one of the five Myo-o."
Fudong,m,"福东, 复东, 扶东, 富栋, etc.(Chinese)|福東, 復東, 扶東, 富棟, etc.(Traditional Chinese)",Chinese,/fu.tʊŋ/,,,,,,,"From Chinese 福 (fú) meaning ""happiness, good fortune, blessing"", 复 (fù) meaning ""return, resume, recover"", 扶 (fú) meaning ""help, support"" or 富 (fù) meaning ""abundant, rich, wealthy"" combined with 东 (dōng) meaning ""east"" or 栋 (dòng) meaning ""pillar, beam"". Other character combinations can form this name as well."
Fuduan,f,芙端(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 芙 (fú) meaning ""hibiscus"" and 端 (duān) meaning ""end, beginning""."
Fudzuki,f,"ふづき(Japanese Hiragana)|婦月, 芙月, 楓月, 風月, 文月, 歩月, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FUU-DZUU-KEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 婦 (fu) meaning ""married woman, woman, lady"", 芙 (fu) meaning ""lotus, Mt Fuji"", 楓 (fu) meaning ""maple"", 風 (fu) meaning ""wind, air, style, manner"", 文 (fu) meaning ""sentence"" or 歩 (fu) meaning ""walk"" combined with 月 (dzuki) meaning ""moon"". Other combinations of kanji are possible."
Fue,f,"芙峨, 芙娥(Chinese)",Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 芙 (fú) meaning ""hibiscus"" and 峨 (é) meaning ""high mountain, lofty"" or 娥 (é) meaning ""be beautiful, good""."
Fueang,f,เฟื่อง(Thai),Thai,/ˈfwa̯ŋ˦˥/,,,,,,,"Means ""flourishing, prosperous, blooming"" in Thai."
Fueangfa,f,เฟื่องฟ้า(Thai),Thai,/fwa̯ŋ˦˥.ˈfaː˦˥/,,,,,,,"From Thai เฟื่อง (fueang) meaning ""prosperous, flourishing, blooming"" and ฟ้า (fa) meaning ""sky blue""."
Fuechy,m,,Hmong,Fu-chi,,,,,,,"Means ""power, control"" in Hmong."
Fuencisla,f,,Spanish (European),/fwen.ˈθis.la/(European Spanish),,,,,,,"Said to be taken from Latin fōns stīllāns meaning ""dripping well, dripping fountain"" (the first element also used in Fuensanta), taken from the Spanish titles of the Virgin Mary, La Virgen de la Fuencisla and Nuestra Señora de la Fuencisla, meaning ""The Virgin of Fuencisla"" and ""Our Lady of Fuencisla.""She is the patron saint of Segovia in the autonomous community of Castile and León in north-western Spain, hence the particularly high concentration in the city and province of the same name."
Fuente,f,,"Spanish (European, Rare)",/ˈfwen.te/(European Spanish),,,,,,,"Means ""fountain"" in Spanish, derived from the title of the Virgin Mary Nuestra Señora de la Fuente meaning ""Our Lady of the Fountain"". She is the patron saint of various Spanish towns like Munera (Albacete) and Vilallonga (Valencia)."
Fuentes,f,,"Spanish (European, Rare)",/ˈfwen.tes/(European Spanish),,,,,,,"Means ""fountains"" in Spanish, derived from the title of the Virgin Mary Nuestra Señora de las Fuentes meaning ""Our Lady of the Fountains"". She is the patron saint of the Spanish town of Villalón de Campos (Valladolid). It is sometimes used in Valencia and Albacete in conflation with the name Fuente."
Fufei,f,宓妃(Chinese),Chinese Mythology,,,,,,,,"From a combination of the characters 宓 (fu, a surname) and 妃 (fei, meaning ""concubine, consort""). More commonly known as Luoshen (洛神), Fufei is the goddess of the Luo River in Chinese mythology, first appearing in the Chuci (楚辞) or Elegies of Chu as the wife of the river god Hebo. Later she became a muse for Chinese poets, with the 2nd-century Goddess of the Luo River (洛神赋) being her most famous representation. In it, the author Cao Zhi briefly encounters and falls in love with her before they are separated forever.Her origins are unknown, but some sources identify her as a daughter of Fuxi."
Fufetia,f,,Ancient Roman,,,,,,,,Not available.
Fufluns,m,,Etruscan Mythology,FOO-floons,,,,,,,"Etruscan deity, predominantly of wine, health, happiness, and growth. He is often considered the Etruscan equivalent of the Greek god Dionysus and the Roman god Bacchus."
Fufu,f,"風々, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese|Portuguese,FUU-FUU,,,,,,,"From Japanese 風 (fu) meaning ""wind"" combined with 々, a phonetic character indicting a duplication of the beginning kanji. Other kanji combinations are possible."
Fugel,m,,Anglo-Saxon|Medieval Scandinavian,,,,,,,,"Derived from Old English fugel ""fowl, bird"", probably originally a byname or nickname. It is a cognate of Old Norse Fugl."
Fugen,m,普賢(Japanese Kanji)|ふげん(Japanese Hiragana),Buddhism,/ɸɯᵝ.ɡe̞ɴ/(Japanese),,,,,,,"Japanese form of Puxian, used as the Japanese name for the bodhisattva Samantabhadra."
Fughle,m,,Old Swedish,,,,,,,,Old Swedish form of Fugli.
Fugli,m,,Old Norse,,,,,,,,"From Old Norse fugl ""bird""."
Fuglo,m,,Medieval English,,,,,,,,Not available.
Fuhe,f,芙荷(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 芙 (fú) meaning ""hibiscus"" and 荷 (hé) meaning ""lotus""."
Fuhito,m,"史, 史人, 文人, 富仁, 不比等, 不比人, 不比斗, etc.(Japanese Kanji)|ふひと(Japanese Hiragana)|フヒト(Japanese Katakana)",Japanese (Rare),[ɸɯ̟́ᵝ.çì̥.tò̞],,,,,,,"From archaic 史 (fuhito) referring to a court historian, shifted from earlier fumihito which is made up of 文/書 (fumi) meaning ""letter, writings"" and 人 (hito) meaning ""person.""It can be written with other kanji, such as 富 (fu) meaning ""riches, wealth, fortune,"" 不 (fu), 比 (hi), 仁 (to) meaning ""benevolence, compassion, humanity,"" 等 (to) and 斗 (to), referring to the Chinese constellation known as the Dipper. The combination 不比等 was used on Fujiwara no Fuhito (藤原 不比等) (659–720), who was the second son of Fujiwara no Kamatari and a powerful member of the imperial court of Japan during the Asuka and Nara periods.This name is rarely used."
Fuhong,f,"馥虹, 馥鸿(Chinese)",Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 馥 (fù) meaning ""fragrance, aroma"" and 虹 (hóng) meaning ""rainbow"" or 鸿 (hóng) meaning ""wild swan""."
Fuhuan,f,芙欢(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 芙 (fú) meaning ""hibiscus"" and 欢 (huān) meaning ""happy, joyous; joy""."
Fuifui,,,Samoan|Tongan,FOO-EE-FOO-EE,,,,,,,"Tongan and Samoan meaning ""love love; connected, joined together"", to indicate ""greatly loved"". A famous namesake is Tongan-born international rugby league footballer Fuifui Moimoi."
Fuji,,"藤, 富士, 不二, 婦志, 二治, etc.(Japanese Kanji)|ふじ(Japanese Hiragana)|フジ(Japanese Katakana)",Japanese,[ɸɯ̟́ᵝ.ʑì],,,,,,,"From either 藤 (fuji) meaning ""wisteria"" or 富士 (Fuji), from the place name Fuji. Other kanji used for this name in 2-kanji combinations include 不, a phonetic kanji that is otherwise used as a negative prefix​, or 婦 meaning ""woman, lady"" for the first element and 志 meaning ""will, aim, goal"" or 治 meaning ""cure; management"" for the second element (二, meaning ""two,"" is used on both, stemming from its native and Sino-Japanese readings futa(tsu) and ji)."
Fujie,f,"藤枝, 藤恵, 藤絵, 藤江, 藤重, 藤衛, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FOO-ZHEE-EH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 藤 (fuji) meaning ""wisteria, creeper plant, rattan"" combined with 枝 (e) meaning ""branch, limb"", 恵 (e) meaning ""favour, benefit"", 絵 (e) meaning ""picture, painting"", 江 (e) meaning “bay, inlet”, 重 (e) meaning “layers, folds”, or 衛 (e) meaning “guard, protect”. Other kanji combinations are also possible."
Fujihiko,m,,Japanese,,,,,,,,Not available.
Fujihito,m,,Japanese,,,,,,,,Not available.
Fujika,f,"藤佳, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,,,,,,,,"From Japanese 藤 (fuji) meaning ""wisteria"" combined with 佳 (ka) meaning ""beautiful, good"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Fujiko,f,"ふじこ(Japanese Hiragana)|藤子, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FOO-ZHEE-KO,,,,,,,From 藤 (fuji) meaning “wisteria” combined with 子 (ko) meaning “child”. Other kanji combinations are possible.
Fujimaru,m,"藤丸, 富士丸, etc.(Japanese Kanji)|ふじまる(Japanese Hiragana)|フジマル(Japanese Katakana)",Japanese (Rare),[ɸɯ̟̀ᵝ.ʑí.mà.ɾɯ̟̀ᵝ],,,,,,,"From Fuji combined with the suffix 丸 (maru) meaning ""circle, round,"" used before the Meiji Period (1868-1912) as a suffix denoting affection (along with -maro (麿/麻呂)) and was given to boys of upper class until they came of age, usually at ages 13 to 17.It is rarely used."
Fujimi,f,"ふじ美(Kanji/Hiragana)|藤美, 布治民, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FOO-JEE-MEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 藤 (fuji) meaning ""wisteria"" combined with 美 (mi) meaning ""beautiful"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Fujimori,m,"藤盛, 藤森, 藤守, 富士盛, etc.(Japanese Kanji)|ふじもり(Japanese Hiragana)|フジモリ(Japanese Katakana)","Japanese (Rare)|South American (Modern, Rare)",[ɸɯ̟̀ᵝ.ʑí.mò̞.ɾʲì](Japanese)|/fu.xi.ˈmo.ɾi/(Spanish),,,,,,,"Combination of Fuji and 盛 (mori) meaning ""heap, pile,"" 森 (mori) meaning ""forest"" or 守 (mori) meaning ""protection."" It is rarely used in Japan and is more often found as a surname.It has been used as a rare masculine name in Peru (more often a middle name than a forename), in honour of former controversial Peruvian president Alberto Fujimori (1938-), especially around the year 1990 when he won his first term."
Fujina,f,"藤菜, 藤那, 不二奈, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FUU-JEE-NAH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 藤 (fuji) meaning ""wisteria"" combined with 菜 (na) meaning ""vegetables, greens"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Fujino,f,"藤乃, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,,,,,,,,"From Japanese 藤 (fuji) meaning ""wisteria"" combined with 乃 (no), a possessive particle. Other combinations of kanji characters can also form this name."
Fujinosuke,m,,Japanese,FOO-JI-NO-SOO-KAY,,,,,,,"Fuji, meaning ""Wisteria"" and Nosuke, meaning ""Assistant"", or ""Guardian""."
Fujio,m,"ふじお(Japanese Hiragana)|不二夫, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FOO-ZHEE-O,,,,,,,"From Japanese 富 (fu) meaning ""wealth, enrich, abundant"", 芙 (fu) meaning ""lotus, Mt Fuji"", 次 (ji) meaning ""next, order, sequence"", 二 (ji) meaning ""two"", or 治 (ji) meaning ""reign, be at peace, calm down, subdue, quell, govt, cure, heal, rule, conserve"", combined with 生 (o) meaning ""raw, live"", 男 (o) meaning ""male"", 暢 (o) meaning ""stretch"", 夫 (o) meaning ""man, husband"", 雄 (o) meaning ""hero, manly"", 郎 (o) meaning ""son"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Fujitaka,m,藤孝(Japanese Kanji),Japanese (Rare),Foo-jee-tah-ka,,,,,,,"Fuji means ""Wisteria Flower"" and Taka means ""High(er)"". Fujitaka Kinomoto is a fictional character in ""Cardcaptor Sakura"" and Hosokawa Fujitaka was a Japanese samurai daimyo in the Sengoku Period."
Fuju,m,"复榘, etc.(Chinese)",Chinese,,,,,,,,Not available.
Fukan,f,,Bontoc,/fu.ka̝n/,,,,,,,"Meaning unknown. The name was used in Bontoc Mythology, the former name of Kayapon, a Bontoc girl who married Lumawig, the supreme deity."
Fuk'anggan,m,ᡶᡠᠺᠠᠩᡤᠠᠨ(Manchu),Manchu,/fuk.aŋ.ŋan/,,,,,,,Not available.
Fukashi,m,"洋, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FUU-KAH-SHEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 洋 (fukashi) meaning ""ocean"" or other kanji which are pronounced the same way."
Fukayna,f,,"African American (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Not available.
Fuki,,"フキ(Japanese Katakana)|蕗, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,,,,,,,,Variant transcription of Fuuki.
Fukiko,f,"ふき子(Kanji/Hiragana)|蕗子, 富姫子, 楓磯子, 婦葵子, 風杞子, 甫季子, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FOO-KEE-KO,,,,,,,"From 蕗 (fuki) meaning ""giant butterbur, Petasites japonicus"" or 富 (fu) meaning ""to enrich, abundance, wealth"" and 磯 (ki) meaning ""beach, seashore"", that is then combined with 子 (ko) meaning ""child"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Fukuichi,m,"富久一, 二九一, 富久市, 復一, 福一, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FUU-KUU-EE-CHEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 富 (fu) meaning ""wealth"", 久 (ku) meaning ""long time"" combined with 一 (ichi) meaning ""one"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Fukuichiro,m,"ふくいちろ(Japanese Hiragana)|フクイチロ(Japanese Katakana)|復一朗, 福一郎, 冨久一 狼, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese (Rare),FOO-KOO-EE-CHEE-ROO,,,,,,,Variant transcription of Fukuichirou.
Fukuichirou,m,"ふくいちろう(Japanese Hiragana)|フクイチロウ(Japanese Katakana)|復一朗, 福一郎, 冨久一 狼, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese (Rare),FOO-KOO-EE-CHEE-ROO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 復 (fuku) meaning ""revert, resume, return to, restore"" or 福 (fuku) meaning ""good luck, good fortune"" combined with 一 (ichi) meaning ""one"" and 狼 (rou) meaning ""wolf"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Fukuji,m,"福道, 福司, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese (Rare),FOO-KOO-ZHEE,,,,,,,"From 福 (fuku) meaning ""happiness, good fortune, blessing"" combined with 道 (ji) meaning ""course, journey, road, street, moral principle"". This name can also be formed by other combinations of kanji."
Fukujiro,m,"福次郎, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FOO-KOO-ZHEE-RO,,,,,,,"From 福 (fuku) meaning ""happiness, good fortune, blessing"", 道 (ji) meaning ""course, journey, road, street, moral principle"", and 郎 (ro) meaning ""son"". This name can also be formed by other combinations of kanji."
Fukujirou,m,"冨久二郎, 復二郎, 福次郎, 福治郎, 福二郎, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FUU-KUU-JEE-ṘO:,,,,,,,"From Japanese 冨 (fu) meaning ""rich, wealthy"", 久 (ku) meaning ""long time"" or 復 (fuku) meaning ""return"", 福 (fuku) meaning ""good luck, good fortune"" combined with 二 (ji) meaning ""two"", 次 (ji) meaning ""next"" or 治 (ji) meaning ""govern, regulate, administer"" combined with 郎 (rou) meaning ""son"". Other combinations of kanji characters can also be possible."
Fukujū,m,,Japanese,,,,,,,,Not available.
