Furana,f,"風楽那, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FUU-ṘAH-NAH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 風 (fu) meaning ""wind"", 楽 (ra) meaning ""comfort, music"" combined with 那 (na) meaning ""what"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Furano,f,"ふらの(Japanese Hiragana)|フラノ(Japanese Katakana)|富良野, 芙醇野, 風螺乃, 蕗蘭乃, etc.(Japanese Kanji)","Japanese (Modern, Rare)",FOO-RA-NO,,,,,,,"Derived from the place names in Hokkaido, spelled with Japanese 富 (fu) meaning ""wealth"", 良 (ra) meaning ""good"" and 野 (no) meaning ""area, field"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Furawa,f,"フラワ(Japanese Katakana)|ふらわ(Japanese Hiragana)|歩倭和, 楓良環, 不楽羽, 婦蘭琶, etc.(Japanese Kanji)","Japanese (Modern, Rare)",FOO-RA-WA,,,,,,,"This name comes from the Japanese pronunciation of the English word flower. It can be spelled with Japanese 風 (fu) meaning ""wind"", 羅 (ra) meaning ""lightweight fabric or clothing; silk gauze; thin silk"" combined with 和 (wa) meaning ""peaceful; harmonious"". Other kanji combinations are also possible."
Furcas,m,,Judeo-Christian-Islamic Legend,,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Latin furca ""fork, pitchfork"" or Greco-Roman furca ""sepulcher"". According to demonology, this is the name of a knight of Hell shown in the Ars Goetia."
Fürchtegott,m,,German (Archaic),/ˈfʏʁç.tə.ˌɡɔt/,,,,,,,"A pietistic name with the literal meaning ""fear God!""."
