Furen,f,芙葚(Chinese),Chinese,,,,,,,,"From the Chinese 芙 (fú) meaning ""hibiscus"" and 葚 (rèn) meaning ""mulberry fruit""."
Furesshu,f,"心初, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FUU-ṘE-SHUU,,,,,,,"From Japanese 心 (fure) meaning ""heart, mind, soul"" combined with 初 (sshu) meaning ""beginning, start, first"". Other kanji combinations are possible.Usage of this name is, most likely, influenced by the word fresh."
Furfur,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Either from Latin furfur meaning ""bran, husk of grain"" or (more likely) a corruption of furcifer meaning ""scoundrel, rogue"". This is the name of a demon who appears in several works on demonology, including the 17th-century grimoire The Lesser Key of Solomon (author unknown), the Pseudomonarchia Daemonum (1563) by Dutch physician Johann Weyer and the Dictionnaire Infernal (1818) by French occultist Jacques Collin de Plancy."
