Furnilla,f,,Ancient Roman,,,,,,,,"Diminutive of Furnia, as it contains the Latin feminine diminutive suffix -illa.A notable bearer of this name was the Roman noblewoman Marcia Furnilla, who was the second and last wife of the Roman emperor Titus (1st century AD). She was also the aunt of the Roman emperor Trajan 1, as she was a sister of his mother."
Furnius,m,,Ancient Roman,,,,,,,,"Roman nomen gentilicium (family name) which was possibly derived from the Latin noun furnis meaning ""oven"" as well as ""bakery"".A notable bearer of this name was the Roman tribune Gaius Furnius (1st century BC), who was a friend and correspondent of Cicero."
Furnley,m,,"English (Australian, Rare)",FURN-lee(Australian English),,,,,,,"Transferred use of the surname Furnley. Frank Leslie Thomson Wilmot (1881-1942), who published his work under the pseudonym Furnley Maurice, was a noted Australian poet."
Fūro,f,風露(Japanese Kanji)|ふうろ(Japanese Hiragana)|フウロ(Japanese Katakana),Japanese,FOO:-RO,,,,,,,"Taken from 風露草 (fūrosō), the Japanese word for ""geranium""; this name combines 風 (fū) meaning ""wind"" with 露 (ro) meaning ""dew"". Other kanji or kanji combinations can form this name as well. Skyla from Pokémon Black, White, Black 2, and White 2, who is also known as Fūro (フウロ) in the Japanese versions is a fictional bearer of this name."
Furonda,f,,African American,,,,,,,,Not available.
Furong,,"富荣, etc.(Chinese)|富榮, etc.(Traditional Chinese)",Chinese,/fu.ɻʊŋ/,,,,,,,"From Chinese 富 (fù) meaning ""abundant, rich, wealthy"" combined with 荣 (róng) meaning ""glory, honour, flourish, prosper"". Other character combinations can form this name as well."
Furor,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"Furor is the wrathful spirit in Book 2, Canto 4 of ""The Faerie Queene"". He captures Phedon and is subdued by Guyon."
Furo-ria,f,"華, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,FUU-ṘO-ṘEE-AH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 華 (furo-ria) meaning ""flower"". Other kanji or kanji combinations can form this name as well.Usage of this name is, most likely, influenced by the name Floria."
