Gadelas,m,,Irish Mythology,,,,,,,,"In Irish mythology, Gadelas is the semi-mythical ancestor of the Gaels. According to medieval texts like the Lebor Gabála Érenn, he was the son of Niul and Scota, credited with creating the Gaelic language and lending his name to the Gaelic people.According to legend, Gadelas was bitten by a venomous serpent as a young child in Egypt. He was healed by Moses, who laid his rod upon the wound. Moses famously prophesied that no venomous snake or creature could ever thrive in any land where Gadelas or his descendants would live—a folklore explanation for the absence of snakes in Ireland. His descendants, the Milesians (sons of Míl Espáine), are said to have wandered for generations through Scythia and the Iberian Peninsula (Spain) before sailing to and conquering Ireland."
