Gagains,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"A variant form of Gawaine. The eldest son of King Lot of Orkney and Queen Morgawse (or Anna), Arthur’s sister or half-sister. Brother of Gareth, Gaheris and Agravaine, chief of the Orkney clan (which included his brothers and half brother Mordred), Arthur’s favorite nephew, and one of the most strongest and famous knights of the Round Table.When the early Welsh character Gwalchmei, photo Gawaine transitioned into continental Europe via Latin and French translations, his name went through a massive linguistic evolution. French and Anglo-Norman poets working in the twelfth century struggled with the Celtic and Latinized phonetic sounds (like Walwanus or Gualguanus). This resulted in nasalized and guttural variations starting with ""G"" instead of ""W""The variant ""Gagains"" is used in the Le Roman d'Yder (The Romance of Yder), an Old French/Anglo-Norman verse romance dating back to the early 13th century (circa 1210–1220)The ending ""-s"" in Gagains (or Gauvains) is a classic feature of Old French grammar. In Old French, the case subject (nominative case) for masculine nouns and names typically added an ""-s"" at the end when the character was the subject performing the action. When he was the object of the action, the ""-s"" was dropped (becoming Gagain or Gauvain)"
Gagan,,"गगन(Hindi, Nepali)|ਗਗਨ(Gurmukhi)|ଗଗନ(Odia)",Hindi|Punjabi|Odia|Nepali|Sundanese,/ˈɡə.ɡən/(Hindi)|/ˈɡə.ɡə.nə̆/(Punjabi)|/ˈɡɔ.ɡɔ.n̪ɔ/(Odia)|/ɡʌ.ˈɡ̞ʌn/(Nepali),,,,,,,"From Sanskrit गगन (gagana) meaning ""atmosphere, sky"". As a Sikh (Punjabi) name it is sometimes feminine."
