Galang,m,,Indonesian,/ˈɡa.laŋ/,,,,,,,"Means ""support, beam, foundation"" in Indonesian."
Galanthis,f,,Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"In Greek mythology, Galanthis was the woman who interfered with Hera's plan to hinder the birth of Heracles in favor of Eurystheus, and was changed into a weasel or cat as punishment for being so insolent as to deceive the goddesses of birth that were acting on Hera's behalf."
Galaor,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Invented name from the Old French word gale meaning ""joy, pleasure"". In the book 'Amadis de Gaula', Galaor is the brother of Amadis."
Galareka,f,,Navajo,,,,,,,,Not available.
Galarr,m,,Old Norse|Norse Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""screamer"" in Old Norse. In Norse mythology this is the name of both a giant and a dwarf. The dwarf and his brother, Fjalarr, murdered Kvasir and brewed the mead of the skalds from his blood. They also killed Gillingr and his wife, and were then killed by Gillingr's son, Suttungr."
Galasso,m,,Medieval Italian,,,,,,,,"Variant form of Galeazzo. Known Italian bearers of this name include the military leader Galasso da Montefeltro (died in 1300), the painter Galasso Galassi (c. 1420-1473) and the architect Galasso Alghisi (1523-1573)."
