Galvagin,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"A variant form of Gawaine. The eldest son of King Lot of Orkney and Queen Morgawse (or Anna), Arthur’s sister or half-sister. Brother of Gareth, Gaheris and Agravaine, chief of the Orkney clan (which included his brothers and half brother Mordred), Arthur’s favorite nephew, and one of the most strongest and famous knights of the Round Table."
Galvaginus,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"A variant form of Gawaine. The eldest son of King Lot of Orkney and Queen Morgawse (or Anna), Arthur’s sister or half-sister. Brother of Gareth, Gaheris and Agravaine, chief of the Orkney clan (which included his brothers and half brother Mordred), Arthur’s favorite nephew, and one of the most strongest and famous knights of the Round Table.The variant spelling ""Galvaginus"" is famously used on the Modena Archivolt, a stone bas-relief carving located above the Porta della Pescheria (Fishmarket Gate) of the Modena Cathedral in Italy. The sculpture depicts a proto-Arthurian rescue mission. A castle is being attacked by several knights to rescue a captured woman named Winlogee (Guinevere).Every figure is explicitly labeled with an inscription chiseled into the stone. One knight, depicted in full mail armor and fighting a giant named Carrado (Carados), is carved with the name GALVAGINVS.The carving is dated by art historians to the early 12th century, roughly between 1100 and 1140. It represents the earliest known Continental form of Gawain's name. The existence of Galvaginus in Italy at such an early date proves that tales of Sir Gawain were already being carried across Europe by traveling Norman minstrels, Breton storytellers, etc., long before the legends were formalized into written books.Unlike the Old French variants (Gauvain, Gagains) which dropped the internal ""l"" sound, Italian and Spanish adaptations preserved it. Galvaginus is a Latinized translation of a Breton or Norman-French spoken dialect that still carried the heavy phonetic structure of the original Welsh name, Gwalchmei. Over the next two centuries, Italian scribes dropped the Latin suffix ""-us"" and softened the harsh ""g"", transforming Galvaginus into the popular medieval Italian name Galvano."
