Gangr,m,,Norse Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""traveller"" in Old Norse, ultimately derived from Proto-Germanic *gangaz ""walking, going"". Gangr is a jötunn in Norse Mythology. He is the brother of Iði and Þjazi."
Gangráðr,m,,Old Norse|Norse Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""he who knows the way"" or might be derived from gangr (""walking, motion, path"") and ráð (""advice, counsel""). Odin uses this as an alias during his battle of wits with Vafþrúðnir."
Gangsar,m,,Javanese,/ˈɡaŋ.sar/,,,,,,,"Means ""smooth, quick"" in Javanese. This name was traditionally given to a child whose birth was smooth and easy."
Gang-u,m,"강우(Korean Hangul)|剛雨, 剛佑, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/kaŋ.u/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 剛 (gang) meaning ""hard, rigid, strong"" combined with 雨 (u) meaning ""rain"" or 佑 (u) meaning ""help, protect, bliss"". Other hanja combinations can form this name as well."
Gang-uk,m,강욱(Korean Hangul)|江旭(Korean Hanja),Korean,[ka̠ŋ.uk̚],,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 江 (gang) meaning ""river"" and 旭 (uk) meaning ""rising sun""."
