Garðar,m,,Icelandic|Faroese,/ˈkar.ðar/(Icelandic)|/ˈkɛaː.ɹaɹ/(Faroese),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old Norse elements gerðr meaning ""enclosure, yard, protection"" and the name suffix -arr, which can derive from herr ""army, warrior"", geirr ""spear"", or -varr ""defender, guardian"" (from warjaną). Garðarr Svavarsson (sometimes spelled Garðar) was a Swede who was the first Scandinavian to stay over winter in Iceland, in the 860s. He named it Garðarshólmur (Garðar's Island) after himself, and the country bore the name for a while."
Garðarr,m,,Old Norse,/ˈɡɑrð.ˌɑrː/,,,,,,,"Old Norse form of Garðar. Garðarr Svavarsson (sometimes spelled Garðar) was a Swede who was the first Scandinavian to stay over winter in Iceland, in the 860s. He named it Garðarshólmur (Garðar's Island) after himself, and the country bore the name for a while."
