Geryon,m,Γηρυών(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/ˈɡɛ.ri.ən/(English)|/ɡɛː.ry.ɔ̌ːn/(Attic Greek)|/ʝi.ry.ˈon/(Koine Greek)|/ʝi.ri.ˈon/(Constantinopolitan Greek)|[ˈɡeː.ry.oːn](Classical Latin)|[ˈd͡ʒɛː.ri.on](Ecclesiastical Latin),,,,,,,"Probably means ""the shouter"", from Ancient Greek γῆρυς (gerys) meaning ""voice, speech"", though it has also been connected to γῆ (ge) meaning ""earth, ground"". This was the name of a three-headed (or three-bodied) giant in Greek mythology, said to live on the island of Erytheia with a herd of bright red cattle, which Heracles was sent to steal as his tenth labour."
