Gnosiphilos,m,Γνωσίφιλος(Ancient Greek),Late Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun γνῶσις (gnosis) meaning ""seeking to know, inquiry, knowledge"" (see Gnosis) combined with the Greek noun φίλος (philos) meaning ""friend, lover""."
Gnosis,m,Γνῶσις(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,NO-sis(English)|/ˈɣno.sis/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Means ""knowledge, inquiry"" in Ancient Greek. The inscription Gnosis epoesen ""Gnosis created"" appears on the Stag Hunt mosaic (c. 300 BCE; found in a wealthy home in ancient Macedonia), which may indicate that the author was named Gnosis or possibly refers to an abstract pronoun, since gnosis is also the Greek word for knowledge (one scholar, for example, thinks it should be read as ""Apelles' Knowledge Made It""). If Gnosis was in fact the name of the artist, it is the first known signature of a mosaicist and the only artist name surviving on a pebble floor."
