Gorgias,m,Γοργίας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/ˈɡɔːr.dʒi.əs/(English)|/ɡor.ɡí.aːs/(Attic Greek)|/ɣor.ˈʝi.as/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek γοργός (gorgos) meaning ""grim, fierce, terrible"" as well as ""spirited, vigorous"". This was the name of several historical figures, including an ancient Greek sophist, philosopher and rhetorician of Sicily."
Gorgidas,m,Γοργίδας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""son of Gorgias/Gorgos"" in Greek, derived from the name Gorgias or Gorgos combined with ίδας (idas), which is the Aeolic and Doric Greek form of the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides).This name was borne by a Theban general from the 4th century BC."
Gorgippos,m,Γόργιππος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from either the Greek adjective γοργός (gorgos) meaning ""grim, fierce, terrible"" or the Greek verb γοργεύω (gorgeuo) meaning ""to move rapidly, to hasten"". Also compare the Greek verb γοργόομαι (gorgoomai) meaning ""to be spirited"". The second element of this name is derived from the Greek noun ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"". As such, the name refers to either a fierce horse or a fast horse.Gorgippos was the name of a Spartocid king of the Bosporus (4th century BC)."
Gorgippus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Gorgippos. This was the name of a Spartocid king of the Bosporus (4th century BC).
Gorgitione,m,,Italian,,,,,,,,Italian form of Gorgythion.
Gorgius,m,,Late Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Variant of Gorgias.
Gorgo,f,Γοργώ(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|History,,,,,,,,"Most likely derived from Greek γοργός (gorgos) meaning ""grim, fierce, terrible"". However, it's also possible that the name is derived from the Greek verb γοργεύω (gorgeuō) meaning ""to move rapidly, to hasten"", which itself is related to the Greek noun γοργία (gorgia) meaning ""agility, nimbleness, mobility"". Another possibility is a derivation from the Greek verb γοργόομαι (gorgoomai) ""to be spirited"", which itself is related to the Greek noun γοργότης (gorgotēs) meaning ""liveliness, vigour"" (sometimes it's erroneously translated as ""rapidity""). Gorgo was the name of the wife of king Leonidas I of Sparta (5th century BC)."
Gorgon,m,Γόργων(Ancient Greek),"Ancient Greek|History (Ecclesiastical, Gallicized)",,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek γοργός (gorgos) meaning ""grim, fierce, terrible"" as well as ""spirited, vigorous"". This is also the French name of Saint Gorgonius."
