Gorgus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Gorgos. In Greek mythology, this was the name of an uncle of king Periander of Corinth."
Gorgyra,f,Γόργυρα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/ɡɔːr.ˈdʒaɪ.rə/(English),,,,,,,"Means ""underground drain, sewer"" as well as ""dungeon"" and ""water jug"" in Ancient Greek. Gorgyra, also called Orphne and Styx, was a nymph goddess of the Underworld (Hades) in Greek mythology. She was the mother by the river-god Acheron of the daimon Askalaphos, who was transformed into a screech owl after preventing the goddess Persephone from leaving the Underworld forever."
Gorgythion,m,Γοργυθίων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Patronymic or diminutive form of the given name Γόργυθος (Gorgythos), of uncertain etymology. Possibly derived from Ancient Greek γοργός (gorgos) meaning ""grim, fierce, terrible"". Alternatively, it could derive from Γέργιθες (Gergithes), the name of an ancient town near Troy, or perhaps was intended to faintly echo Γάργαρος (Gargaros), the topmost peak of Mount Ida in Anatolia (now Turkey). In Greek mythology, Gorgythion was a son of King Priam and Castianeira."
