Goumang,m,句芒(Chinese),Chinese Mythology,,,,,,,,"From a combination of the characters 句 (gou, meaning “hooked”) and 芒 (mang, meaning “awn”). Goumang is the Chinese god of wood who oversees the spring and the east, especially the rising place of the sun. He is commonly depicted as a figure with a human face and bird’s body, standing astride two dragons. Goumang is also a messenger of the supreme god Di."
Goun,,"고운(Korean Hangul)|高雲, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean (Modern),/ko̞.un/,,,,,,,"From the present determiner form of adjective 곱다 (gopda) meaning ""beautiful, pretty, fine, soft."" It can also be written with hanja, combining a go hanja, e.g. 高 meaning ""high, tall,"" with an un hanja, e.g. 雲 meaning ""cloud."""
