Gowan,,,Scottish|Medieval English|African,GOW-en(Scottish),,,,,,,"From a Scots name for the daisy and other golden or white field flowers, perhaps ultimately from Old Norse gollinn ""golden"". Robert Burns' poem ""To a Mountain Daisy"" (1786) was originally titled ""The Gowan"". In the 17th and 18th centuries it occurs in the region as a masculine name, possibly a variant of Gawain or from the surname Gowan, which is from Gaelic gobhan ""metal worker, blacksmith"" (genitive gobhainn; compare Gobán)."
