Gundelina,f,,"Spanish (Philippines, Rare)|History (Ecclesiastical)",,,,,,,,"Variant of Gundelinda, the Spanish form of Gundelindis. Gundelina (or Gundlinda) (c. 692 – c. 740) was the third daughter of Duke Adalbert of Alsace and his first wife Gerlinda and niece to the famous blind Saint Odilia, the abbess of Hohenburg. Gundelina was raised by her aunt amongst the nuns at Hohenburg, and when Odilia founded the abbey of Niedermünster, Gundelina became a nun there (c. 717). With the death of her aunt Gundelina, succeeded her as abbess of Niedermünster (723). Though she was long venerated as a saint, the date of her commemoration is now lost."
Gundelind,f,,Germanic,,,,,,,,"Derived from Old High German gund ""war"" combined with Old High German lind or lindi ""soft, tender."""
Gundelinda,f,,"Spanish (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Spanish form of Gundelindis.
Gundelinde,f,,French|German|Swedish,,,,,,,,"French, German, and Swedish form of Gundelind."
Gundelindis,f,,Frankish (Latinized)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Gundelind. This was borne by a niece of Saint Odilia.
Gundeloh,m,,"German (Rare, Archaic)",GUWN-də-lo,,,,,,,"A dithematic name formed from Germanic gunda ""fight, battle"" and a second element loh that is less clear. It can be related to Celtic leuco-, louco- (Irish luach) ""bright, white"", or it can be a form of the element leih ""play"".The name Gundeloh is traditional in the von Guttenberg family."
Gundemar,m,,Germanic|History,,,,,,,,"Means ""famous war"", derived from Old High German gund ""war"" combined with Old High German mâri ""famous."" Gundemar I was a 7th-century king of the Visigoths in Hispania."
