Gwendolin,f,,German,,,,,,,,German form of Gwendoline.
Gwendolina,f,,English|Belgian|Breton|German (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Gwendolen.
Gwendoloena,f,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"Arthur’s queen in De Ortu Waluuanii, probably a variation of Guinevere.Unique to this story, she is given prophetic powers which allow her to predict Gawain’s arrival at court."
Gwendolyna,f,,English (Rare),gwen-də-LEEN-ə,,,,,,,Elaboration of Gwendolyn.
Gwendolyne,f,,"English (British, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Gwendolyn.
Gwendolynette,f,,"English (American, Rare)",,,,,,,,Combination of Gwendolyn with the suffix -ette.
Gwendolynn,f,,English (Rare),GWEN-də-lin,,,,,,,Variant of Gwendolyn.
Gwendolynne,f,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Gwendolyn.
Gwendora,f,,English,,,,,,,,"Possibly a blend of Gwendolen and Glendora. Very seldom encountered, it has been used from at least 1901, when a baby of this name was registered in England, one of several registered in the opening years of the 20th century. It has also been used in the United States."
Gwendy,f,,English,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Gwendolyn.
Gwendydd,f,,Welsh Mythology,,,,,,,,Variant of Gwenddydd.
Gwendylan,f,,Mexican,Gwen-dih-lahn,,,,,,,"""Starlit Bat"" — A modern Mexican feminine name coined in 2025. Created by combining elements that symbolize starlight (“Gwen”) and bat (“Dylan”), it represents a soul who thrives in darkness guided by celestial light. Gwendylan embodies balance, intuition, transformation, and guardianship — the bat soaring beneath the stars."
Gwendylin,f,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Gwendolen.
Gwenegan,m,,Breton (Rare),,,,,,,,"Derived from Breton gwenn ""white, fair, blessed"" and possibly Old Irish gal ""valour""."
Gweneira,f,,Welsh (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Gwyneira.
Gwenen,f,,Breton,,,,,,,,Variant of Gwennenn.
Gwener,f,,Welsh,GWEN-er,,,,,,,"This name is the Welsh form of Venus, referring to the Roman goddess of Love and Beauty.In Welsh, the term for the fifth day of the week is dydd gwener, ""Day of Venus""; whereas in English it is Friday, ""Day of Frigg"", Frigg being the Queen of the Norse gods."
Gwenetta,f,,English (Rare)|African American (Rare),,,,,,,,Elaborated form of Gwen.
Gwenette,f,,English (Rare),gwə-NET,,,,,,,"Elaborated form of Gwen, using the suffix -ette."
Gweneviere,f,,"English (American, Rare)",/ˈɡwɛn.ə.ˌvɪɹ/(American English),,,,,,,Variant of Gwenevere.
Gwenevieve,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",/ˈɡwɛn.ə.ˌviv/,,,,,,,Blend of Gwenevere and Genevieve.
Gwenez,f,,Breton,,,,,,,,Variant of Gwenna.
Gwenfair,f,,Welsh,[ˈɡwɛn.vair],,,,,,,"Derived from Welsh gwen ""fair; white; blessed"" combined with the name Mair (compare Mairwen)."
Gwenffrewi,f,,Medieval Welsh,[gwɛn.ˈfre.ui],,,,,,,Variant of Gwenfrewi.
Gwenffrwd,,,Welsh (Rare),,,,,,,,"From a Welsh place name meaning ""white stream""."
Gwenfron,f,,Welsh|Welsh,[ˈɡwɛn.vrɔn]|Gwenvron,,,,,,,"Derived from Welsh gwen ""white; fair; blessed"" and bron ""breast""."
Gwengad,m,,Welsh (Archaic),,,,,,,,"Old Welsh male name, from gwyn ""white, fair, blessed"" and cat ""battle""."
Gwenhivar,f,,Breton (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Gwenivar.
Gwenhwyfach,f,,Welsh Mythology,,,,,,,,"Meaning uncertain, perhaps from the name Gwenhwyfar combined with Welsh ach, a suffix which ""evokes unpleasantness"" (according to Patrick Sims-Williams). This was the name of Gwenhwyfar's sister, found in the Welsh triads and 'Culhwch and Olwen', who ""may represent an unpleasant or evil form of Gwenhwyfar herself;"" according to two triads, Gwenhwyfach instigated the battle of Camlann when she struck her sister. Their names have been falsely interpreted as Gwen-hwy-fawr ""Blessed the great"" and Gwen-hwy-fach ""Blessed the lesser""."
Gwenhwyvach,f,,Medieval Welsh,,,,,,,,Middle Welsh form of Gwenhwyfach.
Gwenia,f,,"Welsh (Rare, Archaic)|English (Rare)",,,,,,,,Elaboration of Gwen.
Gwenifer,f,,"Welsh|English (British, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Anglicized form of Gwenhwyfar (see Guinevere), particularly found in Wales and the Marches."
Gweniffer,f,,"English (Rare, Archaic)",/ˈɡwɛn.ɪ.fəɹ/,,,,,,,Variant of Gwenifer.
Gwenina,f,,English (Rare)|Breton (Rare)|Welsh (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Gwennina.
Gweninen,f,,Breton,,,,,,,,Variant of Gwenina.
Gwenisha,f,,"English (American, Rare)",,,,,,,,Combination of the name Gwen and the popular suffix isha.
Gwenishia,f,,Obscure (Rare),,,,,,,,Elaboration of Gwenisha.
Gwenivar,f,,Breton,,,,,,,,Breton form of Guinevere.
Gwenivere,f,,English (American),/ˈɡwɛn.ə.ˌvɪɹ/(American English),,,,,,,Variant of Guinevere.
Gwenlaouen,,,Breton (Rare),,,,,,,,Masculine and feminine variant of Gwellaouen.
Gwenlian,f,,English (Rare)|Welsh (Rare),GWEN-lee-ən(English),,,,,,,Anglicized form as well as a Welsh variant of Gwenllian.
Gwenlli,f,,Welsh (Rare),,,,,,,,Diminutive of Gwenllian.
Gwenlliana,f,,Medieval Welsh,,,,,,,,Medieval Latinization of Gwenllian.
Gwenllwyfo,f,,Medieval Welsh,,,,,,,,"From Welsh gwen (the feminine form of gwyn) meaning ""white, fair, blessed"" and llwyf meaning ""elm""."
Gwenlyn,f,,English,,,,,,,,Not available.
Gwenna,f,,Cornish|Breton,,,,,,,,Younger Cornish form of Wenna and Breton variant of Gwenn.
Gwennaëlle,f,,Breton (Gallicized),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Guénaël.
Gwennan,f,,Welsh|Breton,,,,,,,,"Younger form of Gwennant, itself derived from the Welsh elements gwen ""white, fair, blessed"" and nant ""stream"". This name was borne by a daughter of Brychan Brycheiniog."
Gwennant,f,,Welsh,[ˈgwɛ.nant],,,,,,,Older form of Gwennan.
Gwenndolyn,f,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",GWEHN-də-lin(American English),,,,,,,Variant of Gwendolyn.
Gwennenn,f,,Breton,,,,,,,,Variant of Gwenn and Gwenna.
Gwennia,f,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,Rare elaboration of Gwen.The variant Gwenia coincides with the name of town in Ghana.
Gwennie,f,,English,GWEN-ee,,,,,,,Diminutive of Gwen.
Gwennin,m,,Breton,,,,,,,,Masculine form of Gwenn.
Gwennina,f,,Breton,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Gwennin.
Gwenno,f,,Welsh,[ˈgwɛ.nɔ],,,,,,,"Diminutive of Gwenllian and other names beginning with Gwen, used independently since the 19th century. It coincides with the medieval Welsh name for the planet Venus (literally ""little white one"" or ""little bright one""). This is borne by Welsh musician Gwenno Saunders (1981-)."
Gwennol,f,,Cornish (Modern),GWEH-nohl,,,,,,,"Derivd from Cornish gwennel ""swallow (the bird)"". This is a modern Cornish name."
Gwenny,f,,English,/ˈɡwɛn.i/,,,,,,,Diminutive of Gwyneth.
Gwennyn,f,,Breton,,,,,,,,Variant of Gwennenn.
Gweno,f,,"Welsh (Rare, Archaic)",[ˈɡwɛ.nɔ],,,,,,,Variant of Gwenno.
Gwenog,f,,Welsh,,,,,,,,"Old Welsh diminutive of Gwen. This was the name of an obscure early Welsh saint. It was mentioned in J. K. Rowling's 'Harry Potter' series of books as the name of a witch, Gwenog Jones."
Gwenola,f,,Breton,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Gwenole.
Gwenole,m,,Breton,,,,,,,,Original Breton form of Guénolé.
Gwenonwy,f,,Welsh (Rare),,,,,,,,"Directly taken from Welsh gwenonwy ""lily of the valley"". In local folklore this was the name of King Arthur's sister; Maen Gwenonwy, a large rock off Porth Cadlan in Gwynedd, Wales, is named for her.As a given name, Gwenonwy has occasionally been found from the late 19th century onwards."
Gwenora,f,,Cornish,,,,,,,,A Cornish form of Guinevere.
Gwenore,f,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,Form of Guinevere used in Thomas Chestre's romance Sir Launfal and in Sir Gawain and the Green Knight.
Gwenou,m,,Breton (Rare),,,,,,,,"Derived from Breton gwenn ""white, fair, blessed"" and possibly Celtic gnou ""known""."
Gwent,m,,Welsh,,,,,,,,After the county in south Wales.
Gwenthlian,f,,Medieval Welsh,,,,,,,,Either a variant or a semi-Anglicization of Gwenllian.
Gwenvael,m,,Breton,[gwɛn.ˈvɛːl],,,,,,,"Combination of Breton gwen ""white; (and by extension) fair, blessed"" and Mael."
Gwenvaela,f,,Breton,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Gwenvael.
Gwenvred,f,,Medieval Breton,,,,,,,,Breton form of Winifred.
Gwenwledyr,f,,Welsh Mythology,,,,,,,,"The first element is Welsh gwen ""fair, white, blessed""; the second element, gwledyr, is uncertain. In the tale of Culhwch and Olwen (which appears in the Mabinogion, a collection of tales from Welsh myth), Gwenwledyr was a lady who lived at Arthur's court, the daughter of Gwawrddur the Hunchback and sister of three of Arthur's warriors: Duach, Brathach and Nerthach."
Gwenwynwyn,m,,Medieval Welsh,,,,,,,,"Famous bearer is Gwenwynwyn ab Owain Cyfeiliog, the last major ruler of mid Wales before the completion of the Norman English invasion."
Gwenydd,f,,Welsh,,,,,,,,"Means ""joy"" in Welsh. It has been used in Wales since the mid-19th century.K.M. Sheard notes, 'Although Gwenydd has been in use pretty much as long as Gwyneth, its use since the latter part of the 19th century has almost certainly been down to the resemblance. Similarly, Gwenith - gwenith ""wheat"" - is also used.'"
Gwenynen,f,,Obscure,,,,,,,,"Directly taken from Welsh gwenynen ""bee"", this name was adopted by Augusta Hall, Baroness Llanover, a Welsh heiress, best known as a patron of the Welsh arts, as her bardic name (Gwenynen Gwent ""the bee of Gwent"")."
Gwereg,m,,Medieval Breton,,,,,,,,Not available.
Gwerful,f,,Medieval Welsh,,,,,,,,Form of Gweirful. This was the name of two Welsh poets in the 15th century.
Gwerfyl,f,,Medieval Welsh|Welsh (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Gweirful.
Gwern,m,,Welsh Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Welsh gwern ""alder tree"". Gwern is a minor figure in Welsh tradition. He is the son of Matholwch, king of Ireland, and Branwen, sister to the king of Britain. He appears in the tale of Branwen, daughter of Llŷr, in which his murder at the hands of his sadistic uncle Efnysien sparks a mutually destructive battle between Britain and Ireland."
Gwernfyl,f,,Welsh,GWERN-vil,,,,,,,"Means ""alder tree"" in Welsh."
Gwerydd,f,,Medieval Welsh,,,,,,,,Not available.
Gweth,,,Luo (Modern),GWEHT /gwɛt̪ /,,,,,,,"""blessings"""
Gwezheneg,m,,Breton (Rare),,,,,,,,"Derived from Breton gwezhen ""combat"". This was the name of the son of Saint Gwenn and Saint Fragan. He is also known by the name Kavan."
Gwgon,m,,Medieval Welsh,[ˈɡuː.ɡɔn],,,,,,,Gwgon ap Meurig was a 9th century king of Ceredigion and Ystrad Tywi in southwest Wales.
Gwhd,f,,Western African|Anaang,,,,,,,,Means “disgraceful; unworthy being” in Anaang.
Gwiawn,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,Not available.
Gwid,m,,Pictish,,,,,,,,Name of a 7th century Pictish warband leader.
Gwidka,f,,Kashubian,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Gwidona.
Gwido,m,,Polish|Kashubian,,,,,,,,Variant of Gwidon.
Gwidon,m,,Polish,GVEE-dawn,,,,,,,A Polish form of Guido.
Gwidona,f,,Polish|Kashubian,,,,,,,,Polish and Kashubian feminine form Gwido as well as a Polish feminine form of Gwidon.
Gwijde,m,,Dutch (Rare),KHVAY-də|KHVIE-də,,,,,,,Dutch form of Guy 1.
Gwili,m,,Welsh,,,,,,,,After the name of a river in Carmarthenshire.
Gwindor,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Gwindor was an Elf of Nargothrond in the First Age. He was the son of Guilin and a Prince of Nargothrond.Gwindor consists of the Ñoldorin word gwind (""pale blue""), or in Quenya, vinya (""young""). Dor has several meanings (""high, lofty, noble, king, or master"")."
Gwinian,m,,Breton,,,,,,,,Not available.
Gwinore,f,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,A variant of Guinevere
Gwinyai,m,,Shona,,,,,,,,"Gwinyai means ""be strong"". The Zimbabwean tennis player Gwinyai Tongoona is a famous bearer of this name."
Gwiomarc'h,m,,Medieval Breton,,,,,,,,Variant of Gwivarc'h.
Gwion,m,,Welsh Mythology,,,,,,,,"Possibly related to the Welsh element gwyn meaning ""fair, blessed"". This was the original name of Taliesin, a legendary bard, before he was cast into the ""cauldron of knowledge"", after which he became Taliesin, bard and seer."
Gwir,f,,Medieval Welsh,,,,,,,,Not available.
Gwivarc'h,m,,Medieval Breton,,,,,,,,"Derived from Breton gwiv ""lively, cheerful"" and marc'h ""horse""."
Gwi-yeong,f,"귀영(Korean Hangul)|貴永, 鬼永, 貴靈, 鬼靈, 貴榮, 鬼榮, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/kɥi.jʌ̹ŋ/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 貴 (gwi) meaning ""precious"" or 鬼 (gwi) meaning ""spirit, ghost"" combined with 永 (yeong) meaning ""eternal, forever"" or 靈 (yeong) meaning ""soul, spirit, deity"" or 榮 (yeong) meaning ""glory, honour, prosper"". Other hanja combinations can form this name as well."
Gwladwen,f,,Welsh (Rare),,,,,,,,Not available.
Gwledyr,f,,Medieval Welsh,,,,,,,,Not available.
Gwlithyn,f,,Welsh,[ˈgwliˑ.θɪn],,,,,,,"Derived from Welsh gwlith ""dew, dew-drop""."
Gwøni,f,,Faroese,,,,,,,,Faroese variant of Gvøðni.
Gwrgenau,m,,Medieval Welsh,,,,,,,,"From Welsh gwor- ""over"" (intensifying prefix) and cenau ""cub, whelp""."
Gwrhyd,m,,Old Welsh,,,,,,,,"Means ""valour"", or alternately ""fathom, six feet; the length of a man's outstretched arms""."
Gwri,m,,Welsh Mythology,,,,,,,,"Probably derived from Proto-Celtic *wiro- ""man"" (the source of modern Welsh gŵr ""man, husband""). In the 'Mabinogion', this was the name given by Teyrnon to the infant Pryderi."
Gwriad,m,,Medieval Welsh,[ˈɡuːr.jad],,,,,,,"Potentially meaning ""man"" or ""husband"", from gwr, derived from Old Welsh gur, from Proto-Brythonic *gwur, from Proto-Celtic *wiros, from Proto-Indo-European *wiHrós.The second element may be made up of the iad meaning ""crown of the head"" or ""top, summit"", or the suffix -iad, which shows the action of a verb or its result.Gwriad ab Elidyr was an 8th-century Welsh figure who is thought to have been king of the Isle of Man in the late 8th century."
Gwril,m,,Welsh,,,,,,,,"Welsh name, that some translated as ""lordly,"" ""heroic act,"" or ""combating"""
Gwrwst,m,,Welsh,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Proto-Celtic *wiros meaning “man” and *gustus meaning “excellence, force”."
Gwyar,m,,Welsh Mythology,/ˈɡʊɨ̯.ar/,,,,,,,"Means ""gore, blood"" in Welsh. In Welsh legend Gwyar was the father of Arthur's warriors Gwalchmei and Gwalhafed. He appears in Culhwch and Olwen (late 11th century), Breudwyt Rhonabwy (The Dream of Rhonabwy; 13th century) and Geraint and Enid (13th century)."
Gwyda,f,,English,GWI-də,,,,,,,"Meaning and origin uncertain. A famous bearer was Gwyda DonHowe, an American stage and screen actress."
Gwyddno,m,,Welsh Mythology,,,,,,,,"Possibly from Welsh gwydd ""face, appearance; presence"" and -no ""knowing, knowledge"".This was the name of a legendary king, who supposedly ruled a sunken land off the coast of Wales."
Gwydyr,m,,Welsh,,,,,,,,"Welsh name meaning ""wrathful"""
Gwyl,f,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"One of Arthur’s three mistresses, according to the Welsh Triad 57.She was also the daughter of Gendawd."
Gwylan,f,,Welsh (Rare),,,,,,,,"Directly taken from Welsh gwylan ""seagull"". This name has been used from the early 20th century onwards."
Gwyllim,m,,Welsh,,,,,,,,Variant spelling of Gwilym
Gwyllyn,m,,"English (Canadian, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Perhaps a variant of Gwillym influenced by Glyn. This was the birth name of Glenn Ford (1916-2006), a Canadian-born American actor."
Gwylog,m,,Old Welsh,[ˈɡʊ̯i.lɔɡ],,,,,,,"Unknown etymology.Gwylog ap Beli was one of the rulers of the Kingdom of Powys during the late 7th to early 8th century, son of Beli ap Eiludd. He is recorded on the Pillar of Eliseg as Guoillauc."
Gwylon,m,,Welsh,,,,,,,,Not available.
Gwylym,m,,Welsh (Rare),/ˈɡwɪ.lɪm/,,,,,,,Variant of Gwilym.
Gwynach,f,,Welsh,,,,,,,,Welsh name meaning felicity
Gwynant,m,,Welsh,Gwy-nant,,,,,,,"It comes from the name of a Valley in Wales, Nant Gwynant, in Snowdonia; the name, composed by gwyn and nant. Means ""white valley""."
Gwyndaf,m,,Welsh,Gwyn-dav,,,,,,,Celtic saint name.
Gwyndolen,f,,English (Rare),GWIN-də-lin,,,,,,,Variant of Gwendolen.
Gwyndolyn,f,,English (American),,,,,,,,Variant of Gwendolen.
Gwyndon,m,,"English (American, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Not available.
Gwyne,f,,American (Rare),,,,,,,,Possibly a variant of Gwen or a variant of Gwynne.
Gwynette,f,,Filipino,,,,,,,,Not available.
Gwynevere,f,,"English (American, Rare)",/ˈɡwɪn.ə.ˌvɪɹ/(American English),,,,,,,Variant of Guinevere. This spelling was used for a character in the popular fantasy video game Dark Souls (2011).
Gwynfa,f,,Welsh (Rare),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Gwynfor.
Gwynfryn,m,,Welsh,,,,,,,,"From the name of the village Gwynfryn in Wrexham - the name of which derives from the Welsh name elements bryn - meaning ""hill"", and gwyn, meaning ""white"", thus meaning ""white hill"". Earliest known usage as a given name dates to the late 19th century - reached peak popularity in the 1910s."
Gwynhoiarn,m,,Medieval Breton,,,,,,,,Not available.
Gwynhwyfar,f,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Gwynhwyfar is an alternate spelling of Gwenhwyfar, used by the author Gillian Bradshaw in her HAWK OF MAY series.Jennifer is based on this name."
Gwynlais,m,,Welsh,Gwyn-lais,,,,,,,"From the name of the river in Glamorgan. The river name is composed of ""gwyn"", meaning ""white"" and ""lais"", meaning ""river"", so the meaning is ""white river""."
Gwynllyw,m,,Old Welsh|History|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Derived from Welsh gwyn ""white"" and llyw ""leader"". This was the name of a Welsh king, also known as Woolos in English. It was also borne by Saint Gwynllyw (sometimes called Woollos or Gundleus), husband of Saint Gladys and father of Saint Cadoc, a hermit of Wales. He and Gladys were reportedly bandits in Kind Arthur’s time, but they repented and became eremites."
Gwynna,f,,"English (American, Rare)",,,,,,,,Not available.
Gwynne,f,,English,GWIN,,,,,,,"Feminine variant of Gwyn. The surname of English actress and royal mistress Nell Gwyn (1650-1687) is variously spelled Gwynne, Gwynn and Gwyn."
Gwynneth,f,,Welsh,,,,,,,,Variant of Gwyneth.
Gwynnever,f,,Arthurian Cycle|Cornish,,,,,,,,Cornish form of Guinevere.
Gwynno,m,,Welsh,,,,,,,,"Name of a Celtic Christian saint, apparently from Gwynn- (first part of compound names beginning with Welsh gwyn ""white, fair, holy"", e.g. Gwynoro, Gwynlliw) + diminutive suffix -o (cf. Gwenno). Currently used as a masculine given name (a revival of the saint's name)."
Gwynonfryn,m,,Old Welsh,,,,,,,,Not available.
Gwynoro,m,,Welsh (Rare),[gwə.ˈnoˑ.rɔ]|[gwɪ.ˈnoˑ.rɔ],,,,,,,"Derived from Welsh gwyn meaning ""white, fair, blessed"" combined with an uncertain second element, possibly gawr ""shout"" or gorŵydd ""steed"" or gwared ""deliverance, relief"". This was the name of an early Welsh saint."
Gwynplaine,m,,Popular Culture (Rare),,,,,,,,The main character in Victor Hugo's The Man Who Laughs and inspiration for The Joker in Batman.
Gwynyth,f,,English (Rare),/ˈɡwɪn.ɪθ/,,,,,,,Variant of Gwyneth.A notable bearer of this name is Canadian actress Gwynyth Walsh (1956-).
Gwythr,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,One of Arthur’s warriors in Culhwch and Olwen. He was the son of Greidawl Galldonyd.
Gy,,,Obscure,,,,,,,,Not available.
Gya,f,,Swedish (Archaic),,,,,,,,Dialectal form of Gyda and Gyrid found in the late 1600s and throughout the 1700s in Scania and Blekinge.
