Gylfi,m,,Icelandic|Old Norse|Norse Mythology,[ˈcɪl.vɪ](Icelandic)|[ˈɡyl.ve](Old Norse),,,,,,,"Modern form of GylfR, an Old Norse name possibly derived from gjálfr ""roar (of waves), surf, ocean surge"", which is related to gylfr ""sea"" and perhaps gylfinn ""monster, werewolf"". Alternatively, it could mean ""grain cultivator"", from gólf ""floor (on which grain is turned)"". In Norse mythology, Gylfi was the name of a sea giant. It was also the name of a mythical Swedish king."
