Harpalion,m,Ἁρπαλίων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from either Ancient Greek ἁρπαλέος (harpaleos), which can mean ""attractive, alluring, charming"" or ""devouring, consuming, grasping, greedy"", or from ἁρπαλίζω (harpalizo) meaning ""to catch up, to be eager to receive"". Harpalion is the name of several characters in Greek mythology."
Harpalyce,f,Ἁρπαλύκη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Harpalyke. This is the name of several minor characters in Greek mythology, including a daughter of Harpalykos and a daughter of Clymenus."
Harpalycus,m,Ἁρπάλυκος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Roman Mythology,/har.ˈpa.ly.kos/(Greek Mythology)|/hɑr.ˈpa.lʏ.kus/(Roman Mythology),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἁρπάζω (harpázō) meaning ""to seize, snatch, carry off"" or ἅρπαξ (harpax) meaning ""rapacious, ravening; thief, robber"" combined with λύκος (lykos) meaning ""wolf"". This was the name of several figures in Greek and Roman mythology, including a Thracian king (the father of Harpalyce), one of the fifty sons of Lycaon (who in some accounts were transformed into wolves), and the fencing instructor of Heracles."
Harpalyke,f,Ἁρπαλύκη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/hɑːr.ˈpæ.lə.kiː/(English),,,,,,,"Feminine form of Ἁρπάλυκος (Harpalykos) (see Harpalycus). This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including a daughter of King Clymenus who in some accounts was a lover of Zeus. One of the moons of Jupiter"
