Hatorangan,m,,Batak,,,,,,,,"Means ""information"" in Toba Batak."
Hatori,m,はとり(Japanese Hiragana)|ハトリ(Japanese Katakana),Popular Culture,ha-to-ṙee(Popular Culture)|HA-TO-LEE(Japanese),,,,,,,"Technically, this name doesn't mean anything since it is written in kana.One fictional bearer of this name is Hatori Sōma (草摩 はとり), a character from the popular manga and anime series, Fruits Basket. In this case, the creator, Natsuki Takaya derived his name from the fourth month of the Japanese calendar (konohatorizuki/konohatoridzuki (木葉採月/このはとりづき), which is a rare form of the word for the fourth month, which would usually be uzuki/udzuki (卯月/うづき)). The derivation means ""month of taking leaves of trees.""While it's not used as an actual name in Japan, it is used as a surname."
Hatsadi,,ຫັດສະ(Lao),Lao,,,,,,,,"Means ""laugh, laughter"" in Lao."
Hatsu,f,"初, 波津(Japanese Kanji)|はつ(Japanese Hiragana)|ハツ(Japanese Katakana)",Japanese,HAHTS|HAH-TSUU,,,,,,,"This name can be used as 初 (sho, ui-, -so.meru, -zo.me, haji.me, haji.mete, hatsu, hatsu-) meaning ""beginning, first time"" or 波津 with 波 (ha, nami) meaning ""billows, Poland, waves"" and 津 (shin, tsu) meaning ""ferry, harbour, haven, port.""When used as 初, it is, according to Suzuki Sakaye, usually used as the name of the first daughter.Hatsu was very popular in the Edo period (1603-1868), but by the latter part of the Meiji period (1868-1912) it was dropping down in popularity and by the Taishō period/era (1912-1926), it became uncommon."
Hatsuaki,m,"初秋, 初明, 肇暉, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HAH-TSOO-AH-KEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 初 (hatsu) meaning ""beginning, start, first"" combined with 秋 (aki) meaning ""autumn"". Other combinations of kanji characters can also form this name."
Hatsuharu,,初春(Japanese Kanji)|はつはる(Japanese Hiragana)|ハッスハル(Japanese Katakana),Japanese,Ha-tsu-ha-ru,,,,,,,"Hatsuharu is a japanese name used for both boys and girls. It is uncommon, along with its meaning: “First spring day” “First spring” “Start of spring” “First spring month” “Early spring”"
Hatsuhide,m,"廿秀, 肇秀, 発秀, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HAH-TSOO-KHEE-DEH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 廿 (hatsu) meaning ""twenty"", 肇 (hatsu) meaning ""begin, start"", or 発 (hatsu) meaning ""set off, depart, emit, discharge"" combined with 秀 (hide) meaning ""excellent, outstanding"". Other kanji combinations are usable."
Hatsuhiko,m,"初彦, 発彦, 廿彦, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HAH-TSOO-KHEE-TO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 廿 (hatsu) meaning ""twenty"", 肇 (hatsu) meaning ""begin, start"", or 発 (hatsu) meaning ""set off, depart, emit, discharge"" combined with 彦 (hiko) meaning ""boy, prince"". Other kanji combinations are usable."
Hatsuhito,m,"初人, 初仁, 初一, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HAH-TSOO-KHEE-TO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 初 (hatsu) meaning ""first, original, beginning"" combined with 人 (hito) meaning ""person"". Other kanji combinations are usable."
Hatsuho,f,"はつほ(Japanese Hiragana)|初穂, 一歩, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HAH-TSOO-HO,,,,,,,"From 初 (hatsu) meaning ""first, beginning, new"" and 穂 (ho) meaning ""grain"". Other kanji combinations can be used.A hatsuho (初穂) is a ripe rice ear that Shinto, or God, receives before the yearly autumn harvest for rice."
Hatsuji,m,"初児, 初二, 初司, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HAH-TSOO-ZHEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 初 (hatsu) meaning ""first, original, beginning"" combined with 児 (ji) meaning ""child, kid, teenager, young person"". Other kanji combinations are usable."
Hatsujiro,m,"初二郎, 初司郎, 初児郎, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HAH-TSOO-ZHEE-RO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 初 (hatsu) meaning ""first, original, beginning"" combined with 児 (ji) meaning ""child, kid, teenager, young person"" and 郎 (rō) meaning ""son"". Other kanji combinations are usable."
Hatsujirou,m,,Japanese,,,,,,,,Variant transcription of Hatsujiro.
Hatsuka,f,"初夏, 初佳, 初花, 廿河, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HAH-TSUU-KAH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 初 (hatsu) meaning ""beginning, start, first"" combined with 夏 (ka) meaning ""summer"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hatsukazu,m,"初一, 発一, 廿一, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese (Rare),HAH-TSOO-KAH-ZOO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 初 (hatsu) meaning ""first, original, beginning"" combined with 一 (kazu) meaning ""one"". Other kanji combinations are usable."
Hatsuki,,,Japanese,,,,,,,,Variant of Hadzuki.
Hatsuko,f,"はつこ(Japanese Hiragana)|ハツコ(Japanese Katakana)|初子, 葉津子, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HAH-TSOO-KO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 初 (hatsu) meaning ""first, new, beginning"" or 葉 (ha) meaning ""leaf"", 津 (tsu) meaning ""haven, harbour, port"" combined with 子 (ko) meaning ""child, first sign of the Chinese zodiac, the sign of the rat"", as well as other kanji combinations."
Hatsume,f,"極芽, 初芽, 初女, 初萌, 波津女, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HAH-TSUU-ME,,,,,,,"From Japanese 極 (hatsu) meaning ""very, extremely"" combined with 芽 (me) meaning ""bud, sprout, shoot"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hatsumi,f,"ハツミ(Japanese Katakana)|初美, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HAH-TSOO-MEE,,,,,,,"From 初 (hatsu) meaning ""first, new"" and 美 (mi) meaning ""beautiful, beauty"". Other kanji combinations can spell this name."
Hatsumomo,f,初桃(Japanese Kanji)|はつもも(Japanese Hiragana)|ハツモモ(Japanese Katakana),Literature|Popular Culture,[hä.t͡sɨᵝ.mo̞.mo̞](Japanese),,,,,,,"From Japanese 初 (hatsu) meaning ""first, new"" combined with 桃 (momo) meaning ""peach,"" as borne by the fictional character Hatsumomo in the 1997 book 'Memoirs of a Geisha' by Arthur Golden and its 2005 movie adaptation."
Hatsuna,f,"はつな(Japanese Hiragana)|羽津菜, 初奈, 八生, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HAH-TSOO-NAH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 羽 (ha) meaning ""feathers"", 津 (tsu), a place name, such as Tsu city in Mie prefecture combined with 菜 (na) meaning ""vegetables, greens"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hatsune,f,"はつね(Japanese Hiragana)|初音, 肇音, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HAH-TSOO-NEH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 初 (hatsu) meaning ""first, new"" or 肇 (hatsu) meaning ""begin, start"" combined with 音 (ne) meaning ""sound"". Other kanji combinations can be used."
Hatsuno,f,"はつ乃(Kanji/Hiragana)|はつの(Japanese Hiragana)|ハツノ(Japanese Katakana)|初乃, 服埜, 萩角, 発乃, 葉都野, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese (Rare),HA-TSOO-NO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 初 (hatsu) meaning ""beginning, start, first"" combined with 乃 (no), a possessive particle. Other combinations of kanji characters can also form this name."
