Hegesippos,m,Ἡγήσιππος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from the Greek noun ἥγησις (hegesis) meaning ""command"". This word is ultimately derived from the Greek verb ἡγέομαι (hegeomai) meaning ""to go before, to lead (the way)"" as well as ""to guide"", which is closely related to the Greek verb ἄγω (ago) meaning ""to lead, to guide"" as well as ""to bring, to carry"". The second element of this name is derived from the Greek noun ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse""."
Hegesippus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Hegesippos. This name was borne by a Greek statesman and orator from the 4th century BC.
Hegesipyle,f,Ἡγησιπύλη(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/hɛː.ɡɛː.si.pý.lɛː/(Attic Greek)|/i.ʝi.si.ˈpy.li/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from the Ancient Greek noun ἥγησις (hegesis) meaning ""command"", from ἡγέομαι (hegeomai) meaning ""to go before, to lead (the way), to guide"", and πύλη (pyle) meaning ""gate, entrance"".This was the name of a Thracian princess who married the Athenian statesman and general Miltiades around 515-513 BC."
Hegesistratos,m,Ἡγησίστρατος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from the Greek noun ἥγησις (hegesis) meaning ""command"". This word is ultimately derived from the Greek verb ἡγέομαι (hegeomai) meaning ""to go before, to lead (the way)"" as well as ""to guide"", which is closely related to the Greek verb ἄγω (ago) meaning ""to lead, to guide"" as well as ""to bring, to carry"". The second element of this name is derived from the Greek noun στρατός (stratos) meaning ""army""."
Hegesistratus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Hegesistratos. This name was borne by a Greek diviner from the 5th century BC.
Hegestratos,m,Ἡγέστρατος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from the Greek verb ἡγέομαι (hegeomai) meaning ""to go before, to lead (the way)"" as well as ""to guide"", which is closely related to the Greek verb ἄγω (ago) meaning ""to lead, to guide"" as well as ""to bring, to carry"". The second element of this name is derived from the Greek noun στρατός (stratos) meaning ""army""."
Hegestratus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Hegestratos. This name was borne by an eponymous archon of Athens, who lived in the 6th century BC."
Hegetor,m,Ἡγήτωρ(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""leader, guide, commander"" in Ancient Greek, derived from ἡγέομαι (hegeomai) meaning ""to go before, precede, lead the way; to command, be a leader""."
Hegetoria,f,Ἡγητορία(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""bundle of dried figs used in a procession"" (specifically during the Plynteria festival), derived from ἡγητήρ (hegeter) meaning ""guide, leader, commander"". This was the name of a nymph of Rhodes in Greek mythology, wife of Ochimus and mother of Cydippe (named later as Cyrbia)."
