Henuttaneb,f,,Ancient Egyptian,,,,,,,,"From Egyptian ḥnwt-tꜢ-nb meaning ""lady of all lands"", derived from ḥnwt meaning ""lady, mistress"", tꜢ meaning ""land, realm"" and nb meaning ""all, every"". This name was borne by several Egyptian royals, including a daughter of Amenhotep III and one of Rameses II."
Henuttawy,f,"𓎛𓎺𓏏𓇿𓇿𓁐, 𓎺𓏏𓇿𓇾, 𓎺𓏏𓇾𓇾(Hieroglyphs)",Ancient Egyptian,,,,,,,,"From Egyptian ḥnwt-tꜣwj meaning ""mistress of Egypt"", or more literally ""lady of the two lands"", derived from ḥnwt ""lady, mistress"" combined with the dual form of tꜣ ""land, realm, country"", referring to the single entity of Upper and Lower Egypt. This was used as a royal title as well as a given name."
