Heywood,m,,English,HAY-wuwd,,,,,,,"The most common interpretation of the name is that it comes from the Anglo-Saxon haga (hedge), with Heywood meaning ""the wood surrounded by a hedge"", or, more likely, an enclosure within a wood. In Old English the word hæge meant ""hedge, fence or boundary""."
