Hiichirou,m,"飛一郎, 陽一郎, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HEE-EE-CHEE-ṘO:,,,,,,,"From Japanese 飛 (hi) meaning ""to fly"" or 陽 (hi) meaning ""light, sun, male"", 一 (ichi) meaning ""one"" combined with 郎 (rou) meaning ""son"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hiie,f,,Estonian (Rare),,,,,,,,"Allegedly derived from Estonian hiie, the genitive singular / attributive form of hiis “sacred grove”."
Hiʻilani,,,Hawaiian,/hi.ʔi.ˈla.ni/,,,,,,,"Means ""held in the arms of heaven,"" from hiʻi ""bear, hold/carry in the arms"" and lani meaning ""sky, heaven, heavenly, spiritual, royal, exalted, noble, aristocratic."""
Hiʻilei,f,,"Hawaiian (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Means ""child carried in the arms"" from Hawaiian hiʻi ""carry in the arms"" and lei ""wreath"" (by extension ""child"", carried on the shoulders like a lei)."
Hiina,f,"ひいな(Japanese Hiragana)|ひい菜(Kanji/Hiragana)|雛, 一夏, 秀奈, 雛菜, 日衣菜, 緋奈, 陽衣菜, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-NAH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 雛 (hiina) meaning ""young bird; chick​, hina doll; doll displayed during the Girls' Festival"". Other kanji or kanji combinations can form this name as well."
Hiiragi,,"柊, 柊希(Japanese Kanji)|ひいらぎ(Japanese Hiragana)|ヒイラギ(Japanese Katakana)",Japanese (Modern)|Popular Culture,[çíì.ɾà.ɡʲì](Japanese),,,,,,,"From 柊 (hiiragi), referring to the flowering plant known variously as 'holly osmanthus', 'holly olive', or 'false holly', derived from a combination of stem hiira, something that is throbbing or aching (in reference to its thorny and prickly leaves), and 木 (ki) meaning ""tree.""A ki kanji can be suffixed to 柊, e.g. 希 meaning ""rare.""One fictional (male) bearer of this name is Hiiragi Nono (野々 柊) from the 2016 anime 'BBK/BRNK'."
Hiiro,,"ひいろ(Japanese Hiragana)|一彩, 光彩, 灯彩, 日彩, 陽彩, 柊彩, 暉彩, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-EE-RO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 一 (hi) meaning ""one"", 光 (hi) meaning ""light"", 灯 (hi) meaning ""a lit flame such as a candle or torch"", 日 (hi) meaning ""day, sun, Japan"", 陽 (hi) meaning ""light, sun, male"", 柊 (hi) meaning ""holly olive, Osmanthus heterophyllus"" or 暉 (hi) meaning ""sunshine"" combined with 彩 (iro) meaning ""colour"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hiirou,m,"飛偉楼, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HEE-ṘO:,,,,,,,"From Japanese 飛 (hi) meaning ""to fly"", 偉 (i) meaning ""admirable"" combined with 楼 (rou) meaning ""floor, level"". Other kanji combinations are possible.Usage of this name is, most likely, influenced by the word hero."
