Hipparchia,f,Ἱππαρχία(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Hipparchos. This was the name of a 4th-century BC Cynic philosopher.
Hipparchos,m,Ἵππαρχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/híp.par.kʰos/(Attic Greek)|/ˈip.par.xos/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)|/ˈi.par.xos/(Constantinopolitan Greek)|/hɪ.ˈpɑːr.kəs/(English)",,,,,,,"Means ""master of horses, cavalry commander"" in Greek, ultimately derived from ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" and αρχος (archos) meaning ""master, leader, ruler""."
Hipparchus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Hipparchos. This name was borne by a Greek astronomer from the 2nd century BC.
Hipparete,f,Ἱππαρέτη(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" and ἀρετή (arete) meaning ""goodness, virtue"". This was the name of the wife of Alcibiades. In Greek mythology it was borne by one of the Danaids (i.e. the 50 daughters of Danaus)."
Hipparkh,m,Гіппарх(Ukrainian),Ukrainian,,,,,,,,Ukrainian form of Hipparchos.
Hipparque,m,,History (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Hipparchus.
Hippe,m,,West Frisian|East Frisian,HIP-pə,,,,,,,Variant of Hibbe.
Hippea,f,Ἱππεία(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""riding of horses, horsemanship, cavalry"" in Ancient Greek, derived from ἵππος (hippos) ""horse""."
Hippeus,m,Ἱππεύς(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""horseman, rider, charioteer; knights"" in Ancient Greek, derived from ἵππος (hippos) ""horse"". This was the name of one of the fifty (or fifty-one) grandsons of Thespius fathered by Heracles in Greek mythology, his mother being Procris and his twin brother Antileon."
Hippia,f,,Hungarian,,,,,,,,"Coined by Imre Madách for a character in his play 'The Tragedy of Man' ('Az ember tragédiája' in Hungarian, 1861), which is considered to be one of the major works of Hungarian literature. He likely based the name on Hippolita."
Hippias,m,Ἱππίας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/hip.pí.aːs/(Classical Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" combined with the masculine name suffix -ίας (-ias). This was the name of the last tyrant of Athens, ruling during the 5th century BCE. This was also the name of a 5th-century BCE Greek sophist, a contemporary of Socrates whom Plato characterizes as vain and arrogant in his dialogues Hippias Minor and Hippias Major."
Hippo,f,Ἱππώ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of (hippos) meaning ""horse"" in Ancient Greek. This was the name of an Oceanid in Greek mythology, as well as of one of the fifty daughters of King Thespius, mother of Capylus by Heracles."
Hippocleides,m,,Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,Alternate Latinization of Hippokleides.
Hippocles,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Hippokles.
Hippoclides,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Hippokleides. This name was borne by an eponymous archon of Athens, who lived in the 6th century BC."
Hippocoön,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Hippokoon. This is the name of several characters from Greek mythology, one of which is a king of Sparta."
Hippocrate,,Ἱπποκράτη(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Gallicized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"French form of Hippocrates (masculine) and Latinized form of Hippokrate (feminine), the latter of which was an ancient Greek feminine form of Hippokrates."
Hippocratidas,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|History,,,,,,,,Latinized form of Hippokratidas. This was the name of a Eurypontid king of Sparta from the 7th century BC.
Hippodamie,f,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized)|Theatre,,,,,,,,French form of Hippodamia.
Hippodamos,m,Ἱππόδαμος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/hip.pó.da.mos/(Classical Greek),,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Greek ἱππόδαμος (hippodamos) meaning ""tamer of horses"", itself from Greek ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" and δαμάζειν (damazein) ""to tame"". This was an epithet of individual heroes, and of the Trojans.Alternatively, the second element may be derived from Doric Greek δᾶμος (damos) ""the people"", a variant of δῆμος (demos)."
Hippodamus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Hippodamos.
Hippodice,f,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Hippodike. In Greek mythology, Hippodice was a daughter of Danaus."
Hippodike,f,Ἱπποδίκη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" combined with δίκη (dike) meaning ""justice, judgement"" as well as ""custom, usage"". This was the name of a Danaid in Greek mythology, one of the fifty daughters of King Danaus, who murdered her husband Idas on their wedding night."
Hippokleides,m,Ἱπποκλείδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""son of Hippokles"" in Greek, derived from the name Hippokles combined with the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides)."
Hippokles,m,Ἱπποκλῆς(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,"/hip.po.klɛ̂ːs/(Attic Greek)|/ip.po.ˈklis/(Koine Greek, Byzantine Greek)",,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" and κλέος (kleos) meaning ""glory""."
Hippokoon,m,Ἱπποκόων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" combined with κοῶ (koo), which is a contracted form of the Greek verb κοέω (koeo) meaning ""to know, to be aware"" as well as ""to mark, to perceive, to hear"". As such, the meaning of this name must be something along the lines of ""alert horse"" or ""perceptive horse""."
Hippokratidas,m,Ἱπποκρατίδας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Aeolic and Doric Greek form of Hippokratides, because it contains ίδας (idas), which is the Aeolic and Doric Greek form of the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides).This name was notably borne by a Eurypontid king of Sparta from the 7th century BC. He is usually known under the latinized form of his name, which is Hippocratidas."
Hippokratides,m,Ἱπποκρατίδης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Means ""son of Hippokrates"" in Greek, derived from the name Hippokrates combined with the patronymic suffix ἴδης (ides)."
Hippola,f,,"Greek (Archaic, ?)",,,,,,,,"A derivative of Ancient Greek ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse""."
Hippoliet,m,,Dutch (Archaic)|Flemish (Archaic),,,,,,,,Dutch form of Hippolytos via its French form Hippolyte 2. Known bearers of this name include the Belgian physician and playwright Hippoliet Van Peene (1811-1864) and the Belgian writer and poet Hippoliet Ledeganck (1846-1903).
Hippolit,m,,Hungarian,,,,,,,,Hungarian form of Hippolytos.
Hippolita,f,,"English (Rare, Archaic)|French (Rare)|Hungarian",,,,,,,,Variant of Hippolyta.
Hippolochos,m,Ἱππόλοχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" combined with the Greek noun λόχος (lochos) meaning ""ambush"", a word that later came to signify a tactical sub unit of the ancient Greek army. The word is ultimately derived from the Greek verb λοχάω (lochao) meaning ""to lie in wait for, to waylay"" as well as ""to snare""."
Hippolochus,m,,Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Hippolochos. This name was borne by a Macedonian writer from the early 3rd century BC.In Greek mythology, Hippolochus was the name of a son of Bellerophon and Philonoe."
Hippolüté,f,,Hungarian (Rare),,,,,,,,Hungarian form of Hippolyte 1.
Hippolütosz,m,,Hungarian (Rare),,,,,,,,Hungarian form of Hippolytus.
Hippolyt,m,,"German (Archaic)|German (Swiss, Rare)|Alsatian",/hɪ.ˈpo.lyt/(German),,,,,,,"German form of Hippolytos. A notable bearer is Hippolyt Kempf (born 1965), a Swiss skier and Olympic medalist."
Hippolythe,m,,French (Archaic)|French (Quebec),,,,,,,,Variant of Hippolyte 2.
Hippomachos,m,"Ἱππόμαχος, Ἱππομάχος(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek adjective ἱππόμαχος (hippomachos) meaning ""fighting on horseback"", which consists of the Greek noun ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" combined with the Greek noun μάχη (mache) meaning ""battle"".This was the name of an Olympic victor from the 4th century BC."
Hippomachus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Hippomachos. This was the name of one of the Thirty Tyrants of Athens (5th century BC).In Greek mythology, Hippomachus is one of the suitors of Penelope."
Hippomedon,m,Ἱππομέδων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,/hip.po.mé.dɔːn/(Attic Greek)|/ip.po.ˈme.ðon/(Koine Greek)|/hɪ.ˈpɒ.mɪ.dən/(English),,,,,,,"Means ""horse-ruler, ruler of horses, king of horsemen"", derived from Ancient Greek ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" combined with μέδων (medon) meaning ""ruler"" (see Medon).A notable bearer of this name was the Greek politician Hippomedon of Sparta (3rd century BC)."
Hippomedusa,f,Ἱππομέδουσα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Ἱππομέδουσα (Hippomedousa), the feminine form of Hippomedon. According to Apollodorus, she was the wife of Alcmenor."
Hippomenes,m,Ἱππομένης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,/hɪ.ˈpɒ.mɪ.niːz/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" combined with either μένος (menos) meaning ""power, strength, spirit"" or μένω (meno) meaning ""to stay, wait, remain, last"". This name was borne by an eponymous archon of Athens, who lived in the 8th century BC. It was also the name of the only successful suitor of Atalanta in Greek mythology, defeating her in a race by distracting her with golden apples."
Hippon,m,Ἵππων(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/híp.pɔːn/(Attic Greek)|/ˈip.pon/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse""."
Hipponax,m,"Ἱππώναξ, Ἱππῶναξ(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,/hip.ˈpoː.naks/(Classical Latin)|[hɪp.ˈpoː.näks̠](Classical Latin)|/ip.ˈpo.naks/(Ecclesiastical Latin)|[ip.ˈpɔː.näks](Ecclesiastical Latin),,,,,,,"Means ""master of the horse(s)"", derived from Greek ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" and ἄναξ (anax) meaning ""master, lord, king"".A notable bearer of this name is Hipponax of Ephesus, an ancient Greek poet from the 6th century BC."
Hipponicus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Hipponikos. This name was borne by an Athenian military commander from the 5th century BC, who was the father-in-law of the statesman Alcibiades."
Hipponikos,m,Ἱππόνικος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" combined with the Greek noun νίκη (nike) meaning ""victory""."
Hipponome,f,Ἱππονόμη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἱππονόμος (hipponomos) meaning ""keeping horses"" or Greek ἱππονομεύς (hipponomeus) meaning ""horse keeper""."
Hipponoos,m,Ἱππόνοος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" and νόος (noos) meaning ""mind""."
Hipponous,m,῾Ιππόνοος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Hipponoos.
Hippophile,f,Ἱπποφίλη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Theorised original form of Leipephilene, perhaps derived from Ancient Greek ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" and φίλος (philos) meaning ""friend, lover; beloved""."
Hipposthenes,m,῾Ιπποσθένης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" and σθένος (sthenos) meaning ""vigour, strength"". This name belonged to a 7th-century BC Greek wrestler and Olympic victor from Sparta."
Hippostratos,m,Ἱππόστρατος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the elements ἵππος (hippos) ""horse"" and στρατός (stratos) ""army"" meaning ""army horse""."
Hippostratus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized)|Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Hippostratos.
Hippothoe,f,Ἱπποθόη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Late Greek,,,,,,,,"Feminine form of Hippothous, derived from Ancient Greek ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"" and θοός (thoos) meaning ""quick, swift"". This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including one of the Nereids, an Amazon warrior, a Mycenaean princess who was abducted by Poseidon, and one of the fifty daughters of King Danaus, who killed her husband, Bromios or Obrimos, on their wedding night."
Hippothoon,m,Ἱπποθόων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/hɪ.ˈpɒθ.oʊ.ən/(English),,,,,,,Variant of Hippothous.
Hippothous,m,Ἱππόθοος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),/hip.pó.tho.os/(Attic Greek)|/ip.ˈpo.θo.os/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Means ""swift-riding"" in Ancient Greek, derived from ἵππος (hippos) ""horse"" and θοός (thoos) ""quick, swift"". This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including a son of Poseidon and Alope, a prince of Troy, and one of the fifty sons of King Aegyptus, being killed by his wife, Gorge, on their wedding night."
Hipseo,m,,Spanish,,,,,,,,Spanish form of Hypseus.
Hipseu,m,,Portuguese,,,,,,,,Portuguese form of Hypseus.
Hi-ra,,"희라(Korean Hangul)|姬路, 熙羅, 熙路, 姬羅, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/çi.ɾa̠/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Korean Hangul 희라 (see Hui-ra).
Hira,f,"日羅, 飛羅, 平, 陽羅, 陽良, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese|Albanian|Māori,HEE-ṘAH|hee-rah,,,,,,,"From Japanese 日 (hi) meaning ""sun, day"" combined with 羅 (ra) meaning ""lightweight fabric"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hiraaki,m,"救章, 平昭, 平明, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RAH-KEE,,,,,,,"It can be spelled with 平 (hira) meaning ""peace, flat, level, even"" and 察 (aki) meaning ""guess"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hiraaman,m,हीरामान(Hindi),Indian (Rare),/ɦiː.raː.maːn/|/ɦiː.raː.mɑːn/|/ɦiː.ɾaː.maːn/|/ɦiː.ɾaː.mɑːn/,,,,,,,Variant transcriptino of Devanagari हीरामान (see Hiraman).
Hiraani,f,,English (Rare),hə-RAH-nee,,,,,,,Not available.
Hiraeth,,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"From Welsh hiraeth which is widely regarded as an untranslatable word, denoting a deep longing for something or nostalgia."
Hiragi,f,"柊(Japanese Kanji)|ひいらぎ, ひひらぎ(Japanese Hiragana)|ヒイラギ(Japanese Katakana)",Japanese (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Hiiragi.
Hirak,m,,Hinduism,he-rak,,,,,,,"not sure about the history but the name means 'diamond keeper' and for the girls its hiral, which also means the same"
Hiral,,,Indian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Hiralkumar,m,,Indian,,,,,,,,Combination of Hiral and Kumar.
Híram,m,,Icelandic,,,,,,,,Icelandic form of Hiram.
Hiramaan,m,हीरामान(Hindi),Indian (Rare),/ɦiː.raː.maːn/|/ɦiː.raː.mɑːn/|/ɦiː.ɾaː.maːn/|/ɦiː.ɾaː.mɑːn/,,,,,,,Variant transcription of Devanagari हीरामान (see Hiraman).
Hiraman,m,हीरामान(Hindi),Indian,/ɦiː.raː.maːn/|/ɦiː.raː.mɑːn/|/ɦiː.ɾaː.maːn/|/ɦiː.ɾaː.mɑːn/,,,,,,,"From Sanskrit हीर (hīrā) meaning ""diamond"" and मान (mān) meaning ""respect, pride""."
Hiran,,হিরণ(Bengali)|হিৰণ(Assamese)|ହିରଣ(Odia)|හිරාන්(Sinhala)|หิรัญ(Thai),Bengali|Assamese|Odia|Sinhalese|Thai,/ˈhi.ɾon/(Bengali)|/ˈhi.ɹon/(Bengali)|/hi.ˈɾɔ.ɳɔ/(Assamese)|/hi˨˩.ˈran˧/(Thai),,,,,,,"From Sanskrit हिरण (hiraṇa) meaning ""gold"" (as a Thai word, however, it more commonly means ""silver"" or ""money""). It is a unisex name in India, while it is solely masculine in Sri Lanka and Thailand."
Hiranmay,m,হিরন্ময়(Bengali),Indian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Hirantsoa,,,Malagasy,,,,,,,,"From the Malagasy hirana meaning ""glimmer of light"" and soa meaning ""good""."
Hiranyagarbha,m,हिरण्यगर्भ(Sanskrit),Hinduism,,,,,,,,"Means ""gold fetus, gold womb"", from Sanskrit हिरण्य (hiraṇya) meaning ""gold"" and गर्भ (garbha) meaning ""embryo, fetus"". In Hindu cosmology, it represents the source of the creation of the universe."
Hiranyaksha,m,,Hinduism,TBA,,,,,,,A demon in Hindu mythology
Hirao,m,,Chamorro,,,,,,,,"Means ""affection"" in Chamorro."
Hirari,f,"ひらり(Japanese Hiragana)|桜舞, 蝶, 天空, 天舞, 日蘭莉, 妃蘭璃, 飛来里, 柊凜, 陽舞, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-ṘAH-ṘEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 桜 (hi) meaning ""cherry blossom"", 蝶 (hirari) meaning ""butterfly"", 天 (hi) meaning ""heaven, sky"", 日 (hi) meaning ""sun, day"", 妃 (hi) meaning ""empress"", 飛 (hi) meaning ""to fly"", 柊 (hira) meaning ""holly olive"" or 陽 (hi) meaning ""light, sun, male"", 蘭 (ra) meaning ""orchid"" or 来 (ra) meaning ""to come"" combined with 莉 (ri) meaning ""white jasmine"", 璃 (ri) meaning ""glassy"" or 里 (ri) meaning ""village"", 舞 (rari) meaning ""dance"", 空 (rari) meaning ""sky"" or 凜 (ri) meaning ""dignified, severe, cold"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hirawanu,m,,Māori|Moriori,hee-ra-wan-noo(Māori),,,,,,,Māori form of Silvanus. Hirawanu Kaimokopuna was a chief of the Rangitāne and Ngāti Mutuahi tribes. This was also the name of Owenga Moriori leader Hirawanu Tapu (d. 1900) who helped preserved Moriori traditions.
Hiraya,f,,Tagalog,/hi.ˈɾa.jaʔ/,,,,,,,"Means ""imagination, vision"" in Tagalog."
Hirbod,m,,Persian,,,,,,,,Iranian translation of Herbert.
Hircine,m,,Popular Culture,hur-SEEN,,,,,,,"Meaning ""goat"", from the Middle English hircyne, from Latin hircinus, from hircus, goat.Hircine is the name of the Daedric prince of the hunt and animals in the popular video game series 'The Elder Scrolls'. He is also know as 'The Huntsman of The Princes' and 'The Father of Manbeasts' (due to him being the creator of were-beasts, such as lycanthropes). Hircine's traits and appearance are similar to that of the Celtic god Cernunnos, and images of him depict him with the head of a horned beast (a goat-like head but with antlers like a deer)."
Hireglas,m,,Arthurian Cycle,,,,,,,,"Nephew of Bedivere and a knight of Arthur’s court in Geoffrey of Monmouth’s Historia.During the Roman War, he saw his uncle killed by King Boccus of Media, and he became enraged. He avenged Bedivere’s death by slaying Boccus.The French Liber de Compositione Castri Ambaziae renames him Billeius and the Vulgate Merlin calls him Segart."
Hirek,m,,Polish,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Hieronim.
Hirelis,f,,Obscure (Rare),,,,,,,,Of unknown origin and meaning.
Hiren,m,,Indian,,,,,,,,Sanskrit name which has been translated as either “lord genius” or “lord of gemstones and pearls”.
Hirenes,f,,Montenegrin (Archaic),,,,,,,,Recorded in Montenegro in the early 1600s.
Hirguan,,,Guanche Mythology|Spanish (Canarian),"eer-GWAN(Guanche Mythology, Canarian Spanish)",,,,,,,Variant of Iruene.
Hirha,m,Хырха(Karachay-Balkar),Karachay-Balkar,,,,,,,,"Means ""hoarse"" in Karachay-Balkar."
Hirian,m,,American,,,,,,,,Not available.
Hiril,f,,Literature,,,,,,,,"A character mentioned in JRR Tolkien's works. The name is derived from the word hiril meaning ""lady"" in the fictional Sindarin language."
Hirini,m,,Māori,Hear Rin knee,,,,,,,Māori form of Sidney.
Hiristiniye,f,,Ottoman Turkish,,,,,,,,Cognate of Christina recorded among the Christian community in the Ottoman Empire.
Hiro,m,"裕, 寛, 浩, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese|Polynesian Mythology|Tahitian,KHEE-RO|hee-ro(Tahitian),,,,,,,"From 裕 (hiro) meaning ""abundant"", 寛 (hiro) meaning ""generous, tolerant"" or 浩 (hiro) meaning ""prosperous"". Other kanji or kanji combinations are possible."
Hiroa,f,"裕愛, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,,,,,,,,"From Japanese 裕 (hiro) meaning ""courage"" combined with 愛 (a) meaning ""love, affection"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hiroaki,m,"広明, 洸彬, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-AH-KEE,,,,,,,"From 広 (hiro) meaning ""widespread, broad"" and 明 (aki) meaning ""bright, sparkle"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hirobumi,m,"博文, 裕史, 紘文, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-ROO-BOO-MEE,,,,,,,Variant of Hirofumi.
Hirochika,m,"広近, 広周, 広親, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HEE-ṘO-CHEE-KAH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 広 (hiro) meaning ""vast, wide"" combined with 近 (chika) meaning ""nearby, close"", 周 (chika) meaning ""circuit, lap"" or 親 (chika) meaning ""parent, elder"". Other kanji combinations are also possible."
Hiroe,f,"ひろえ(Japanese Hiragana)|博榎, 博江, 浩栄, 寛愛, 弘絵, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-E,,,,,,,"From Japanese 浩 (hiro) meaning ""prosperous"" combined with 榎 (e) meaning ""Chinese hackberry"". Other kanji combinations are possible.Famous bearer is Hiroe Oka who is a Japanese actress and voice actress from Tokyo Prefecture."
Hirofumi,m,"浩文, 浩史, 博文, 博史, 弘文, 裕史, 宏文, 広文, 洋文, 拓史, etc.(Japanese Kanji)|ひろふみ(Japanese Hiragana)",Japanese,/çi.ɾo̞.ɸɯᵝ.mi/,,,,,,,"From Japanese 浩 (hiro) meaning ""prosperous"", 博 (hiro) meaning ""command, esteem"", 弘 (hiro) meaning ""enlarge, expand, great"", 裕 (hiro) meaning ""abundant, rich, plentiful"", 宏 (hiro) meaning ""wide, spacious, great, vast"", 広 (hiro) meaning ""broad, wide, extensive"", 洋 (hiro) meaning ""ocean"" or 拓 (hiro) meaning ""expand, open, support"" combined with 文 (fumi) meaning ""writing"" or 史 (fumi) meaning ""history"". Other kanji combinations can form this name as well."
Hiroharu,m,"寛治, 広春, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-ṘO-HAH-ṘUU,,,,,,,"From Japanese 寛 (hiro) meaning ""tolerant, generous"" combined with 治 (haru) meaning ""govern, regulate, administer"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hirohide,m,"裕英, 大豪, 浩豪, 宏栄, 公秀, 寛英, 博栄, 裕秀, 洋日出, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-ṘO-KHEE-DEH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 丈 (hiro) meaning ""height, jō, a traditional Japanese unit of length based on 10 shaku, 31⁄33 meters"" or 裕 (hiro) meaning ""abundant, rich, plentiful"" combined with 豪 (hide) meaning ""magnificent, bold, Australia"" or 英 (hide) meaning ""excellent, fine"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hirohiko,m,"ヒロヒコ(Japanese Katakana)|ひろひこ(Japanese Hiragana)|浩彦, 弘彦, 博彦, 裕彦, 広彦, 飛呂彦, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HEE-RO-KHEE-KO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 裕 (hiro) meaning ""abundant"", combined with 彦 (hiko) meaning ""boy, prince"". Other kanji combinations can also form this name."
Hirohisa,m,"裕尚, 寛尚, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-KHEE-SAH,,,,,,,"From 裕 (hiro, hiroshi) meaning ""abundant"", 寛 (hiro) meaning ""tolerant, generous"", 浩 (hiro) meaning ""prosperous"", and 大 (hiro, tai, dai) meaning ""big, great"" combined with 久 (hisa) meaning ""long time, long time ago"", 尚 (hisa) meaning ""still, incidentally or 寿 (hisa) meaning ""long life, lifespan"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hiroji,m,"浩次, 博二, 博治, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-ZHEE,,,,,,,"From 浩 (hiro) meaning ""prosperous"" and 次 (ji) meaning ""second, next"". Other kanji combinations can be used."
Hiroka,f,"ひろか(Japanese Hiragana)|ヒロカ(Japanese Katakana)|拓賀, 明可, 容果, 裕霞, 優日, 緋路嘉, 比芦架, 火露伽, 妃蕗遥, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-KA,,,,,,,"From Japanese 嘉 (hiro) meaning ""applaud, praise, esteem, happy, auspicious"", 海 (hiro) meaning ""vastly gathered, wide, large, sea, ocean"", 寛 (hiro) meaning ""tolerant, generous, lenient"", or 敬 (hiro) meaning ""honor, reverence, respect"" combined with 郁 (ka) meaning ""cultural progress, perfume"", 賀 (ka) meaning ""congratulations, joy"", 耶 (ka) meaning ""question mark"", 和 (ka) meaning ""harmony, tranquility, peace, serenity"", or 雅 (ka) meaning ""gracious, elegant, graceful, refined"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hirokatsu,m,"寛克, 啓克, 廣勝, 弘勝, 浩且, 博捷, 裕克, 洋克, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HEE-ṘO-KAH-TSUU,,,,,,,"From Japanese 寛 (hiro) meaning ""tolerant"" combined with 克 (katsu) meaning ""to overcome, to subdue, to overthrow"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hirokazu,m,"洋一, 洋和, 広一, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-KAH-ZOO,,,,,,,"From 洋 (hiro) meaning ""sea, ocean"" and 一 (kazu) meaning ""one"". Other kanji combinations can be used."
Hirokiyo,m,"ひろきよ(Japanese Hiragana)|ヒロキヨ(Japanese Katakana)|海淨, 裕磨, 大精, 浩人, 祐潔, 博清, 広浄, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-KEE-YO,,,,,,,"From 海 (hiro) meaning ""ocean, sea, large, wide, vastly gathered"" combined with 人 (kiyo) meaning ""person"". Other kanji combinations can be used."
Hirokuni,m,"裕邦, 弘国, 広邦, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-KOO-NEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 裕 (hiro) meaning ""abundant, rich, plentiful"" combined with 邦 (kuni) meaning ""home country (usually refers to Japan)"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hiroma,m,"ひろま(Japanese Hiragana)|優真, 大真, 尋真, 泰磨, 裕真, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-ṘO-MAH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 優 (hiro) meaning ""tenderness, excel, surpass, actor, superiority, gentleness"", 大 (hiro) meaning ""large, big"", 尋 (hiro) meaning ""fathom"", 泰 (hiro) meaning ""calm, peaceful, Thailand"" or 裕 (hiro) meaning ""abundant, rich"" combined with 真 (ma) meaning ""sincere, true, reality"" or 磨 (ma) meaning ""improve, polish, scour, grind, brush (teeth)"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hiromasa,m,"博正, 宏昌, 弘昌, 浩正, 大将, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-MAH-SAH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 博 (hiro) meaning ""wide, broad, rich, abundant, plentiful"" combined with 正 (masa) meaning ""justice, right, proper, correct"". Other kanji combinations are possible.A bearer of this name is Hiromasa Yonebayashi (1973-) He is a Japanese animator and director for Studio Ghibli."
Hirome,f,"浩芽, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HEE-ṘO-ME,,,,,,,"From Japanese 浩 (hiro) meaning ""prosperous"" combined with 芽 (me) meaning ""bud, sprout, shoot"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hiromichi,m,"ひろみち(Japanese Hiragana)|ヒロミチ(Japanese Katakana)|皓路, 博通, 倫径, 広典, 啓達, 廣長, 宙皆, 泰亨, 宏方, 宥迪, 弘導, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-MEE-CHEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 寛 (hiro) meaning ""tolerant"" combined with 道 (michi) meaning ""road; way"". Other kanji combinations are possible.Famous bearers are Hiromichi Ishige (1956-), a retired Japanese professional baseball player and manager in Japan's Nippon Professional Baseball and Hiromichi Kataura (1959), a Japanese scientist."
Hiromiko,f,"ひろみこ(Japanese Hiragana)|裕美子, 洋美子, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese (Rare),KHEE-RO-MEE-KO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 裕 (hiro) meaning ""abundant"" or 洋 (hiro) meaning ""ocean"", 美 (mi) meaning ""beautiful"" combined with 子 (ko) meaning ""child"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hiromitsu,m,"博光, 宏光, 景光, 弘光, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-MEE-TSOO,,,,,,,"From 啓 (hiro) meaning ""disclose, open, to say"" or 敬 (hiro) meaning ""respect, honor, reverence"" combined with 光 (mitsu) meaning ""light, radiance"". Other kanji combinations can be used."
Hiromu,m,"弘, 大夢, etc.(Japanese Kanji)|ひろむ(Japanese Hiragana)|ヒロム(Japanese Katakana)",Japanese,/çi.ɾo̞.mɯᵝ/,,,,,,,"From Japanese 弘 (hiromu) meaning ""enlarge, expand, great"". It can also be formed from 大 (hiro) meaning ""big, great, vast, high"" combined with 夢 (mu) meaning ""dream, vision"", as well as other combinations of kanji."
Hiron,m,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,Probably a short form of Hieronymus.
Hirona,f,"ひろな(Japanese Hiragana)|緩菜, 紘菜, 弘奈, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-NAH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 緩 (hiro) meaning ""slow down, loose, to slacken"" combined with 菜 (na) meaning ""vegetables, greens"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hirondina,f,,Portuguese (Rare)|Portuguese Creole|Portuguese (African),,,,,,,,"Derived from the Latin word hirundo meaning ""swallow (bird)"". A notable bearer is Mozambican poet Hirondina Joshua (1987-)."
Hironimus,m,,Indonesian,,,,,,,,Indonesian form of Hieronymus (see Jerome).
Hirono,f,"比呂乃, 絢乃, 広野(Japanese Kanji)|ひろの(Japanese Hiragana)|ヒロノ(Japanese Katakana)",Japanese,HEE-ṘO-NO,,,,,,,"From the Japanese kanji 比 (hi) meaning ""comparison; equal"" combined with 呂 (ro) meaning ""spine"" and 乃 (no) a possessive particle. It can also derive from 絢 (hiro) meaning ""brilliant fabric design"" and 乃 (no), the possessive particle. Another option is from 広 (hiro) meaning ""wide"" and 野 (no) meaning ""field; plain"".Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hironobu,m,"ヒロノブ(Japanese Katakana)|博信, 浩宣, 宏伸, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-NO-BOO,,,,,,,"From 裕 (hiro) meaning ""abundant"" combined with 信 (nobu) meaning ""trust, faith, believe"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hironori,m,"ひろのり(Japanese Hiragana)|裕儀, 浩象, 洋敬, 宏雅, 弘典, 大容, 寛鑑, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-NO-REE,,,,,,,"From 裕 (hiro) meaning ""abundant"" combined with 儀 (nori) meaning ""ceremony, rule, affair, case, a matter"" or 象 (nori) meaning ""elephant"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hīrons,m,,Latvian,,,,,,,,Latvian form of Chiron. This name is not generally used as a given name.
Hiroo,m,"弘雄, 広生, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-ṘO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 弘 (hiro) meaning ""wide, spacious, vast, broad, large"" combined with 雄 (o) meaning ""male"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hiroomi,m,"大臣, 博臣(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-MEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 大 (hiro, o) meaning ""big, great"" or 博 (hiro) meaning ""command, esteem"" combined with 臣 (omi) meaning “vassal, subject”. Other kanji are possible."
Hiroraki,f,"裕羅紀, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HEE-ṘO-ṘAH-KEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 裕 (hiro) meaning ""abundant, rich, plentiful"", 羅 (ra) meaning ""lightweight fabric"" combined with 紀 (ki) meaning ""century"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hirori,f,"拡莉, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,,,,,,,,"From Japanese 拡 (hiro) meaning ""broaden, extend, expand, enlarge"" combined with 莉 ""white jasmine"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hirosa,f,恕瑳(Japanese Kanji)|ひろさ(Japanese Hiragana),Japanese,,,,,,,,"Hirosa (恕瑳) means ""vivid generosity""."
Hiroshige,m,"敬繁, 啓成, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-SHEE-GE,,,,,,,"From 敬 (hiro) meaning ""respect, honor, reverence"" or 啓 (hiro) meaning ""to say, open, disclose"" combined with 繁 (shige) meaning ""flourishing, luxuriant"" or 成 (shige) meaning ""become""."
Hiroši,m,Хироши(Serbian),Serbian,,,,,,,,Serbian description of Japanese Hiroshi. Hiroši is name of Branko Vukelić's son. Branko Vukelić are been most biggest spy in second world wear. Name mean tolerant on japanese.
Hirosuke,m,"央介, 寛介, 寛助, 寛典, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HEE-ṘO-SKE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 寛 (hiro) meaning ""tolerant"" combined with 介 (suke) meaning ""shell, shellfish"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hirota,m,"ひろた(Japanese Hiragana)|ヒロタ(Japanese Katakana)|宏太, 広太, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese (Rare),KHEE-RO-TA,,,,,,,"From Japanese 宏 (hiro) meaning ""wide, spacious, great, vast"" or 広 (hiro) meaning ""vast, wide"" combined with 太 (ta) meaning ""thick, big"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hirotaka,m,"洋孝, 宏考, 宏高, 広高, 広隆, 広孝, 弘隆, 仁貴, 啓貴, 弘高, 大敬, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,HEE-ṘO-TAH-KAH,,,,,,,"From Japanese 大 (hiro) meaning ""big, large, great"" or 博 (hiro) meaning ""command, esteem"" combined with 貴 (taka) meaning ""expensive, valuable"" or 孝 (taka) meaning ""filial piety"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Hirotami,m,"啓民, 裕民, 大民, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KHEE-RO-TAH-MEE,,,,,,,"From 裕 (hiro) meaning ""abundant"", or 大 (hiro) meaning ""big, great"", combined with 民 (tami, tamiyo) meaning ""people, nationals, citizens, civil"" or 黎⁠ (tami, rei, ri, kuro.i, rai) meaning ""bluish black, gloomy, dusky"". Other kanji can be used."
