Hlín,f,,Old Norse|Norse Mythology|Icelandic,,,,,,,,"Means ""protection"" in Old Norse, the root of which is Old Icelandic hleina ""to save, protect, defend"" (ultimately relating to Old English hlæna and modern English lean; also the related noun hlein is used of the upright warp-weighted loom, which is leaned against a wall in use). It was also used to mean ""woman"" in a large number of poetic compounds, such as hrínga-hlín, bauga-hlín ""ring-lady"". This Old Norse name is usually found as a goddess-name, a wife of Odin, possibly a byname of Frigg."
