Hulan,,Хулан(Khakas),Mongolian|Khakas,hoo-lahn(Khakas),,,,,,,"A Khakas variant of Kulan/Qulan (""Asiatic wild ass/onager""), this name reflects the characteristic phonological shift from K to H distinctive to the Khakas language. Derived from the Proto-Common Turkic root kulan, the name embodies the spirit of the steppe: unfettered speed, agility, and endurance.Historically documented as qulān as early as the Karakhanid era (9th–13th centuries), the term appears prominently in the Dīwān Lughāt al-Turk (Dictionary of the Turkic Languages) by Mahmud al-Kashgari (c. 1072–1074). As this seminal medieval source predates the Mongolian Khulan, it confirms the term’s status as a Turkic loanword into the Mongolian language.Taxonomically and behaviorally distinct from domesticated equines, the Hulan stands as a powerful, enduring symbol of untamed freedom and wild resilience."
Hulda,f,,Brazilian (Rare),,,,,,,,"Possibly a variant of Hilda. Also, compare the names Astrid and Astrud, which could be more examples of how an I can turn into a U."
