Hunayn,m,حنين(Arabic),Arabic|Medieval Arabic,,,,,,,,"Means ""longing, yearning, nostalgia, homesickness"" in Arabic, from the root حنّ (ḥanna). It is related to the second element in the Hebrew name Yoḥanan (see John). According to the Quran, this is the name of a valley where the Battle of Hunayn took place in 630 AD shortly after the conquest of Mecca. The battle was won by the Muslim army led by the Prophet Muhammad.A famous and influential bearer of this name was Hunayn ibn Ishaq (809-873), known in Latin as Iohannitius, an Arab Christian scholar, physician and scientist."
