Hyas,m,Ὑάς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/ˈhaɪ.əs/(English)|/hy.ás/(Attic Greek)|/y.ˈas/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Hyas was a son of Atlas in Greek mythology, and the brother (or in some accounts father) of the Hyades nymphs. When he was killed in a hunting accident, by a wild boar in most accounts, the Hyades wept so much that the gods took pity on them and made them stars in the head of the constellation Taurus. His name is probably a back formation of Hyades, often said to derive from Ancient Greek ὕω (hyo) meaning ""to rain, to be drenched with rain"", but ὗς (hys) meaning ""pig, swine"" is also a possibility."
Hyateguash,f,,Circassian|Caucasian Mythology,,,,,,,,Name of the Circassian goddess of Beauty and Gardens.
