Hyben,f,,"Danish (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Taken directly from Danish hyben ""rosehip""."
Hybla,f,Ὕβλα(Greek),Mythology,,,,,,,,"The name of a goddess of earth and fertility in Sicel or Siceliot mythology, worshiped in ancient Sicily by the Sicels. Multiple cities were named after her."
Hybris,f,Ὕβρις(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/ˈhaɪ.brɪs/,,,,,,,"From Greek ὕβρις (hybris) meaning ""insolence, arrogance, wanton violence, outrage, insult"" (originally ""presumption toward the gods""). In Greek mythology Hybris was a spirit or goddess of violence, insolence and outrageous behaviour. According to Apollodorus, she was the mother of Pan by Zeus."
Hydajet,m,,Albanian,,,,,,,,Albanian form of Hüdayet.
Hydajete,f,,Albanian,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Hydajet.
Hydarnes,m,Ὑδάρνης(Ancient Greek),Old Persian (Hellenized),,,,,,,,Hellenized form of Vidarna.
Hydaspes,m,Ὑδάσπης(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/haɪ.ˈdæs.piːz/(English),,,,,,,"Borrowed from Sanskrit वितस्ता (Vitasta), the name of what is now called the Jhelum river. It has also been proposed that this name borrows from Old Persian *Vidāspa-, literally ""caring for horses"", from *vīd- ""to take care of"", and 𐎠𐎿𐎱 ""horse"", but this name is unattested in relation to any rivers. In Greek mythology, Hydaspes was a Pentapotamia river god with an extraordinary swift stream that flows into the Saronitic Syrtis."
Hyddwn,m,,Welsh Mythology,[ˈhə.ðʊn],,,,,,,"Hyddwn (from the Welsh hydd, ""stag"") appears in the fourth of the Four Branches of the Mabinogi and is one of the three sons of brothers Gwydion and Gilfaethwy. As punishment for assaulting Goewin, Math banished his nephews, turning them into a breeding pair of deer for a year, then pigs, and finally wolves. They had three children over the three years: Hyddwn, Hychddwn, and Bleiddwn. Hyddwn is the offspring of the deer pairing. Math took him and transformed him into a human being, baptising him Hyddwn (""Stag-man"")."
Hyde,m,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,"From the English surname Hyde. Fictional bearers of the surname include the criminal Edward Hyde from Robert Louis Stevenson's 1886 novella Strange Case of Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde and Steven Hyde, known simply as Hyde, a character played by Danny Masterson on the American television sitcom That '70s Show (1998-2006)."
Hydee,f,,English (Rare)|Filipino,,,,,,,,Variant of Heidi.
Hydeia,f,,Obscure,,,,,,,,"Hydeia Loren Broadbent (June 14, 1984 – February 20, 2024) was an American HIV/AIDS activist who advocated through appearances in national media and as a spokesperson for related foundations."
Hydi,f,,English,,,,,,,,Variant of Heidi.
Hydrangea,f,,Obscure,/haɪ.ˈdɹeɪn.d͡ʒə/|/haɪ.ˈdɹeɪn.d͡ʒi.ə/,,,,,,,"From the flower (species Hydrangea macrophylla), also called hortensia in English. The name is derived from Greek ὕδωρ (hudor) meaning ""water"" and ἀγγεῖον (angeion) meaning ""vessel"", in reference to the bulb or cone shape of its seed capsules."
Hydref,,,Welsh,hə-DRAHV,,,,,,,"Means ""autumn, October"" in Welsh, ultimately derived from hydd ""stag"" and bref ""call, cry; lowing, bleat""."
Hye-bin,f,혜빈(Korean Hangul),Korean,HYE-BEEN,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 慧 ""bright, intelligent"" and 彬 ""cultivated, well-bred""."
Hye-deok,m,혜덕(Korean Hangul)|惠德(Korean Hanja),Korean,,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 惠 ""favor, benefit, confer kindness"" (hye) and 德 ""ethics, morality, virtue"" (deok). Other Hanja combinations are also possible."
Hye-eun,f,"혜은(Korean Hangul)|惠恩, 蕙銀, 慧銀(Korean Hanja)",Korean (Rare),HYE-UN,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 惠 (hye) ""favor, confer kindness"", 蕙 (hye) ""orchid"" or 慧 (hye) ""bright, intelligent"" and 恩 (eun) meaning ""kindness, mercy, charity"" or 銀 (eun) meaning ""hapiness, happy, smile"""
Hye-gyeong,f,혜겅(Korean Hangul),Korean,HYE-GYUNG,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 惠 ""favor, confer kindness"", 蕙 ""orchid"" or 慧 ""bright, intelligent"" (hye) and 慶 ""congratulate"" or 敬 ""respect"" (gyeong).A famous bearer is Lady Hyegyeong (1735-1816), the wife of Crown Prince Sado and mother of King Jeongjo of Joseon."
Hye-in,f,"혜인(Korean Hangul)|慧仁, 惠仁, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/çje.in/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 慧 (hye) meaning ""bright, intelligent"" or 惠 (hye) meaning ""favour, benefit"" combined with 仁 (in) meaning ""compassionate"". Other hanja combinations can form this name as well."
Hye-ja,f,혜자(Korean Hangul),Korean,HYE-JAH,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 惠 ""favor, benefit, confer kindness"" and 子 ""child"". The same characters can be read Keiko in Japanese. Hye-ja is one of a number of Japanese-style names ending in ja that were popular when Korea was under Japanese rule, but declined in popularity afterwards. A famous bearer is South Korean singer Patti Kim, born Kim Hye-ja (1938-)."
Hye-jeong,f,혜정(Korean Hangul),Korean,HYE-JUNG,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 惠 (hye) ""favor, benefit, confer kindness"" and 貞 (jeong) ""virtuous, chaste, pure; loyal."
Hye-ji,f,"혜지(Korean Hangul)|慧智, 惠智, 慧知, 惠知, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/çje.t͡ɕi/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 慧 (hye) meaning ""bright, intelligent or 惠 (hye) meaning ""favour, benefit"" combined with 智 (ji) meaning ""wisdom, intellect"" or 知 (ji) meaning ""know, perceive, comprehend"". Other hanja combinations can form this name as well."
Hyejong,m,,Korean,,,,,,,,Not available.
