Hyfaidd,m,,Medieval Welsh,[ˈhɪv.ai̯ð]|[ˈhiːv.ai̯ð],,,,,,,"Suffix -aidd is an adjectival suffix, comes from Middle Welsh -eid, from Proto-Celtic *-adyos. Cognate with Old Irish -de, Greek -άδα (-áda), Latin -idus.Hyfaidd ap Bleddri (Hyfaidd son of Bleddri) was a 9th century king of Dyfed, Wales."
Hyġd,f,,Anglo-Saxon,/xyjd/(Old English),,,,,,,"Derives from the Old English element hyge ""thought, mind, mood, desire"". A notable bearer was the wife of Hyġelāc in the poem Beowulf."
