Hymeneus,m,,Biblical|Biblical Latin,,,,,,,,Variant of Hymenaeus used in some versions of the Vulgate (Latin Bible) as well as at least two English Bibles: the Douay-Rheims Bible (1582-1610) and the original 1611 edition of the King James Bible.
Hymir,m,,"Norse Mythology|Icelandic (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Of uncertain origin, possibly related to Old Norse húm meaning ""semi-darkness, twilight"". In Norse mythology this was the name of a giant (jǫtunn), according to Hymiskviða the father of the god Tyr, from whom Thor wanted to fetch a cauldron for the Æsir. In one myth Thor baited a fishing line with an ox skull in hopes of catching Miðgarðsormr (""world serpent""), also called Jörmungandr, but Hymir cut the line."
Hymn,,,English (Puritan),/ˈhɪm/,,,,,,,"From the English word hymn meaning a song of praise or worship, ultimately derived from Ancient Greek ὕμνος (hymnos) ""song, hymn, ode (in praise of heroes or gods)""."
Hymnal,m,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,Not available.
Hymnia,f,,English,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Polyhymnia.
Hymy,,,Finnish (Rare),,,,,,,,"Means ""smile"" in Finnish."
Hync,m,,Sorbian,,,,,,,,Lower Sorbian cognate of Hinc.
Hynca,m,,Anglo-Saxon,,,,,,,,"From Old English hun meaning ""bear cub""."
Hynde,m,,Medieval Baltic,,,,,,,,Diminutive of Hinrick.
Hynden,f,,"English (Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Transferred use of the surname Hynden.
Hyndla,f,,Norse Mythology|Old Norse,,,,,,,,"Means ""the dog"". In Norse mythology this is the name of a giantess who insults Freyja."
Hyndrix,m,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Hendrix.
Hyness,m,,Popular Culture,,,,,,,,"Hyness is the principal villain in Kirby Star Allies, serving as the penultimate boss in Story Mode."
Hyniek,m,,Silesian,,,,,,,,Silesian form of Heniek.
Hynki,m,,Vilamovian,,,,,,,,Vilamovian form of Henry.
Hynrick,m,,Medieval Baltic,,,,,,,,Variant of Hinrick.
Hynryck,m,,Medieval Baltic,,,,,,,,Variant of Hinrick.
Hynryk,m,,Medieval Baltic,,,,,,,,Variant of Hinrick.
Hyodeok,m,,Korean,,,,,,,,"Prince Hyodeok was a Goryeo royal family member as the first son of Wang Uk and a grandson of Wang Geon, its founder. He was the oldest brother of King Seongjong, Queen Heonae, and Queen Heonjeong. Since their parents had died when he was young, he was raised by his paternal grandmother, Queen Sinjeong alongside his siblings."
Hyōe,m,"兵衛(Japanese Kanji)|ひょうえ, ひょうゑ(Japanese Hiragana)|ヒョウエ, ヒョウヱ(Japanese Katakana)",Japanese,/ço̞ː.e̞/,,,,,,,"This name combines 兵 (hyou, hei, tsuwamono) meaning ""army, soldier"" and 衛 (ei, e) meaning ""defense, protection.""Hyōe is classified as a hyakkanna (百官名), a court rank-style name that samurai used to announce oneself and give himself authority, since it comes from the name of a government office under the Ritsuryō system (兵衛府/Hyōefu).It is also used when combining with other kanji (like Hanbē) with its combining form -bē/bee/bei, unless it combines with 左 or 右 (in this case, it can be read as either Sahē & Uhē or Sahyōe & Uhyōe)."
Hyoeun,m,,Korean,,,,,,,,Not available.
Hyōga,m,氷河(Japanese Kanji)|ひょうが(Japanese Hiragana),Japanese (Rare),,,,,,,,"From Japanese 氷河 (hyōga) meaning ""glacier""."
Hyo-jae,,"효재(Korean Hangul)|孝才, 曉才, 孝財, 曉財, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/çjo.t͡ɕɛ/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 孝 (hyo) meaning ""filial piety, obedience"" or 曉 (hyo) meaning ""dawn"" combined with 才 (jae) meaning ""talent, ability"" or 財 (jae) meaning ""wealth, riches"". Other hanja combinations can form this name as well."
Hyoje,m,,Korean,,,,,,,,Not available.
Hyo-jeong,,효정(Korean Hangul),Korean,HYO-JUNG,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 孝 ""filial piety, obedience"" or 曉 ""dawn, daybreak; clear, explicit"" (hyo), and 貞 ""virtuous, chaste, pure; loyal"", 正 ""right, proper, correct"" or 庭 ""courtyard"" (jeong)."
Hyoji,m,,Korean,,,,,,,,Not available.
Hyo-jin,,효진(Korean Hangul),Korean,HYO-JEEN,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 孝 ""filial piety, obedience"" (hyo), and 眞 ""real, actual, true, genuine"" or 珍 ""precious, valuable, rare"" (jin)."
Hyo-ju,f,효주(Korean Hangul),Korean,HYO-JOO,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 孝 ""filial piety, obedience"" and 周 ""circumference""."
Hyo-jun,m,효준(Korean Hangul),Korean,HYO-JOON,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 孝 ""filial piety, obedience"" and 準 ""rule, guideline, standard""."
Hyok,m,혁(Korean Hangul),Korean,HYUK,,,,,,,Variant transcription of Hyeok.
Hyolin,f,"효린(Korean Hangul)|孝潾, 曉璘, 孝璘, 曉潾(Korean Hanja)",Korean,HYO-LEEN,,,,,,,Variant of Hyo-rin.
Hyolyn,f,"효린(Korean Hangul)|孝潾, 曉璘, 孝璘, 曉潾(Korean Hanja)",Korean (Anglicized),HYO-LEEN,,,,,,,Anglicized form of Hyo-rin and/or Hyolin.
Hyo-min,f,"효민(Korean Hangul)|孝珉, 孝玟, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/çjo.min/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 孝 (hyo) meaning ""filial piety, obedience"" combined with 珉 or 玟 (min) meaning ""jade, stone resembling jade"". Other hanja combinations can form this name as well."
Hyomohk,m,,Korean,,,,,,,,Not available.
Hyomyong,m,,Korean,,,,,,,,Not available.
Hyo-ri,f,효리(Korean Hangul),Korean,HYO-REE,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 孝 ""filial piety, obedience"" and 利 ""gains, advantage, profit, merit"". A famous bearer is South Korean singer Lee Hyori (1979-)."
Hyo-rin,f,"효린(Korean Hangul)|孝潾, 曉璘, 孝璘, 曉潾(Korean Hanja)",Korean,HYO-REEN,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 孝 ""filial piety, obedience"" or 曉 ""dawn, daybreak; clear, explicit"" (hyo) and 潾 ""clear water"", 璘 ""luster of jade"" (rin)"
Hyo-seo,f,"효서(Korean Hangul)|孝瑞, 曉瑞, 孝舒, 曉舒, 孝徐, 曉徐, 孝序, 曉序, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/çjo.sʌ/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 孝 (hyo) meaning ""filial piety, obedience"" or 曉 (hyo) meaning ""dawn"" combined with 瑞 (seo) meaning ""felicitous omen, auspicious"" or 舒 (seo) meaning ""open up, unfold, comfortable, easy"" or 徐 (seo) meaning ""slowly, calmly, composed, dignified"" or 序 (seo) meaning ""series, sequence"". Other hanja combinations can form this name as well."
Hyosung,m,,Korean,,,,,,,,Not available.
Hyōta,m,"瓢太, 瓢多, 瓢田, 豹太, 豹多, 豹田(Japanese Kanji)|ひょうた(Japanese Hiragana)|ヒョウタ(Japanese Katakana)",Japanese,HYO:-TA,,,,,,,"From Japanese 瓢 (hyō) meaning ""gourd"" or 豹 (hyō) meaning ""leopard, panther"" combined with 太 (ta) meaning ""thick, big"", 多 (ta) meaning ""many"", or 田 (ta) meaning ""rice paddy"". Other kanji combinations are possible. This name is also derived from 瓢箪 (hyōtan), the calabash gourd. Oreburgh City Gym Leader Roark (who is also known as Hyōta) in the Japanese version of Pokémon Diamond, Pearl, and Platinum is a fictional bearer of this name."
Hyounjoo,,,Korean,,,,,,,,Not available.
Hyousuke,m,"ひょうすけ(Japanese Hiragana)|ヒョウスケ(Japanese Katakana)|豹亮, 彪介, 兵輔, 平翼, 氷佐, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese (Rare),HYO-SKE,,,,,,,"From 氷 (hyo) meaning ""frozen water, ice shard"" combined with 資 (suke) meaning ""material, resource, property, capital, fund"". Other kanji combinations can be used."
Hyo-won,,"효원(Korean Hangul)|孝元, 孝媛, 孝原, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/çjo.wʌn/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 孝 (hyo) meaning ""filial piety, obedience"" combined with 元 (won) meaning ""first, origin"" or 媛 (won) meaning ""beautiful woman"" or 原 (won) meaning ""source, origin, beginning"". Other hanja combinations can form this name as well."
Hyo-yeon,f,효연(Korean Hangul),Korean,HYO-YUN,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 孝 ""filial piety, obedience"" and 淵 ""gulf, abyss, deep""."
Hyo-yeong,,"효영(Korean Hangul)|孝英, 孝榮, 孝瑛, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/çjo.jʌ̹ŋ/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 孝 (hyo) meaning ""filial piety, obedience"" combined with 英 (yeong) meaning ""flower, petal, brave, hero"" or 榮 (yeong) meaning ""glory, honour"" or 瑛 (yeong) meaning ""jade"". Other hanja combinations can form this name as well."
Hyo-young,,"효영(Korean Hangul)|孝英, 孝榮, 孝瑛, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/çjo.jʌ̹ŋ/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Korean Hangeul 효영 (see Hyo-yeong).
Hypace,m,,History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,French form of Hypatius.
Hypatie,f,,History (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Hypatia.
Hypatios,m,Ὑπάτιος(Ancient Greek),Late Greek,,,,,,,,Masculine form of Hypatia.
Hypatius,m,,Late Greek (Latinized)|Late Roman,,,,,,,,Latinized form of Hypatios. Bearers of this name include Roman senator Flavius Hypatius (4th century AD) and saint Hypatius of Gangra (4th century AD).
Hypenos,m,Ὕπηνος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"From Ancient Greek ὑπήνη (hypene) meaning ""moustache, beard""."
Hypenus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Hypenos. Hypenus of Elis was an ancient Greek athlete from Elis who won the double race (Diaulos) of the 14th Ancient Olympic Games in 724 BC.
Hypera,f,"Ὑπέρα, Ὑπέρη(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ὑπέρ (hyper) meaning ""over, above"". In Greek mythology, Hypera was a woman who travelled in search of her brother Anthus, who disappeared as a child. She found him in the house of Acastus where he worked as a slave."
Hyperbius,m,Ὑπέρβιος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Means ""of overwhelming strength; headstrong, powerful"" or ""arrogant, lawless, wanton"" in Ancient Greek, from ὑπέρ (hyper) meaning ""over, above"" and βία (bia) meaning ""force, power"". This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including one of the fifty sons of king Aegyptus, and a son of Ares credited as being the first person to kill an animal."
Hyperbolos,m,Ὑπέρβολος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/hy.pér.bo.los/(Attic Greek)|/y.ˈper.βo.los/(Koine Greek)|/y.ˈper.vo.los/(Byzantine Greek)|/I ˈper.vo.los/(Constantinopolitan Greek),,,,,,,"Means ""to throw over"" or ""to overshoot"", metaphorically ""to exceed, excel, surpass"", derived from Greek ὑπέρ (hyper) meaning ""over, above"" and βάλλω (ballo) meaning ""to throw, cast; to strike""."
Hyperechios,m,Ὑπερέχιος(Ancient Greek),Late Greek|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek verb ὑπερέχω (hyperecho) meaning ""to be above, to rise over the horizon"" as well as ""to excel, to outdo"".Also compare the Greek verb ὑπερηχέω (hyperecheo) meaning ""to outroar, to drown out (of sound and noise)"".This name was borne by two saints, one of whom was a Desert Father who lived in the 4th or 5th century AD."
Hyperechius,m,,Late Greek (Latinized)|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Hyperechios. This name was borne by two saints, one of whom was a Desert Father who lived in the 4th or 5th century AD."
Hypereides,m,"Ὑπερείδης, Ὑπερίδης(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,/hy.pe.rěː.dɛːs/(Attic Greek)|/y.pe.ˈri.ðis/(Koine Greek)|/i.pe.ˈri.ðis/(Constantinopolitan Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from either Greek ὑπέρ (hyper) meaning ""over, above"" and εἶδος (eidos) meaning ""kind, type"", or from ὑπερείδω (hypereidos) meaning ""put under as a support; lean upon, lift, carry"", itself from ὑπό (hypo) meaning ""under, beneath"" and ἐρείδω (ereido) meaning ""to prop, support""."
Hyperenor,m,Ὑπερήνωρ(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ὑπέρ (hyper) meaning ""over, above, beyond"" combined with the Greek noun ἀνήρ (aner) meaning ""man"".This is the name of several characters in Greek mythology, one of which is one of the suitors of Penelope."
Hypérion,m,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),/i.pe.ʁjɔ̃/,,,,,,,French form of Hyperion.
Hyperippe,f,Ὑπερίππη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/hɪ.pə.ˈrɪp.i/,,,,,,,"Probably derived from Ancient Greek ὑπέρ (hyper) meaning ""over, above"" and ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse"". This was the name of several characters in Greek mythology, including one of the Daenids (who killed her husband Hippocorystes on their wedding night), and a princess of Molossia who was turned into a diving bird (a shearwater or loon) by Zeus as she dove into water to escape her burning home."
Hyperochus,m,Ὑπέροχος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Means ""eminent, distinguished above others, mighty"" in Ancient Greek, ultimately derived from ὑπέρ (hyper) meaning ""above, over, across, beyond"" and ἔχω (ekho) meaning ""to hold, possess, contain""."
Hypnus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Hypnos.
Hypocrisy,m,,Literature,,,,,,,,"Middle English from Old French ypocrisie, via ecclesiastical Latin, from Greek hupokrisis ‘acting of a theatrical part’, from hupokrinesthai ‘play a part, pretend’, from hupo ‘under’ + krinein ‘decide, judge.’"
Hypolite,m,,French (Rare)|French (Cajun),,,,,,,,Variant of Hyppolite.
Hypolith,m,,German (Bessarabian),,,,,,,,Bessarabian German form of Hippolytos.
Hyppoliet,m,,Flemish,,,,,,,,Variant of Hippolyte 2. (Also compare Hyppolite.)
Hyppolit,m,,Hungarian (Rare),,,,,,,,Hungarian form of Hippolytos.
Hyppolite,m,,French,,,,,,,,Variant of Hippolyte 2.
Hyppolito,m,,"Portuguese (Brazilian, Rare)",,,,,,,,Brazilian Portuguese form of Hippolytos
Hypsée,m,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Hypseus.
Hypsenor,m,Ὑψήνωρ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun ὕψος (hypsos) meaning ""height"" as well as ""top, summit"" and ""grandeur"" combined with the Greek noun ἀνήρ (aner) meaning ""man"".In Greek mythology, this is the name of two men who fought in the Trojan War: one fought on the side of the Achaeans, the other on the side of the Trojans."
Hypseus,m,Ὑψεύς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/ˈhɪp.si.əs/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ὕψος (hypsos) meaning ""height, top, summit"". This name was borne by three characters in Greek mythology, one of them being the king of the Lapiths and son of the river god Peneus by the nymph Creusa."
Hypsicles,m,Ὑψικλῆς(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of the Greek name Ὑψικλῆς (Hypsikles), derived from ῠ̔́ψῐ (hypsi) meaning ""on high, aloft"" and κλέος (kleos) meaning ""glory""."
Hypsipyle,f,Ὑψιπύλη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ὑψίπυλος (hypsipylos) meaning ""high-gated, with lofty gates"", composed of ὕψι (hypsi) meaning “on high, aloft” and πύλη (pyle) meaning ""door, gate, entrance"". This was the name of a queen of Lemnos in Greek mythology. When the women of Lemnos massacred all of the men on the island, Hypsipyle saved her father Thoas."
Hyrah,f,,English,,,,,,,,Not available.
Hyrcanus,m,Υρκανός(Greek),History,/hɜːr.ˈkeɪ.nəs/,,,,,,,"Means ""of Hyrcania"", Hyrcania being a Greek form of the Persian name Verkâna, which meant ""wolf-land"" and referred to a historical region in Persia. Hyrcanus was a member of the Hasmonean dynasty, the Jewish High Priest and King of Judea in the 1st century BC."
Hyrie,f,"Ὑρίη, Θυρίη(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"This name originated from the myth of Cycnus and Phylius, Hyrie is the mother of Cycnus, a beautiful vain young boy who repels all others except for Phylius. In order to have Phylius prove his loyalty, Cycnus makes Phylius complete a series of impossible tasks. When Phylius does not complete the last task and instead leaves, Cycnus jumps from a cliff and is turned by his father Apollo into a swan. Thinking Cycnus is dead, depending on the source, Hyrie either jumps from the same cliff and the lake is named after her, or she cries until her tears for lake Hyrie."
Hyrieus,m,Ὑριεύς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ˈhɪr.i.ˌuːs/(English),,,,,,,"Uncertain etymology. This was the name of a son of Poseidon in Greek mythology, in most accounts the eponymous king of Hyria. In later sources, he is sometimes said to be the father of Orion."
Hyrkanos,m,Ὑρκανός(Ancient Greek),Late Greek|Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Greek form of Hyrcanus.
Hyrom,m,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Hiram
Hyronimus,m,,Banat Swabian|German (Bessarabian),,,,,,,,Variant of Hieronymus.
Hyrrokkin,f,,Norse Mythology,/ˈhyr.ˌrokː.enː/,,,,,,,"Combination of Old Norse hyrr ""fire"" and a second element of debated meaning. Proposed interpretations of the second element are ""steamer"", ""curly, wrinkled"", ""withered"", and ""smoked"". Hyrrokkin is a jötunn (giantess) in Norse mythology. She launched Baldr's funeral ship, Hringhorni, after all the other Aesir gods were unable to."
Hyrtacus,m,Ὕρτακος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),/ˈhɜːr.tə.kəs/(English)|/hýr.ta.kos/(Attic Greek)|/ˈyr.ta.kos/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Possibly a hellenised form of Hittite ḫartakkaš meaning ""bear (animal)"", cognate with Ancient Greek ἄρκτος (arktos). Alternatively, it could be derived from an ancient Cretan city of the same name. This was the name of a comrade of King Priam of Troy in Greek mythology. He married Arisbe, daughter of King Merops of Percote, after Priam had divorced her to marry Hecabe."
Hysaé,f,,French (Rare),,,,,,,,Variant of Hisaé
Hysebeorht,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈxy.se.ˌberxt/(Old English)|[ˈhy.ze.ˌberˠxt](Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements hyse ""warrior, young man"" and beorht ""bright""."
Hysen,m,,Albanian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Hysenoð,m,,Anglo-Saxon,/ˈxy.se.noːθ/(Old English),,,,,,,"Derived from the Old English elements hyse ""warrior, young man"" and noð ""boldness, daring""."
Hysni,m,,Albanian,,,,,,,,Albanian form of Husni.
Hysnie,f,,Albanian,,,,,,,,Variant of Hysnije.
Hysnije,f,,Albanian,,,,,,,,Albanian form of Hüsniye.
Hyssop,f,,Medieval English (Rare),,,,,,,,"Derived from Latin hyssopus ""hyssop"" (Middle English ysope). This rare name was not used outside of England."
Hystaspes,m,Ὑστάσπης(Ancient Greek),Old Persian (Hellenized)|History,/hys.tás.pɛːs/(Attic Greek)|/ys.ˈtas.pis/(Kione Greek),,,,,,,Hellenized form of Vishtaspa. This was the name of the father of Darius the Great.
Hystercine,f,,Obscure,,,,,,,,Given name of Mississippi born quilter and folk artist Hystercine Gray Rankin.
Hytalo,m,,"Portuguese (Brazilian, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Italo.
Hyug,m,,Scots,,,,,,,,Variant of Shug.
Hyūga,m,"日向, 陽向, 飛向, 陽雅, 彪翔, 彪我, 彪駕, 日向雅, etc.(Japanese Kanji)|ひゅうが(Japanese Hiragana)|ヒュウガ(Japanese Katakana)",Japanese (Modern),[çɨ́ᵝɨ̀ᵝ.ɡà],,,,,,,"Derived from place name Hyūga, as a name, also written as 陽向.Other kanji in use include 飛 meaning ""fly, soar"" or 彪, which can refer to a small tiger or otherwise the stripes of a tiger, for the first element, and 雅 meaning ""elegance, grace,"" 翔 meaning ""fly, soar,"" 我 meaning ""self, ego"" or 駕 meaning ""vehicle, horse-drawn carriage​"" for the second element."
Hyuga,m,"日向, 陽向, 飛向, 陽雅, 彪翔, 彪我, 彪駕, 日向雅, etc.(Japanese Kanji)|ひゅうが(Japanese Hiragana)|ヒュウガ(Japanese Katakana)",Japanese (Modern),[çɨ́ᵝɨ̀ᵝ.ɡà],,,,,,,Variant transcription of Hyūga.
Hyugo,m,Хьюго(Russian)|Г’юґо(Ukrainian),Russian|Ukrainian,/ˈxʲjʉ.go/(Russian)|/ˈxʲjʉ.gə/(Russian)|/ˈɦju.go/(Ukrainian),,,,,,,"Russian and Ukrainian variant of Hugo, reflecting the English pronunciation."
Hyuk,m,혁(Korean Hangul),Korean,HYUK,,,,,,,Variant transcription of Hyeok.
Hyulnara,f,Гюльнара(Ukrainian),Muslim (Ukrainianized),,,,,,,,Ukrainianised form of Gulnara and variants.
Hyuna,f,현아(Korean Hangul)|泫雅(Korean Hanja),Korean,,,,,,,,"Moon Hyuna (born 1987), stage name Hyuna, South Korean singer, former member of girl group Nine MusesKim Hyuna (born 1992), stage name Hyuna, South Korean singer, former member of girl groups 4minute and Wonder Girls"
Hyun-ah,f,,Korean,,,,,,,,Not available.
Hyunbin,,현빈(Korean Hangul),Korean,/hjʌn.bin/,,,,,,,"Derived from 賢(hyun) meaning ""clever, benevolent"" or 顯(hyun) meaning ""prominent, to appear"" combined with 彬(bin) meaning ""bright, shine"". Other hanja combinations can also form this name."
Hyuncheol,m,현철(Korean Hangul),Korean,,,,,,,,"From 賢 ""virtuous, worthy, good"" and 哲 ""wise, sagacious""."
Hyun-chul,m,"현철(Korean Hangul)|賢哲, 顯哲, 炫哲, 鉉哲, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/çʌ̹n.t͡ɕʰʌ̹ɭ/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Korean Hangeul 현철 (see Hyeon-cheol).
Hyung-bae,m,형배(Korean Hangul),Korean,HYUNG-BEH,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Korean Hangul 형배 (See Hyeong-bae)
Hyung-joon,m,"형준(Korean Hangul)|泂俊, 泂准, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/çʌ̹ŋ.d͡ʑun/,,,,,,,Alternate transcription of Korean Hangul 형준 (see Hyeong-jun).
