Icih,f,ᮄᮎᮤᮂ(Sundanese),Sundanese,,,,,,,,Variant of Esih.
Icilda,f,,Jamaican Patois,ee-ZEEL-da,,,,,,,Possibly a variant of Izilda.
Icilia,f,,Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,Feminine form of Icilio.
Ick,m,,English,IK,,,,,,,Diminutive of Ichabod.
Icka,f,,Low German|East Frisian,IK-a,,,,,,,Feminine form of Icko.
Icke,,,German|Low German (Archaic)|East Frisian (Archaic),IK-ə,,,,,,,"Icke is a nickname for people from Berlin alluding to the Berlin dialekt word Icke (High German Ich) ""I"".Icke is the popular nickname for the German football player (winner of the 1990 World Cup) Thomas Häßler.Icke was also used in East Friesland and Northern Germany as a variant form of either Icko or Icka."
Ickea,f,,East Frisian|Low German,IK-ay-a,,,,,,,Clearly feminine form of Icke.
Icko,m,,Low German|East Frisian,IK-o,,,,,,,"Short form of names built from the name element agjō ""edge (of a sword)"" like Ekkehard."
İclal,f,,Turkish,EEJ-LAL,,,,,,,Not available.
Icnocuauh,m,,Nahuatl,,,,,,,,"Means ""humble eagle"" or ""sad eagle"" in Nahuatl, from icno- ""humble; in a sad state, bereaved"" (from icnotl ""an orphan, someone or something poor, humble, worthy of compassion and aid"") and cuauhtli ""eagle"". It can also be derived directly from the vocabulary word icnocuauhtli, ""unfortunate eagle warrior"" or ""humble eagle warrior""."
Icnonemitl,m,,Nahuatl,,,,,,,,"Means ""one who lives humbly"" in Nahuatl, from icno- ""humble; in a sad state, bereaved"" (from icnotl ""an orphan; someone or something poor, humble, worthy of compassion and aid"") and nemi ""to dwell, to live (as)""."
Icnoyotl,,,Nahuatl,,,,,,,,"Means ""misery, poverty"" or ""compassion, mercy"" in Nahuatl."
Ico,m,イコ(Japanese Katakana),Popular Culture|Guanche|Spanish (Canarian),"EE-ko|EE-ko(Guanche, Canarian Spanish)",,,,,,,"Ico is the name of the protagonist in the Japanese action-adventure game 'Ico', developed by Team Ico (named after the game) and Sony Computer Entertainment. Ico is a young boy who was born with horns, which his village considers a bad omen. Warriors lock him away in an abandoned fortress and during his explorations of the fortress, Ico encounters Yorda, the daughter of the castle's Queen. The Queen plans to use Yorda's body to extend her own lifespan. Learning this, Ico seeks to escape the castle with Yorda, keeping her safe from the shadow-like creatures that attempt to draw her back.The name Ico was created by the developers as a play on the Japanese ikou (行こう) meaning ""let's go"", referencing the fact that Ico is leading Yorda through the castle in their escape."
Icolton,f,,Nahuatl,,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from icoltia ""to desire, to want"" and the diminutive suffix -ton."
Icovellauna,f,,Celtic Mythology,,,,,,,,"meaning is unknown, name of a Celtic water goddess"
İçten,f,,Turkish,/it͡ʃ.ˈten/,,,,,,,"Means ""sincere"" in Turkish."
Ictinus,m,"Ἰκτῖνος, Ἴκτινος(Ancient Greek)",Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,/ɪk.ˈtaɪ.nəs/(English),,,,,,,"Means ""red kite (bird)"" in Ancient Greek. This was the name of one of the architects of the Parthenon, as well as of a figure in Greek mythology who was turned into a kite after attempting to assault his daughter."
Icuța,f,,Romanian (Rare),,,,,,,,Diminutive of Ica.
Icuthiel,m,,Biblical|Biblical Latin,,,,,,,,Form of Jekuthiel used in the Douay-Rheims Bible (1582-1610) and the Clementine Vulgate (1592). The latter was the official Bible of the Roman Catholic Church for nearly four centuries: from the year it was published until 1979.
Icy,f,,"English (American, Rare, Archaic)",IE-see(American English),,,,,,,"Variant of Icie. The spelling was perhaps influenced by the English word ""icy"" meaning ""pertaining to, resembling, or abounding in ice; cold; frosty; or characterized by coldness, as of manner, influence""."
Icyang,m,,Amis,/i.t͡sjaŋ/,,,,,,,Not available.
Icylinn,f,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,Variant of Icelyn.
Iczo,,,Nahuatl,,,,,,,,"Derived from Nahuatl izcotl, a kind of yucca tree."
I-da,f,"이다(Korean Hangul)|利多, etc.(Korean Hanja)",Korean,/i.da̠/,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 利 (i) meaning ""advantage, benefit"" combined with 多 (da) meaning ""much, many"". Other Hanja combinations are possible.This could alternatively be a Korean borrowing of the name Ida."
Ída,f,,Icelandic,,,,,,,,Icelandic form of Ida.
Îdå,f,,Walloon,,,,,,,,Variant of Ida.
Ida,f,"इडा(Sanskrit, Hindi, Marathi, Nepali)|Ида(Russian)",Sardinian|Irish (Anglicized)|Sanskrit|Hindi|Indian|Hinduism|Marathi|Nepali|East Frisian (Archaic)|Sundanese|Soviet,/ˈi.da/,,,,,,,Both a borrowing of the Italian name and a short form of Vitalia via the short form Fida.
Idà,f,,Provençal|Niçard,,,,,,,,Niçard diminutive of Adelaìda.
Ida Ayu,f,ᬇᬤᬅᬬᬸ(Balinese),Balinese,/ˈi.da ˈa.ju/,,,,,,,Indicates that a person is from the Brahmana caste
Ida Bagus,m,ᬇᬤ​ᬩᬕᬸᬲ᭄(Balinese),Balinese,/ˈi.da ˈba.ɡʊs/,,,,,,,Indicates a person from the Brahmana caste
Idabel,f,,"Spanish (Latin American, Rare)|Spanish (Caribbean, Rare)|American (Hispanic, Rare)",,,,,,,,Contraction of Ida and Isabel.
Idabell,f,,"English (American, Rare)",,,,,,,,Combination of Ida and Bell.
Idabelle,f,,English (Rare),,,,,,,,"Combination of Ida and Belle, perhaps influenced by Isabelle."
Idaberga,f,,Old High German,,,,,,,,Old High German variant of Iduberga.
Idabet,f,,"Spanish (Latin American, Rare)|American (Hispanic, Rare)",,,,,,,,Combination of Ida and Elisabet.
Idabritt,f,,Swedish (Rare),,,,,,,,Very rare combination of Ida and Britt.
