Idalius,m,,"Germanic (Latinized, ?)|Medieval Spanish (?)",,,,,,,,"Masculine form of Idalia. This name was borne by a 7th-century bishop, Idalius of Barcelona."
Idally,f,,"Spanish (Latin American, Latinized)|American (Hispanic)",,,,,,,,Not available.
Idalma,f,,Spanish (Latin American)|Spanish (Caribbean)|Portuguese (Brazilian),[i.ˈdal.ma](Latin American Spanish),,,,,,,"Elaboration of Idalia, probably influenced by the word alma ""soul""."
Idalmis,f,,Caribbean,,,,,,,,Not available.
Idalys,f,,Spanish (Latin American),,,,,,,,Not available.
Idamae,f,,English,,,,,,,,Combination of Ida and Mae.
Idamaja,f,,Swedish (Rare),,,,,,,,Combination of Ida and Maja 1 or Maja 2.
Idamalia,f,,"German (Modern, Rare)",ee-da-MA-li-a,,,,,,,Blend of Ida and Amalia.
Idamaria,f,,Finnish (Rare)|Swedish (Rare)|Italian (Rare),,,,,,,,Combination of Ida and Maria.
Idamarie,f,,English (?)|Danish|Swedish,,,,,,,,"Combination of Ida and Marie, see Idamaria"
Idana,f,עידנה(Hebrew),"Hebrew (Modern, Rare)",ee-DAHN-nah,,,,,,,Feminine form of Idan.
Idanell,f,,"English (American, Rare)|American (South, Rare)",,,,,,,,"Combination of Ida and Nell. This name was borne by Idanell 'Nellie' Connally (1919-2006), wife of the 39th governor of Texas, John Connally."
Idania,f,,Spanish (Caribbean)|Spanish (Latin American)|Spanish (Rare),,,,,,,,Not available.
Idanli,,עידן-לי(Hebrew),"Hebrew (Modern, Rare)",ee-DAHN-lee,,,,,,,"Combination of the names Idan and Lee means ""my era"" in Hebrew."
Idanya,,עידניה(Hebrew),"Hebrew (Modern, Rare)",ee-DAHN-yah,,,,,,,"Means ""era of God"", from the Hebrew name Idan and the letters יָה (yah) referring to the Hebrew God."
Idar,m,אידר(Hebrew),"Norwegian|Swedish (Rare)|Danish (Rare)|Hebrew (Modern, Rare)",ee-DAHR,,,,,,,"Possibly intended to be a masculine form of Ida created by combining the Old Norse element ið ""industrious, work, activity"" (compare the Germanic element id) and the common name suffix -r, taken from Norse herr ""army, warrior""."
Idara,f,,Hausa,,,,,,,,"Means ""joy"" in Hausa."
İdare,,,Turkish,,,,,,,,"From the Arabic إدارة (idâre(t)) meaning ""managing and organising""."
Idas,m,Ἴδας(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ˈiː.dəs/(English)|/ˈaɪ.dəs/(English)|/ǐː.daːs/(Attic Greek)|/ˈi.ðas/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Ancient Greek ἴδη (ide) meaning ""timber tree, wood; wooded hill"". This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including a Messenian prince who was a member of the Argonauts, one of the fifty sons of king Aegyptus who was murdered by his wife Hippodice on their wedding night, a Dactyl representing the pinky finger, and an Ethiopian chief."
Idashia,f,,African American,,,,,,,,Not available.
Idäus,m,,East Frisian,,,,,,,,Variation of Idde recorded in East Frisia in the 19th and 20th century.
Idawati,f,,Indonesian,/i.da.ˈwa.ti/,,,,,,,Combination of the name Ida and the feminine suffix -wati.
Idaya,,,English,,,,,,,,Not available.
Idbert,m,,East Frisian,,,,,,,,Variation of Edbert.
Idburg,f,,Germanic,,,,,,,,"Old Saxon combination of idis ""woman"", ""virgin"" and burg ""castle""."
