İdil,f,𐰦𐰵𐰠(Orkhon Turkic: İtil / İdil),"Turkish (Modern, Archaic)",ee-DEEL,,,,,,,"İdil is a feminine Turkish name, which is the modern Turkish name for the Volga River (historically known as İtil or Atil).There are two primary etymological theories regarding the origin of the river's name:1. It is believed to stem from the Old Turkic root ediz, meaning ""high"", ""exalted"", or ""magnificent"", reflecting the majesty and scale of the river.2. Many linguists and historians point to the Proto-Turkic numeral yetti or Old Turkic yedi (""seven"") as the root. Due to the Volga’s vastness and its numerous sprawling branches, it was historically perceived as the ""Seven-Fold"" or ""Seven-Branched"" river. Over centuries, phonetic shifts (such as Yedi-il to Edil/İtil) smoothed into the modern İdil.Crucially, the native Turkic name İdil is entirely unrelated to the European loanword idyll or idylle (from Greek ""eidyllion,"" meaning ""rural tranquility""), despite occasional folk-etymological conflations or superficial phonetic similarities introduced in mid-20th-century dictionaries. The name is inherently geographic and hydronymic in origin.The Tatar variant of the name, Edil (or İdel), is used as a gender-neutral name."
Idil,f,,Turkish|Somali,,,,,,,,"Turkish feminine name derived from idil ""idyll"".İdil Biret (*1941) is a Turkish concert pianist, renowned for her interpretations of the Romantic repertoire.İdil Üner (*1971) is a German-Turkish actress."
