İla,f,إِلا(#Ila#) Arabic Script (Mahmud al-Kashgari)|𐰃𐰜𐰀(#Yila# / #Ila#) Old Turkic Runic (Orkhon)|Іле(İle) Cyrillic Script (Central Asian Context),"Medieval Turkic (Modern, Archaic)",ee-LAH,,,,,,,"İla is a newly rising feminine name of Old Turkic origin, historically functioning as a major hydronym in Central Asia. The name is derived from the Old Turkic root ıl- or yıla-, which relates to ""flowing water, current,"" ""to flow gently,"" """"to flow, to run (water)"", standard in ancient Turkic river-naming conventions. The ancestor of modern Turkish ılık and ılıca. In this context, the hydronym originally meant ""the warm, gently flowing river/spring"".According to the 11th-century Turkic lexicographer Mahmud al-Kashgari in his monumental work Divânu Lugâti't-Türk, the name is defined as follows: ""İla: Bir ırmak adıdır. Türklerden Yağma, Toxsı (Tuhsi) ve Çiğillerden bir bölüğün indiği bir deredir. Bu ırmak Türk illerinin Ceyhun'udur."" (The river Ila is a stream where branches of the Turkic Yağma, Tukhsı, and Chigil tribes settled. This river is the Oxus/Jayhun of the Turkic lands). Kashgari's comparison to the Oxus (Amu Darya) highlights the river's massive cultural and geographic significance to the medieval Turkic peoples, particularly those whose modern descendants (such as the Uyghurs and Kazakhs) still live along its basin (now widely known as the Ili River).Rebuttal of Alternative Etymologies:1. The Mongolian Theory (İli / İlansu):Some alternative views attempt to link the name to the Mongolian word ili (meaning ""clear, open, visible"") or ilansu. However, this is chronologically and historically inaccurate. The hydronym İla was already deeply established in Turkic tribal geography and recorded in Turkic runic inscriptions long before the Mongol expansion or any significant Mongol dominance in the region. During the period when the river was firmly named and documented by Kashgari, Mongol presence in the Ili basin was minimal and non-dominant, proving that the name originates from the indigenous Turkic populations (Yağma, Tukhsı, Chigil) who controlled and inhabited the valley.2. The Indo-European Theory (Saka, Yuezhi, or Tocharian):Other hypotheses suggest a connection to Indo-European languages once spoken in Eastern Turkestan (such as Saka or Sanskrit), claiming roots that mean ""water, river, current"" or ""shining, clean water"". These claims lack solid phonetic and morphological evidence within the specific context of the region's historical toponymy. The consistent structural appearance of the name in the earliest Turkic texts reinforces its native Turkic adaptation and development, distinct from Indo-European roots.3. The Contemporary Arabic Expat Theory:Modern etymological claims (such as those found in Nişanyan's lexicon) try to categorize modern instances of the name İla exclusively as a recent Arabic/Syrian expat import (إيلا), potentially inspired by the Western name Ella. While a homonymous modern Arabic name exists, applying this etymology to the traditional name İla is a historical oversight. It completely ignores a thousand-year-old Central Asian Turkic heritage. The modern resurgence of İla in the Turkic world is a conscious revival of the historic, prestigious Turkic river name rather than a borrowing from Western-influenced Arabic neologisms."
