Iliona,f,"Ἰλίωνα, Ἰλιόνη(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Albanian,,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Ilione, as well as the Italian, French, Russian and Ukrainian form. Alternatively, it might be spelled Ἰλίωνα (Iliona) in Greek. In Greek myth Iliona or Ilione was a Trojan princess, a daughter of Priam and Hecuba and wife of Polymestor, a king in Thrace."
Ilione,f,Ἰλιόνη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek place name Ἴλιον (Ilion), an alternative name of Troy, the ancient city that was besieged by the Greeks in Homer's Iliad. In Greek mythology Ilione or Iliona is a Trojan princess, the eldest daughter of Priam, who later becomes a queen of the Thracian Chersonese as the wife of Polymnestor or Polymestor. She is briefly mentioned in Virgil's Aeneid: Aeneas gives her scepter to Dido."
Ilioneus,m,,Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology|Roman Mythology,,,,,,,,"Ilioneus is the name of a few characters throughout Greek and Roman mythology, including the Aeneid, the Iliad, and Metamorphoses."
Ilios,m,Ήλιος(Greek),Greek (Rare),,,,,,,,Modern Greek transcription of Helios.
Iliostalakti,f,Ηλιοστάλακτη(Greek),Greek (Rare),,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek ἥλιος (helios) meaning ""sun"" and the verb stalazo meaning ""to drip down slowly"", taken from a word used in the Greek Orthodox religion to describe God's throne. The word ἡλιοστάλακτος (heliostalaktos) is used in Greek texts to describe the Dormition of the Mother of God (which corresponds to the Assumption in the Western Church). For this reason, the name day for Iliostalakti in the Orthodox liturgical calendar is August 15 - the Feast of the Dormition."
