İlkem,f,,Turkish (Modern),eerl-KEM,,,,,,,"Derived from the Turkish noun İlke meaning ""principle"", which itself comes from the Old Turkic root ilk meaning ""first"". The name is formed by attaching the Turkish first-person singular possessive suffix -m, giving it the literal meaning of ""my principle"" or ""my first"". It is conceptually related to the names İlke and İlkim. In a cultural context, the name carries a deep sense of foundational values and personal ethics, beautifully illustrated by the traditional Turkish Student ideal: ""İlkem; küçüklerimi sevmek, büyüklerimi saymaktır"" (""My principle is to love the young and respect the old"")."
İlkgüz,f,,Turkish,eelk-GOYZ,,,,,,,"Derived from the Turkish vocabulary words ilk meaning ""first"" and güz meaning ""autumn, fall"". In old Turkish calendar traditions and certain regional dialects, the term ilkgüz literally translates to ""first autumn"" and is traditionally used as a poetic or colloquial name for the month of ""September"".This name reflects the serene beauty of the changing seasons and the poetic beginning of harvest time. It is linguistically related to other seasonal time-markers such as Ortagüz (literally ""middle autumn"", meaning ""October"") and Songüz (literally ""last autumn"", meaning ""November""), forming a harmonious trilogy of classical Turkish autumn names."
İlkhan,m,,Turkish,,,,,,,,"From Turkish ilk meaning ""first"" and han meaning ""khan, ruler""."
Ilkhomjon,m,,Uzbek,,,,,,,,Variant of Ilhomjon.
İlkim,f,,Turkish,EEL-keem,,,,,,,"From the Turkish word ilk meaning ""first"", combined with the first-person possessive suffix -m (""my""). The name literally translates to ""my first"".In Turkish cultural context, the word carries a deep emotional weight of endearment, often associated with the traditional expression ""ilkgöz ağrım"" (literally meaning ""the first pain of my eye""), which is used to describe one's absolute firstborn child or first true love. It beautifully reflects the concepts of primacy, uniqueness, and lifelong devotion, often paired in literary contexts with the sentiment ""İlkim ve sonum"" (""My first and my last"")."
Ilko,m,"Илко(Bulgarian)|Ілько(Ukrainian, Rusyn)",Bulgarian|Ukrainian|Rusyn|German (Rare),/ˈilʲ.ko/(Ukrainian),,,,,,,"Bulgarian diminutive of Iliya. Since the 1960s it has been in occasional use in German-speaking countries. As Ukrainian diminutive, it is used more commonly in Western Ukraine (as diminutive for Illya)."
