Imentet,f,,Egyptian Mythology,,,,,,,,"Means ""she of the west"". In Egyptian mythology she was the goddess representing the necropolis west of the Nile River and the consort of Aken. Typically depicted wearing the hieroglyph for 'west' on her head, she often appeared on tombs to welcome the deceased into the afterlife. She may also possibly be just an alternate form of Hathor or Isis, as she was closely linked with them."
Imenti,m,,Kikuyu,,,,,,,,"Means ""believer of faith"" in Kikuyu."
İmer,f,,Turkish,[ee-MEHR]|[ee-mahr],,,,,,,"İmer is an authentic Turkish name rooted in Old Turkic economic, social, and nomadic vocabulary. It carries two prominent, deeply historical semantic definitions. In Western Oghuz and Anatolian folk dialects (heavily documented in the national Derleme Sözlüğü), it translates to ""wealthy,"" ""prosperous,"" ""bountiful,"" or ""exceedingly generous and open-handed."" Concurrently, in its deeper Central Asian tribal context, it means ""companion,"" ""trusted friend,"" or ""kinsman from the same clan.""Linguistically, İmer is a direct phonetic evolution of the archaic Turkic root Bay / May / İmay, which signifies spiritual and material abundance, divine favor, and ulu (exalted) stature.Other Origins:Due to its minimalist phonetic architecture, İmer is highly prone to cultural convergence and accidental homophony across Western and Near Eastern languages:Albanian: It exists as the distinct masculine name Imer, which is a regional, Balkan-influenced variant of the classical name Ymer.Arabic: It is frequently conflated by folk etymologists with Ömer (عمر), meaning ""life,"" ""long-lived,"" or ""thriving,"" or with Emir (أمير), meaning ""commander"" or ""prince,"" due to late Ottoman administrative registers and phonetic assimilation in mixed geographic regions.For parents, İmer represents a beautiful balance between material success and communal altruism. It carries the ancestral wish that a child will not only achieve a life of profound abundance and emotional wealth but will also possess the noble, open-handed nature to share that fortune, acting as a reliable companion and a source of prosperity for their entire family."
