Ipalnemohuani,m,,Aztec and Toltec Mythology|Nahuatl,/iː.pɑl.ne.mo.ˈwɑ.ni/(Nahuatl),,,,,,,"Means ""he through whom we live"" or ""giver of life"" in Nahuatl. This was one of several names for a supreme Aztec deity. It later came to be used as a term for the Christian god."
İpar,f,إپار(Uyghur Arabic)|ئىپار(Modern Uyghur)|Ипар(Cyrillic),"Medieval Turkic (Rare, Archaic)",[ee-PAHR],,,,,,,"Derived from the Old Turkic root yipar meaning ""musk"" or ""fragrance."" In ancient Central Asian contexts, the term specifically referred to the rare and precious scent of the musk deer, symbolizing something of divine origin or extreme rarity.The name is inextricably linked to the legendary 18th-century figure İparhan (also known as the ""Fragrant Concubine/Xiang Fei""), an Uyghur princess from the Khoja clan of Altishahr. According to legend, her body possessed a natural, enchanting scent of yipar without the use of perfumes. Her history is one of tragic defiance; following the Qing conquest of her homeland, she was taken to the court of the Qianlong Emperor. In Uyghur tradition, she is celebrated as a symbol of national loyalty and chastity, having resisted the Emperor’s advances to remain faithful to her heritage and her late husband.She is often identified with or viewed as the historical counterpart to Dilşad Hatun, the ""Joyful Lady,"" whose narrative mirrors the themes of beauty, grief, and the preservation of one's dignity under foreign rule.For parents, the name symbolizes a child who carries a soul as enduring and evocative as the scent of the desert bloom. It suggests a personality that is both resilient, like the silken strength of a nomadic legacy, and unforgettable, possessing an innate purity that lingers in the hearts of others like a sacred memory. To name a child İpar is to bestow upon them a legacy of ""living fragrance""; a beauty that does not merely fade with the eye, but remains anchored in the spirit, a testament to unwavering loyalty and grace."
Ipar,m,,Basque|Basque Mythology,[i.par](Basque),,,,,,,"Derived from Basque ipar ""north; north wind"". In Basque mythology, Ipar, the north wind, is married to the daughter of the north-easterly wind who calms his anger."
