Iphianassa,f,Ίφιάνασσα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Late Greek,/ˌɪ.fi.ə.ˈnæ.sə/(English),,,,,,,"Means ""powerful queen"" or ""rule strongly"", derived from Greek ἶφι (iphi) meaning ""by force, mightily"" (compare ἴφιος (iphios) ""strong, stout"") and ἄνασσα (anassa) meaning ""queen"" (feminine form of ἄναξ (anax) ""lord, master""). This was the name of several characters in ancient Greek mythology, including one of the Nereids."
Iphianeira,f,Ἰφιάνειρα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἴφιος (iphios) meaning ""strong, stout"" and possibly ἀνήρ (aner) meaning ""man""."
Iphicles,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Iphikles. In Greek mythology, Iphicles was the mortal twin to Heracles, son of Alcmene and Amphitryon."
Iphiclus,m,Ἴφικλος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),/ˈɪf.ɪ.kləs/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἴφιος (iphios) meaning ""strong, stout"" and κλέος (kleos) meaning ""glory"", making it an alternative form of Iphicles."
Iphicrate,m,,History (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Iphicrates.
Iphicrates,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Iphikrates. This name was borne by an Athenian general from the 4th century BC.
Iphigene,f,,"American (Rare, Archaic)",,,,,,,,Altered form of Iphigenia.
Iphigénie,f,,"French (Rare)|French (Belgian, Rare)",,,,,,,,French form of Iphigenia.
Iphikles,m,Ἰφικλῆς(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ˈɪf.ɪ.ˌkliːz/(English)|/ˈaɪ.fɪ.ˌkliːz/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἴφιος (iphios) meaning ""strong, stout, mighty"" combined with κλέος (kleos) meaning ""glory"", making it an alternative form of Iphiclus."
Iphikrates,m,Ἰφικράτης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/iː.pʰi.krá.tɛːs/(Attic Greek)|/i.ɸi.ˈkra.tis/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἴφιος (iphios) meaning ""strong, stout, mighty"" combined with κράτος (kratos) meaning ""power, strength"". This was the name of a famous Athenian general of the 4th century BCE."
Iphimedeia,f,Ἰφιμέδεια(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/ˌɪf.ɪ.mɪ.ˈdaɪ.ə/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἴφιος (iphios) meaning ""strong, stout"" combined with either μεδω (medo) meaning ""to protect, to rule over"" or the related μέδομαι (medomai) meaning ""to be mindful of, provide for, think on"". In Greek mythology, Iphimedeia was a Thessalian princess, the daughter of King Triopas (a son of Poseidon and Canace). She fell in love with Poseidon and bore him children including Otus and Ephialtes (who were called the Aloadae after their stepfather), as well as Cercyon and the bandit Sciron. She was one of the heroines whose spirits Odysseus encountered at the entrance of the Underworld."
Iphimédie,f,,Greek Mythology (Gallicized),,,,,,,,French form of Iphimedeia.
Iphimedusa,f,Ἰφιμέδουσα(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἴφιος (iphios) meaning ""strong, stout"" and μέδω (medo) meaning ""to protect, to rule over"". This was the name of one of the fifty daughters of King Danaus in Greek mythology, killing her husband, Euchenor, on their wedding night."
Iphinoe,f,Ιφινόη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Ancient Greek ἴφιος (iphios) meaning ""strong stout"" or ἶφι (iphi) meaning ""by force, mightily"" combined with νόος (noos) meaning ""mind, thought"". This is the name of several characters in Greek mythology, including an Argive princess and a Lemnian herald."
Iphinoé,f,,"Greek (Gallicized, Rare)",,,,,,,,French form of Iphinoe. A notable bearer is Greek-French synchronized swimmer Iphinoé Davvetas (1992-).
Iphis,,Ἶφις(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ˈaɪ.fɪs/(English)|/ˈɪf.ɪs/(English)|/ˈi.ɸis/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἶφι (iphi) meaning ""by force, mightily"" (see ἴφιος (iphios) ""strong, stout""). This was the name of several characters in Greek myth, both male and female, including one of the fifty daughters of king Thespius, a king of Argos, a slave woman gifted to Patroclus by Achilles, an Argonaut (also called Iphitos), and a Cretan youth born a girl, raised as a boy, and eventually transformed into a man by the goddess Isis."
Iphition,m,Ἰφιτίων(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Patronymic form of Iphitos. In Greek mythology, this is the name of two Trojans."
Iphitos,m,Ἴφιτος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/ˈi.ɸi.tos/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἴφιος (iphios) meaning ""strong, stout, mighty"". This was the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including two different Argonauts and a king of Elis who restored the Olympic Games after the Dorian invasion."
Iphitus,m,,Greek Mythology (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Iphitos. This is the name of several characters in Greek mythology, one of them being a king of Elis."
Iphogenia,f,,English,,,,,,,,variation of Iphigenia
Iphthime,f,Ἰφθίμη(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,/iph.thǐː.mɛː/(Attic Greek)|/iɸ.ˈθi.mi/(Koine Greek)|/ɪp.ˈθaɪ.miː/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἴφθιμος (iphthimos), an epithet approximately meaning ""strong, stalwart"" as well as ""comely, goodly"" and perhaps ""loyal, valiant"". This name was borne by a sister of Penelope in Homer's epic the Odyssey."
