Iraj,m,"ایرج(Persian)|ईरज(Sanskrit, Hindi, Marathi, Nepali)",Persian|Persian Mythology|Indian,EEraj,,,,,,,"From Avestan 𐬀𐬌𐬭𐬌𐬌𐬀 (airiia), literally ""Aryan""; ultimately cognate to Arya 1. In Persian traditional history he was the youngest son of Fereydoun and the eponymous hero of the Iranians."
Iraja,f,,Old Coptic|History (Ecclesiastical),,,,,,,,"Possibly a form of Herais. Iraja and her brother Abadir are saints in the Coptic Church and the Roman Catholic Church. They are reported to have been children of the sister of Basilides, the father of kings. According to legend, Abadir and Iraja fled from Antioch to Alexandria, were arrested there, brought to Antinoöpolis and beheaded there with a physician and priest named Cluthus and 3,685 other companions."
Irakleios,m,Ηράκλειος(Greek),Greek (Rare),,,,,,,,Modern Greek form of Herakleios.
Iral,m,,African American,,,,,,,,Not available.
Iraleine,f,,English,,,,,,,,Not available.
Iram,f,إرم(Arabic)|ارم(Urdu),Arabic (Rare)|Urdu,/ˈʔiː.ram/(Arabic),,,,,,,"From the name of a mythical lost city mentioned in the Qur'an, possibly derived from an Arabic word meaning ""toss, throw off"". According to legend, Allah destroyed the city with a sandstorm after its inhabitants (called Ad or Aad) refused to accept the teachings of Islam."
Irama,m,,Aymara,,,,,,,,"Means ""hillside"" in Aymara."
Irán,f,,Spanish (Rare),,,,,,,,Irán Castillo (1977-) is a Mexican singer and actress.
