Irantzu,f,,Basque,/i.ˈran.tsu/,,,,,,,"Derived from Basque ira, meaning ""fern"", and the abundancy suffix -tzu. It roughly translates as ""fernland"".There's a Cistercian monastery called ""Santa María la Real de Irantzu"" in the Abartzuza municipality in Navarre."
Irany,f,,Brazilian|Tupi,,,,,,,,Variant of Irani.
Iranzu,m,,Ancient Near Eastern,,,,,,,,"Of uncertain etymology, name borne by a king of Mannae (a small kingdom in present day north-western Iran) that ruled during the reign of Tiglath-Pileser."
Iras,f,,Theatre,,,,,,,,Meaning unknown. This name was used by Shakespeare for one of Cleopatra's maids of honour in his tragedy 'Anthony and Cleopatra' (1607).
Irasema,f,,Tupi|Portuguese (Brazilian)|Spanish (Mexican),/i.ɾa.ˈsẽ.mɐ/(Brazilian Portuguese),,,,,,,"Variant of Iracema.A known bearer of this name was the Brazilian actress Irasema Dilián (1924-1996), who was of Polish descent."
Iratsume,f,郎女(Japanese Kanji),Japanese,EE-ṘAH-TSUU-ME,,,,,,,"From Japanese 郎女 (iratsume) meaning ""a familiar or friendly term for a young woman""."
Iratxe,f,,Basque,/i.ˈɾa.t͡ʃe/,,,,,,,"From the name of a monastery located in Navarre, Spain, possibly derived from Basque iratze, meaning ""fern grove""."
Iratzeni,f,,"Spanish (Mexican, Rare)",,,,,,,,Not available.
Iraultza,,,Basque,,,,,,,,"Means ""revolution"" in Basque. It was coined at the beginning of the 21st century and used in Pamplona before the Spanish Civil War. During the dictatorship it was prohibited for two reasons: because the use of Basque names was forbidden and due to its 'subversive' meaning. Nowadays, it came back to use."
Iraupen,f,,Basque (Rare),ee-ROW-pen,,,,,,,Basque equivalent of Constancia.
Iravan,m,इरावान्(Sanskrit),Hinduism,,,,,,,,"From Sanskrit इरावत् (irāvat) meaning ""possessing food, full of food, granting refreshment, satiating"". In the Hindu epic the Mahabharata Iravan is the son of the hero Arjuna and the naga princess Ulupi. He is particularly revered in Tamil Nadu, where he is considered a village deity."
Iravati,f,इरावती(Sanskrit),Hinduism,/i.ˈɾɑː.ʋɐ.t̪iː/(Sanskrit),,,,,,,"Feminine form of Sanskrit इरावत् (irāvat) meaning ""possessing food, full of food, granting refreshment, satiating"". In Hindu mythology Iravati is the daughter of Kashyapa and Kadru as well as the mother of Airavata. She is often associated with the Ravi River in northern India."
Irawan,m,,Indonesian|Indonesian Mythology,/i.ˈra.wan/(Indonesian),,,,,,,"Indonesian form of Iravan, also featured as a character in the Javanese version of the Hindu epic the Mahabharata."
Irawati,f,,Indonesian,/i.ra.ˈwa.ti/,,,,,,,Indonesian form of Iravati.
Irawaty,f,,Indonesian,/i.ra.ˈwa.ti/,,,,,,,Variant of Irawati.
Irawo,,,Yoruba,,,,,,,,"Means ""star"" in Yoruba."
