Ísadóra,f,,Icelandic (Rare),,,,,,,,Icelandic form of Isadora. Icelandic singer Björk gave this name to her daughter in 2002.
Isae,f,"功栄, 功英, 功恵, 功枝, 功江, 伊佐江, 伊佐恵(Japanese Kanji)|いさえ(Japanese Hiragana)|イサエ(Japanese Katakana)",Japanese (Rare),EE-SAH-E,,,,,,,"This name can be used as 功栄, 功英, 功恵, 功枝, 功江, 伊佐江 or 伊佐恵 with 功 (ku, kou, isao, isa) meaning ""achievement, credit, honour, merits, success"", 伊 (i, kare) meaning ""Italy, that one"", 佐 (sa) meaning ""assistant, help"", 栄 (ei, you, e, saka.eru, ha.e, ha.eru, -ba.e) meaning ""flourish, glory, honour, prosperity, splendour"", 英 (ei, hanabusa, e) meaning ""England, English, excellent"", 恵 (e, kei, megu.mi, megu.mu) meaning ""blessing, favour, grace, kindness"", 枝 (shi, eda, e) meaning ""bough, branch, limb, twig"" and 江 (kou, e) meaning ""bay, creek, inlet.""This name is rarely given to girls, if given at all."
Isael,m,,Portuguese (Brazilian)|Spanish (Mexican),/i.za.ˈɛw/(Brazilian Portuguese)|/i.sa.ˈel/(Mexican Spanish),,,,,,,"Invented name, likely inspired by similar names like Ismael, Israel and Misael. Sometimes it has been reanalyzed as a combination of the Hebrew roots יָשַׁע (yashaʿ) meaning ""to save"" and אֵל (ʾel) meaning ""God"", thus ""God is salvation"" (similarly to Isaiah)."
Isaeus,m,,Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,Latinized form of Isaios. This name was borne by an Attic orator from the 4th century BC.
Isafira,f,,Indonesian,,,,,,,,Not available.
Ísafold,f,,Icelandic (Rare),,,,,,,,"From a poetic name for Iceland, derived from Old Norse íss meaning ""ice"" and fold meaning ""earth, land; field""."
Isagel,f,,Swedish (Rare)|Literature|Astronomy,,,,,,,,"Isagel is a fictional character and spaceship pilot appearing in Swedish author Harry Martinson's poem of science fiction 'Aniara', published in 1956. Isagel is also the name of an exoplanet (HD 102956 b) orbiting the star Aniara (HD 102956)."
Isagi,,"潔, 伊佐木, 伊佐義(Japanese Kanji)|いさぎ(Japanese Hiragana)|イサギ(Japanese Katakana)","Japanese (Modern, Rare)",IH-SAH-GEE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 伊佐 (isa) meaning ""that"" combined with 義 (gi) meaning ""brave, valiant, courageous"" or 木 (gi) meaning ""tree or wood"". Derived from Japanese 潔い (isagiyoi) meaning ""pure, sportsmanlike, manly, clean, undefiled, upright or straightforward"", 潔く (isagiyoku) meaning ""honorable, manfully or bravely"" and 潔さ (isagiyosa) meaning ""purity or integrity"".It's very rarely used as a given name."
Isago,f,"砂, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,EE-SAH-GO,,,,,,,"From Japanese 砂 (isago) meaning ""sand"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
Isagoras,m,Ἰσαγόρας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/i.sa.ˈɣo.ras/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἴσος (isos) meaning ""equal"" combined with either ἀγορεύω (agoreuo) meaning ""to orate, to speak publicly"" or ἀγορά (agora), meaning ""assembly, speech, marketplace"". This name was borne by an Athenian aristocrat from the late 6th century BC."
Isah,f,,Sundanese,,,,,,,,"Sundanese diminutive of feminine names containing the sound isah (or other similar sounds), such as Aisyah."
Isahag,m,Իսահակ(Armenian),"Armenian (Western, Rare)",,,,,,,,Western Armenian transcription of Isahak.
Isahak,m,Իսահակ‎(Armenian),Armenian (Archaic),,,,,,,,"Derived from Իսահակ‎ (Isahak) by dropping the first letter. Իսահակ ‎(Isahak) was considered a more scholarly form of Isaac, whereas Սահակ ‎(Sahak) was a popular form."
