Isandro,m,,"Spanish (Latin American, Rare)|Portuguese (Rare)|Portuguese (Brazilian)|Antillean Creole",,,,,,,,Spanish and Portuguese form of Isander.
Isandros,m,Ἴσανδρος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek ἴσανδρος (isandros) meaning ""like a man"", composed of ἴσος (isos) meaning ""equal"" combined with ἀνδρός (andros) meaning ""of a man""."
Isane,f,勇音(Japanese Kanji),Japanese,EE-SA-NE,,,,,,,"""courage""; ""sound"""
Isanelly,f,,English (American),/ɪz.ə.ˈnɛl.i/(American English),,,,,,,Mix of Isabella and Nelly.
Isanfrid,m,,Germanic,,,,,,,,"Derived from isan, which comes from îsarn ""iron"", and Old High German fridu ""peace."""
Isang,m,이상(Korean Hangul)|伊桑(Korean Hanja),Korean|German (Rare)|Filipino,EE-SAHNG(Korean)|ee-zang(German)|/ˈi.saŋ/,,,,,,,"Isang is a traditional Korean name arbitrarily formed of two syllables. The hanja used are 伊 (i) ""he, she, it"" and 桑 (sang) ""mulberry tree"".The same characters are used in Chinese Bibles to render the Hebrew name Ethan, but this is probably a pure coincidence.Notable namesakes include the Korean-German composer Isang Yun and the German cellist Isang David Enders."
Isangar,m,,Germanic,,,,,,,,"The first element of this Germanic name is derived from isan, which comes from îsarn ""iron."" The second element is derived from Gothic gairu (gêr in Old High German) ""spear"", or from garva (garo in Old High German, and gearu in Anglo-Saxon) ""ready, prepared."""
Isangard,f,,Germanic,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from isan, which comes from îsarn ""iron."" The second element is derived from gardan ""to hedge in, to enclose, to fence in"" or from Gothic gards ""house, garden, (court)yard."""
Isangarda,f,,Occitan,,,,,,,,Occitan form of Isangard.
Isangild,f,,Germanic,,,,,,,,"Derived from isan, which comes from îsarn ""iron"", and Gothic gild ""sacrifice."""
Isangrim,m,,Germanic,,,,,,,,"Derived from isan, which comes from îsarn ""iron"", and Old Norse grîma ""mask."""
Isanguly,m,,Turkmen,,,,,,,,Not available.
Isanhar,m,,Germanic,,,,,,,,"Derived from isan, which comes from îsarn ""iron"", and Old High German hari ""army."""
Isanhard,m,,Germanic,,,,,,,,"Derived from isan, which comes from îsarn ""iron"", and Gothic hardus (hart in Old High German) ""brave, hardy."""
