Ixone,f,,Basque,[i.ˈʃo.ne],,,,,,,"This name was recorded in Vitoria/Gasteiz in 1513, with its original meaning unknown. However, it has been revived since the 1970's, probably interpreted as a combination of Basque ixo (meaning ""hush"") and the modern feminine suffix -ne."
Ixora,f,,"English (American, Modern, Rare)",,,,,,,,"From the name of a tropical flower (and genus) also known as West Indian jasmine. It is derived from Sanskrit ईश्वर (īśvara), itself derived from ईश् (īś) meaning ""to rule"" and वर (vará) meaning ""best""."
