Iyannough,m,,Wampanoag,HI-an-o,,,,,,,"Means ""Captain"" or ""One who intimidates"" in Wampanoag. It is the name of the chief sachem of the Mattakeeset tribe who helped the Pilgrims find a lost boy, John Billington."
Iyanu,,,Yoruba,[Ee] [Yawn] [New],,,,,,,"Yoruba name of Nigerian origin meaning ""miracle"" or ""wonder"". It is frequently used as a shortened form of Iyanuoluwa, meaning ""God's miracle"" or ""wonder of God"". Often given to signify a child as a special blessing, the name is popular for its deep spiritual significance and joyful connotation. The name is from the Nigerian American cartoon character Iyanu from the cartoon network show Iyanu: The Child of Wonder."
Iyanuolawa,,,Yoruba,[Ee] [Yawn] [New] [Oh] [La] [Wah],,,,,,,"Iyanuoluwa is a name of Yoruba origin, predominantly found in Nigeria and among the Nigerian diaspora. The name combines two Yoruba words: 'Iyanu' meaning 'miracle' or 'wonder' and 'Oluwa' meaning 'God' or 'the Lord.' When combined, Iyanuoluwa translates to 'God's miracle' or 'miracle of God,' expressing gratitude for a child seen as a divine blessing."
Iyar,,אייר(Hebrew),"Hebrew (Modern, Rare)",ee-YAHR,,,,,,,"Iyar is the eighth month in the jewish calendar. The name was brought from the Babylonian exile and originates from the Akkadian word for ""light"". His name is in the Bible ""Yerach Ziv"", means ""bright moon"". Making it related to the name Ziv."
Iyari,,,Mexican,,,,,,,,"Possibly from Huichol 'iyáari meaning ""heart, soul""."
Iyarri,m,,Near Eastern Mythology|Hittite Mythology|Luwian Mythology,,,,,,,,"Meaning uncertain. Iyarri was a god of plague and war in both the Hittite and Luwian pantheons. He is known from oaths sworn in the in the Šuppiluliuma-Šattiwazza treaty, where he is depicted as a man standing on the back of a lion."
