Iyel,m,"इयेल(Hindi, Marathi)",Sanskrit|Indian,eeyel(Sanskrit),,,,,,,"MEANING : to come, to cast, to become quiet Origin : Sanskrit Usage : Sanskrit, Indian, Tamil, Telugu, Nepali, Sinhala, Hindi, Sikh, Buddhist Sanskrit script : इयेल"
İyem,f,,Turkish,[ee-YEHM]|[i-YEM],,,,,,,"İyem is a contemporary Turkish name steeped in Proto-Turkic phonetic aesthetics, translating to ""my goodness,"" ""my health,"" ""my virtue,"" or ""my beautiful benefit."" It is an elegant product of modern onomastic revitalization, selected by parents who desire a deeply rooted, native name with a soft, melodic contemporary resonance.Linguistically, the name is built upon the classic Common Turkic path of moral and physical excellence. Its directly traced evolutionary chain flows smoothly from the Proto-Turkic eygü, which shifted into the Old Anatolian Turkish eyi, and ultimately crystallized into the modern Turkish iyi (good, wholesome, superior). By taking this pure root and anchoring it with the first-person singular possessive suffix -m, the name transforms into an intimate, protective declaration: ""My utmost good"" or ""My blessing.""Unlike names borrowed from classical literary languages, İyem relies on the raw, unadorned semantic power of native Turkic vocabulary. It serves as an onomastik amulet, celebrating the concepts of benevolence, spiritual health, and moral integrity.For parents, İyem represents a child who is viewed as a literal manifestation of goodness brought into the family's life. It suggests a personality that acts as a natural balm to those around her; someone characterized by a gentle temperament, ethical clarity, and an innate capacity to heal, comfort, and bring out the best in humanity."
Iyeoka,f,,Western African|Yoruba,,,,,,,,"Means ""mother who speaks the word"" in Yoruba.A famous bearer is Iyeoka Okoawo (1975-) a Nigerian-American poet, recording artist, singer, activist, educator and TEDGlobal Fellow."
Iyeon,f,이연(Korean Hangul),Korean,/i.jʌn/,,,,,,,"From Korean Hanja 伊(i) meaning ""this"" or 梨(i) meaning ""pear (fruit)"" combined with 緣(yeon) meaning ""fate"" or 姸(yeon) meaning ""beautiful, delicate"" or 軟(yeon) meaning ""soft, vulnerable"". Other combinations can also form this name."
I-yeong,f,이영(Korean Hangul),Korean,EE-YUNG,,,,,,,"From Sino-Korean 俐 ""smooth; active; clever, sharp"" and 英 ""petal, flower, leaf; brave, a hero""."
