İynem,f,,Turkish,eey-NEHM,,,,,,,"Derived from the Old Turkish root in- (inişmek) or iy- (iymek) meaning ""to get close, to trust, to warm up to someone"" or structurally related to iç (inner circle). With possessive and nominal suffixes, it translates to ""my companion, my close friend, my dear confidant,"" reflecting deep emotional proximity and absolute trust."
Iynx,f,Ιυνξ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"From Greek mythology. The name of a nymph who invented the magical love-charm known as the iynx--a spinning wheel with a wryneck bird attached, according to mythology she either used the charm to make Zeus fall in love with her or with another nymph, Io. Hera was furious and and transformed her into a wryneck bird. The name Iynx comes from the name of this charm, and is linked to the modern English word ""jinx""."
Iyo,f,"以世, 伊世, 以夜, 以予, 伊余, 依世, 依予, 惟世, etc.(Japanese Kanji)|イヨ(Japanese Katakana)|ゐよ, いよ(Japanese Hiragana)",Japanese|Manchu,EE-YO|Ee-yo,,,,,,,"From Japanese 以 (i) meaning ""compared to"" combined with 世 (yo) meaning ""world"". Other kanji combinations are possible."
