Janamejaya,m,जनमेजय(Sanskrit),Sanskrit|Hinduism,,,,,,,,"Possibly means ""causing men to tremble, terrifying men"" from Sanskrit जन (jana) meaning ""man, person"" and एजय (ejaya) meaning ""stirring, rousing, causing to tremble"". Alternately it may mean ""victorious from birth"", derived from जन्मन् (janman) meaning ""birth, origin"" and जय (jaya) meaning ""victory, conquest"".This was the name of a celebrated king of Hastinapura in northern India, a grandson of Abhimanyu and great-grandson of Arjuna, who reigned during the Middle Vedic period (12th to 10th centuries BC). He appears as a figure in the Mahabharata and the Puranas."
