Joh,,,Swedish (Rare)|Norwegian (Rare)|Danish (Rare),,,,,,,,"Truncated form of Johan, Johannes and its feminine equivalents. It's used as a short form of Johannesevangeliet (Gospel of John) in the Scandinavian translations of the Bible.Joh can be found in old church records in Sweden, but was never actually used as a name at the time. Priests sometimes used a truncated form of a name when making notes, for example Johan Eriksson became Joh Ersson and Anders Olofsson became And Olsson."
Joha,m,יוֹחָא(Ancient Hebrew),Biblical,,,,,,,,"A biblical name possibly meaning ""Jehovah gives life"".In the Bible, there are two people named Joha. The first, in 1 Chronicles 8:16, is part of the Genealogy of Saul the Benjamite. He is the son of Beriah, brother of Ahio, Shashak, Jeremoth, Zebadiah, Arad, Eder, Michael, and Ishpah; grandson of Elpaal, and great-grandson of Shaharaim and Hushim.The second Joha, in 1 Chronicles 11:45, is one of David's warriors. He is the son of Shimri and brother of Jediael."
