Juut,f,,Dutch,/ˈjyt/,,,,,,,"Variant of Juud.This name is extremely rare as an official name on birth certificates; it is almost always used informally.Also, juut is a Dutch slang word for a cop. It's etymologically related, for it is derived from the Dutch noun Jood meaning ""Jew"", which ultimately comes from Yehudah (see Judah)."
Juutarou,m,"受太郎, 重太郎, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,JOO-TAH-ṘO:,,,,,,,"From Japanese 受 (juu) meaning ""to receive; to accept"" or 重 (juu) meaning ""heavy, weighty"", 太 (ta) meaning ""thick, big"" combined with 郎 (rou) meaning ""son"". Other combinations of kanji characters are also possible.Famous bearers of this name are Japanese baseball pitcher Jutaro Kimura and Japanese mixed martial artist Jutaro Nakao."
Juuzou,m,"十三, 寿三, 柔三, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,JUU:-ZO:,,,,,,,"From Japanese 十 (juu) meaning ""ten"", 寿 (juu) meaning ""longevity, long life"" or 柔 (juu) meaning ""weak, softness"" combined with 三 (zou) meaning ""three"". Other kanji combinations are possible.Famous bearers of this name are Japanese actor and film director Juuzou Itami, Japanese baseball player Juuzou Sanada and Japanese manga artist Juuzou Yamasaki."
