Kachina,f,,"New World Mythology|English (American, Rare)|English (Canadian, Rare)",/kə.ˈtʃiː.nə/(English),,,,,,,"From the name of a type of supernatural entity in the mythology of the Hopi and other Pueblo peoples of the American Southwest. In the Hopi tradition, Katsinam (singular Katsina) are intermediaries between the human and spiritual realms, bringing blessings such as rain, fertility and health.In her Dictionary of Given Names (1934), Flora Haines Loughead claims that this is a 'North American Indian name' meaning ""sacred dancer"". Contrary to popular belief, it does not mean ""sacred dancer"" in Hopi, nor is it used as a name by the Hopi or Puebloan peoples themselves. There is a traditional dance called Kachina dance, but this masked ceremony, primarily performed during the agricultural cycle from December to July, is related to the calling of the supernatural beings."
