Kakeru,m,"翔, 駆, 翔琉, 翔瑠, 翔流, 駆流(Japanese Kanji)|かける(Japanese Hiragana)|カケル(Japanese Katakana)",Japanese,KAH-KE-ṘUU,,,,,,,"This name can be used on its own as 翔 (shou, kake.ru, to.bu) meaning ""fly, soar"" or it can be combined with 琉 (ryuu, ru) meaning ""gem, lapis lazuli, precious stone,"" 瑠 (ryuu, ru) meaning ""lapis lazuli"" or 流 (ryuu, ru, naga.re) meaning ""a sink, current, flow, forfeit.""Other forms of this name include 駆 (ku, ka.keru, ka.ru) which means ""advance, drive, gallop, impel, inspire, run"" and 駆流.As a verb, 翔 and 駆, added with る (ける for 駆), mainly means ""to run, to dash."" However, their second meanings are different for both, 翔る being ""to fly, to soar"" and 駆ける being ""to canter, to gallop"" (galloping as in galloping one's horse)."
Kakha,m,კახა(Georgian),Georgian,/kʼɑ.xɑ/,,,,,,,"Variant of Kakhi. This name is also frequently used as a short form of the related name Kakhaber, and sometimes also of unrelated names like Akaki and Aleksandre.Notable Georgian bearers of this name include the politicians Kakha Bendukidze (1956-2014), Aleksandre ""Kakha"" Lomaia (b. 1963) and Kakhaber ""Kakha"" Kaladze (b. 1978), the latter of which was previously a successful professional soccer player."
Kakhaber,m,კახაბერ(Georgian),Georgian,/kʼɑ.xɑ.bɛr/,,,,,,,"Means ""monk from Kakheti"", derived from Georgian კახი (kakhi) meaning ""of Kakheti"" (see Kakhi) combined with Georgian ბერი (beri) meaning ""monk"" (see Bera).Notable Georgian bearers of this name include the duke Kakhaber I Gurieli (14th century) and the soccer players Kakhaber ""Kakha"" Gogichaishvili (b. 1968) and Kakhaber ""Kakha"" Kakashvili (b. 1993)."
Kakhi,m,კახი(Georgian),Georgian,/kʼɑ.xi/,,,,,,,"Derived from Georgian კახი (kakhi), which can mean ""Kakhetian, of Kakheti"" (adjective) as well as ""Kakhetian, person from Kakheti"" (noun). Kakheti is the name of a region in eastern Georgia.This name is also frequently used as a short form of the given name Kakhaber, which is etymologically related.Notable Georgian bearers of this name include the actor Kakhi Kavsadze (b. 1935) and the soccer players Kakhi Asatiani (1947-2002) and Kakhaber ""Kakhi"" Makharadze (b. 1987)."
