Kakuyama-no-uneo-no-konoshita-ni-zasu-kami,f,香山の畝尾の木の下に坐す神(Kanji/Hiragana),Japanese Mythology,,,,,,,,"An epithet of the spring water goddess Nakisawame. It is derived from 香 (kaku) meaning ""pleasant scent, fragrance"", 山 (yama) meaning ""mountain"", の (no) meaning ""of"", 畝 (une) meaning ""raised earth in a field"" or ""rib"", 尾 (o) meaning ""tail"", ""foot of a mountain"" or ""the end of something"", の (no) meaning ""of"", 木 (ki) meaning ""tree, wood"", の (no) meaning ""of"", 下 (shita) meaning ""the below"", 坐 (za) meaning ""to sit, to bear fruit"" and 神 (kami) meaning ""god, deity, spirit""."
