Kalliteles,m,Καλλιτέλης(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun κάλλος (kallos) meaning ""beauty, nobility"" combined with the Greek noun τέλος (telos) meaning ""purpose, aim, goal"" as well as ""completion, accomplishment, fulfillment"" (see Aristotle).A known bearer of this name was the Olympic victor Kalliteles of Sparta. He had won the wrestling contest during the 68th Olympiad in 508 BC."
Kallithea,f,Καλλιθέα(Greek),Greek (Rare),kal-ih-THEE-ah,,,,,,,"Translated from Greek means “beautiful sight”. It is derived from the Greek words Kalli “beauty or splendid” and Thea “view or sight”. It is stylistically grouped with other traditional Greek names that also build on the kall- “beauty” root, such as Calliope “beautiful voice” and Callisto “most beautiful”. The name Kallithea itself appears more as a place name, frequently seen in Greece."
Kallityche,f,Καλλιτύχη(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek κάλλος (kallos) meaning ""beauty"" and τύχη (tyche) meaning ""chance, luck, fortune"", possibly meaning ""good luck""."
Kallixena,f,Καλλιξένα(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,Feminine form of Kallixenos.
Kallixenos,m,Καλλίξενος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from the Greek noun κάλλος (kallos) meaning ""beauty, nobility"" combined with Greek ξένος (xenos) meaning ""foreign, strange"" as well as ""foreigner, guest""."
