Kangal,m,,Medieval Turkic,/kɑːn-ɡɑːl/(KAHN-gahl),,,,,,,"The name Kangal is etymologically rooted in the Old Turkic word ""Kaŋ"" (meaning a solid, sturdy wheel) and the suffix ""-lı"" (possessive/relative), literally translating to ""those who have carts/wagons."" The Kanglı/Kangar were a prominent nomadic tribal confederation of the Central Asian steppes, known for their mobility and expert livestock management.Historically, it is widely accepted by researchers that the ancestors of the modern Kangal Shepherd Dog accompanied these nomadic Turkic tribes during their migration from Central Asia to the Anatolian plateau (specifically the Sivas region) starting in the 11th century. These tribes practiced pastoral nomadism, and the dogs were indispensable for protecting their large flocks from predators. As the tribes settled, the name of the tribe (Kanglı) and the geographical region (Kangal) became synonymous with the breed. The dogs are not merely ""from"" the town of Kangal; they are considered the direct descendants of the ancient livestock guardian dogs (such as the Tobet of Kazakhstan) brought to Anatolia by the Kanglı/Kangar migration waves.Note: It is important to distinguish the Turkic ethnonym Kangal/Kangli from the Greek loanword kanχáli (meaning a type of container or shell) and the Mongolian Khangal (often associated with religious or spiritual terms like ""fierce"" or ""sacred""), as these share no etymological connection to the Turkic tribal name."
