Kīngi,m,,Māori (Modern),/ˈkɪŋi/,,,,,,,"Māori translation of King. The names Kīngi ""king"" and Kuīni ""queen"", and English language equivalents King and Queen, are on a watchlist maintained by the New Zealand Registrar-General of Births, Deaths and Marriages, alongside other ranks and titles, offensive and obscene words. Currently the names can be used if prospective parents can prove that there is prior use of the names Kīngi and Kuīni in their family ancestry. Sir Kīngi Matutaera Īhaka (1921- 1993) was a New Zealand broadcaster, interpreter, and Anglican priest."
Kingjames,m,,"English (Modern, Rare)|English (African, Rare)",KING-JAYMZ(English),,,,,,,"Combination of King and James, likely used in reference to King James IV and I. (See Kingdavid)"
