Kleitanor,m,Κλειτάνωρ(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"The first element of this name is derived from the Greek adjective κλειτός (kleitos) meaning ""renowned, famous"". It is ultimately derived from the verb κλείω (kleio) meaning ""to celebrate, to make famous"", which is the Epic Greek form of the verb κλέω (kleo) meaning ""to celebrate, to glorify, to make famous"". As such, the first element is closely related to the Greek noun κλέος (kleos) meaning ""glory"".The second element of this name is derived from the Greek noun ἀνήρ (aner) meaning ""man"", so the meaning of this name as a whole is ""a famous man"".Also compare the Greek name Kleitandros, which is closely related and essentially means the same."
Kleitarchos,m,Κλείταρχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/klěː.tar.kʰos/(Attic Greek)|/ˈkli.tar.xos/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek κλειτός (kleitos) meaning ""renowned, splendid, famous"" combined with either ἀρχός (archos) meaning ""master, leader"" or ἀρχή (arche) meaning ""beginning, origin, source""."
Kleitomachos,m,Κλειτόμαχος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/kleː.tó.ma.kʰos/(Attic Greek)|/kli.ˈto.ma.xos/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek κλειτός (kleitos) meaning ""renowned, splendid, famous"" and μάχη (mache) meaning ""battle"".This name was borne by a Theban athlete from the 3rd century BC."
