Kosode,f,"小袖, etc.(Japanese Kanji)",Japanese,KO-SO-DE,,,,,,,"From Japanese 小袖 (kosode), a smaller Japanese robe similar to a kimono, worn either under a kimono or on its own.The word is a compound of 小 (ko) meaning ""small"" + 袖 (sode) meaning ""sleeve"". The ko here refers to the opening of the sleeve, which is smaller than that of the more formal kimono."
Kosono,f,晃園(Japanese Kanji),Japanese,,,,,,,,"Japanese feminine given name derived from 晃 meaning ""clear"" and 園 meaning ""garden""."
Kosovare,f,,Kosovar,,,,,,,,"Derived from Kosovo, the name of a partially recognized country in Europe that declared independence from Serbia in 2008. Kosovare Asllani (b. 1989) is a Swedish soccer player who plays for the Swedish national women's soccer team."
Kosovka,f,Косовка(Serbian),Serbian (Rare),,,,,,,,"A place name derived from Kosovo (Polje), meaning ""Blackbird's field"", the place where Serbian army fought the decisive battle against the Turks in 1389. In the epic poem, Kosovka devojka (""Girl from Kosovo"") stands as an idealized type of Serbian maiden, and is a symbol of fidelity, compassion, and charity."
Kössi,m,,Finnish,KUUS-si,,,,,,,Nickname for Kyösti. Kössi Kenguru was a classic children's cutout animation about a kangaroo.
Kossola,m,,Yoruba,,,,,,,,"Means ""I do not lose my fruits anymore"" or ""my children do not die any more"" in Yoruba.This was the birth name of Cudjoe Lewis (c. 1841-1935), given to him by his mother. Lewis was the third to last adult survivor of the Atlantic slave trade between Africa and the United States.He was later personally interviewed by the American author Zora Neale Hurston (1891-1960), who was intending to write a book about his life. The manuscript for the book was completed by the end of 1931, but she was unable to get it published during her lifetime. It was finally published in 2018 under the title Barracoon: The Story of the Last ""Black Cargo"". Hurston consistently spelled Lewis' birth name as Kossula in the book. It is unclear why other sources (such as Wikipedia) do not follow her example and instead use the spelling Kossola."
