Ktesias,m,Κτησίας(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/ktɛː.sí.aːs/(Attic Greek)|/kti.ˈsi.as/(Koine Greek)|/ˈtiː.ʒəs/(English),,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek κτῆσις (ktesis) meaning ""acquisition, possession, property"", which is ultimately derived from κτάομαι (ktaomai) meaning ""to acquire, to procure for oneself"" as well as ""to possess"". Compare Ktesios."
Ktesibios,m,Κτησίβιος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/ktɛː.sí.bi.os/(Attic Greek)|/kti.ˈsi.βi.os/(Koine Greek)|/kti.ˈsi.vi.os/(Byzantine Greek),,,,,,,"Means ""landowner, one who lives from his property"", derived from Greek κτῆσις (ktesis) meaning ""acquisition, possession, property"", itself from κτάομαι (ktaomai) meaning ""to acquire, to procure for oneself"" as well as ""to possess"", and βιος (bios) meaning ""life"".Alternatively, the second element could derive from βία (bia) meaning ""force, power""."
Ktesikles,m,Κτησικλῆς(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek κτῆσις (ktesis) meaning ""acquisition, possession, property"", which is ultimately derived from κτάομαι (ktaomai) meaning ""to acquire; to possess"", combined with κλέος (kleos) meaning ""glory""."
Ktesios,m,Κτήσιος(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek (Rare),,,,,,,,"Means ""domestic, belonging to property or the household; acquired, owned"" in Ancient Greek, derived from κτῆσις (ktesis) meaning ""acquisition, possession, property"", which is ultimately derived from κτάομαι (ktaomai) meaning ""to acquire; to possess"". This was the name of a minor household god in Greek mythology."
Ktesiphon,m,Κτησιφῶν(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek,/ktɛː.si.pʰɔ̂ːn/(Attic Greek)|/kti.si.ˈɸon/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Possibly derived from Greek κτῆσις (ktesis) meaning ""acquisition, possession, property"", itself from κτάομαι (ktaomai) meaning ""to acquire, to procure for oneself"" as well as ""to possess"", and either φωνή (phone) meaning ""voice"" or φῶς (phos) meaning ""light"". Alternatively, it could derive from the name of an ancient city, hellenized from Middle Persian *Tesifon."
Ktesippos,m,Κτήσιππος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek|Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Greek κτάομαι (ktaomai) meaning ""to acquire"" and ἵππος (hippos) meaning ""horse""."
