Laetus,m,,Ancient Roman|History (Ecclesiastical),[ˈɫae̯.tʊs](Latin),,,,,,,"From a Roman cognomen meaning ""happy, cheerful"", possibly related to the term Laeti, a foreigner permitted to settle in the Roman Empire, of the same meaning. Quintus Aemilius Laetus was prefect of the Roman imperial bodyguard. This was also the name of a 5th-century French saint, often transcribed as Lié."
Laeus,m,Λαιος(Ancient Greek),Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Λαιος (Laios), which is of unknown meaning. This was the name of a king of Thebes in Greek mythology, the husband of Jocasta. Due to a prophecy that he would be killed by his son, Laius left his infant Oedipus for dead. The boy survived but was ignorant of his true parentage. Years later he unwittingly killed Laius in a quarrel on the road."
