Lalarukh,f,لاله رخ(Persian)|لالہ رخ(Urdu)|लालारुख(Hindi),Persian|Urdu|Hindi,,,,,,,,"Deriving from the Persian words laleh (meaning ""tulip"") and rukh (meaning ""face"" or ""cheek""), Lalarukh can be translated as meaning ""face like a tulip"" or ""tulip-cheeked"". It is traditionally seen as a term of endearment in Persian poetry.Lalarukh was popularised in south Asia by Irish poet Thomas Moore's 1817 collection, ""Lalla Rookh"", which links the name to Mughal history. In these poems, Lalla Rookh is the tale's princess and heroine, and Moore drew inspiration for this tale based on Indian writer Inayatullah Kambu's 1784 collection, ""Bahar-i Danish""."
