Lampedo,f,Λαμπεδώ(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology,,,,,,,,"Derived from Ancient Greek λάμπω (lampo) meaning ""to shine"". Compare Lampetia. This was the name of an Amazonian queen in Greek mythology. A chapter is dedicated to Lampedo and her sister Marpesia in Boccaccio's 'On Famous Women' (1374)."
Lampetia,f,"Λαμπετίη, Λαμπετία(Ancient Greek)",Greek Mythology (Latinized),/ˌlæm.ˈpiː.ʃə/(English),,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Λαμπετίη (Lampetiê) meaning ""the shining one, the lustrous one"", a derivative of λαμπέτις (lampetis) ""shining, brilliant, lustrous"", ultimately from λάμπω (lampo) ""to shine"". In Greek mythology Lampetia and her sister Phaethousa were two nymphs who pastured the sacred herds of the sun god Helios on the mythical island of Thrinakie, or Thrinacia."
