Lão Bạng,m,,History,/laːw˦ˀ˥ ʔɓaːŋ˧˨ʔ/(Vietnamese)|/laːw˧˨ ʔɓaːŋ˨˩ʔ/(Vietnamese)|/laːw˨˩˦ ʔɓaːŋ˨˩˨/(Vietnamese),,,,,,,"From the Chinese idiom 老蚌生珠 (lão bạng sinh châu) literally meaning ""an old oyster produces pearls"", figuratively referring to having a child in old age. This was the name of Mai Lão Bạng (1866-1942), a Catholic friar and Vietnamese revolutionary, whose birth name was Châu. His father was in his later years when he was born."
Laodamas,m,Λαοδάμας(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology|Ancient Greek,/leɪ.ˈɒ.dəm.əs/(English)|/laː.o.dá.maːs/(Attic Greek)|/la.o.ˈða.mas/(Koine Greek),,,,,,,"Means ""man-tamer, tamer of people"" in Ancient Greek, derived from λαός (laos) meaning ""people, folk"" and δαμάζω (damazo) meaning ""to tame, subdue""."
Laodamia,f,Λαοδάμεια(Ancient Greek),Greek Mythology (Latinized)|Ancient Greek (Latinized),,,,,,,,"Latinized form of Greek Λαοδάμεια (Laodameia), a feminine form of Laodamas. This was the name of several characters in Greek legend, including the mother of Sarpedon, who was a daughter of Bellerophon. William Wordsworth used it for the title heroine of his poem ""Laodamia"" (1815)."
